window.CPP_EN=window.CPP_EN||{};window.CPP_EN["investigations"]={"investigations-1":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-1-e0\">1.1 The Nature of Investigation Management</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Investigation</strong>, according to the ANSI/ASIS INV.1-2015 standard (ANSI: American National Standards Institute; ASIS: ASIS International):<ul>\n<li>It is a fact-finding process.</li>\n<li>It gathers and documents information in a logical, methodical, and lawful way.</li>\n<li>Its aim is to reach, objectively, a reasonable conclusion based on the facts found.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The investigator may not be asked for conclusions. Their results and interviews guide others, for example legal counsel.</li>\n<li>The definition applies equally in the public and private sectors. It covers everything from background checks to administrative inquiries and criminal matters.</li>\n<li><strong>Management</strong>, according to Merriam-Webster (2020): the act of conducting or supervising something. Also: “judicious use of means to accomplish an end.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Purposes of an investigation (Webster University 2020)</h4>\n<p>Especially in business, the purpose falls into one or more of these 8 categories:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Thoroughly document incidents, for example those reported to security personnel or officers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Identify the cause of undesirable situations when malicious activity is suspected. Examples: notable losses, a sudden drop in revenue or market share, unexplained shrinkage, inventory discrepancies, defalcation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Document and correlate the facts of any situation or allegation of misconduct or inappropriate behavior.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Identify, interview, or monitor suspects in a crime or misconduct.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Gather information that proves or refutes an allegation, or that implicates or exonerates a suspect.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Allow a trust-level or suitability determination. It may concern a person (e.g., personnel security clearance) or an organization (e.g., a potential partner or acquisition target).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Conduct threat assessments to prevent workplace violence, whether internal or from third parties.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Gather crime data and other material in a site inspection, to mitigate the company’s liability and risks.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Defalcation</strong>, according to 1.1: failing to account properly for money held as a fiduciary.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book defines “defalcation” in two ways. In 1.1 it is failing to account for money held in a fiduciary capacity. In 1.9.1 it is misappropriating entrusted property, which is the proper definition of embezzlement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Purpose, at two levels</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>What the purpose does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Case level</td>\n<td>Sets the context of the case. Keeps focused those who work it and those who manage it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic level</td>\n<td>Dictates the planning, organization, equipment, staffing, and preparation needed.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Unit heads and security directors must anticipate all possible purposes. They concentrate resources on the most frequent cases, but think ahead about the others.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e1\">1.2 Qualities of an Effective Investigation</h3><ul>\n<li>There are five attributes:<ol>\n<li>objectivity;</li>\n<li>thoroughness;</li>\n<li>relevance;</li>\n<li>accuracy;</li>\n<li>timeliness.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Management must ensure them at the strategic level and in each case. It does so with two things:<ul>\n<li>a system that facilitates them;</li>\n<li>good case review and quality control.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The investigative process is science and art. Much of the art lies in these qualities.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quality</th>\n<th>Short definition (book’s box)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Objectivity</td>\n<td>Remain impartial and use a rational investigative hypothesis, without introducing prejudice.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thoroughness</td>\n<td>Follow all relevant leads to their logical conclusion. Corroborate all key findings.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relevance</td>\n<td>Decide whether information applies to the case, with just the right breadth and depth. Without accumulating so much that it confuses, bogs down, or hides the truth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accuracy</td>\n<td>The credibility of a source. It may be human, physical or electronic evidence, or the result of an observation or surveillance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timeliness</td>\n<td>Finish quickly, but not too quickly. Resist outside pressures that rush or slow the case and harm its resolution.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>1.2.1 Objectivity</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> the ability to remain objective and focused.<ul>\n<li>Information, evidence, and interviews are reported without internal or external bias.</li>\n<li>Prejudices and unfounded hypotheses are avoided.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It requires setting aside feelings, opinions, and outside influences. It is among the hardest things, even for an expert, because it goes against human nature.</li>\n<li><strong>Working hypothesis:</strong><ul>\n<li>It can be formed at the start or later, and change one or more times.</li>\n<li>It is a valid tool as long as it respects objectivity.</li>\n<li>A logical hypothesis starts from understanding the information, the evidence, and the mission context.</li>\n<li>If it is not based on evidence, confirmed information, and known facts, it usually leads down the wrong path.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Typical mistake: trying to prove a theory instead of following every lead to its logical conclusion.</li>\n<li>Do not jump to conclusions without independently corroborating the facts. This holds even in the face of seemingly overwhelming evidence. The facts must speak for themselves.</li>\n<li>Each investigator must recognize their biases and neutralize them, including when forming the hypothesis. Findings form impressions, not the other way around.</li>\n<li><strong>Approach and demeanor:</strong><ul>\n<li>The first thing is to project objectivity.</li>\n<li>This is achieved by watching your words, facial expressions, and body language.</li>\n<li>If you act like someone who knows what they are doing, others will assume it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>An unsolved murder case:</strong><ul>\n<li>Investigators assumed the culprit was a prison escapee, because his father lived next door to the victim.</li>\n<li>While chasing him, they ignored leads and physical evidence.</li>\n<li>When he was captured, it turned out he was in a county jail for public intoxication when the crime occurred.</li>\n<li>Years later, a cold case investigator followed the physical evidence.</li>\n<li>A bloody fingerprint matched the victim’s boyfriend. Time-clock records disproved his alibi.</li>\n<li>The boyfriend confessed, gave details that confirmed it, and asked why it had taken so long.</li>\n<li>Lesson: a prior hypothesis blinds the investigator; follow the evidence.</li>\n<li>Note: the book tells this case twice (pp. 3 and 4), with details that complement each other.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2.2 Thoroughness</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Review all leads, and the key ones more than once, to check their consistency.</li>\n<li>Corroborate what is important with different sources and, where possible, of different types. Types the book cites (open list):<ul>\n<li>interviewees or witnesses;</li>\n<li>subject matter experts;</li>\n<li>physical evidence;</li>\n<li>electronic evidence;</li>\n<li>public records;</li>\n<li>surveillance results;</li>\n<li>open sources;</li>\n<li>databases.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Also corroborate the statements of witnesses and of the subject of interest.</li>\n<li><strong>A case with 13 victims:</strong> for six months, a suspect in jail gave detailed alibis for each date. The investigator showed that all were false. The suspect confessed when confronted with his deception.</li>\n<li>Some leads generate others, secondary and tertiary. They seem superfluous, but may be valuable directly or as corroboration.</li>\n<li>Two things must be balanced: following every possible lead, but without accumulating so much information that it confuses or hinders.</li>\n<li>That is why independent case management matters, in both the public and private sectors.</li>\n<li>There is often pressure to cut the investigation short for lack of time or resources, especially in business.</li>\n<li>Document all leads and all information. What seems irrelevant today may be important later.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2.3 Relevance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Information must relate to the subject of the investigation and be at the right level of detail. Deciding how much to dig is another difficult part of the job.</li>\n<li>It has two determinants:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Determinant</th>\n<th>What it decides</th>\n<th>Book example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Spectrum of details</td>\n<td>How wide a net is cast</td>\n<td>Robbery in an office building: do you investigate construction at the building next door? Answer: “maybe.” Child sexual abuse case in a neighborhood: the dates coincided with the start and end of a local construction project. The suspect came from the crew and confessed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Depth of details</td>\n<td>How deep you dig</td>\n<td>Asked “What did you do yesterday?”, one person answers with excessive detail (bed, teeth, face, shaving, cereal, plate to the sink). Another says only “I got up, went to work, came back.” The right answer is in between.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scripting an alibi:</strong> an employee with access to the safe volunteered, without being asked, a minute-by-minute log of where he had been. The sheets bore a date prior to the theft.</li>\n<li>With some interviewees it is hard to get the right depth:<ul>\n<li>the less intelligent;</li>\n<li>the very intelligent (e.g., scientists and engineers);</li>\n<li>the nervous;</li>\n<li>those in a hurry;</li>\n<li>those hiding something;</li>\n<li>the very quiet;</li>\n<li>the very talkative.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The skilled interviewer steers the conversation to the right detail without diminishing the value of the information. They use open-ended questions to keep information flowing.</li>\n<li><strong>Cause and effect:</strong><ul>\n<li>Bad credit history in a background investigation: it is not enough to report it; its cause must be found and verified.</li>\n<li>It may be identity theft.</li>\n<li>Or debts inherited on the death of a close relative, until the estate is settled.</li>\n<li>If it is already resolved, the weakness disappears.</li>\n<li>The candidate’s criminal record: it is wise to discuss it with the candidate, for three reasons.</li>\n<li>Court and police records show the conviction, not the facts behind it.</li>\n<li>Records may be incomplete.</li>\n<li>Government guidelines call for assessing the relevance of the conviction and possible rehabilitation.</li>\n<li>If the candidate admits and describes a disqualifying crime, they are confessing by self-disclosure.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2.4 Accuracy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>According to the National Research Council (2014), eyewitnesses are key to identifying culprits, but they make mistakes. Their memory can be altered even by the police procedures that test it.</li>\n<li><strong>Innocence Project (2020):</strong> erroneous witness identifications contributed to more than 70% of wrongful convictions in the United States later overturned with post-conviction DNA evidence.</li>\n<li>The mental processes that organize what the senses take in make errors.<ul>\n<li>In the clear air of Arizona, a mountain seems to be half a mile away and is three miles away.</li>\n<li>In a training exercise, one observer described a suspect as short, chubby, with a green golf shirt, light brown hair, and a mustache.</li>\n<li>Another observer described him as thin, of medium height, with a blue T-shirt, dark hair, and a mustache.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In business, many employee tips about losses, sexual harassment, and other incidents are credible. But employees also give many false or misunderstood tips.</li>\n<li><strong>Verification rules:</strong><ul>\n<li>Check frequently, as in a financial audit, which assesses the accuracy, timeliness, and completeness of records.</li>\n<li>If something can be physically measured, it must be measured.</li>\n<li>If an informant is the only source of a key fact, it must at least be tested for consistency.</li>\n<li>Look for inherent contradictions in the information itself. Example: a car trip that, given distance and time, would require traveling at 135 miles per hour.</li>\n<li>Examine the informant’s motives, because they can bias how complete and accurate their information is.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Accept nothing at first glance:</strong><ul>\n<li>Date and time stamps from computers, photo or video cameras, and cell phones fail if the internal clock is set wrong.</li>\n<li>An access control record showing someone passing through a door is suspect until two things are checked.</li>\n<li>Whether the system can be tampered with or spoofed.</li>\n<li>Whether another person used that person’s card.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Background and preemployment:</strong> some believe the listed references the candidate gives are useless, because they are friends. Experience says otherwise: they often provide unfavorable information. That fact forces you to think about the interviewee’s motives.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2.5 Timeliness</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>You must act without delay to take advantage of memory and evidence before they are lost. Three rules:<ol>\n<li>Open the investigation as soon as possible.</li>\n<li>Complete it as quickly as possible.</li>\n<li>Do not close it prematurely.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>They seem contradictory; the key is balance.</li>\n<li>ASIS International standard (2015): investigate as soon as possible, respecting the requirements of the jurisdiction. This prevents human, physical, or electronic evidence from degrading.</li>\n<li>Witnesses report better with the event fresh. Over time:<ul>\n<li>memory fades;</li>\n<li>it is contaminated with rationalizations, as they think about the consequences of their answers;</li>\n<li>it is colored by news, talks with other witnesses, and other influences.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Surveillance video can be lost if there is a delay. Many systems record a fixed number of days and then overwrite.</li>\n<li>Finishing quickly serves to:<ul>\n<li>save resources;</li>\n<li>return operations to normal quickly;</li>\n<li>deter other potential offenders;</li>\n<li>show professionalism, which increases the effectiveness of the investigation or security team.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>You must not rush at the expense of quality, thoroughness, or accuracy.<ul>\n<li>Typical pressures to close: lack of time or resources, publicity, and political factors. Where possible, do not give in.</li>\n<li>The unit head or security director may have to explain to senior management the harms of rushing, including greater legal risk.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Not all cases are solved, either quickly or ever. If management believes otherwise, it expects too much of the investigative unit (IU) or the security department.</li>\n<li>Remedy: regular, timely communication between security or investigation managers and the organization’s decision makers.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e2\">1.3 Public Sector versus Private Sector Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>Tools, techniques, and processes are similar. The fundamental difference is in whom each serves.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Public sector (generally, law enforcement)</th>\n<th>Private sector</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Aim</td>\n<td>Serve and protect society at large</td>\n<td>Protect the interests of the company that employs it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How it is measured</td>\n<td>Number of arrests and convictions</td>\n<td>Recovery, restitution, litigation mitigation, and risk reduction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What it seeks in an internal crime</td>\n<td>Prosecute and imprison the offender</td>\n<td>Some combination of accepting restitution and taking administrative or employment action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard of proof</td>\n<td>Often higher</td>\n<td>Dictated by the outcome sought in the case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>A specific act or criminal violation</td>\n<td>Broader: policy violations and underlying system problems that call for changing processes or policies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Public strategies for a series of thefts:</strong><ul>\n<li>proactive, with surveillance;</li>\n<li>reactive, assigning more personnel to solve the cases. Crime scene investigation is a reactive measure.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Public agencies with an investigative mission:</strong><ul>\n<li>national and local law enforcement;</li>\n<li>regional or special-purpose units (airport, transit, or park authorities);</li>\n<li>intelligence and counterintelligence agencies;</li>\n<li>inspector general offices;</li>\n<li>regulatory agencies that enforce rules or administrative laws.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Private actors</strong>, in two categories:<ol>\n<li>In-house investigative or security units within a company or organization.</li>\n<li>Companies that provide investigative services or special expertise on a fee basis.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The chapter focuses on in-house units, but mentions outside providers where applicable.</li>\n<li><strong>The role of private investigation is growing because:</strong><ul>\n<li>the police with jurisdiction often decide not to investigate because of their workload and other priorities;</li>\n<li>public resources are limited and some crimes are of no interest to conventional police;</li>\n<li>the police are barred from investigating civil or internal company matters, unless there is a specific crime.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The difference affects four things: legal authority, resource allocation, program management, and outcomes.</li>\n<li><strong>Burden of proof:</strong><ul>\n<li>In US criminal courts, the prosecutor must prove beyond a reasonable doubt.</li>\n<li>In civil matters, preponderance of evidence decides.</li>\n<li>Prosecuting someone usually requires more proof than disciplining or firing them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Even if restitution and dismissal are chosen over criminal prosecution, the investigation must be done professionally and reach a reasonable conclusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Six differences between public and private investigation (ASIS International investigations standard, 2015)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Powers of arrest.</li>\n<li>Search and seizure.</li>\n<li>Testimony.</li>\n<li>Prosecution.</li>\n<li>Due process.</li>\n<li>Consequences.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Acceptable outcomes and the role of legal counsel</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>At the start of the private case, the company must define which outcome or outcomes it accepts. To do so it considers:<ul>\n<li>the legal requirements of the jurisdiction;</li>\n<li>current or under-negotiation union contracts;</li>\n<li>public and investor relations;</li>\n<li>what the company did before in similar cases.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It may be wise to have clear policies on outcomes and the measures that follow. Legal counsel is always consulted.</li>\n<li>A formal relationship must be created between the investigation and legal counsel, in-house or outside.<ul>\n<li>This protects the results, exhibits, and data from discovery by the opposing party in a trial.</li>\n<li>The information becomes <strong>attorney work product</strong>.</li>\n<li>The investigator communicates only through the assigned attorney, or with those the attorney approves.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Other features of private units</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Corporate investigators usually focus on one or a few types of case.<ul>\n<li>Example: at one company, 90% of the time goes to theft.</li>\n<li>Other units focus on safety violations, employee misconduct, sexual harassment, or property damage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Even so, they must be ready for unusual cases with little notice. There are two routes:<ul>\n<li>maintain some capability of their own (expertise, training, equipment);</li>\n<li>have outside resources to call on.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>IU leaders must make their expertise known and build relationships throughout the company. Otherwise the company will assign investigations to human resources or legal counsel and will not use the IU.</li>\n<li>Corporate IUs also handle external matters. This happens at internet providers, telecommunications companies, insurers, and utilities, whose services attract fraud or illegal use.</li>\n<li>Internal problems arise from missing or weak internal controls.</li>\n<li>Many companies separate the internal-case unit from the external-case one. Advantages:<ul>\n<li>focus on a single activity;</li>\n<li>special expertise;</li>\n<li>liaison networks;</li>\n<li>contacts with prosecutors;</li>\n<li>less confusion about outcomes and processes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Some organizations hire outside firms for their investigations, internal or external.</li>\n<li>In many places, the public sector sometimes hires a private firm for specific expertise (e.g., forensic accounting) within a police investigation. This may signal more public-private cooperation.</li>\n<li>Private IUs also use outside experts, for example in the face of acts or threats of workplace violence. Assessing the threat and interviewing the person of interest calls for experience the IU almost never has.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e3\">1.4 Management Issues in Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>Management is key to the success of security and criminal investigations in business.</li>\n<li>Managing investigations uses the five basic management functions. All five apply at the three levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.1 — Investigative management functions at each level (source: Webster University 2020)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Five functions (apply at all three levels)</th>\n<th>Three levels</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Plan · 2. Organize · 3. Direct · 4. Coordinate · 5. Control</td>\n<td>Strategic · Operational · Case</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>What it covers</th>\n<th>Topics it addresses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic</td>\n<td>High-level management of the whole unit and its relationship with senior management. Legal counsel should take part here, to focus cases according to company policy and civil or criminal law. Investigators are formally hired here, to have the protection of attorney work product.</td>\n<td>Who leads the function. Where it fits in the structure. What its strategic goals and objectives are. Where it focuses its efforts. How financial and personnel resources are assigned to it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational</td>\n<td>The technical aspects of the function and how it works within its department.</td>\n<td>Caseload, case evaluation, quality control, investigative policies and procedures, report formats, liaison, team composition, supplies and equipment, evidence management, outside contracts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Case</td>\n<td>Each individual investigation (Webster University 2020).</td>\n<td>The assigned investigators, investigative techniques, and case management protocols.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The person responsible for the function may be the IU head, the chief security officer (CSO), the security director, or the director of investigations. The book allows any other title.</li>\n<li>That person must think at once about the three levels and the factors that cut across them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Goals, objectives, and metrics</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Goals</td>\n<td>Long-term, overall end states. They may be expressed in general terms. They must be consistent with the organization’s strategic direction. They are included in the IU’s mission statement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Objectives</td>\n<td>They come from the goals, but are more specific and may be more immediate. They are generally measurable. They serve to measure the unit’s progress, success, or achievements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>The specific and measurable. They serve to evaluate people, teams, and organizations. They must be tied to the general goals, not be an end in themselves.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book calls “specific, measurable goals” “metrics.” Just before, it says goals are general and that what is measurable are objectives.</li>\n<li>Every investigative strategy must be compatible with the organization’s strategic business plan.</li>\n<li>An investigation’s objectives are tactical and respond to what is expected in the short term.<ul>\n<li>The IU head consults the tactical business plan so they are compatible.</li>\n<li>Example: a complaint arises within an undercover project. If an investigation is opened, its objectives must be consistent with those of the project.</li>\n<li>Changes to operational objectives are considered carefully and treated as “change orders,” as in project management.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Ethics</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a theme that cuts across the three levels.</li>\n<li>Organizational measures with great impact:<ul>\n<li>publish written standards of ethical conduct;</li>\n<li>adopt a zero-tolerance policy toward ethical lapses;</li>\n<li>sanction those who commit them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Without sound ethical standards, the effectiveness of the IU and of each investigator suffers. Both depend on their credibility and respectability to gather, corroborate, and integrate facts.</li>\n<li>Almost all human sources provide information out of some degree of trust in the investigator or agency:<ul>\n<li>victims;</li>\n<li>witnesses;</li>\n<li>first responders;</li>\n<li>liaison contacts;</li>\n<li>subject matter experts;</li>\n<li>informants.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A reputation for lacking ethics reduces the quantity, accuracy, and candor of information. It also lowers respect for the unit within the company, and with it how the unit is used and how its members are treated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Unethical or dishonest conduct that is unacceptable (9):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Opening, closing, speeding up, or slowing down cases for convenience, because of a relationship with the victim, the suspect, or another key player, or for personal gain.</li>\n<li>Mishandling evidence to influence the outcome.</li>\n<li>Mishandling evidence or information through incompetence.</li>\n<li>Fabricating evidence or information.</li>\n<li>Making improper threats or promises when interviewing a suspected employee.</li>\n<li>Leaking or compromising sensitive investigative information.</li>\n<li>Using techniques that are unscientific, unreliable, inappropriate, or needlessly heavy-handed.</li>\n<li>Mistreating liaison contacts, for example by giving them misleading or false information, or taking advantage of the relationship.</li>\n<li>Lying in a judicial or administrative proceeding.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Any of these behaviors merits the most severe punishment.</li>\n<li>It taints all of the offender’s prior cases: everything they did in their post before being discovered becomes suspect. In addition, it can be demanded as evidence at trial (discoverable) and is often admitted.</li>\n<li>Lack of ethics often leads to replacing IU leaders.<ul>\n<li>Case: a senior vice president of corporate investigations was fired. It came out that some memoranda addressed to local management were not made known in time to the other employees. The jury found the company negligent for failure to warn.</li>\n<li>Pending: the case appears cut off in the text; it is not clear what specific ethical lapse was charged.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Lack of ethics also exposes the organization to civil or criminal liability.</li>\n<li>IU heads must make ethics a pillar of their operations, procedures, and relationships, and instill it in staff.</li>\n<li>A case is resolved sooner by the constant pursuit of truth than by aggressive tactics or deception.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e4\">1.5 Organizing for Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>If there is no IU, or one is being expanded or redesigned, one of the first decisions is how to organize the function.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of organization</th>\n<th>Logical option</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Small, with few investigative needs</td>\n<td>Outsource to an outside agency or service. Many have consultants or private detectives on retainer to respond quickly. You pay only per case, and only when needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large, or with constant need</td>\n<td>A full-time investigator or team may be justified.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Most medium and large companies have enough work to keep an investigator or a team busy. All the more so because investigations and regulatory compliance are increasingly complex.</li>\n<li><strong>Matters that call for investigation in business:</strong><ul>\n<li>personnel background investigations (BIs);</li>\n<li>product security or product tampering;</li>\n<li>white-collar crimes: procurement fraud, other types of fraud, and embezzlement;</li>\n<li>employee misconduct;</li>\n<li>theft and pilferage;</li>\n<li>information loss;</li>\n<li>due diligence;</li>\n<li>abuse of information technology (IT) systems;</li>\n<li>regulatory compliance.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance investigation:</strong> its main purpose is to provide guidance on possible violations of regulatory requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Cost, budget, and return</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Cost almost always weighs heavily in deciding whether to outsource or have in-house capability. Cost elements:<ul>\n<li>personnel expenses;</li>\n<li>contracts and retainers;</li>\n<li>equipment;</li>\n<li>consumables;</li>\n<li>overhead;</li>\n<li>labor;</li>\n<li>office expenses.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Return on investment (ROI) can be shown with restitution figures.</li>\n<li>Cost-benefit analysis helps decide whether to outsource the unit.</li>\n<li><strong>Beware of restitution as the main proof of value:</strong><ul>\n<li>Some suspects agree to pay to avoid criminal prosecution and later claim they were coerced.</li>\n<li>The investigator must not touch restitution until the subject confesses or the facts allow a reliable conclusion.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The budget is hard to predict:</strong> one or two unexpected large cases drive up costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommended bases for estimating it:</strong><ul>\n<li>historical data;</li>\n<li>quantitative estimates of the risk avoided;</li>\n<li>support for the organization’s goals;</li>\n<li>anticipated changes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Changes in the company (growth, new facilities, international expansion, new markets) and changes in regulation can move the budget a great deal.</li>\n<li>Without a history, you must benchmark against companies similar in size, location, and mission.</li>\n<li>These costs must also be added:<ul>\n<li>equipment;</li>\n<li>supplies;</li>\n<li>outside services;</li>\n<li>local and out-of-area travel;</li>\n<li>administrative support;</li>\n<li>contract or personnel management.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Section 1.9.2 expands on the topic of funding.</li>\n<li>Outsourced investigations can be charged to the business area where they are done or for which they are done. That cost burdens the user’s budget, and only for the actual service.</li>\n<li>The IU must also set spending limits for outside consultants. Setting timelines and benchmarks allows information to be reviewed and a cost-benefit analysis to be done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.2 — Investigative services: outsourced versus in-house (source: Innovative Protection Solutions, LLC, 2020)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Outsourcing</th>\n<th>Internal unit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Advantages</td>\n<td>1. Staff can be expanded or reduced according to actual need. 2. Equipment, supplies, and training become the contractor’s responsibility. 3. Specialists and experts are called in when needed. 4. May be more cost-effective. 5. Reduces overhead. 6. Investigators may have a broader base of expertise and experience. 7. Gives the investigation an independent, external, and objective image.</td>\n<td>1. The company fully controls the process and the people. 2. Allows closer liaison with local agencies and internal contacts. 3. Better protects sensitive information and keeps it within the organization. 4. Investigators usually know the organization, its mission, its culture, and its structure much better.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disadvantages</td>\n<td>1. Quality control is left almost entirely in the contractor’s hands. 2. Investigators may know less about the client’s company, mission, organization, and culture. 3. Requires contract management effort.</td>\n<td>1. Maintaining in-house capability is expensive. 2. Equipment must be maintained that may go unused for long periods. 3. A staff must be maintained, with its training, licenses, and personnel management. 4. Especially if there is a trial, the investigation can be tainted by being done under the company’s direction.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e5\">1.6 Fitting the Investigative Mission into the Organization</h3><ul>\n<li>The place and use of the IU vary greatly among organizations.<ul>\n<li>Many companies have the IU within the security department or under the CSO.</li>\n<li>Others have no in-house investigative capability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Ideally, the structure is decided with a needs assessment and a cost-benefit analysis.</li>\n<li>It is often decided arbitrarily, without criteria or without context.</li>\n<li>Senior security, assets protection, or risk management professionals should advise management. The advice covers the real investigative needs and the most effective structure to meet them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Most common structures (7)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Reports to</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Separate IU, with unit head or senior investigator</td>\n<td>Corporate security director or CSO</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Separate IU, with unit head or senior investigator</td>\n<td>Risk management or assets protection director</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Separate IU, with unit head or senior investigator</td>\n<td>Legal department</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Specialized investigations supported by internal audit or by IT</td>\n<td>Pending: the book does not say to whom they report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Independent function, such as an inspector general or equivalent</td>\n<td>Directly to senior executive management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The security director investigates in person, because there is no IU or investigator</td>\n<td>Not applicable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>The security director oversees an outsourced capability. Calls an outside firm or investigator under a prior agreement</td>\n<td>Not applicable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Regional IUs:</strong> in large or widely dispersed companies. Advantages:<ul>\n<li>they save travel time and expenses;</li>\n<li>their investigators know the local scene: culture, geography, procedures, laws, and regulations;</li>\n<li>they work better with local liaison contacts.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Units by business area.</strong> Examples:<ul>\n<li>one IU for the commercial division and another for the government division;</li>\n<li>one for consumer products and another for business-to-business.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Areas of focus</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The main need varies. In some organizations it is personnel screening. In others it is misconduct, external theft, or fraud prevention and detection.</li>\n<li>The main focus guides how:<ul>\n<li>the unit is organized;</li>\n<li>staff are hired and trained;</li>\n<li>the office is set up;</li>\n<li>equipment and supplies are purchased;</li>\n<li>communication channels are created;</li>\n<li>relationships are established.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Managers must assume, and tell management, that other types of case will arise. Example: a background-focused IU may suddenly have an assault or theft case.</li>\n<li>Investigators must be ready for that, mentally and in equipment, contacts, and outside vendors.</li>\n<li>The network of liaison contacts, outside sources, specialists, and equipment providers is adjusted to the main focus, with some flexibility.</li>\n<li>Whatever the structure, a specific person is named, with an alternate, to manage outside resources. This provides continuity and allows capabilities to be activated quickly.</li>\n<li>Investigative needs often arise with little notice and on a surge basis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Reporting chain</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is critical: it affects the outcome of cases and the effectiveness of the unit.</li>\n<li>In general, the shortest chain between the source of the information and the decision maker is best.</li>\n<li>If a trial is expected or company fault is suspected, the investigator delivers the report directly and only to the company’s legal counsel of record.<ul>\n<li>This lets them protect the entire investigation as attorney work product.</li>\n<li>The attorney maintains attorney-client privilege when advising the company.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The final decision maker</strong> may be the chief executive officer (CEO), the chief operating officer (COO), the head of legal, the president, or another executive.<ul>\n<li>You must identify them, win their trust, and create a formal reporting mechanism.</li>\n<li>It is wise to have an alternate channel in case that person is unavailable or is implicated in the matter.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The reporting chain does not have to follow the day-to-day organization chart. If departing from it clashes with the company culture, an inspector general with a direct, independent channel may work better.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Physical location</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In some cases the aim is to be near the areas dealt with most:<ul>\n<li>the executive office;</li>\n<li>general counsel;</li>\n<li>human resources;</li>\n<li>internal audit;</li>\n<li>IT;</li>\n<li>security.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In other cases it is better to be far from the areas that try to micromanage investigations.</li>\n<li>More detail in section 1.8.4.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Changes over time</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The function can grow or shrink with the business environment.<ul>\n<li>With cutbacks, a company with IUs worldwide may reduce their number.</li>\n<li>Or it may eliminate regional units and concentrate everything at headquarters.</li>\n<li>Outsourcing in those regions may be the solution.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Threat management team (TMT):</strong> another possible response.<ul>\n<li>It is multidisciplinary: human resources, safety, security, legal, and other areas according to the company’s objectives.</li>\n<li>Depending on the type of case, it is activated at the start to contribute different expert views.</li>\n<li>It weighs in on disciplinary measures and recommends changes to procedures and policies.</li>\n<li>It is very useful in workplace violence cases.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The company’s response to crime and unethical conduct also changes:</strong><ul>\n<li>Before, it could pursue all complaints vigorously, internal and external.</li>\n<li>The new model may be limited to cases with large restitution or recovery, or high-profile ones.</li>\n<li>Or it may concentrate resources on certain types, for example a heavy hand with IT abuse and a blind eye to minor thefts.</li>\n<li>Government regulations may also dictate steps and actions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>These cycles of emphasis follow the company’s financial health, the evolution of the sector, or public awareness of a problem.<ul>\n<li>After the attacks of September 11, 2001, on the World Trade Center and the Pentagon, the FBI (Federal Bureau of Investigation) shifted much of its focus from organized and economic crime to counterterrorism.</li>\n<li>After the Enron and WorldCom scandals (2001 and 2002), companies gave much more weight to fraud and financial mismanagement.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Those responsible must give the function agility to meet its objectives in a changing environment.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e6\">1.7 Outsourcing Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>For a corporate investigative function, outsourcing can mean two things:<ol>\n<li>Outsourcing the whole function to an outside agency under contract.</li>\n<li>Contracting only selected or specialized support services, when needed.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The decision and approach are usually based on a cost-benefit analysis and anticipated needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Reasons to outsource:</strong><ul>\n<li>almost all organizations watch costs;</li>\n<li>many investigate only occasionally;</li>\n<li>some prefer an independent source;</li>\n<li>a corporate policy that encourages outsourcing peripheral services.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Contracting for the full-scope service is less common than contracting as-needed.</li>\n<li>Outsourcing specialized and expert services is almost always an important part of the budget.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Specialized services that commonly support corporate investigations (19)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Computer forensics.</li>\n<li>Forensic auditing.</li>\n<li>Handwriting analysis.</li>\n<li>Questioned document examination.</li>\n<li>Statement analysis.</li>\n<li>Workplace violence assessments and intervention.</li>\n<li>Harassment and sexual harassment complaints.</li>\n<li>Forensic psychology.</li>\n<li>Forensic polygraph, where legal.</li>\n<li>Technical surveillance or countersurveillance, to deter criminal activity.</li>\n<li>Investigative project management: specialized projects, undercover work, labor disputes (open list in the book).</li>\n<li>Criminal behavioral science or profiling.</li>\n<li>Physical evidence collection.</li>\n<li>Laboratory services.</li>\n<li>Audio or video enhancement.</li>\n<li>Criminal intelligence analysis.</li>\n<li>Brand integrity investigations that require geographic density.</li>\n<li>International investigations that call for local knowledge or contacts.</li>\n<li>Investigations where there is a perceived conflict of interest.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Choosing a vendor</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>There are two key decisions:<ul>\n<li>the size of the preferred vendor;</li>\n<li>whether services will be distinct or bundled.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Vendor</th>\n<th>Advantages and warnings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Large, national or international in scope</td>\n<td>Brings many resources to the project.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small or local</td>\n<td>More personal service and local experience. May be the recommended choice for specialized or expert support. Be wary if it claims to be expert in everything: it is extremely rare to master computer forensics, ballistics, background checks, and executive kidnapping all at once.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Both</td>\n<td>There may be differences in cost and availability.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>At any size, clear criteria must be set and applied. It is recommended to pre-approve vendors with a vetting process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Questions to identify and select a vendor (18):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Does it have a reputation for ethical and honest service?</li>\n<li>Has it demonstrated the technical expertise the client needs?</li>\n<li>Is it licensed in all the areas to which the investigation might lead?</li>\n<li>Are its investigators trained and certified in the techniques and equipment they will use?</li>\n<li>Has it served other companies in the same sector as the client?</li>\n<li>Has it served other companies in the same geographic area?</li>\n<li>Does it have staff and physical resources for short-notice, long-term, and high-demand needs?</li>\n<li>Does it have a staff of investigators with talent, experience, and stability?</li>\n<li>How does it bill? Includes peripheral services, on-demand and subcontracted, and reimbursable expenses.</li>\n<li>Does it maintain professional relationships with liaison contacts, agencies, and other firms (including possible subcontractors) that improve its effectiveness?</li>\n<li>What mechanisms does it use to ensure quality and receive feedback on the contract?</li>\n<li>What formal and informal mechanisms does it use to communicate with the client, in routine and extraordinary matters?</li>\n<li>Is it known for protecting its clients’ information and interests?</li>\n<li>If it provides the full service, does it have its own capability for specialized and one-off support? For example: surveillance, technical services, computer forensics.</li>\n<li>What is the professional background of its principal partners and key staff?</li>\n<li>What is its history of acquisitions and mergers?</li>\n<li>What professional, civic, and business associations does it belong to?</li>\n<li>Does it have sufficient professional liability insurance?</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>1.7.1 Identifying Potential Vendors</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Recommendations from colleagues and peers (word of mouth). It is a form of benchmarking that provides candid and valuable information.</li>\n<li>Credible professional associations or networks. Example: Intellenet (International Intelligence Network), which lists vetted detectives and investigative firms.</li>\n<li>Private detective associations in most US states, Canadian provinces, and countries of the world.<ul>\n<li>They often impose on their members an ethical code and training and licensing requirements.</li>\n<li>They often have a vetting process.</li>\n<li>They help find qualified vendors by region.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>When contracting:</strong> the IU head must ensure that key clauses pass down to the vendor’s consultants or subcontractors. Especially on these topics:<ul>\n<li>evidence handling;</li>\n<li>information protection;</li>\n<li>public disclosure of information;</li>\n<li>licensing requirements;</li>\n<li>availability to testify;</li>\n<li>legal liability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Ultimate responsibility for ensuring that contracted investigators have training and licenses rests with the contracting organization.</li>\n<li>It must be checked thoroughly during selection. Some unscrupulous firms interpret lightly the requirements for licensing, training, and weapons qualification.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e7\">1.8 Establishing an IU</h3><ul>\n<li>There is no typical structure for the investigative function in business.</li>\n<li>Factors that define it: the sector, and the mission, size, and scope of the company.</li>\n<li>When creating or redesigning the function, the IU head starts from what was covered in this chapter.</li>\n<li>They work with senior management so that the decision is not driven by emotion, improvisation, or lack of information.</li>\n<li>The structure and operation of the function can greatly influence the financial and operational health of the organization.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.8.1 Functional Charters and Policy Statements</h4>\n<p>There are three levels of defining the unit:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Document</th>\n<th>Content and features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Functional charter</td>\n<td>Gives credibility and authority to the IU. It is issued by the CEO or an equivalent position. It is brief, often a page or less. It sets the purpose and direction of the function and its strategic goals. It defines who is responsible, the nature and main emphasis of the function, and its essential internal relationships.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Policy statement</td>\n<td>Describes how investigations are initiated and conducted. It addresses reporting channels, coordination mechanisms, and final resolution (disposition). It also governs other departments: for example, it explains to human resources how to request background investigations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Objectives</td>\n<td>Unlike the previous two, they are reviewed often, normally every year. They include performance metrics and improvement goals for the next period.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Doing these three documents well at the start greatly helps the unit’s startup and long-term effectiveness.</li>\n<li>The next step is to obtain the resources, based on those documents. There are three categories: human, physical, and other.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.8.2 Human Resources</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Size of the unit.</strong> It is estimated with these factors:<ul>\n<li>the expected caseload;</li>\n<li>the type of cases;</li>\n<li>the geographic area covered;</li>\n<li>the administrative and other support needs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If it cannot be projected or compared, the best approach is to start small, outsource when needed, and grow over time.</li>\n<li><strong>The type of case weighs heavily on size.</strong><ul>\n<li>An investigator can handle many more background cases than complex procurement fraud.</li>\n<li>A fraud-focused unit needs more investigators, with more experience, and more administrative support.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The investigator’s three “I”s:</strong><ol>\n<li>information;</li>\n<li>interrogation;</li>\n<li>instrumentation.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Even so, the complexity and profile of the cases dictate the training and experience required.</li>\n<li>Many positions call for special fields: computer investigation, contract fraud, or financial crimes.</li>\n<li>The company’s internal unit is called a proprietary IU.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investigator from the public sector or police</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Situation</td>\n<td>Historically, a large percentage of corporate investigative staff comes from there.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Advantages</td>\n<td>Experience, excellent training, and a proven performance record. Eases liaison with police and other first responders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>May not fit the company. Must move well in a business environment and deal with senior management and with staff at all levels and areas.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Differences from business</td>\n<td>The lines of authority and jurisdiction. In part, the objectives of the investigation. The type of relationships and what coordination demands.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skill required</td>\n<td>Using tools such as scatter graphs to find weaknesses in manual or computer systems.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conclusion</td>\n<td>The transition is sometimes difficult and depends on the candidate’s personality.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>1.8.3 Investigator Qualifications (Webster University 2020)</h4>\n<p>Professional qualifications and personal traits are valued.</p>\n<p><strong>Professional qualifications (6)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Qualification</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>Many positions require at least a bachelor’s degree. Even if not in the field, it indicates intelligence, maturity, discipline, and broad knowledge. It helps in understanding culture and society, which gives perspective on the real facts. It shows the capacity to keep learning. Specialists need specific training, often graduate degrees (e.g., forensic science, behavioral psychology).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training</td>\n<td>There are many courses on general techniques and on specific aspects. They are given by police agencies, universities, and commercial providers. The level, currency, and source (agency or school) of the training are weighed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Certification</td>\n<td>Examples: Professional Certified Investigator (PCI), Certified Protection Professional (CPP), Certified Fraud Examiner (CFE), Certified Forensic Interviewer (CFI). They prove knowledge, commitment to the field, and effort to stay current. They should weigh heavily in hiring and promotion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experience, general</td>\n<td>Rule of thumb: two years doing investigations, preferably of several types. It must include interviews and interrogations, evidence handling, liaison, surveillance, records searches, photography, reports, and case presentation. Patrolling as a police officer does not count as investigating, unless the person did specific, detailed investigative work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Experience, specialized</td>\n<td>Experience in the sector, in the business environment, or in a specialty adds value, sometimes a great deal. It brings lessons learned and best practices that improve the unit. Specialist positions require it; detailed information must be requested from the candidate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communications skills</td>\n<td>Drawing out information (the core of every investigation) from people, cooperative or not, with different views and levels. Presenting orally and in writing to executives, lawyers, prosecutors, police, and security professionals. Being concise and convincing at once, balancing facts and conclusions. It is partly a personal trait, but is treated as a professional qualification.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Personal traits (10)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Trait</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>High ethical standards</td>\n<td>A demonstrated record of honesty and professional ethics. It marks their whole relationship with the unit and with those they deal with on the organization’s behalf.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Persistence</td>\n<td>Pressing on despite dead ends and frustrations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Balance</td>\n<td>Pursuing success firmly without skipping rules or protocols. That way they do not jeopardize the legal basis of the case or raise the company’s legal risk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maturity</td>\n<td>A mature, realistic view of themselves and their surroundings. It allows putting in context the information, facts, and situations that affect lives, careers, and the organization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ability to deal effectively with people</td>\n<td>Despite technology, people are the center of almost every investigation. They must deal with all kinds of people, in any role.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-motivating and self-starting</td>\n<td>There is almost no direct supervision, apart from legal and regulatory. They are expected to work alone, on their own drive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ability to multitask</td>\n<td>Every case has many elements and sources, and they usually carry several cases at once.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Professional demeanor</td>\n<td>Maintaining it when dealing with people, gathering and analyzing information, and presenting facts and conclusions. Otherwise they lose effectiveness and damage the credibility of the unit and the organization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Observational skills</td>\n<td>Observing people, places, activities, and situations; the most important thing is curiosity. It feeds the information base and guides next steps. Observing, interpreting, and relating well often makes good investigators.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flexibility</td>\n<td>Moving well in very different environments and situations. Knowing when to yield and when to insist.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>1.8.4 Physical Resources</h4>\n<p><strong>Office space</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Open or shared offices are a serious problem, especially next to other departments.</li>\n<li>If possible, the team should have private or semiprivate offices.</li>\n<li>Private spaces are also needed for meetings, interviews, and calls, and locked spaces for sensitive files and evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Interview room</strong>, aspects to consider:<ul>\n<li>size and comfort;</li>\n<li>furniture;</li>\n<li>layout;</li>\n<li>possible recording equipment;</li>\n<li>whether it has windows, which may or may not be desirable.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Access with privacy for the person arriving for the interview is advisable.</li>\n<li>If there are frequent meetings with outside people, the room may be outside the controlled area (e.g., next to the outer lobby). That way visitors do not sign in at visitor control.<ul>\n<li>It is practical for outside informants, liaison contacts, outside vendors, and police.</li>\n<li>Human resources can share it for exit interviews or meetings with terminated employees, without giving them access to the controlled area.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Case board:</strong> complex cases are made easier with a manual storyboard (e.g., whiteboard) or software. It serves to track leads, show links (link analysis), and plan next steps.<ul>\n<li>That information must be protected from the view of anyone not on the team.</li>\n<li>A “war room” may serve: a separate, secure, private space for teamwork.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Evidence storage</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not all units need it, but temporary or long-term storage must be planned.</li>\n<li>Only a few people have keys, combinations, or codes.</li>\n<li>Most have individual locked containers.</li>\n<li>A separately zoned intrusion detection system is often advisable.</li>\n<li>Access control is key for practical reasons and for the chain of custody.</li>\n<li>Provision must be made for evidence with special storage needs:<ul>\n<li>very large items;</li>\n<li>items that require climate control;</li>\n<li>evidence that must be stored outdoors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Records and file storage</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many files are highly sensitive or confidential and have practical and legal access limits.</li>\n<li>Retention periods vary widely. They must be stored securely and in an orderly way, to keep or destroy them on time (retention and destruction or disposition).</li>\n<li>All documents and evidence are kept in their original format until all legal action, including appeals, ends.</li>\n<li><strong>Electronic or cloud storage:</strong><ul>\n<li>it takes up less space and usually makes retrieval easier;</li>\n<li>secure backup copies must be off-site and instantly accessible;</li>\n<li>they are useful if the data, the system, or the IU’s site fails (e.g., cyberattack, natural disaster, or other catastrophe);</li>\n<li>for that reason secure cloud services are preferred.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Even if everything is digitized, sometimes the originals must be kept. Anything that may be evidence (e.g., original photos and written statements) is not destroyed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Investigative supplies and equipment</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It varies greatly. Some units only interview and report; others need to collect evidence, technical equipment, and consumables.</li>\n<li>Most need at least a basic evidence collection kit and audio and video recording equipment.<ul>\n<li>A voice-activated digital recorder and a digital camera are usually enough.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Beware of the smartphone:</strong><ul>\n<li>the device itself can become evidence and be out of use for a long time;</li>\n<li>investigative material must be kept strictly separate from personal or organizational content.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The unit must assess its needs realistically. Sometimes it can borrow equipment from the audiovisual department.</li>\n<li>Original audio, video, or IT evidence must not be left in doubt by copying, enhancing, or manipulating it.</li>\n<li>Staff must master the equipment before really using it.<ul>\n<li>Test it in various conditions: light levels, microphone position, background noise, indoors and outdoors.</li>\n<li>Practice using it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It must be set in advance how audiovisual and electronic evidence, recordings, and removable media are labeled, logged, and stored.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Physical investigative aids</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A crime lab is not needed, but some simple aids are. Many corporate IUs have:<ul>\n<li>a latent fingerprint kit;</li>\n<li>evidence bags and containers;</li>\n<li>latex gloves;</li>\n<li>materials to mark or identify evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Marking materials: substances that glow under ultraviolet light, barcode labels, quick response (QR) codes, etching pens. Also radio frequency identification (RFID) and global positioning system (GPS) tracking devices.</li>\n<li>Police suppliers sell kits that combine several aids at a reasonable cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other equipment</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Computing:</strong> laptops, tablets, cell phones, and special peripherals.<ul>\n<li>Besides administrative work, they serve to manage digital records, do computer forensics, and store electronic evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Interview recording:</strong> turnkey digital systems on the market, for police and investigators. There are fixed ones, installed in a room, and portable ones.</li>\n<li>Separate equipment may be needed to process cases, handle evidence, and analyze, in addition to office equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Covert surveillance:</strong> photographic equipment, vehicles, and communication systems.<ul>\n<li>It is no longer expensive, and wireless technology makes it easier to install.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Drones:</strong> increasingly common. They serve for surveillance, assessments, aerial photos for reports, and other support.</li>\n<li>Basic equipment must be ready to deploy quickly.<ul>\n<li>It must be maintained.</li>\n<li>Whoever uses it needs full training and practice.</li>\n<li>Before each deployment, the legal requirements for surveillance and recording are checked, up to date.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Vehicle:</strong> preferably low-profile and without company logos.</li>\n<li>Secure communication equipment may be useful. Procedures for its use and for communications security must be set.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.8.5 Additional Resources (6)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Resource</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Training</td>\n<td>Requires much time and money. Includes informal in-house training, courses, and conferences. Specialists need more, but everyone must update periodically. Topics: investigative techniques, legal matters, and making use of available technology.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationships</td>\n<td>Internal and external liaison contacts are a resource. They must be cultivated on purpose, especially when creating the function.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communications</td>\n<td>Effective channels within the organization and with third parties (e.g., police and other liaison contacts). Clear, realistic procedures for sharing information, reporting cases, requesting support, and assigning resources. Formal and informal channels, for routine matters and for crises.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financial resources</td>\n<td>Well-planned budgets, with the best possible projections. Sometimes it is wise to collaborate with other departments to take advantage of their budgets (e.g., facilities, security, safety, legal, risk management).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Information assets</td>\n<td>Information is the lifeblood of the mission. They are created at the start and maintained always: recruiting, handling, and closing informants; liaison contacts; internal and external databases; access to key people in the organization; open source information; operational data. Also an employee hotline for complaints, reports, and tips. It is a cheap way to learn of misconduct and receive anonymous tips.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Geographic assets</td>\n<td>Many organizations cover wide areas or the whole world. It is wise to have sources, liaison contacts, backup vendors, and other resources in each area. Agreeing on those relationships and having equipment, supplies, and perhaps staff ready greatly improves operations.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e8\">1.9 Managing Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>The book announces four key management areas, at the strategic and day-to-day level:<ol>\n<li>investigative functions;</li>\n<li>investigative resources;</li>\n<li>unit management;</li>\n<li>case management.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> within 1.9 only three areas are developed (1.9.1 to 1.9.3). Case management appears separately, as section 1.11.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.9.1 Investigative Functions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Most IUs focus on one function or a group of functions.</li>\n<li>They range from simple to complex. For example, from documenting how the security guard responded to a workplace incident to complex procurement fraud.</li>\n<li>They are called types of investigation. The unit’s incident management system is usually organized by type of incident.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Typical types of investigation in business (25)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Incident or accident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Employee misconduct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Misuse or abuse of computers or IT systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Substance abuse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Due diligence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Regulatory compliance violation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Lifestyle or financial inquiries of executives and staff</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Personnel security or background</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Theft, pilferage, or misappropriation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Lapping: crediting one account with money taken from another account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Assaults and crimes against persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Property damage and vandalism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Inventory discrepancies or unexplained shrinkage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Sabotage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Industrial espionage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Copyright and proprietary information violations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Embezzlement or defalcation: misappropriation of property by the person to whom it was entrusted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Fraud: general, procurement, insurance, travel, accounting (open list in the book)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>Product tampering, real or hoax</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Diverted or counterfeit product</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>Skimming: keeping part of the cash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22</td>\n<td>Communicating threats</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>23</td>\n<td>Harassment, including sexual harassment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>Workplace violence, real or potential</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>Litigation support. Varies depending on whether the company is plaintiff or defendant.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Other uses by sector</strong>, where the IU supports the core mission:<ul>\n<li>a real estate company uses it to locate unknown owners or do difficult property title searches;</li>\n<li>others use it for market research, competitive intelligence, and other corporate functions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In many organizations, the IU is a corporate resource serving business objectives.</li>\n<li>IU managers (and security directors) must understand how the unit fits and how management sees it (the big picture).</li>\n<li>Ideally the IU manager or security director defines that fit. Their role may be direct or subtle, but they must influence as much as possible without losing sight of the business.</li>\n<li>Section 1.13 expands on several important types:<ul>\n<li>incident investigations, the most common type in many organizations;</li>\n<li>compliance investigations;</li>\n<li>background investigations;</li>\n<li>substance abuse investigations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Due diligence</strong> (see chapter 3): a very important type, but often neglected.<ul>\n<li>Before a new business relationship, the prudent organization verifies what the future partner says.</li>\n<li>It is usually requested as negotiations close and must be finished on time.</li>\n<li>The skilled investigator quickly confirms the subject’s assets, liabilities, legal problems, possible conflicts of interest, and hidden affiliations or identities.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.9.2 Investigative Resources</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Main resources of an IU (5):</strong><ol>\n<li>people;</li>\n<li>information;</li>\n<li>credibility;</li>\n<li>physical assets;</li>\n<li>financial assets.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The main functional focus dictates the structure and the distribution of resources. It defines:<ul>\n<li>what kind of investigators are hired;</li>\n<li>what mechanisms are created to obtain information;</li>\n<li>how and where the unit is structured;</li>\n<li>what equipment and supplies it needs;</li>\n<li>to a large extent, what the IU requests in budget.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Funding is hard to project: one or a few large cases and new regulations or laws alter it. Justifying the budget is an arduous task.</li>\n<li>The best route is to demonstrate value, or that the unit pays for itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Supports for justifying the budget (5):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>A correct focus, which supports the mission and the strategic and business goals.</li>\n<li>An accurate and detailed record of investigative costs.</li>\n<li>Effective cost control and efficiency measures.</li>\n<li>Demonstrating the benefits of restitution and recovery.</li>\n<li>Estimating in money the risk avoided (quantitative estimation of risk avoidance).</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Controlling operating and overhead costs well greatly improves the response to funding requests.</li>\n<li>Costs can be tracked by type of case, location, business area, or other variables.</li>\n<li>Recoveries and restitutions are recorded and reported to senior management. That way they show the financial benefit and support the ROI argument.</li>\n<li>Often the IU demonstrates ROI through civil recovery. It recovers the losses and also the costs of the investigation.</li>\n<li>Personnel costs and outside services are usually the largest items. Overhead is usually the easiest to control.</li>\n<li>To save money, managers rely on liaison contacts and on other departments: IT, facilities, accounting and finance, legal, and risk management.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.9.3 Unit Management</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The IU manager must balance administrative and operational duties.</li>\n<li>Figure 1.3 shows the investigative life cycle and many of the functions under the manager’s charge.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The four phases of the cycle, according to the text</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Initiation</td>\n<td>Ways to initiate a case: incident report, complaint, discrepancy report, or investigation request. Includes the reason for the case and the mechanism by which it is opened.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Investigation</td>\n<td>Examples of case types on two tracks: incident, allegation, or situation; and level of trust.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Reporting</td>\n<td>Findings are reported and their later use is anticipated. Almost all the information is formally retained, often in an automated database, in field, summary, or full format.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Use of results</td>\n<td>Use in the specific case: disciplinary action, referral to the police or the judicial system, or adjudication. Aggregate or later use: statistical analysis, program evaluation, strategic planning, and budget forecasting.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.3 — The Investigative Life Cycle</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Incident, allegation, or situation track</th>\n<th>Level of trust track</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Initiation</td>\n<td>Triggers: Incident Report, Complaint, Audit or Discrepancy. Origin: Incident, Allegation, Situation (known or unknown subject).</td>\n<td>Need for Level of Trust; Request for Investigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Investigation</td>\n<td>Criminal Investigation; Administrative Inquiry; Misconduct Investigation; Incident Investigation; Compliance Investigation</td>\n<td>Background; Due Diligence; Financial/Lifestyle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reporting and recording</td>\n<td>Report of Investigation; Retention and/or Database</td>\n<td>Retention and/or Database</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use in the case</td>\n<td>Action/Case Disposition</td>\n<td>Adjudication/Determination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Later uses</td>\n<td>Trend Analysis; Statistical Reports; Program Management Evaluation; Strategic Planning; Resource Allocation; Criminal Intel. Analysis</td>\n<td>Access Authorization; Visit Requests; Concurrent Clearances; Corporate Decisions; Reinvestigation Alert</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Pending: the figure’s text was damaged and was read by position. The arrows and the exact track of “Report of Investigation,” “Action/Case Disposition,” and “Adjudication/Determination” remain to be verified against the original figure.</li>\n<li>The IU manager designs and applies systems to manage each of the four phases.</li>\n<li>It is easy to sink into the day-to-day, but the management and administration of the unit must not be neglected.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Initiation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A convenient mechanism is needed for employees and others to provide information worth investigating or that supports an open case.</li>\n<li>An important part of credible information comes from these sources:<ul>\n<li>whistleblowers;</li>\n<li>employee tips, anonymous or not;</li>\n<li>complaints that arrive through supervisors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>That information covers incidents and conditions that cause losses, legal liability, and other problems, including possible violence.</li>\n<li>Dismissing anonymous complaints goes against good practice. Even so, false reports, retaliation, and misinformation must be considered.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal framework for whistleblower protection:</strong><ul>\n<li>Laws protect against retaliation, but vary from country to country.</li>\n<li>The United Nations Convention against Corruption (2003) made that protection part of international law (National Whistleblower Center 2020).</li>\n<li>In the United States, two rules require an internal reporting system without retaliation, as part of an effective compliance and ethics program:</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Whom it binds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002</td>\n<td>Publicly traded companies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Federal Sentencing Guidelines</td>\n<td>Companies convicted of criminal activity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Many organizations have channels to report fraud, waste, abuse, threats, security violations, and other adverse situations.</li>\n<li>It is also important to have an awareness program.</li>\n<li>Companies must have a process to evaluate each communication and investigate those that call for follow-up.</li>\n<li>They must do everything possible to prevent retaliation against the whistleblower. That is why it is advisable for all reports to be anonymous.</li>\n<li>In some organizations, the channel is shared with the areas that record safety risks, IT threats, or discrimination complaints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Investigation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The manager must implement a case management system and administrative controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Automated systems:</strong> nearly all organizations use them, and many integrate with the security incident management system.<ul>\n<li>Well configured and with proper permissions, they integrate security, investigation, human resources, and facilities data.</li>\n<li>They make it easier to collect and retain data, write and share reports, and coordinate with legal and finance.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Case assignment and workload management:</strong><ul>\n<li>Programs help; in small units it can be done by hand.</li>\n<li>In large or dispersed units, assignment is based on caseload, experience, specialization, geography, or a mix of factors.</li>\n<li>It must be done carefully and deliberately.</li>\n<li>Poor assignment demotivates investigators and lowers the IU’s effectiveness. Some are overloaded and others have little work.</li>\n<li>It can also happen that a specialist handles general cases while their cases go to others.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Supervision and controls:</strong><ul>\n<li>Keeping the investigative assignment ledger is the task of the controller or the manager.</li>\n<li>The operational and administrative aspects of each case (e.g., costs) are reviewed at least once a month.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Minimum checks and balances they must ensure (11):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>That investigation requests are documented and answered.</li>\n<li>That there is proper authorization to open a case.</li>\n<li>That each investigator carries an appropriate number of cases, and that the right investigator carries the right case.</li>\n<li>That the status of each case is reported regularly, inside and outside as appropriate.</li>\n<li>That leads are followed and recorded as appropriate.</li>\n<li>That asset values (estimated loss, recoveries) are recorded and updated.</li>\n<li>That liaison contacts are recorded and managed.</li>\n<li>That periodic written reports are provided as planned.</li>\n<li>That the case’s disposition and objectives are updated regularly.</li>\n<li>That the case’s resources are recorded and managed.</li>\n<li>That post-case information (disposition, files) is recorded.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The controls must allow three things:</strong><ul>\n<li>that all investigations are authorized;</li>\n<li>that all the work assigned to each investigator is known;</li>\n<li>that the results and pending tasks of each case are quickly known.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>At the start of a new IU, formal controls may not seem necessary. As the unit, its activity, or staff turnover grows, a formal system is needed. That way the maximum benefit is drawn from the accumulated data.</li>\n<li>The system should try to answer two questions:<ul>\n<li>how much a competent investigator should accomplish in a workday;</li>\n<li>how long an investigation should take.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>What affects the investigator’s output:</strong><ul>\n<li>their temperament, competence, and work pace;</li>\n<li>the complexity of the case;</li>\n<li>the availability of informants and other sources.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Often it cannot be predicted what will be achieved in a day. That is why the skilled investigator opens at once all the leads they can handle. That way they generate information to use when the opportunity arises, in the least time.</li>\n<li><strong>Measure of effectiveness: the time it takes to close a case.</strong><ul>\n<li>It varies enormously, but with statistics averages are drawn by type of lead or case.</li>\n<li>The average is used to schedule, budget, and assign cases.</li>\n<li>It does not work in all environments, but it helps in some, both in case management and in strategic planning.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reporting</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The formats, frequency, distribution, and other reporting requirements must be in the policies and procedures, and carefully followed.</li>\n<li>Formal documentation is usually the first thing neglected when caseload rises and time gets tight.</li>\n<li>Poor documentation and careless records can lead to failure. It is tedious, but it is an important management responsibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Follow-up</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Management and IU managers must understand that this phase goes beyond deciding disciplinary, administrative, or judicial measures.</li>\n<li>Sometimes the findings point, besides individual guilt, to system failures or business practices that caused the problem. Among them, at times, security vulnerabilities. Those failures call for corrective measures.</li>\n<li><strong>Synergy:</strong> using the results to resolve the case and also to correct the conditions that allowed the incident.</li>\n<li>The results are kept in files or databases, according to the records retention policy. They serve for:<ul>\n<li>statistics and trend analysis;</li>\n<li>evaluating the effectiveness of investigative, security, or assets protection programs;</li>\n<li>supporting resource allocation decisions;</li>\n<li>refining policies and procedures;</li>\n<li>feeding criminal intelligence analysis, in general or in future cases. For example, with the same people, organizations, departments, places, or modus operandi.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e9\">1.10 Other Management Matters</h3><p>Three key topics remain for running the investigative unit (IU): setting objectives, motivating staff, and training them.</p>\n<h4>1.10.1 Setting Objectives</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a responsibility that is often neglected. It serves to operate well and to demonstrate value to the organization.</li>\n<li>Objectives must be <strong>specific and measurable</strong>.</li>\n<li>They are set for different aspects of the unit: financial management, case resolution, overall unit performance, information flow, and inquiries/complaints/leads handled. The book leaves the list open.</li>\n<li>They are measured, documented, and updated periodically: <strong>at least once a year</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.10.2 Motivation</h4>\n<p>Situations that affect the investigator’s self-image and attitude (9):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Situation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Testifying at trial or in administrative proceedings: intense questioning, reprimands, and challenges from lawyers or other officials.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Investing a lot of time in cases that later turn out to be based on false allegations, reports, or data. A sense of wasted effort.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Unsolved cases, cases closed before being resolved, or with a resolution the investigator considers inappropriate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Very long cases, sometimes years (e.g., fraud and white-collar crime): a sense of not making progress.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Ethical temptations: dealing with dishonest people who prosper through their conduct, bribes, use of inside information for personal gain, abuse of authority, improper or illegal techniques (open list in the book).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Uncooperative or frustrating witnesses, interviewees, and liaison contacts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Senior management’s decision not to pursue a matter (before or after investigating), when the investigator sees potential.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Reluctance of the police or the prosecutor to accept a case after much effort.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Cynicism: carrying work pressures over into personal life.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The IU manager must detect signs of frustration, without acting as an “armchair psychologist.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Measures to motivate and maintain peak performance (7):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Require periodic breaks or vacations.</li>\n<li>Send to training courses (this also gives time away from cases).</li>\n<li>Organize social events in the office.</li>\n<li>Form teams of investigators so they motivate each other.</li>\n<li>Recognize personal and professional achievements.</li>\n<li>Support participation in professional and community organizations.</li>\n<li>Use a participative management style.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>1.10.3 Training</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It benefits the unit and the investigator. It takes time and money, but is essential in a professional unit.</li>\n<li><strong>Internal and periodic:</strong> review policies, procedures, protocols, and topics that affect the organization.</li>\n<li><strong>External:</strong> courses and seminars on specialized topics and on advanced general techniques.</li>\n<li>Specialized topics must be tied to the position or the industry. The book cites 16:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Computer investigations and forensics</td>\n<td>Surveillance technology</td>\n<td>Evidence handling</td>\n<td>White-collar crime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forensic auditing</td>\n<td>Criminal intelligence analysis</td>\n<td>Environmental investigations</td>\n<td>Safety investigations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accident investigation</td>\n<td>Sexual harassment</td>\n<td>Workplace violence</td>\n<td>Insurance fraud</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interviewing and interrogation</td>\n<td>General management</td>\n<td>Communication</td>\n<td>Self-improvement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e10\">1.11 Case Management</h3><ul>\n<li>Besides the unit’s objectives, each case needs its own.</li>\n<li>Even if the objective seems obvious, it is wise to identify and document three things:<ul>\n<li>the <strong>basis or foundation of the case</strong> (case predication);</li>\n<li>a <strong>primary objective</strong>;</li>\n<li>normally, <strong>secondary objectives</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>This keeps the investigation focused and guides the steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Detailed investigative plan:</strong> required in complex or nonroutine investigations and in <strong>all</strong> proactive ones. It is coordinated with all relevant parties. It is approved by the IU manager or a higher authority.</li>\n<li>Objectives may change during the case. Every change is documented.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.11.1 Case Files</h4>\n<p>It is opened at the start of each investigation, with the IU manager’s approval.</p>\n<p>Initial contents of the file (6):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Original complaint form or other initiating mechanism.</li>\n<li>Assigned case or file number.</li>\n<li>Name of the assigned investigators.</li>\n<li>Complaint or subject information.</li>\n<li>Initial leads.</li>\n<li>Record of management’s approval.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Steps that opening sets in motion (5):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Assign a case or file number.</li>\n<li>Record the case in the case management system and in other databases as appropriate.</li>\n<li>Notify management and others, as appropriate.</li>\n<li>Coordinate with staff, as appropriate.</li>\n<li>Try to corroborate the initial information.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Administrative data of the file, tracked by hand or with an automated system (11):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Data</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Type or category of case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Case title or subject (if known)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>File number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Location of the incident that originated it, if any</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Value of assets lost or stolen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Assigned investigators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Opening date (and place, if applicable)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Deadline for the report or other action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Dates of interim reports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Date of the final or closing report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Value of assets recovered</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>1.11.2 Following the Progress of a Case</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Case chronology form,</strong> automated or by hand. It records activity that does not warrant an interim report. Examples:<ul>\n<li>setting appointments to interview witnesses;</li>\n<li>setting appointments to review documentary evidence with records custodians;</li>\n<li>organizing the information to define pending actions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The chronology plus the interim reports serve two purposes:<ul>\n<li>they demonstrate the investigator’s diligence;</li>\n<li>they allow the manager to evaluate the efficiency and effectiveness of staff.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Automated system:</strong> generates reports of open cases by deadline, by assigned investigator, or by other factors. The manager reviews the status of any case at any time.</li>\n<li>Interim reports are reviewed in electronic or paper format. Periodic oral meetings on case status are also recommended.</li>\n<li><strong>Large IUs:</strong> may have an operations coordinator who reviews cases, guides, and recommends steps.</li>\n<li>It is wise to use supervisors and colleagues as sounding boards or for brainstorming. Investigation is partly creative and the best ideas usually come from collaborating.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>File custody. Even a small IU or security department needs a formal system.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Format</th>\n<th>Controls</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Paper</td>\n<td>Secure container (safe or high-quality locking file cabinet). One or more people designated as responsible. Checkout card at the place of the removed file: who took it out and when. That way it is located when the manager or another team member asks for it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic (ongoing and closed)</td>\n<td>Access permissions and audit trail. They are set by the systems administrator or another official designated by the IU manager.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says that “no one” may add or remove a file for any reason. Right after, it requires a card recording who removed it. It is an inconsistency in the text itself.</p>\n<p>Tasks of the lead investigator during the case (7):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Record the liaison contacts and the information they provide.</li>\n<li>Keep the surveillance logs, physical and technical.</li>\n<li>Keep the evidence logs, with receipts and chain of custody documents.</li>\n<li>File original written statements and documentary evidence.</li>\n<li>When applicable, record costs per case. Sometimes they are charged to another corporate or outside account.</li>\n<li>Keep the suspense log: report deadlines, status reviews, leads, and other milestones. Ensure delegated tasks are completed on time.</li>\n<li>Report to regulatory agencies when mandatory.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These steps bring three benefits: legal credibility of the case, greater likelihood of success, and more efficiency.</p>\n<h4>1.11.3 Case Reporting and Information</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Results are normally communicated in a <strong>formal report of investigation</strong>.</li>\n<li>There may be a final report, a series of interim reports, and periodic and final summaries or synopses.</li>\n<li><strong>Interim report rule:</strong> each one includes all the information from the previous interim. The reader does not have to go back to the earlier ones: the latest report contains everything known to date.</li>\n<li>Two things must be balanced:<ul>\n<li>sharing what is relevant with those who need it to support the investigation or decide;</li>\n<li>protecting the information (integrity and privacy of those involved).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Most IUs have dissemination policies, but gray areas arise. Dissemination of sensitive information is coordinated with the IU manager or the appropriate authority.</li>\n<li><strong>Case initiated by an attorney</strong> with attorney work product privilege: nothing is disclosed unless the attorney orders it.</li>\n<li>Every disseminated report, on paper or electronic, indicates:<ul>\n<li>its sensitivity level;</li>\n<li>the dissemination controls;</li>\n<li>the disposition and retention instructions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Recipients are warned, when applicable, that they may not forward or share the information.</li>\n<li><strong>Distribution log:</strong> to whom it was sent and when, or who accessed it electronically and when. Also file downloads or modifications.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e11\">1.12 Other Investigative Management Issues</h3><h4>1.12.1 Legal Issues</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The book is based on the United States, but says that similar issues apply in most jurisdictions.</li>\n<li>Legal matters are considered for two reasons:<ol>\n<li>to protect the company against legal liability;</li>\n<li>to ensure an appropriate conclusion if the case goes to trial or another legal forum.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other legal considerations (4):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Comply with the laws on private investigator licensing and on the conduct of private investigations.</li>\n<li>Meet regulatory reporting requirements in all applicable jurisdictions.</li>\n<li>Possible civil lawsuits (sometimes criminal complaints) for defamation, false imprisonment, false arrest, retaliation, and harassment.</li>\n<li>Possible subpoenas to testify, to hand over the written records of the investigation, or both.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>The IU head must ensure that the organization knows the applicable laws, regulations, and corporate policies. The task grows if there are locations in several jurisdictions or countries.</li>\n<li><strong>Venue:</strong> may not be the expected one. It may be:<ul>\n<li>where the corporate headquarters is;</li>\n<li>where the incident occurred (perhaps off company property);</li>\n<li>where the complainant is;</li>\n<li>another place tied to the incident.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Laws, regulations, and restrictions may vary among those jurisdictions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Local Licensing Requirements</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most jurisdictions require paid investigators to be licensed. Requirements vary greatly among states and countries.</li>\n<li>They are checked before hiring investigators and before sending them to other jurisdictions.</li>\n<li><strong>Outsiders</strong> (outsourced investigators, consultants, or experts): the license of the individual <strong>and</strong> of the firm that employs them is verified. It cannot be assumed.</li>\n<li>Violations can cause significant legal liability, fines, and the dismissal of a case that was good.</li>\n<li>With operations in several jurisdictions, the law of each one where an investigation is conducted is reviewed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.12.2 Liaison</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Professional relationships are invaluable for an effective IU. Establishing them, nurturing them, and using them well is a key management responsibility.</li>\n<li>Types of contacts: internal and external, formal and informal, individual and organizational.</li>\n<li>More detail in Chapter 8.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.4. Suggested Liaison Contacts</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Internal — 15</th>\n<th>External — 15</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Executive management</td>\n<td>Local law enforcement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facilities</td>\n<td>Other first responders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Human resources</td>\n<td>Local FBI, including InfraGard representatives*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Information technology</td>\n<td>Other federal or regional investigative agencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal counsel</td>\n<td>Military</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operations</td>\n<td>Expert consultants or firms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public relations</td>\n<td>Crime laboratory services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Purchasing</td>\n<td>Outside employee assistance program providers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety/occupational health</td>\n<td>Equipment or supply vendors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security, if separate dept.</td>\n<td>Local Chamber of Commerce</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Labor relations</td>\n<td>Other business associations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employee councils</td>\n<td>Professional associations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Department or division contacts within the company</td>\n<td>Company suppliers and vendors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accounting and finance</td>\n<td>Local community contacts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other informants</td>\n<td>Other informants</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>* Figure note: most FBI (Federal Bureau of Investigation) field offices have representatives for corporate relations, computer crime, or economic espionage.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> “Other informants” appears in both columns. In the external column there are two nearly identical entries: “Equipment or supply vendors” and “Company suppliers and vendors.” The assignment to each column was taken from the position of the text on the page.</p>\n<h4>1.12.3 Criminal Intelligence Analysis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is advisable, or essential, in organizations with frequent, large, or complex investigations.</li>\n<li>For INTERPOL, information is the lifeblood of police work. Timely and accurate analysis is key to understanding how crime and criminal enterprises work and what drives them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two types of analysis according to INTERPOL (2020):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Operational / tactical</th>\n<th>Strategic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Supports a specific investigation</td>\n<td>Addresses a general criminal threat or trend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aim</td>\n<td>A specific police or investigative result: arrest, seizure, forfeiture of assets or proceeds of crime, or dismantling of a criminal group</td>\n<td>Inform high-level decision makers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical support</td>\n<td>1) Identify links between suspects and their involvement in crimes. 2) Identify key investigative or information gaps. 3) Develop profiles of known or suspected criminals.</td>\n<td>Early warning of threats. Helps set priorities in the face of emerging criminal problems. Examples: allocate resources to types of crime; strengthen training in a technique.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Benefit</td>\n<td>Immediate, in the case</td>\n<td>Long-term</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Application in the private sector:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is just as important for corporate IUs.</li>\n<li>It supports the security risk management process. Its usual use is threat analysis, but it also reveals vulnerabilities and impacts of crime or other loss events.</li>\n<li>Example: comparing the impact of theft or loss of intellectual property among several research projects or business units.</li>\n<li>It can be strategic or tactical (operational).</li>\n<li>The strategic type informs resource allocation, focus or priorities by risk category, and long-term planning. It also directly supports investigative operations.</li>\n<li>An important aspect of the strategic type is a formal process very similar to the National Security Intelligence Cycle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.5. The Intelligence Collection Cycle</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It can be used by police (at all levels), investigative organizations, and corporate IUs with large, complex, or interconnected investigations.</li>\n<li>Pending: the figure is a graphic with no extractable text. The five steps below come from the text.</li>\n<li>The book’s example for the whole cycle: a company concerned about an organized retail crime (ORC) network that exploits its warehouses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the same cycle gets three names. It is “very similar” to the National Security Intelligence Cycle, the figure calls it the “Intelligence Collection Cycle,” and the text calls it the “criminal intelligence cycle.”</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n<th>ORC example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Requirements</td>\n<td>What information must be gathered or what questions must be answered. Those questions are called interrogatories.</td>\n<td>They are set by the whole organization or by a business unit, region, or project. The more specific they are, the more useful the result, but they may cost more or take longer.</td>\n<td>Tactics against warehouses, changes in modus operandi, data on specific groups, where they operate, which groups act together and how they communicate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Collection</td>\n<td>Gathering data from various sources and types of source to cover the requirements after analysis.</td>\n<td>Witting and unwitting participants. Possible collectors: competitive intelligence function, online, in-person, print, and other open source intelligence (OSINT), direct observation or surveillance, internal market research, specialized outside firms. Information is inside and outside the organization: do not neglect either. Best is a variety of sources: different perspectives, origins, sectors, and eras (old and recent).</td>\n<td>Police or task forces, media, crime and threat databases, supply chain security experts, already victimized companies or facilities, past or current members of ORC groups. Warehouse risk assessments. Physical surveillance at or near warehouses. A mix of internal collectors, a contracted intelligence collection firm, and a private investigation firm. Define the geographic scope from the start.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Integration/Analysis</td>\n<td>The heart of the process: turns information and data into actionable intelligence.</td>\n<td>Information is received, cross-referenced, and sorted: assigned to requirements or to general-use files. The credibility of sources (people, organizations, publications, media) and the reliability of the information are assessed. Analysts and collectors communicate back and forth.</td>\n<td>Everything is analyzed and shared with those who prepare intelligence products for “consumers” and stakeholders. It is checked whether the requirements were met and how to share what goes beyond them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Dissemination</td>\n<td>Who receives what, when, in what format, and how it is delivered.</td>\n<td>Sometimes the requirements set it; it is commonly left open. It can be simple or very complex. It is affected by whether there are third-party recipients. Classification: in the private sector, normally “confidential” or “proprietary” (or equivalent). Retention: how and how long, for both the initial data and the finished intelligence. Laws, regulations, and policies govern, and vary among jurisdictions and international borders (physical or logical).</td>\n<td>Recipients: senior management (C-suite), asset owners, warehouse management, human resources, personnel security, physical security, operations, information technology (IT) and, more limitedly, trusted transport and supply chain providers. Nondisclosure agreements (NDAs) required. Possible coordination with police or security system providers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Evaluation</td>\n<td>Periodic review to improve the process. It is easy to neglect.</td>\n<td>Three questions: 1) Were the requirements met? It is evaluated when one is closed or on an interim basis. 2) Is the function meeting its goals and objectives, and efficiently? 3) How can the process be improved?</td>\n<td>Does the IU know what it needs to stop the current threat? What else will be needed now or in the future? Is it ready to coordinate with police, legal counsel, or others? How can it improve in the short and long term?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Integration and analysis activities (5):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Corroborate the information and compare it with other information of a similar nature.</li>\n<li>Break down each input, study the meaning and relevance of each part, and recompose it into a coherent piece of data.</li>\n<li>Combine fragments of information like a jigsaw puzzle.</li>\n<li>Put the information in context and make sense of it according to the organization’s mission and intelligence needs.</li>\n<li>Decide the best use of the intelligence and recommend how to disseminate and apply it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Internal analysis teams and fusion centers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many companies have internal intelligence analysis teams: from high-tech research and pharmaceuticals to cruise lines and theme parks.</li>\n<li>That cell can function as a <strong>mini-intelligence fusion center</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fusion center:</strong> normally a multijurisdictional police working group. It receives threat, organizational, and other information from many sources and agencies, and cross-references and analyzes it.</li>\n<li>It usually has a focus (terrorism, drug smuggling, counterfeiting, or human trafficking), but may combine several.</li>\n<li>In a company it may include business units, departments, corporate functions, internal stakeholders, and even key suppliers or partners.</li>\n<li>Advantages: broader sources, more perspectives for analysis, and dissemination, with protections, to a larger audience. A more useful result for the organization.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sources that are also customers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The police and asset owners can be both “source” and “customer” or recipient of the finished intelligence. It is common and logical.</li>\n<li>Processing in the analysis phase avoids simply returning what the source gave and adds value for all parties.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tactical focus and link analysis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tactical analysis is usually tied to an ongoing investigation or operation.</li>\n<li>It also serves to visualize complex threat trends and interdependencies relevant to the organization’s risk posture.</li>\n<li><strong>Most-used tool: link analysis.</strong><ul>\n<li>It is very old. It probably originated in the early detective agencies, such as Pinkerton.</li>\n<li>It has been promoted by professional associations, especially the International Association of Law Enforcement Intelligence Analysts (IALEIA).</li>\n<li>It used to be done with whiteboards and sticky notes. Today it uses specialized software and data analytics.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> it represents graphically and logically the connections among people, places, and activities. It seeks to reveal patterns or other information useful for investigating.</li>\n<li><strong>Uses:</strong> solving crimes (the best known) and, proactively, threat analysis, predictive analysis, and study of criminal or adversary organizations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elements that can be data points in a link analysis (11):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Element</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Personal connections (2nd-, 3rd-, or 4th-degree, for example)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Communications among individuals, groups, or organizations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Travel patterns (local, national, or international)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Events attended (social, professional, educational, organizational)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Internet and social media connections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Employment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Locations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Vehicles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Memberships (formal or informal groups, criminal or not)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Activities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Financial patterns or transactions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A complete analysis tracks these elements for a crime, threat, group, or situation. It also detects changes and trends over time.</li>\n<li>General objective: “connect the dots” and obtain actionable intelligence.</li>\n<li>Applications of strategic and tactical analysis:<ul>\n<li>executive protection (static, local movements, and travel);</li>\n<li>crime prevention programs;</li>\n<li>threat assessment;</li>\n<li>fraud detection.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Both can reveal systemic weaknesses that facilitate crime or aggravate risk. For example, in security or business practices, decision making, project management, or facility design.</li>\n<li><strong>Social media:</strong> both types depend heavily on them, to detect and track threats and as a source of leads.<ul>\n<li>In the strategic type they are used mainly in collection. They should also be used in analysis, to give context or corroborate.</li>\n<li>In the tactical type they are invaluable for identifying suspects, connections, and threat actors, and for uncovering illicit activity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Data mining, data analytics, and link analysis tools are also valuable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.12.4 Protecting Investigative Information</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It receives less attention than it should, especially in the private sector.</li>\n<li>Once information is compiled, its content and the result are permanent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Harms if information is compromised:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It affects the future effectiveness of investigators or the IU.</li>\n<li>It exposes the organization to civil liability.</li>\n<li>It damages the reputation and careers of people: subjects, witnesses, interviewees, managers, colleagues (open list in the book).</li>\n<li>It damages important liaison relationships.</li>\n<li>It fuels rumors that harm the investigation, the stock price, market share, and corporate reputation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Legal framework:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In many jurisdictions, electronic and documented information requires extraordinary protection.</li>\n<li>There are regulatory sanctions for failing to protect personal information obtained in investigations and surveillance.</li>\n<li>In most European countries, Australia, and Canada, strict laws protect the privacy of personal information.</li>\n<li><strong>European Union General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), implemented in 2018:</strong><ul>\n<li>restricts what personal information is collected, shared, and accessed;</li>\n<li>affects security professionals and investigators, even in preemployment background investigations;</li>\n<li>applies even if the company or the investigator is not in the European Union.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cases where protecting information is especially important (5):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subjects who are senior executives or high-profile people.</li>\n<li>Joint task forces.</li>\n<li>Large compliance or regulatory investigations.</li>\n<li>Sensitive topics: terrorism, child pornography, workplace violence, major fraud, or product safety.</li>\n<li>Personal information protected by privacy laws.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Joint task forces:</strong> it is especially hard to protect information. Each agency has different policies, definitions, perspectives, and attitudes about what is sensitive and how to protect it.</li>\n<li>Protecting critical information is key in every investigation. Even in background investigations (BIs).</li>\n<li>A well-known principle of BIs: the investigator does not reveal one interviewee’s unfavorable information to subsequent interviewees.</li>\n<li>Besides formal protocols (such as government classification systems), there are two effective means: operational security and operations security.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Operational security</th>\n<th>Operations security (OPSEC)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Definition</td>\n<td>The total set of security and planning measures for the success of a specific operation and the safety of investigators</td>\n<td>A simple, systematic method of applying safeguards to protect critical information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Content</td>\n<td>Cover stories; backstopping (actions that sustain fictitious covers or identities); communication protocols; agent infiltration plans; countersurveillance; covert meet security (measures to hide a secret meeting); carding (keeping data on information sources); technical monitoring</td>\n<td>A five-step cyclical process (see below)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When it applies</td>\n<td>In the public and private sectors, in special activities such as undercover or sting operations. It is expanded in Chapter 2.</td>\n<td>In general, to protect critical information</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The five cyclical steps of OPSEC:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the assets (the critical information).</li>\n<li>Define the threat (collectors, capabilities, motivations).</li>\n<li>Assess the vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li>Analyze the risk (impact, priority, existing countermeasures; open list in the book).</li>\n<li>Develop and implement countermeasures.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Step 1. Define the critical information:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a key step but often forgotten.</li>\n<li>It consists of deciding realistically what information is sensitive at each phase and deserves protection.</li>\n<li>In joint task forces, a deliberate process is carried out at the start. All agencies must agree on what is critical information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 2. Define the threat:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identify, within reason, all possible collectors or adversaries. They may use legal or illegal means.</li>\n<li>Likely adversaries (5):<ol>\n<li>people or organizations with an interest in the outcome;</li>\n<li>friends or sympathizers of the subject;</li>\n<li>as-yet-unidentified accomplices (people or organizations);</li>\n<li>the media and plain public curiosity (especially in high-profile cases);</li>\n<li>possible copycats or others who commit similar offenses.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Rule of thumb: never underestimate how far the adversary will go.</li>\n<li>Many criminals use the same surveillance and human collection techniques as the police and legitimate investigators:<ul>\n<li>they monitor communications: radio, landline, social media, and cell phones (open list in the book);</li>\n<li>they collect information from courts and proceedings (public records);</li>\n<li>they use social engineering;</li>\n<li>they exploit public relations offices;</li>\n<li>they use open sources, such as news and the Internet;</li>\n<li>they go through the trash;</li>\n<li>they attack computers and mobile devices, sometimes even Internet of Things (IoT) devices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 3. Vulnerabilities:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Direct</th>\n<th>Indirect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical or electronic interception of information</td>\n<td>Telltale signs, such as more activity in the security department right before an important operation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Both must be considered. The investigator asks whether they show predictable patterns or do something the adversary can use to frustrate the operation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 4. Risk analysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is based on four factors: the nature of the critical information, credible threats, identified vulnerabilities, and the consequences of a compromise.</li>\n<li>It assigns levels or priorities to information risks. They are then addressed with countermeasures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 5. Countermeasures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Countermeasure:</strong> anything that negates or reduces the adversary’s ability to exploit information or vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li>It can be something as simple as changing behaviors or routines.</li>\n<li>Other countermeasures (12):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Countermeasure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Restrict conversations where others can hear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Limit the information visible on social media (Twitter, Instagram, Facebook, LinkedIn)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Limit the use of cell phones, radio, and wireless communications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Encrypt whenever possible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Avoid conversations in public places, such as restaurants or airports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Carefully choose surveillance vehicles, techniques, and participants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Coordinate closely with the public relations offices of the participating agencies and companies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Apply the “need-to-know” principle in the IU, agency, or company</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Protect written information and offices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Immediately destroy trash containing sensitive information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Apply good cybersecurity practices on systems with sensitive information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Include information protection clauses in security or investigation service contracts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.6. Process Flow Chart for Protecting Investigative Information</strong> (© 2020 Innovative Protection Solutions, LLC, used with permission)</p>\n<p>Pending: the arrows are graphics. The path was reconstructed from the position of the YES/NO labels on the page; it should be confirmed against the figure.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Question or action (figure text)</th>\n<th>If YES</th>\n<th>If NO</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Is the information sensitive?</td>\n<td>Step 2</td>\n<td>Monitor for changes in information value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Is the information public knowledge?</td>\n<td>Step 3</td>\n<td>Step 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Protect it anyway?</td>\n<td>Step 4</td>\n<td>Monitor for changes in information value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Are there adversaries?</td>\n<td>Step 5</td>\n<td>Monitor for development of potential adversaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Do they have motivation?</td>\n<td>Step 6</td>\n<td>Continue to monitor to determine if motivation and/or capability develops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Do they have capability?</td>\n<td>Step 7</td>\n<td>Continue to monitor to determine if motivation and/or capability develops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Identify capabilities to collect against target investigation/activity</td>\n<td>Step 8</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Select/implement countermeasures based on specific collection capabilities</td>\n<td>Step 9</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Determine effectiveness of countermeasures</td>\n<td>Step 10</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Restart the process</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Protective measures must not hinder the investigation, but reinforce it.</li>\n<li>All participants and anyone with access must apply cost-effective countermeasures.</li>\n<li>Benefits: they protect evidence, favor good outcomes (convictions or restitution), and protect personal safety.</li>\n<li><strong>Summary, at the start of every investigation or operation:</strong><ol>\n<li>define the critical information;</li>\n<li>assess the adversary’s capability and motivation to collect it;</li>\n<li>determine their likely collection methods;</li>\n<li>apply countermeasures to prevent, neutralize, or mitigate that collection.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.12.5 Working in Multicultural or International Environments</h4>\n<p>Because of globalization, public and private investigators must know how to work in multicultural environments. Examples (6):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Investigation in a domestic company with subjects or witnesses of another ethnic origin or nationality.</li>\n<li>Investigation that takes place in another country or follows leads there.</li>\n<li>Investigation with partners, subsidiaries, joint ventures, vendors, or suppliers located or incorporated in another country.</li>\n<li>Due diligence on companies or persons linked in some way to another country.</li>\n<li>Investigation with international or multinational organizations.</li>\n<li>Joint task force with agencies from more than one country.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Key considerations:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Laws</td>\n<td>They vary among countries and among jurisdictions within the same country. Agreements and rules of regional entities may have an influence: European Union, free trade zones, and others. The investigator must know and respect local laws, regulations, and customs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liaison</td>\n<td>It is approached with cultural factors in mind. Abroad, liaison with local police, security, and intelligence is common and often necessary. The relationship can range from very close and trusting to very fragile. Even so, it is usually wise to work with them, in general and in specific cases. A close relationship can bring enormous benefits and speed resolution. <strong>Before</strong> consulting foreign agencies, it is critical to discreetly research their reputation and practices. In multinational task forces, the team’s internal liaison is also essential: knowing each member’s tactics, legal limits, and culture improves effectiveness and reduces wasted and duplicated effort. Liaison as a force multiplier is expanded in Chapter 8.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cultures and Approaches</td>\n<td>Some techniques and strategies fit better in some cultures than others. Example: paying for information is an insult in some cultures and almost obligatory in others. Legal counsel must be consulted before offering payments. Interview techniques, the relevance of certain types of evidence, surveillance, and technical surveillance also depend on culture.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Other differences among nations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They interpret crime and technology very differently. Not all countries see computer crimes as a serious threat.</li>\n<li>Rules of evidence and the technical capability of the police vary. That affects their ability and willingness to help in technology cases.</li>\n<li>They have different perspectives on drug crimes and their investigation.</li>\n<li>Political, diplomatic, or economic relations matter. If they are not cordial, their representatives may not cooperate against cross-border criminals.</li>\n<li><strong>Cultural differences are the hardest problem in international investigations.</strong> The investigator must understand cultural diversity in general and the cultural and legal issues of the specific country or ethnic group.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.12.6 Ensuring an Appropriate Case Conclusion</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The general objective is a successful outcome or resolution of the case, whether the suspect turns out to be implicated or is exonerated.</li>\n<li><strong>Basic qualities of an investigation (5):</strong> objectivity, thoroughness, relevance, accuracy, and timeliness.</li>\n<li>Management must also attend to three groups of issues:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal</td>\n<td>Licensing and legal liability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Technical</td>\n<td>Appropriate investigative techniques and protection of information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management</td>\n<td>Effective coordination, documentation, and presentation of the case</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Balancing these issues well helps resolve the case and protect the organization’s assets.</li>\n<li>On closing any case, the findings are evaluated to see other uses or benefits for the organization. Detail in Chapter 8.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e12\">1.13 Types of Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>Most IUs focus on one or a few types of case, those that make up the bulk of their caseload. The list of typical types is in section 1.9.1.</li>\n<li>Some types deserve separate treatment. Background investigations are a distinct function because of their focus, resources, reporting, case resolution, and legal issues.</li>\n<li>In all cases, an effective and reliable investigation is timely and accurate.</li>\n<li>For each type, the book explains what distinguishes it, what special techniques apply, and what investigators and managers should know.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.13.1 Incident Investigations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Initiating event in the private sector:</strong> normally, a security force incident report. Appendix 1A has two formats used in commercial and government facilities.</li>\n<li>Other initiating events: direct complaints, whistleblower reports, and security audits or assessments. That is why they are also called <strong>complaint investigations</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>It is the most common type</strong> in many companies and organizations.</li>\n<li>It is needed in almost every organization, although many have no formal IU. That is why it is usually done by a security supervisor or the security director, not a full-time professional investigator.</li>\n<li>A detailed, direct procedural guide is recommended: checklists, forms, and an incident investigation manual.</li>\n<li>That guide is adapted to the organization: to its needs, its culture, and the applicable legal factors.</li>\n<li><strong>Comparison of approach:</strong><ul>\n<li>Incident investigations, like BIs, call for an especially methodical and systematic approach.</li>\n<li>Other types call for more creative problem solving or a flexible approach depending on the situation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>With no IU or full-time investigator: simple procedures (protocols) to investigate and report.</li>\n<li>With an IU: guidelines that delimit responsibilities, perhaps within the investigative policy statement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sample guideline from the book:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investigated by the senior security supervisor, or done under their authority</th>\n<th>Referred to the IU</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Theft (estimated loss &lt; $10,000)</td>\n<td>Theft (estimated loss &gt; $10,000)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unknown loss (&lt; $10,000)</td>\n<td>Unknown loss (&gt; $10,000)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple assault</td>\n<td>Aggravated assault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety hazard</td>\n<td>Property damage or vandalism (&gt; $5,000)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vehicle accident</td>\n<td>Employee misconduct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Property damage or vandalism (&lt; $5,000)</td>\n<td>Executive misconduct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unsecured area</td>\n<td>Workplace violence (real or potential)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Sexual harassment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Sexual assault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Proprietary information loss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Fraud and economic crime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Contract irregularities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Unsecured area or forced entry</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Supervisor: 7 cases. IU: 13 cases.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the guideline uses “&lt; $10,000” and “&gt; $10,000” (and the same with $5,000). It does not say who investigates a loss exactly equal to the threshold. Also, “unsecured area” appears in both columns; in the IU’s it is joined with “forced entry.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>These thresholds can also serve to refer matters to the police or the legal area.</li>\n<li>Even if there is police interest, it can be very costly to prove that a specific crime occurred or to open a criminal investigation.</li>\n<li>It depends on the jurisdiction. Factors: local police priorities, capacity, workload, and prior experience.</li>\n<li>In many jurisdictions, police investigative units are overloaded. They do not investigate unless there is a clear threat to public order or a loss above a certain threshold.</li>\n<li>Each company must set its own loss thresholds. They serve to decide whether to investigate and with what parameters.</li>\n<li><strong>Criminal and civil:</strong> the same facts that prove a crime can prove a civil tort. A for-profit company may be interested in civil remedies as much as in criminal convictions. In both cases a thorough and accurate investigation is needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Embezzlement example:</strong><ul>\n<li>cash disbursements are the most common form of workplace embezzlement;</li>\n<li>that is why the accounts payable ledger is reviewed: dates and amounts paid to vendors.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The results can be crucial in administrative or personnel proceedings.</li>\n<li><strong>Separation of duties:</strong> the IU works with the departments so that an accounts payable employee cannot add or delete names in the master vendor file.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incident investigation process</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The protocols are approved by senior management to give them credibility and authority, whether or not there is an IU or full-time investigator.</li>\n<li>The protocol includes a <strong>decision logic matrix</strong>. It serves to know quickly three things:<ol>\n<li>who is responsible for the investigation;</li>\n<li>who is notified;</li>\n<li>whether an immediate referral to the police or the legal department is warranted.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>After that, the protocol describes the investigative steps.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recommended contents of a protocol (sample from the book, 11 steps):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Assess the credibility of the source and of the information.</li>\n<li>Determine whether there is physical evidence worth collecting. That is, is there a scene to process?</li>\n<li>Conduct initial witness interviews:\n   - priority to those directly involved or with perishable information;\n   - priority to those who may not be available later: visitors, employees about to travel or be reassigned, informants passing through the area (open list in the book);\n   - ask for recommendations on whom else to interview, with their identification or contact data;\n   - document those who refused to give statements or were unavailable (witnesses, bystanders, others).</li>\n<li>Document other relevant situational or environmental conditions.</li>\n<li>List all security personnel, corporate personnel, and officials who took part in the response, assessment, and initial steps.</li>\n<li>Conduct third-party interviews: people or agencies who did not witness the incident or were not present, but may know something.</li>\n<li>Consult security and investigation indexes or databases.</li>\n<li>Make and document all notifications of the incident.</li>\n<li>Detect information gaps and identify sources or methods to fill them: interviews, evidence collection, surveillance, observation, documentary research, records checks (open list in the book).</li>\n<li>Recommend follow-up or referral actions.</li>\n<li>Complete the documentation and the database entry.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Training and complaint handling:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Officers and security staff are trained in the protocols, including information protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Anonymous reports or complaint calls:</strong> the informant must be made to feel that their information is important, appreciated, and will be investigated. Otherwise, valuable future reports are discouraged.</li>\n<li>At the same time, sensitive information is not given to the complainant or to others. The complaint may lead to an undercover operation or other activity that must be confidential.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Report:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is usually a standardized form.</li>\n<li>It must be filed or recorded properly in a database, so it can be retrieved later.</li>\n<li>It must indicate whether follow-up is required or recommended. Also whether referral to the IU, an outside investigative firm, or the police is advisable or mandatory.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.13.2 Misconduct Investigations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is an important subcategory of incident investigations.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes “constructive incident investigations.” It is a possible typo.</li>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> generally internal investigation. It is conducted when an employee or other person closely tied to the organization is suspected of having violated:<ul>\n<li>a written corporate policy;</li>\n<li>a directive;</li>\n<li>their conditions of employment;</li>\n<li>or a federal, state, or local law.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Key feature:</strong> they leave the employer highly exposed to lawsuits from employees or former employees who feel they were treated unfairly.</li>\n<li>Alleged causes: discrimination, wrongful termination, sexual harassment, defamation, and false arrest.</li>\n<li><strong>Best preparation:</strong> coordinate as early as possible with the human resources director and corporate legal counsel (or equivalents). It applies to almost every private investigation, but especially to employee misconduct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Techniques and issues in misconduct cases</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Both perspectives must be considered, that of the complainant and that of the subject:<ul>\n<li>for the employer it is a routine policy or disciplinary matter;</li>\n<li>for the subject it can be traumatic: they feel their career, livelihood, and reputation are at risk.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Experienced corporate investigators do not use techniques that could induce an innocent person to confess.</li>\n<li>Coercive interview techniques damage the reputation for impartiality. The IU and the investigator thereby lose credibility and trust.</li>\n<li>Some people are easily intimidated. Even if innocent, they may confess in the face of unfounded promises of leniency or invented, supposedly overwhelming evidence.</li>\n<li>Inappropriate techniques and failing to protect personal or confidential information can cause three harms: embarrassment for the company, erroneous conclusions, and lawsuits.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Suggested practices (4):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Practice</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Set at the start the anticipated sanction or outcome if the accusation is proven</td>\n<td>Avoids accusations of discrimination and the appearance of changing the sanction depending on what is found or who turns out to be implicated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Use internal <strong>and</strong> external sources to prove or rule out accusations</td>\n<td>Using only internal sources limits the scope and may hide critical facts or data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Gather information from the subject’s former employers</td>\n<td>Shows patterns of behavior, guides the investigation, strengthens the case, explains facts, and provides leads. Today’s high turnover makes it necessary to verify backgrounds thoroughly and to interview former coworkers, supervisors, colleagues, and other associates.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Collect electronic evidence even if it is not a system abuse case: emails, access requests, logons, file downloads, and remote access sessions</td>\n<td>They are valuable sources</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Side effects of the investigation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The possible side effects and the way to handle the case are considered.</li>\n<li>Surveillance: covert surveillance to gather intelligence is difficult and easy to detect if done on the move. Overt surveillance, whose aim is to prevent, can be effective.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> this paragraph of the book mixes surveillance with side effects and ends up confusing. The effects it lists seem to refer to the investigation in general.</li>\n<li>Possible effects:<ul>\n<li>lower productivity;</li>\n<li>strained relations between offices;</li>\n<li>threats and intimidation;</li>\n<li>pitting coworkers against each other and generating friction;</li>\n<li>short- or long-term impact on the work climate and trust.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>This happens even if there is only a rumor of an internal investigation.</li>\n<li>Side effects cannot dictate the aggressiveness or the outcome of the investigation. But they are taken into account in four respects: overall strategy, dissemination of information, treatment of people, and professional conduct of those involved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Examples of misconduct cases</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Frequent topics: conflict of interest, abuse of corporate resources, employee theft, workplace violence, and substance abuse.</li>\n<li>In each one it is critical that a specific corporate policy exist, and the attitude toward it. That determines detection, outcome, and legal sufficiency.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says “the existence of an attitude toward” a policy. Possible typo for “existence of, and attitude toward.”</li>\n<li>Features of a good policy (protect assets and the rights of the accused):<ul>\n<li>it is clear and well communicated: no employee or manager can credibly claim not to have known its meaning and consequences;</li>\n<li>it encourages or requires reporting early warning signs from coworkers, vendors, or customers (suspicious, threatening, or inappropriate behavior);</li>\n<li>it requires the employee to cooperate in any investigation, including misconduct investigations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The investigative process itself is also critical to deter, detect, and resolve misconduct.</li>\n<li><strong>Some investigations are prohibited by law and others are mandatory.</strong> Example: in many jurisdictions harassment complaints must be investigated. The IU manager must know prohibitions and obligations at the international, federal, and local levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Before investigating, it is decided whether the conduct is legitimate grounds for discipline under local and national laws. Factors (3):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Whether it occurred on or off the employer’s property.</li>\n<li>Whether it was during work hours or outside them.</li>\n<li>Whether the offender presented themselves as a representative of the organization.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Once it is started, the company decides three things, in close coordination with legal counsel and other officials:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Whether there was really a fault or violation.</li>\n<li>Whether it is mandatory to report it to the police.</li>\n<li>How to communicate with the employee during the investigation and the resolution.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of case</th>\n<th>What the book says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Conflict of Interest (CoI)</strong></td>\n<td>Its definition varies according to the organization’s mission, structure, and culture. It is usually defined in the policy and in employee materials. Legally it is essential that all employees and affiliates (consultants, contractors, subcontractors, vendors, among others) know the definitions, policies, and consequences. If <strong>the investigator</strong> has a conflict of interest, they must disclose it to the client and not take part in any way in the assignment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Corporate Resource Abuse and Employee Theft</strong></td>\n<td>It can involve a major loss. Possible abuses (5): 1) personal use of IT resources; 2) computer misuse: downloading pornography, accessing unauthorized information, spam, phishing (open list in the book); 3) personal use of company vehicles; 4) unauthorized or inappropriate use of company information; 5) inappropriate use of company aircraft. Main characteristics of internal theft (4): diversion, conversion, disguise, and divergence. The book does not define them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Workplace Violence</strong></td>\n<td>It ranges from simple schoolyard-style bullying to violence with loss of life. Investigation is a central component. Peripheral topics: property damage, computer system abuse, sexual harassment, verbal or physical aggression. Warning signs usually come first, and are often recognized only afterward. <strong>The best prevention is a preemployment background investigation program</strong>, although it does not guarantee that someone will not become violent. It is investigated as soon as possible when signs are reported. The investigator works with threat assessment teams and shares information as far as possible.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Substance Abuse</strong></td>\n<td>See detail below.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the conflict of interest row, “among others” reflects the book’s own “etc.”; the list of affiliates is open.</p>\n<p><strong>Substance abuse</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NORC (2020)</strong>, an acronym the book does not expand: employers spend almost <strong>$9,000 a year</strong> for each employee with an untreated substance use disorder.</li>\n<li>Those costs include absenteeism, lower productivity, accidents, theft, and higher medical expenses.</li>\n<li>It can also threaten public and workplace safety, create friction, and lead to drug dealing at work.</li>\n<li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> annual cost to businesses estimated at <strong>100 billion euros</strong> (UK Addiction Treatment Centers, 2020).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text gives almost $9,000 of spending per untreated employee. The NORC 2020 reference speaks of an average saving of $8,500 for supporting each employee in recovery. Figure and concept do not match.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the United Kingdom figure is in euros. Possible typo, because the British currency is the pound.</li>\n<li><strong>Employee assistance program (EAP):</strong> its use is encouraged. According to some sources, discipline for a drug or alcohol violation should <strong>not</strong> be postponed while the employee is in the program.</li>\n<li><strong>Canada:</strong> substance abuse is considered a physical disability. The employer must comply with reasonable accommodation, for example EAP and recovery time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>False allegations</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In all types of case they are a serious nuisance. They consume resources, damage reputations, generate distrust, and lower investigators’ morale.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Unintentional</th>\n<th>Intentional</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>An observer misinterprets an action as a crime when there was no crime or violation</td>\n<td>A disturbed complainant seeks attention, revenge, or to cover up their own fault by diverting attention to someone else</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Every reasonable lead must be followed, whether or not the allegation is believed to be false.</li>\n<li>Security and investigation professionals must be alert to that possibility and ready to act.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.13.3 Compliance Investigations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They share features with incident and misconduct investigations. In fact, they can fall into either of those two categories.</li>\n<li>Compliance weighs more and more on organizations and on those who run them.</li>\n<li>Having in-house capability to investigate compliance makes business sense. Sometimes the government requires it.</li>\n<li>The team must be trained in current regulatory issues and in investigative methods. It must know the business practices of its industry and in general well.</li>\n<li>Even small and medium-sized companies adopt formal compliance programs and in-house IUs to reduce their exposure to government investigations.</li>\n<li>In the US, the <strong>Sarbanes-Oxley Act</strong> and the <strong>Federal Sentencing Guidelines for Organizational Crime</strong> emphasize compliance programs and the visibility of executives.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text says “Federal Sentencing Guidelines for Organizational Crime.” The chapter’s reference is “Federal Sentencing Guidelines, Chapter 8: Sentencing of Organizations” (U.S. Sentencing Commission 2018).</li>\n<li><strong>Purpose of a compliance program:</strong> to protect the organization against violations of the law, failures of business ethics, and the legal penalties they entail.</li>\n<li>An effective program promotes three things: enforcement, monitoring, and employee training.</li>\n<li>It is built on a clear corporate policy, often expressed in a complete <strong>code of conduct</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Topics the code of conduct may cover (13):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Equal employment opportunity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Harassment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Workplace safety</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Confidential and proprietary information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Copyrights, patents, and trademarks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Company assets and records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Computer systems, access to information, and authorized uses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Conflicts of interest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Government entities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Antitrust violations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Political activities and lobbying</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Securities requirements of various jurisdictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Regulatory violations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Noncompliance can damage the company’s results. That is why employees are trained to recognize a violation at once and know whom to report it to.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reporting mechanism and hotline</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>An effective reporting mechanism is a key element of every program.</li>\n<li>Risks of hotlines:<ul>\n<li>several lines confuse;</li>\n<li>lack of reaction or follow-up discourages reports.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Some companies have separate lines for: regulatory violations; fraud, waste, and abuse; security violations; safety hazards; and management issues.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommended:</strong> one consolidated line, with well-trained professional operators and an effective system of distribution and follow-up. That way quality, valid, and useful reports are obtained.</li>\n<li>It can be run in-house or by an outside firm.</li>\n<li><strong>Advantage of the outside firm:</strong> employees trust more that their voice will not be recognized.</li>\n<li>An effective line greatly reduces losses in most organizations.</li>\n<li>Its effectiveness improves with an ongoing awareness program for all employee groups and with visible backing from senior management.</li>\n<li>Hotlines and other mechanisms demonstrate commitment to compliance, because they uncover and deter misconduct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Scope by type of violation</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Violation of internal policies or procedures</th>\n<th>Violation of government law (e.g., insider trading)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>It does not usually have the judicial scrutiny of a criminal investigation</td>\n<td>Narrow scope because of legal restrictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It can address broad business practice issues</td>\n<td>It can involve serious criminal penalties</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>With credible information of a violation, a thorough investigation gathers and relates the facts.</li>\n<li>Companies must have in-house capability and be ready to cooperate with police or regulatory agency investigations.</li>\n<li>Having an effective program to detect and prevent violations <strong>reduces the culpability</strong> of the organization in some cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>U.S. Sentencing Commission</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>2004</strong> it tightened the standards of the compliance and ethics program. It gave boards and executives more responsibility for its oversight and management.</li>\n<li>The guidelines give a reasonable time for the internal investigation. They assume the company’s cooperation will be timely and thorough.</li>\n<li><strong>Two factors that mitigate an organization’s penalty</strong> (U.S. Sentencing Commission 2018):<ol>\n<li>that an effective compliance and ethics program exists;</li>\n<li>self-reporting, cooperation, or acceptance of responsibility.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.7. Examples of Regulatory Agencies with Enforcement Authority</strong> These are international and US (federal, state, and local) agencies with an enforcement interest, role, or mission in corporate compliance investigations.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>International (6)</th>\n<th>National (15)</th>\n<th>Local and Regional (12)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA)</td>\n<td>Nuclear agencies</td>\n<td>Health departments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>European Union Network for the Implementation and Enforcement of Environmental Law (IMPEL)</td>\n<td>Communications agencies</td>\n<td>Motor vehicle agencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>International Network for Environmental Compliance and Enforcement (INECE)</td>\n<td>Aviation agencies</td>\n<td>Courts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>United Nations Environmental Programme (UNEP)</td>\n<td>Workplace safety agencies</td>\n<td>Law enforcement entities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secretariat for the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD)</td>\n<td>Environmental protection agencies</td>\n<td>Construction authorities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO)</td>\n<td>Controlled substances agencies</td>\n<td>Zoning authorities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Labor relations agencies</td>\n<td>Licensing authorities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Securities agencies</td>\n<td>Food service authorities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Tax authorities</td>\n<td>Alcohol control agencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Customs</td>\n<td>Animal control agencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Postal authorities</td>\n<td>Utilities agencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Utilities authorities</td>\n<td>Educational authorities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Biological protection agencies</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Public health agencies</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Transportation agencies</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Translations of Figure 1.7:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>International:</strong> International Atomic Energy Agency; European Union network for implementing environmental law; international network for environmental compliance; United Nations Environmental Programme; Secretariat for the Convention on Biological Diversity; International Civil Aviation Organization.</li>\n<li><strong>National:</strong> nuclear; communications; aviation; workplace safety; environmental protection; controlled substances; labor relations; securities; tax authorities; customs; postal; utilities; biological protection; public health; transportation.</li>\n<li><strong>Local and regional:</strong> health departments; motor vehicles; courts; law enforcement; construction; zoning; licensing; food service; alcohol control; animal control; utilities; education.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.13.4 Exploratory (Informational) Investigations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> they are opened to answer questions or explain a situation. They do not seek facts about a specific incident, violation, or possible crime.</li>\n<li>Sometimes they lead to another, more \"accusatory\" investigation. Most of the time they only seek to answer questions such as:<ul>\n<li>Why is this happening?</li>\n<li>What caused or enabled it?</li>\n<li>What exactly happened?</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common forms:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n<th>Where it is expanded</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Background investigation of personnel or level of trust determination</td>\n<td>Includes preemployment, in-employment, and continuous screening. Evaluates the integrity and qualifications of a person in a position of trust. Helps mitigate the insider threat.</td>\n<td>Chapter 4 and the ASIS International Preemployment Background Screening guideline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Due diligence</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Chapter 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enigmatic conditions</td>\n<td>Source of inventory discrepancies, adverse shrinkage trends, productivity or performance variations, accounting anomalies, and other enigmatic conditions</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reputational damage</td>\n<td>Tracing reputational damage or disinformation on social media, online platforms, or through website or browser hijacking</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The explanation may or may not be related to a crime or a security issue. The key is to answer the question.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Situations that facilitate crime (example: human trafficking)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The exploratory category includes investigations that identify situations that facilitate or support criminal activity. They do not examine the criminal incident itself.</li>\n<li>Example: a company looks for conditions that may contribute to human trafficking.</li>\n<li><strong>Human trafficking:</strong> exploitation of people for sexual purposes or forced labor.</li>\n<li>It is serious in specific sectors, especially hospitality and travel.</li>\n<li>Hotels, motels, resorts, and short-stay rentals are often used, sometimes with the knowledge or complicity of owners or managers.</li>\n<li>Investigating trafficking itself is usually a task for law enforcement (LE). Companies and other private organizations, including nonprofits, collaborate closely.</li>\n<li>According to open sources, the police often work in joint task forces. They rely heavily on networks with nongovernmental organizations (NGOs), schools, churches, health centers, civic groups, and businesses.</li>\n<li>These entities can help with:<ul>\n<li>victim support and care;</li>\n<li>background information;</li>\n<li>leads;</li>\n<li>data searches;</li>\n<li>surveillance;</li>\n<li>awareness campaigns;</li>\n<li>employee training;</li>\n<li>sometimes, more direct investigative support.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Companies can also investigate internally for two reasons:<ul>\n<li>to discover conditions that favor illicit or corrupt activities on their premises;</li>\n<li>to mitigate the reputational risk of being associated with criminal enterprises such as trafficking.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Companies or organizations are sometimes invited to join the joint task force and contribute their perspective and capabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.13.5 Investigative Techniques in Exploratory (Informational) Inquiries</h4>\n<p><strong>Background investigations</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They rely mainly on interviews and database searches: criminal record systems, local records, and verification of previous employment.</li>\n<li><strong>Main objective:</strong> to compare what emerges in the investigation with what the subject provided, for example on the job application or in the background questionnaire.</li>\n<li>Inconsistencies may indicate an area of concern, a red flag, perfidy, or an attempt to conceal something.</li>\n<li>Privacy rules must be respected; they vary by jurisdiction and country.</li>\n<li>Sometimes honesty testing or polygraph is included.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other exploratory investigations: business knowledge required</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of investigation</th>\n<th>Knowledge needed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Discrepancies (inventory)</td>\n<td>Inventory management and supply chain administration, including the organization's specific systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accounting anomalies</td>\n<td>Financial crimes and common, lawful accounting procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Due diligence</td>\n<td>Fiduciary relationships, contract law, mergers and acquisitions law and, where applicable, international agreements and standards</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Conclusion: an effective investigator thoroughly understands the business (or the organization) and the investigative process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common traits</strong> of investigations of discrepancies or anomalies, due diligence, and situations that facilitate peripheral crimes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They require knowing the organization's software, applications, data storage, and network communication protocols.</li>\n<li>They require knowing the devices in use:<ul>\n<li>sensors;</li>\n<li>barcode readers;</li>\n<li>radio-frequency identification (RFID) readers;</li>\n<li>point-of-sale (POS) terminals;</li>\n<li>industrial control system components;</li>\n<li>manufacturing or production controllers;</li>\n<li>building controls;</li>\n<li>access control systems.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>They are critical to the business, and their relevance to the investigation must be assessed.</li>\n<li>Examples of critical processes: inventory management programs, automated order fulfillment systems, and electronic financial transactions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Forensic accounting</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is usually associated with fraud and white-collar crime. It is also useful in exploratory investigations, especially of financial discrepancies, anomalies, or irregularities.</li>\n<li>Forensic accountants are trained in investigative techniques and automated tools.</li>\n<li>They reveal irregularities in complex procedures and record-keeping systems, especially in financial and compliance data.</li>\n<li>They also evaluate internal controls and recommend improvements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Root cause analysis</strong> (see Chapter 8)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It fits well in exploratory investigations, because they seek to know exactly why or how something happened.</li>\n<li>Example: a sudden increase in shrinkage may be due to a crime. It may also be due to:<ul>\n<li>careless paperwork by a new manager;</li>\n<li>a change of computer program or a software update;</li>\n<li>warehouse employee turnover;</li>\n<li>a new supply chain vendor;</li>\n<li>a combination of these.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The answer is often not obvious, even after the initial investigation. A suitable root cause technique can resolve it.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e13\">1.14 Investigative Techniques and Tools</h3><ul>\n<li>The section guides managers, unit heads, and security directors on general methods.</li>\n<li>The techniques are subject to the policies and rules of the IU and its jurisdiction.</li>\n<li>Investigators must be trained in three things: basic investigation, relevant specialties, and legal limits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.14.1 Interviewing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>An investigator's success is sometimes measured by their ability to interview or interrogate. It is covered in Chapter 5.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.14.2 Records and Database Checks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are a key element of almost any investigation. See Chapter 8, Online Information Sources.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.14.3 Evidence Collection and Handling</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Collecting, handling, processing, and using evidence is a key component of the investigation.</li>\n<li>Procedures for gathering, protecting, examining, storing, and presenting it are crucial.</li>\n<li>Some cases are resolved quickly with dismissal, restitution, or both. Others go through the judicial system and may take years to reach trial.</li>\n<li>Evidence tasks are tedious and resource-consuming, including a great deal of secure storage space.</li>\n<li>Physical, technical, and other types of evidence can be critical to resolving the case and to judicial or administrative decisions.</li>\n<li>It is expanded in Chapter 6.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.14.4 Surveillance</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Eugene Ferraro, CPP (2012)</td>\n<td>Carefully observing people, places, or things. It does not have to be covert, although it usually is. It is probably the oldest form of investigation and perhaps the most used until the arrival of the Internet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS Investigations Standard (ASIS International 2015)</td>\n<td>Direct and deliberate observation or monitoring of people, places, or things</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Main purpose:</strong> to obtain information about the identity or activities of those under investigation.</li>\n<li>Taking notes during surveillance is essential.</li>\n<li><strong>Three broad categories:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical</td>\n<td>Human observation or following</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychological</td>\n<td>Behavioral analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>Technical or electronic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>It is widely used in public and private investigations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.14.5 Computer Forensics</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> the science of acquiring, preserving, retrieving, and presenting data that has been electronically processed and stored on computer media.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Traditional forensic science</th>\n<th>Computer forensics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Where it is done</td>\n<td>Normally in a laboratory</td>\n<td>In forensic laboratories, data processing departments, and sometimes almost anywhere</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What it produces</td>\n<td>A report based on the objective results of a scientific examination</td>\n<td>Extracts or produces information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Need for case details</td>\n<td>Generally does not need them (a DNA analysis is done without knowing the name of the victim or the subject)</td>\n<td>To be effective, it must be guided by what the investigation discovers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The examiner cannot review all the files. The capacity of a large disk or a central server makes it impossible in the time available.</li>\n<li>That is why the investigator must give the examiner the details of the case.</li>\n<li>The examiner can provide a prefilled list of keywords, phrases, or \"terms\" (in malware-type events). That makes the job much easier.</li>\n<li>To be effective, the examination must be a coordinated effort between investigator and examiner.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-1-e14\">Key terms</h3><p>investigation · fact-finding process · ANSI/ASIS INV.1-2015 · management · defalcation · embezzlement · suitability determination · personnel security clearance · threat assessment · case level · strategic level · operational level · objectivity · thoroughness · relevance · accuracy · timeliness · working hypothesis · corroboration · leads · secondary and tertiary leads · independent case management · spectrum of details · depth of details · scripting an alibi · open-ended questions · cause and effect · self-disclosure · eyewitness identification · Innocence Project · inherent contradictions · spoofing · listed references · investigative unit (IU) · proactive vs reactive strategy · beyond a reasonable doubt · preponderance of evidence · burden of proof · restitution · recovery · powers of arrest · search and seizure · due process · attorney work product · attorney-client privilege · discovery · legal counsel of record · internal controls · plan, organize, direct, coordinate, control · goals · objectives · metrics · mission statement · change orders · zero-tolerance policy · heavy-handed techniques · failure to warn · outsourcing · retainer · cost-benefit analysis · return on investment (ROI) · benchmarking · compliance investigation · tainted investigation · chief security officer (CSO) · needs assessment · inspector general · regional IUs · surge basis · reporting chain · final decision maker · threat management team (TMT) · full-scope vs as-needed services · questioned document examination · statement analysis · forensic polygraph · technical surveillance countermeasures · brand integrity investigations · distinct vs bundled services · vetting · Intellenet (International Intelligence Network) · pass-through contract clauses · functional charter · policy statement · disposition · proprietary IU · three I's (information, interrogation, instrumentation) · scatter graphs · PCI · CPP · CFE · CFI · professional qualifications vs personal traits · storyboard · link analysis · war room · chain of custody · separately zoned intrusion detection system · retention and destruction · turnkey digital recording systems · covert surveillance · low-profile vehicle · liaison contacts · information assets · employee hotline · geographic assets · types of investigations · incident management system · lapping · skimming · product tampering · diverted or counterfeit product · litigation support · due diligence · level of trust investigation · civil recovery · quantitative estimation of risk avoidance · investigative life cycle · initiation · whistleblower · UN Convention Against Corruption (2003) · Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 · Federal Sentencing Guidelines · awareness program · case management system · investigative assignment ledger · checks and balances · trend analysis · criminal intelligence analysis · synergy · specific and measurable objectives · armchair psychologist · participative management style · case predication · primary and secondary objectives · investigative plan · case file · case chronology form · interim report · operations coordinator · checkout card · access permissions · audit trail · lead investigator · surveillance logs · evidence logs · suspense log · report of investigation · attorney work product privilege · dissemination controls · distribution log · venue · licensing · defamation · false imprisonment · false arrest · retaliation · subpoena · liaison · InfraGard · operational (tactical) analysis · strategic analysis · National Security Intelligence Cycle · Intelligence Collection Cycle · requirements · interrogatories · collection · witting and unwitting participants · competitive intelligence · open source intelligence (OSINT) · integration/analysis · actionable intelligence · fusion center · mini-fusion center · dissemination · evaluation · organized retail crime (ORC) · nondisclosure agreement (NDA) · IALEIA · connect the dots · General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) · operational security · cover story · backstopping · countersurveillance · covert meet security · carding · operations security (OPSEC) · critical information · direct and indirect vulnerabilities · countermeasure · need-to-know · force multiplier · incident investigation · complaint investigation · whistleblower report · decision logic matrix · perishable information · third-party interviews · tort · cash disbursements · accounts payable ledger · separation of duties · master vendor file · misconduct investigation · wrongful termination · conflict of interest (CoI) · corporate resource abuse · diversion · conversion · disguise · divergence · workplace violence · threat assessment team · substance use disorder · employee assistance program (EAP) · reasonable accommodation · false allegations · Sarbanes-Oxley Act · compliance program · code of conduct · hotline · insider trading · self-reporting · U.S. Sentencing Commission · exploratory (informational) investigation · level of trust determination · preemployment, in-employment and continuous screening · insider threat · shrinkage · website (browser) hijacking · human trafficking · red flag · perfidy · honesty testing · polygraph · forensic accounting · root cause analysis · surveillance (physical, psychological, data) · computer forensic science</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1.1 The Nature of Investigation Management"},{"id":"e1","t":"1.2 Qualities of an Effective Investigation"},{"id":"e2","t":"1.3 Public Sector versus Private Sector Investigations"},{"id":"e3","t":"1.4 Management Issues in Investigations"},{"id":"e4","t":"1.5 Organizing for Investigations"},{"id":"e5","t":"1.6 Fitting the Investigative Mission into the Organization"},{"id":"e6","t":"1.7 Outsourcing Investigations"},{"id":"e7","t":"1.8 Establishing an IU"},{"id":"e8","t":"1.9 Managing Investigations"},{"id":"e9","t":"1.10 Other Management Matters"},{"id":"e10","t":"1.11 Case Management"},{"id":"e11","t":"1.12 Other Investigative Management Issues"},{"id":"e12","t":"1.13 Types of Investigations"},{"id":"e13","t":"1.14 Investigative Techniques and Tools"},{"id":"e14","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"1.1 The Nature of Investigation Management","h":"the book defines “defalcation” in two ways. In 1.1 it is failing to account for money held in a fiduciary capacity. In 1.9.1 it is misappropriating entrusted property, which is the proper definition of embezzlement."},{"s":"1.4 Management Issues in Investigations","h":"the book calls “specific, measurable goals” “metrics.” Just before, it says goals are general and that what is measurable are objectives."},{"s":"1.9 Managing Investigations","h":"within 1.9 only three areas are developed (1.9.1 to 1.9.3). Case management appears separately, as section 1.11."},{"s":"1.11 Case Management","h":"the book says that “no one” may add or remove a file for any reason. Right after, it requires a card recording who removed it. It is an inconsistency in the text itself."},{"s":"1.12 Other Investigative Management Issues","h":"“Other informants” appears in both columns. In the external column there are two nearly identical entries: “Equipment or supply vendors” and “Company suppliers and vendors.” The assignment to each column was taken from the position of the text on the page."},{"s":"1.12 Other Investigative Management Issues","h":"the same cycle gets three names. It is “very similar” to the National Security Intelligence Cycle, the figure calls it the “Intelligence Collection Cycle,” and the text calls it the “criminal intelligence cycle.”"},{"s":"1.13 Types of Investigations","h":"the guideline uses “&lt; $10,000” and “&gt; $10,000” (and the same with $5,000). It does not say who investigates a loss exactly equal to the threshold. Also, “unsecured area” appears in both columns; in the IU’s it is joined with “forced entry.”"},{"s":"1.13 Types of Investigations","h":"the book writes “constructive incident investigations.” It is a possible typo."},{"s":"1.13 Types of Investigations","h":"this paragraph of the book mixes surveillance with side effects and ends up confusing. The effects it lists seem to refer to the investigation in general."},{"s":"1.13 Types of Investigations","h":"the book says “the existence of an attitude toward” a policy. Possible typo for “existence of, and attitude toward.”"},{"s":"1.13 Types of Investigations","h":"in the conflict of interest row, “among others” reflects the book’s own “etc.”; the list of affiliates is open."},{"s":"1.13 Types of Investigations","h":"the text gives almost $9,000 of spending per untreated employee. The NORC 2020 reference speaks of an average saving of $8,500 for supporting each employee in recovery. Figure and concept do not match."},{"s":"1.13 Types of Investigations","h":"the United Kingdom figure is in euros. Possible typo, because the British currency is the pound."},{"s":"1.13 Types of Investigations","h":"the text says “Federal Sentencing Guidelines for Organizational Crime.” The chapter’s reference is “Federal Sentencing Guidelines, Chapter 8: Sentencing of Organizations” (U.S. Sentencing Commission 2018)."}]},"investigations-2":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-2-e0\">2.1 What Is Undercover Investigation?</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Definition (corporate undercover investigation):</strong> it is the surreptitious placement of a well-trained and qualified investigator in an unsuspecting workforce. The investigator poses as an employee, vendor, or customer. Its main purpose is to gather information.</li>\n<li>The surrogate vendor or customer is usually built on a real, cooperating vendor or customer.</li>\n<li>By nature it is a covert operation. It is complex and does not work in every environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Main difference from other investigations:</strong> the investigator does not hide their presence or try to go unnoticed. What is secret (sub-rosa) is their real identity or the purpose of the investigation.</li>\n<li>The undercover investigator is called an <strong>operative</strong>.</li>\n<li>Like interviewing, it is an <strong>interactive investigation</strong>. That is why the operative obtains data about the problem and also the \"how\" and the \"why\" of what the subjects of the investigation do.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e1\">2.2 The History of Undercover Investigations</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1850</td>\n<td>Alan Pinkerton, a Scottish immigrant, founds one of the first private detective agencies in the United States in Chicago. He appears to be the first to have written about this practice. His operatives stand out as innovative and daring.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1861</td>\n<td>At the start of the Civil War, Pinkerton is named head of the Secret Service of Union General George McClellan.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1883</td>\n<td>He publishes <em>The Spy of the Rebellion</em>. It describes the first organized espionage network in the United States.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apr 13, 1862</td>\n<td>Timothy Webster, his favorite undercover agent, is arrested and convicted as a spy. He is executed in Richmond (Virginia) by order of Confederate President Jefferson Davis. His career was short.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Civil War</td>\n<td>According to Pinkerton, Mrs. Carrie Lawson was the first female undercover agent in the United States. She and her butler John Scobell, also a Pinkerton operative, gave key information to the North.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Postwar</td>\n<td>Pinkerton is sent to Washington, D.C. to form the United States Secret Service.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1865–1875</td>\n<td>Heyday of the Molly Maguires, a secret society of Irish Americans in the mining areas of central and eastern Pennsylvania. They rebelled against labor abuses in coal and the poverty of their towns. The mine owners controlled the police and the courts, so the Mollies murdered or intimidated police, government agents, and executives. The name is said to honor a woman who led a rebellion against landlords in Ireland in the 1840s.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1875</td>\n<td>The Mollies create a union in a region without unions and call a strike. Franklin Gowen, president of the Reading Railroad (with large mining interests), hires Pinkerton. Pinkerton infiltrates his best agent, James McParlan. Result: ten Molly Maguires hanged.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1947</td>\n<td>McParlan died rich, for unknown reasons. The descendants of the convicted suspected collusion and pressed Congress. McParlan's secret reports were not released until 1947.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1920s–1930s</td>\n<td>Detroit automakers use undercover agents against employee theft and alcohol at work. It became known years later, from the operatives themselves.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Until the 1980s</td>\n<td>Pinkertons, Wackenhut, and Burns offered undercover, but only as a secondary service. It does not reappear as an important factor in written accounts until the 1980s.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1981</td>\n<td>Industry recognizes, through press articles, that substance abuse has reached workers. A social problem becomes a workplace problem.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1985</td>\n<td>Undercover becomes, and remains, a preferred method for investigating substance abuse and certain crimes at work.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes \"Alan Pinkerton\" and \"McParlan\". Possible typo: the usual spellings are \"Allan\" and \"McParland\". It is also possibly a typo that Pinkerton founded the United States Secret Service. On the exam, answer according to the book.</p><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e2\">2.3 When Should Undercover Be Used?</h3><ul>\n<li>It is the exception, not the rule. It is considered only when there are no other options.</li>\n<li>It seems simple, but it is complex and demanding. It can bring lawsuits and legal liability for the company and for the investigator.</li>\n<li>It demands time, patience, and resources from the company. It needs a highly structured process and a flawlessly executed plan.</li>\n<li>The operative does not just collect data. They may have to deal socially and professionally with the target group or person.</li>\n<li>Types of relationship with the target:<ul>\n<li>Close and continuous: daily work tasks.</li>\n<li>Periodic or casual: coinciding at a recreational activity or on the same public transport.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Risk: seeking information can lead to <strong>entrapment</strong> (inducing someone to commit a crime). It can also clash with the personal need for safety, immunity, and privacy.</li>\n<li>For all these reasons, undercover is not for everyone or for every organization.</li>\n<li>The form the investigation takes depends on the information sought.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Typical situations suited to undercover (6):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Situation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>There is constant, reliable information of employee misconduct, <strong>conversion</strong>, or criminal activity. But details are lacking to prevent it or identify those involved.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Losses are known to occur in a specific area, for example customer cash that is not recorded. It is not known how they occur, who is responsible, or whether there is <strong>conspiracy</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Management strongly suspects alcohol consumption, drug abuse, or drug dealing at work. Or it has already detected signs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Alcohol or drugs affect performance and supervisors do not react or do not know how to intervene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Actual practices must be compared with required policies and a routine audit is not possible.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Management believes that infiltrating operatives will yield important results and has already ruled out the other reasonable options.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conversion:</strong> receiving money or goods from another and keeping or using them fraudulently for one's own benefit.</li>\n<li><strong>Conspiracy:</strong> two or more people take part.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e3\">2.4 When Undercover Operations Are Not Appropriate</h3><ul>\n<li>Never to collect personal or confidential information.</li>\n<li>Never to intrude into the private life of someone who has a reasonable expectation of privacy.</li>\n<li>Doing so gives rise to lawsuits (actionable) against the company and its investigators.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Do not use undercover in these 3 cases:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>To investigate activities permitted or protected by any government law, rule, or regulation.</li>\n<li>To replace another investigation that would give the same results with less time and fewer resources.</li>\n<li>To investigate protected union activities. U.S. federal law prohibits monitoring or investigating them. The same is true in many other jurisdictions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Unionized workforces:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Undercover is not ruled out just because there is a union.</li>\n<li>Operatives can be placed in a unionized workforce if there is no interference with the lawful and protected activities of the union or its members.</li>\n<li>The company must not use as operatives employees who are already unionized.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e4\">2.5 The Need for Undercover Investigation</h3><ul>\n<li>Done well, it protects assets and employees and helps reduce risks.</li>\n<li>It is chosen only if there are no alternatives and a significant return on investment (ROI) is expected.</li>\n<li>Initial question of every internal investigation: overt or covert?</li>\n<li>Put another way: full transparency from the start, or some covert measures to avoid problems and gather the facts effectively?</li>\n<li>If undercover is mishandled, it can be seen as an attempt to cover up irregularities.</li>\n<li>The purpose and objectives are defined in advance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Legal counsel:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is recommended that counsel take part in planning and oversight.</li>\n<li>The attorney can be the one who hires the investigators.</li>\n<li>In general, attorney–client product and privilege protect confidentiality.</li>\n<li>The chief security officer (CSO) or the highest-ranking security executive must be on the selection team and on the investigative team.</li>\n<li>The attorney may be restrictive because of bar association rules on deceptive practices.</li>\n<li>Example: in 2011, the Professional Responsibility and Professional Ethics committees of the New York bar association studied the subject.</li>\n<li>They proposed amending the New York Rules of Professional Conduct (RPC). The amendment would expressly allow an attorney to advise and supervise undercover investigations that are otherwise lawful (New York City Bar 2019).</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text dates the proposal to 2011, but the cited source is from 2019 (a proposed amendment to rule 8.4).</p>\n<p><strong>Agency and investigator:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A qualified investigator from a reputable agency is chosen to find the facts and run the process.</li>\n<li>The CSO verifies that the agency is licensed and insured.</li>\n<li>The agency gives written assurance that neither it nor its investigator has a conflict of interest.</li>\n<li>A project management team communicates frequently. It is made up of representatives of the company, the attorney, and the agency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Law enforcement:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It gets involved only when needed.</li>\n<li>Sometimes the company is required to report, for example if it discovers a conspiracy or real evidence of a crime.</li>\n<li>After the investigation, communicating well with the workforce can be key.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why secrecy makes sense, at least at the start (3 reasons):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Without more information, management and counsel do not know what to tell employees and customers.</li>\n<li>Often the goal is precisely to find out whether there is anything to disclose.</li>\n<li>If the company is only doing due diligence as a good steward, there may be no legal or business need to make it public.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Every operation has its own problems. They must be anticipated and contingency plans prepared. With good planning and resources, many are avoided.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Main problem: the operation being exposed or compromised.</strong> Its becoming known during or after is usually undesirable:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Who finds out</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Employees, for example through a management announcement before it ends</td>\n<td>Morale and productivity drop.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customers or vendors</td>\n<td>They are upset at having been part of what was investigated. They may cut off the relationship.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The public, if the investigation is compromised</td>\n<td>More resentment and bad publicity.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> only those with an absolute need to know take part or find out. Otherwise the investment and the people, including the investigator, are put at risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow-up:</strong> close monitoring lets management and counsel \"pull the plug\", that is, end the operation.</li>\n<li>It requires time and patience. It rarely yields results at the start. The operative must integrate and earn the acceptance and trust of those being investigated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Figure 2.1 — Considerations Before Approving an Undercover Investigation (Oberheiden, P.C., 2020)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>What it implies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Is a covert investigation viable?</td>\n<td>Do many people know about the problem? Can they be trusted to keep it secret? Is there a legal obligation to report? If there is little chance of maintaining secrecy, it is not recommended.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>How will confidentiality be maintained?</td>\n<td>How is the operative introduced to the workforce? Do they go through normal hiring? Meticulous planning is needed, and everyone must understand the importance of confidentiality.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>How long can the cover be maintained?</td>\n<td>What triggered the investigation may become public through factors outside the company. The decision must take that into account.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>What is needed to avoid accusations of a cover-up?</td>\n<td>People inside and outside may be suspicious because of the secret nature. That creates labor, public image, and legal problems. The company must be able to justify its decisions during and after.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Who should be on the team?</td>\n<td>No one who could be implicated in the allegations. There is usually outside counsel, senior in-house attorneys, corporate security executives, and executives of the outside agency. Every outside person is thoroughly vetted before learning of the matter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Is there anything that must be notified or voluntarily disclosed?</td>\n<td>Example: a consumer data breach may require notifying everyone affected. Some organizations have a federal obligation to self-report. Will that affect secrecy?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>When does it become overt?</td>\n<td>It must be decided when, if at all, employees, stakeholders, or the public are informed. If the suspicion turns out to be true, some corrective action will be needed.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book describes four different compositions of the \"team\". In 2.5: company, attorney, and agency. In Figure 2.1: outside counsel, in-house attorneys, security executives, and agency executives. In 2.6.1 (investigative team, minimum): corporate decision maker, human resources, security, and labor attorney. In 2.6.2 (project management team): five roles. Do not confuse them.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Figure 2.1 cites \"Oberheiden, P.C., 2020\". In the references that source has no year; it only says \"accessed Aug 4, 2020\".</p><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e5\">2.6 The Five Phases of the Investigation</h3><ul>\n<li>No organization should fear undercover if the situation justifies it. It is complex and expensive, but it is managed well with a well-thought-out plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>English name</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Planning and Preparation</td>\n<td>The team is formed, objectives are set, the agency and operative are chosen, and the other phases are planned.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Information Gathering</td>\n<td>The operative works undercover. The project management team coordinates, communicates, reports, and files.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Verification and Analysis</td>\n<td>After the operative is withdrawn, evidence, video, and findings are reviewed. Interviews are conducted and the information is organized by person.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Disciplinary and Corrective Action</td>\n<td>The company's decision makers apply discipline. There may be a referral to law enforcement. Everything goes to the case file custodian.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Prevention and Education</td>\n<td>The causes are corrected: hiring, supervision, and training.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>2.6.1 Phase One: Planning and Preparation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Planning is the key to an effective undercover investigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Investigative Team and Objectives</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimum members (4):<ol>\n<li>A corporate decision maker.</li>\n<li>A human resources representative.</li>\n<li>A corporate security professional.</li>\n<li>An attorney who knows state and local labor law.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The team decides the expected outcome. If misconduct is discovered, there may be discipline, prosecution, and restitution.</li>\n<li>Before choosing the path, 5 things are reviewed: past practices, industry precedents, the organization's policies, applicable labor contracts, and the criminal definition of the suspected conduct.</li>\n<li>The effect on labor relations, public opinion, culture, and reputation is also considered. And the possible ROI when there are no alternatives.</li>\n<li>These considerations may clash with each other. They must be analyzed and weighed before setting realistic objectives.</li>\n<li>Example: the risk of embezzlement is greater where the dominant value of the culture is economic.</li>\n<li>The objectives determine the type and structure of the investigation.</li>\n<li>Information unrelated to the purpose may turn up. Planning must provide a mechanism to handle it.</li>\n<li>There is information useful to the company that is not appropriate to investigate. Example: measuring the strength of a union campaign by identifying supporters. It could be considered an unfair labor practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Possible objectives of an undercover investigation (5):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the true nature, scope, and perpetrators of the problem.</li>\n<li>Gather information so management can apply its policies and take corrective action.</li>\n<li>Collect the information in the way that least disrupts the organization and its operations.</li>\n<li>Ensure the process delivers the best ROI.</li>\n<li>Create better training tools for employees.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>These objectives are attainable and are the hallmark of every successful undercover investigation. They show that the investigators understand what the organization needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Selecting an Agency</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Police can conduct undercover work in companies, but they almost never have the time or resources. That is why most companies hire specialized private agencies.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>What the client should do</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Ask for a copy of the license, insurance, and permits</td>\n<td>In many jurisdictions investigators and their agencies must be licensed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Review the training program and the operatives' training records</td>\n<td>The agency must do rigorous preemployment screening, periodic drug tests after hiring, and professional training.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Ask tough questions</td>\n<td>About its knowledge and experience in similar investigations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Ask for references and check them thoroughly</td>\n<td>The best agencies are known in the business world and active in their industry associations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Know its history of litigation and claims</td>\n<td>Every operative must be willing to testify and to take their case through to the end.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Ask for sample reports</td>\n<td>And examine them closely.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Require a certificate of insurance naming the organization as an additional insured</td>\n<td>Coverage must be occurrence, not claims-made.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Ask for law enforcement references and cases in which the police helped</td>\n<td>Prosecution of employees is not always needed. But drug cases and sex crimes, for example, cannot be handled without the police.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Policy type</th>\n<th>Coverage</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Occurrence-based (the one to require)</td>\n<td>Protects regardless of when the claim is filed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Claims-made</td>\n<td>Protects only while the policy lasts.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The client must also know the duration of the work, all costs, and how client and investigator will communicate.</li>\n<li>The corporate attorney must meet with potential providers. Their role is critical and they must participate actively during the investigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Selecting and Placing an Investigator</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Today corporate operatives are usually well educated, well trained, and highly motivated.</li>\n<li>Drugs:<ul>\n<li>The best agencies drug-test their operatives routinely.</li>\n<li>Tests must be random, because the operative may be exposed to drugs at any time. They protect their credibility and discourage carelessness.</li>\n<li>Unless their life is in danger, the operative never uses or tries illegal drugs. Doing so destroys their credibility and puts the investigation at risk.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Fitting into the workforce: the client helps the agency choose someone who goes unnoticed.<ul>\n<li>Personal factors to consider: sex, age, work experience, education, and ethnicity.</li>\n<li>They must also fit the socioeconomic level and lifestyle of the workers.</li>\n<li>Example: among unskilled workers, without a high school diploma and with low wages, the operative must not seem highly educated or spend more than the typical employee.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Communication: needs good written and oral skills. Writes daily reports and speaks with the agency supervisor and, sometimes, with the client.</li>\n<li>Position:<ul>\n<li>It must give them mobility to deal with many employees and get the information in the shortest time.</li>\n<li>At the same time they must be able to perform their cover position.</li>\n<li>They can pass as an on-site contractor or as an internal employee.</li>\n<li>As an internal employee they are treated the same as the others, with no preferential treatment or special consideration.</li>\n<li>Placement is crucial: the objectives must be achievable from that position.</li>\n<li>Example: if finished products are being diverted at the shipping dock of a distribution center, the ideal position is on the dock or near it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The operative's most important activity is learning the job and becoming familiar with the environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Best internal positions for the operative (5):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Material handling.</li>\n<li>Shipping and receiving.</li>\n<li>Mailroom.</li>\n<li>Customer service.</li>\n<li>Uniformed security.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>If the position requires special skills, the operative must already have them.</li>\n<li>They can be given prior training (remedial training). But it is usually cheaper to choose another investigator who already has that level.</li>\n<li>If they use a vehicle, no one should be able to trace it to the investigator.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ways of entering the position:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Way</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>What the book says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cold hire</td>\n<td>The operative applies for the job and is treated like any candidate.</td>\n<td>It is the preferred one, but perhaps the least practical.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controlled hire</td>\n<td>The hiring process is secretly manipulated to place them in the desired position and place.</td>\n<td>It does not assess it.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Cover story:</strong> a fictitious reason or motive given to hide the true one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It explains the operative's identity and how they got the job.</li>\n<li>It can be superficial or highly elaborate. Rule: the closer the relationship with the target group, the deeper the story must be.</li>\n<li>Social media and the Internet are both a challenge and an opportunity.</li>\n<li>The company and the agency must review them to see whether the operative's identity can be discovered before the investigation advances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Elements of the cover story (4):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Example or rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Connection to the operative's real past</td>\n<td>Ideally they have already held a similar job.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A solid \"back story\", with false personal data but that the target employee knows</td>\n<td>Saying they went to a certain school, worked at a certain place, or lived in the same neighborhood as the target.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Backing for the actions foreseen in the plan</td>\n<td>If they will ask the target to introduce them to their \"fence\", they must have that contact. If they say they have a customized sport utility vehicle (SUV), they must drive one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Documents that support the story</td>\n<td>The normal ones of any employee in their situation. Never carry documents with their real identity on the assignment. Alternative the book cites: hiding their driver's license when driving a company, assigned, rented, or personal vehicle.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>2.6.2 Phase Two: Information Gathering</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It begins when the client, the agency, and the operative agree on the details of the assignment.</li>\n<li>It involves two types of assets:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Asset</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>People</td>\n<td>The members of the project management team.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Processes</td>\n<td>How information is communicated, how it is reported, and how it is kept in the case files.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The Project Management Team</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It oversees the investigative work itself. It can be formed from the investigative team.</li>\n<li>It is five distinct but related people. Each has a different role, and they coordinate and communicate everything among themselves.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Functions</th>\n<th>Requirements and cautions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Undercover investigator (operative)</td>\n<td>Can make or break the investigation. The first thing is \"relationship building\": they learn the job, get to know the environment, and make contacts.</td>\n<td>The book calls this stage \"the most important phase\". They must not press for information or seem curious: it arouses suspicion. They must preserve evidence. Example: if a computer needs forensic analysis, notify managers so nothing is destroyed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Project manager</td>\n<td>Usually from the agency. Handles the day-to-day administration of the case and the daily care and attention of the operative. Keeps regular contact with the employer and the rest of the team. Is the channel through which all case information passes and is distributed.</td>\n<td>Good communicator, with excellent writing and a solid grounding in investigation and management. Police or military experience helps, but is not necessary. High ethics, knows the civil and criminal law applied to the investigation, and treats people well. Needs patience: cases of six months or more bore and frustrate. Must supervise, motivate, and guide the operative. Otherwise an inexperienced operative may invent evidence to keep the case alive. Some operatives have been accused of entrapment and of creating and committing the illegal conduct themselves.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Client representative</td>\n<td>The employer's main point of contact. All information entering and leaving the organization passes through this person. This protects confidentiality and greatly lowers the risk of accidental discovery.</td>\n<td>Be careful how reports are delivered to them. Example: information technology staff may open an email believing it is spam and discover the investigation. Must be high on the organization chart, with authority to decide policy and spend money. Must have a strong character and be trustworthy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corporate attorney</td>\n<td>Main reason to include them: to protect the investigator's work product from disclosure of evidence at trial (discovery).</td>\n<td>Many agencies and clients do not include them, and lawsuits show it. The security professional who relies only on their own knowledge and the agency's attorney risks their career. Must be a labor law specialist with experience in labor claims. Must understand criminal prosecution and its effect on employer liability. Contributes more if they join in the planning phase, because pre-litigation preparation in undercover work is sophisticated and unique. Must be able to resolve labor, contractual, and criminal issues.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Law enforcement representative, if needed</td>\n<td>Has a critical role in most cases. In drug cases, no purchase without approval from the police with jurisdiction. In other cases it provides resources and sometimes helps supervise the operative.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>On the attorney, the book adds a sentence: \"unlike litigation\", the prosecution must present all the evidence it will use, even if it harms it. If it does not, the rest of the evidence would be inadmissible and useless to the case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> that sentence in the book is confusing and does not explain the contrast with \"litigation\". It is reproduced as it stands.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book uses \"attorney–client product and privilege\" (in 2.5 and in case files) and here \"work product\". Possible typo: attorney–client privilege and work product are distinct concepts.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> it calls relationship building the \"most important phase\", although it is not one of the five phases.</p>\n<p><strong>Communication</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The team, however it is formed, does not work without good communication.</li>\n<li>There are three channels: written reports, electronic communications, and telephone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Written reports (the most basic form):</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The operative can begin writing them even before arriving at work.</li>\n<li>Most agencies require a daily report as soon as possible after each shift.</li>\n<li>Basic content: who, what, when, where, how, and why of what they observed and experienced.</li>\n<li>They must be easy to read and describe accurately.</li>\n<li>The operative writes them at the end of the day and sends them to the project manager, who reviews and distributes them.</li>\n<li>One must write as if the reader knew nothing about the case.</li>\n<li>Avoid relative terms: \"short\" for height, \"small amount\" for the drug, \"late day\", \"early hour\".</li>\n<li>Simple, direct language gives accuracy and clarity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Report types in the chapter:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Report</th>\n<th>Appendix</th>\n<th>Who writes it and when</th>\n<th>Features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chronological (timeline) report</td>\n<td>2A</td>\n<td>Operative, daily</td>\n<td>Basic report. The facts follow a logical sequence and each incident is easy to locate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structured narrative report</td>\n<td>2B</td>\n<td>Operative, daily</td>\n<td>One of the most popular formats today. Chronological but with more detail. Simple, readable, and conversational in tone. In third person. Each entry is an independent paragraph.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Investigative summary</td>\n<td>2C</td>\n<td>Project manager; normally after 10 consecutive days of the operative's work</td>\n<td>Brings together the daily reports and orders them by date and by topic. In third person. Replaces the dailies for the investigative team.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special report</td>\n<td>2D</td>\n<td>For a special or out-of-the-ordinary event</td>\n<td>No fixed format. Must be easy to read and prepare. 2D came easily out of 2C. If it may serve as criminal evidence, the operative reviews and signs it before it is delivered to the client.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Why the narrative report is in third person</strong> (\"the operative\", not \"I\"):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reason</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Practical (editing)</td>\n<td>In first person, technically only its author can edit it. Example: \"I observed Ben Richards leave his assigned machine\" would have to be changed to \"The operative observed…\". Then the operative would have to authenticate it again. Writing everything in third person avoids a lot of rewriting and saves time and effort.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Sworn testimony</td>\n<td>If the results are challenged, the project manager may have to testify under oath. They would have to admit they altered the operative's report. They may not know how to point out the changes and make a mistake. That error would be used to suggest that the report delivered to the client was inaccurate. In third person, they can explain that their report is not a doctored version. That way they can vouch for its accuracy and explain the differences from the originals.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>If 2A and 2B are compared with 2C, it is clear that going from 2A to 2C would require a great deal of literary license and a lot of \"red ink\".</li>\n<li>Some investigators begin each paragraph with an alphanumeric code. That way the project manager can pull the dailies together and sort them by topic or by date, quickly and in electronic format.</li>\n<li>The frequency of the summary depends on who uses it. The typical one is after 10 consecutive days of work. That gathers enough information to analyze calmly and with continuity.</li>\n<li>Since the daily is already in third person, it passes into the summary without being redone. Reordering it by topic makes it more readable and useful.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Electronic communications:</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reports uploaded to the agency's network at the end of the day are downloaded, edited, and forwarded to the client within minutes.</li>\n<li>The agency can send video of a drug sale at work to almost anyone with Internet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Telephone:</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In addition to the written report, the operative must talk with the project manager every day.</li>\n<li>Normally they do not need to take or keep notes of those calls.</li>\n<li>The project manager may record the calls to show the client that communication took place.</li>\n<li>In the end, what serves as the basis for action are the written reports.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Case Files</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When it ends, everything is kept: original notes, reports, summaries, and any evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Custodian of record:</strong> the person designated to keep and file the closed case file.</li>\n<li>The attorney can order that all records be delivered to them. That may give them more protection as \"attorney–client product\". They must decide it before the investigation and put it in a written instruction.</li>\n<li>The format is chosen by the project manager, but it must be simple and orderly.</li>\n<li>Minimum content (5): the operative's daily reports, investigation summaries, special reports, correspondence, and billing and expense information.</li>\n<li>It is confidential. Its name must not allude to the operative or the investigation. Even a code name or a case number could raise suspicion.</li>\n<li>Folders and files are downloaded and stored securely in electronic format.</li>\n<li>Duplicates can be deleted to save space. The originals are always kept.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.6.3 Phase Three: Verification and Analysis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>When the operative is withdrawn, the rest of the team takes further investigative steps.</li>\n<li>Evidence, surveillance video, and findings are carefully reviewed.</li>\n<li>The investigation can move from covert to overt, for example with employee interviews.</li>\n<li>Interviews are one of the most critical components of the whole undercover investigation (see Chapter 5).</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text calls Chapter 5 \"Interview and Interrogation\", but in the table of contents it is titled \"Interviewing\".</p>\n<p><strong>Order of interviews:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of investigation</th>\n<th>Order</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>General investigation</td>\n<td>Interviews move closer to the perpetrators over time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After an undercover investigation</td>\n<td>They usually start with the most involved and move toward the least involved.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A good interviewer can see that the statements of the least involved provide information about the most involved.</li>\n<li>There is no fixed formula: each investigation is different.</li>\n<li>Afterward, the team brings together all the information and separates it by person.</li>\n<li><strong>Four sources of information:</strong><ol>\n<li>What the operative obtained.</li>\n<li>What the employee said in their own personal interview.</li>\n<li>What others said in their interviews.</li>\n<li>The physical evidence.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The team guides the company's representatives through all the information on each person.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 2.6.3 places the interviews after the operative is withdrawn. But 2.7 says they can be left in their position during the interviews, and that withdrawing them earlier exposes them as an informant.</p>\n<h4>2.6.4 Phase Four: Disciplinary and Corrective Action</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>When the interviews and the investigation are finished, the team hands the information to the client's decision makers. They decide the discipline.</li>\n<li>The security professional and the rest of the team only gather information. Disciplinary decisions are made by other people in the company.</li>\n<li>After discipline, all files, records, recordings, and evidence go to the custodian of record, for example the attorney.</li>\n<li>Corrective action may be sending the case to the police. Prosecutors and police review the file and decide whether there is criminal prosecution.</li>\n<li>The company may be the victim of the crime, but it is not the one who prosecutes employees. That is decided by the police and prosecutors.</li>\n<li>The file must stand on its own. If not, civil attorneys can file tort claims.</li>\n<li>The book cites false imprisonment and malicious prosecution, and leaves the list open.</li>\n<li>Evidence is kept for the statute of limitations period set by law.</li>\n<li>It may be necessary to communicate with the rest of the workforce. But only after all discipline and corrections have been applied. Meetings must be direct and clear.</li>\n<li>Documentation is crucial. Without it, many juries will think the testimony should be discarded.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.6.5 Phase Five: Prevention and Education</h4>\n<p><strong>Basic elements on which crime at work depends (6):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>The book's nuance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Quality of the people hired</td>\n<td>Past behavior predicts future behavior.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Environment in which those people are placed</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Quality and location of the security that deters misconduct</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Clear and decisive action when an employee misbehaves</td>\n<td>If one waits for the annual review for minor matters, the employee sees it as punishment and injustice. Fairness is key: every employee expects it and deserves it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Quality of supervision of that environment and those people</td>\n<td>A toxic environment can create the motive: the offended employee wants to \"even the score\".</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Initial and periodic training in policies and procedures</td>\n<td>Effective technique: personalize the policy.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Examples for personalizing the policy (2 exercises):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\"Write three situations in which you think it is OK to ___.\" Options the book gives: steal, use drugs at work, harass others at work, sexually harass. The book leaves the list open.</li>\n<li>\"Give me one good reason to excuse an employee who ___.\" Options: steals from you, says you gave them the illegal drugs, sexually harasses you at work. The book leaves the list open.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Most offenders discovered in an undercover investigation should never have been hired. The logical thing is to stop the problem at the door with good preemployment screening.</li>\n<li>The gate that protects the company and the workforce is three things: background investigations, verification of employment references, and good employment interviews. Chapter 4 covers it in detail.</li>\n<li>Hiring badly takes minutes. Undoing the damage takes months or years.</li>\n<li>Undercover leaves lessons: it can reveal training failures and educational gaps.</li>\n<li>The team makes recommendations to management. Management chooses those with the highest ROI.</li>\n<li>Senior management may discover that some managers did not correct or ignored employee warnings before the problem grew.</li>\n<li>Often supervisors and managers lack the skills and tools to keep the problem from recurring. The answer is education and training.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Training modules the book proposes (5):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Detection and prevention of substance abuse at work.</li>\n<li>Loss prevention and asset protection.</li>\n<li>Prevention of employee theft.</li>\n<li>Prevention of workplace violence.</li>\n<li>Basic supervision and people management.</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e6\">2.7 Removing an Operative</h3><ul>\n<li>In every undercover investigation there comes a point when useful information yields a diminishing return. Normally there is already enough information to remove the operative.</li>\n<li>When to remove them is one of the most debated topics among supervisors. The book's answer: keep them as long as possible.</li>\n<li>If they are removed, they must leave with a credible explanation.</li>\n<li>If interviews follow, they can be left in their position so as not to unmask them. They can even be interviewed as if they were a suspect.</li>\n<li>Good time to remove them: when they are named as an offender and when others are dismissed.</li>\n<li>Removing them earlier can arouse suspicion. If they leave before the interviews, everyone will know they were the informant. Remember that they may live in the same community.</li>\n<li>If at the end of the case they are not compromised, they can remain very valuable in their position.</li>\n<li>After a highly visible investigation, communication must be open with all levels of the organization.</li>\n<li>After discipline in a high-profile case, undetected offenders grow confident. Some boast of having escaped. They become easy targets for the operative and for new investigations.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e7\">2.8 Types of Undercover Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>Undercover can reveal many loss prevention problems. Drug and theft investigations are especially well suited.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.8.1 Drug Investigations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Contrary to what is believed, most are relatively easy and not always dangerous.</li>\n<li>Once the operative is accepted by coworkers, they can focus more.</li>\n<li>With the contacts from relationship building, they begin to identify those who use.</li>\n<li>With the guidance of the project manager and the police, they systematically seek information on each identified user.</li>\n<li>They can end up buying drugs from anyone who wants to sell. That way they separate users from dealers.</li>\n<li>The guidelines for buying drugs at work are not public policy or law. They are written by the investigative team to guide the operative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Guidelines for buying drugs (5):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Guideline</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Qualify the dealer</td>\n<td>Entrapment = inducing a person to commit a crime. It is not a crime, but a criminal defense. To neutralize it, the operative \"qualifies\" each dealer with a simple two-point report. First: that the conduct already existed or that the dealer had sold before. Second: why the dealer wants to sell to them; the ideal motive is to make money. With a motive of their own, the dealer cannot claim to have been induced. The operative never invites another employee to commit a crime.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coordinate with law enforcement</td>\n<td>Every purchase is made under the control and supervision of the police. It is not always possible, but one must try to have the competent police know and approve. Especially in the \"buy-bust\" at the end. Without that immunity, the operative and the agency could be prosecuted for possession, transport, or purchase of a controlled substance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buy down, not up</td>\n<td>The police usually buy ever larger quantities to move up the distribution chain to the source. At work the smallest quantity the dealer will accept is bought. The company only has to prove that a policy was violated; the quantity does not matter. That way purchases are made more often and from more people. Larger purchases are left for the end.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Make purchases on company time</td>\n<td>In general, the company does not control what the employee does outside work. The focus is workplace misconduct, not enforcing public law.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoid actual drug use</td>\n<td>Unless their life is at risk, the operative does not use illegal drugs. To maintain their credibility with the client, they are tested routinely. Periodic hair and urine tests show they do not use.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Purchases give credibility to the report of drug use at work. But making no purchase does not sink the case.</li>\n<li>Reasons to forgo a permitted purchase (examples from the book):<ul>\n<li>The dealer insists on buying outside the company, in places where they would be alone with the investigator.</li>\n<li>The dealer insists that the operative use before selling to them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A well-designed investigation anticipates these situations in planning and finds solutions for them.</li>\n<li>Experienced investigators and arbitrators know it: purchases are not needed for a good drug case. \"Good investigations make good drug cases.\"</li>\n<li>More than an operative is needed: use all the available tools. The book cites chemical tests and dogs, and leaves the list open.</li>\n<li>If criminal prosecution is among the objectives, one must confirm that it is needed. Its value is calculated within the total ROI.</li>\n<li>Criminal prosecution of employees rarely gives a real return to the company. Disciplinary action is preferable: criminal prosecution only reduces profits further.</li>\n<li>The company must decide on dismissals without depending on the criminal system. Plea bargains and evidentiary problems do not count in the disciplinary decision (King 2015).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.8.2 Theft Investigations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are like any other undercover investigation, but demand special care in planning.</li>\n<li>Strategy and tactics weigh heavily. The operative must be visible and accessible to those who steal.</li>\n<li>At the start it is not known who they are. That is why this part can be difficult, expensive, and slow.</li>\n<li>Alternative: draw the thieves toward the operative.</li>\n<li>Basic tactic: the operative appears to be already involved in some criminal activity. Someone who already breaks the law in one way easily joins in breaking it in others. This is called <strong>cognitive consistency</strong>.</li>\n<li>A well-known axiom says that where there is substance abuse there is also theft. Whoever investigates a theft must also look for use.</li>\n<li>If they appear to be involved in use, the operative gains direct access to those who steal or divert assets.</li>\n<li>Another investigator or a third party can play the shady partner with criminal contacts. They go in and out of the game without much preparation.</li>\n<li>The operative does not call them a fence, thief, or trafficker. They present them as a trusted friend, relative, or gang member.</li>\n<li>In theft cases, the operative can take part in a plan to steal. But each action must have an explicit purpose and the prior approval of the project management team.</li>\n<li>Lure: offering small electronic devices, cigarettes, or common hand tools at ridiculous prices, so that coworkers think they are stolen. Event tickets are another popular gift or item for sale.</li>\n<li>Caution: bringing in weapons of any kind is an unnecessary danger and almost surely illegal.</li>\n<li>Example: the operative who sells cheap merchandise may be asked for a gun. They must not be allowed anything that reduces their safety or that of the workplace, or anything illegal or inappropriate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> do not confuse. In drug cases, the operative never invites another to commit a crime. In theft, they can take part in a plan to steal, with an explicit purpose and prior approval.</p>\n<p><strong>Retail Theft</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Theft occurs in any business, but it is especially common in retail.</li>\n<li>Retail shrink reached an all-time high in 2019. It was 1.62% of the retailer's \"bottom line\" and cost U.S. retailers about $62 billion (National Retail Federation 2020).</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> possible typo. Shrink is usually measured as a percentage of sales, not of the bottom line. Memorize 1.62%, 2019, and $62 billion.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Organized retail crime (ORC):</strong> it is a big problem and keeps changing because of new channels for selling what is stolen. It is increasingly organized, complex, and lucrative.</li>\n<li><strong>Definition by Matas (2020):</strong><ul>\n<li>Any organized, conspiratorial criminal attack against a retailer.</li>\n<li>It involves two or more people who illegally or fraudulently obtain merchandise, tender, confidential data, and personally identifiable information of customers.</li>\n<li>Its sole purpose is to convert that into criminal profit.</li>\n<li>It is usually coordinated with well-planned procedures, rules, and technical knowledge.</li>\n<li>It can operate at the local, regional, national, or international scale.</li>\n<li>It causes worldwide losses of hundreds of billions of dollars a year.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> do not confuse total U.S. shrink ($62 billion, National Retail Federation) with worldwide ORC losses (hundreds of billions, Matas).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Undercover is a tool against ORC because often an employee is the gang's inside accomplice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Areas where ORC has an impact (12):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Shoplifting.</li>\n<li>Sophisticated fraud schemes.</li>\n<li>Deep supply chain theft.</li>\n<li>Employee theft.</li>\n<li>Identity theft.</li>\n<li>Warehouse theft.</li>\n<li>Complex return frauds.</li>\n<li>Money laundering operations.</li>\n<li>Theft in shipping and transport.</li>\n<li>Creative con games.</li>\n<li>Cyber frauds.</li>\n<li>Counterfeiting.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Public-private partnerships are often formed against ORC.</li>\n<li><strong>CVS case (WLS-TV 2019):</strong><ul>\n<li>11-month undercover investigation in Chicago.</li>\n<li>The CVS Organized Retail Crime Team, the Wilmette and Evanston police departments, and the Criminal Investigations Division of the Illinois Department of Revenue took part.</li>\n<li>Result: 11 search warrants, $2 million in merchandise recovered, and 3 suspects arrested for reselling what was stolen.</li>\n<li>It shows the value of public-private partnerships.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>According to Matas, the gangs hire hackers, mathematicians, and programmers. They get into the computer infrastructure to identify account algorithms.</li>\n<li>With that they launch attacks on the point of sale, attacks on web applications and e-commerce, insider threats, and coordinated attacks on databases and their own operating systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Effective alternative to undercover:</strong> visible loss prevention agents. A study by the John Jay College of Criminal Justice found that they reduced shrink better than undercover agents (Seivold 2018).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Buy-Busts and Sting Operations</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>When and features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Buy-bust</td>\n<td>Arresting the perpetrator at the moment they buy or sell the item. The other party is usually a police officer or a designated agent. On an agreed signal, the person is confronted and arrested on the spot.</td>\n<td>Prevents evidence from being lost or destroyed. Avoids having to pursue dangerous offenders later. Works best at the end, as in drugs. Since it usually closes the investigation, the identity of operatives and police may be revealed on purpose. But it does not always happen or always need to.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sting operation</td>\n<td>Similar, but the term indicates a longer and more complex effort. It usually includes elaborate setups and buy-busts. It can include setting up and running a front business where stolen goods are sold or exchanged.</td>\n<td>Because of its complexity, it is usually expensive and slow.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Other tools in theft cases:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Property markers:</strong> invisible markers and sprays that have existed for years. They secretly mark goods; if stolen and recovered, the owner is identified. They are applied to vulnerable items.</li>\n<li><strong>Corroborating evidence:</strong> in most theft cases, evidence separate from the operative's is worth a great deal.<ul>\n<li>The most common and cheapest form is video.</li>\n<li>The scene is planned so that operative and thief are well framed on camera. That way the stolen item, the money, and the whole transaction are documented.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Multiple buys:</strong> as in drugs, they make the case more solid.<ul>\n<li>The operative should always attempt more than one purchase from each participant.</li>\n<li>They eliminate the argument that it was something spontaneous or isolated.</li>\n<li>They prove the person is a real offender and not someone who made a mistake once.</li>\n<li>At the start a minimum quantity is bought.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Recoveries:</strong><ul>\n<li>Knowing who steals and what they steal is important. Equally valuable is knowing why they steal and how they dispose of what is stolen.</li>\n<li>If third parties receive the stolen goods, there can be civil recovery. The investigation can be designed to recover from them as well, in addition to the main perpetrators.</li>\n<li>The item or its value (consideration) is recovered, and also the costs of the investigation.</li>\n<li>Such an agreement greatly increases the ROI of any investigation, not just undercover.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.8.3 Other Investigations</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>How undercover helps</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Seniors and childcare facilities</td>\n<td>Abuse and mistreatment of the elderly, who often cannot defend themselves or report. In childcare facilities, abuse and crimes against a vulnerable population.</td>\n<td>Operatives posing as staff, nurses, or doctors infiltrate and monitor operations and employees. Not even hidden cameras know what is said behind closed doors or what the children are told to do or think. Well managed, they detect abuse and neglect.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Health care (hospitals)</td>\n<td>Visitors and staff can no longer be allowed to move around freely. Baby theft, drug diversion, equipment sabotage, and assaults are concerns. Because they are open, they are an ideal target for thieves, vandals, and other criminals. More locks, cameras, and guards do not eliminate the problem.</td>\n<td>Operatives inside the staff, or posing as patients and visitors. They are a cost-effective tool that complements the security that already exists. The great mobility of employees and visitors makes the hospital a perfect environment for undercover.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Technology companies</td>\n<td>They must maintain their edge. If not, their own advances will be used to steal secrets, ideas, and people from them.</td>\n<td>Some large U.S. technology companies use operatives discreetly to protect their most important secrets. It is cost-effective and appropriate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Governments</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Investigate discrimination complaints. Secretly monitor food processing plants and waste disposal sites.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transportation and food and drug producers</td>\n<td>Chains of interlocked companies and high turnover make them vulnerable to waste and fraud.</td>\n<td>Could benefit from undercover.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fraud:</strong> intentional deception to unlawfully take possession of another's property.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e8\">2.9 The Cost of Undercover Operations</h3><ul>\n<li>The size, scope, and nature of the problem decide how much the company should invest.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Billing model</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>What the book says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Per week (or for a longer period)</td>\n<td>Weekly rate based on a 40-hour week plus a minimum of \"cover pay\". Cover pay is the wage the operative collects for their cover position. The rate includes the time of the investigator and their supervisor, report writing, transcription, postal mail, and communication with the client contact. It covers everything except out-of-pocket expenses and other special supporting investigations. The operative keeps the cover pay and the agency pays them the difference up to their normal salary.</td>\n<td>It is how most experienced firms charge today.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Per hour</td>\n<td>Report preparation and calls are charged separately. Writing time, travel time, and overtime are also billed.</td>\n<td>Done by less experienced agencies. The client feels \"nickeled and dimed to death\". Complicated invoices suggest everything is charged, minute by minute. Experienced clients know this system sets up hidden charges.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Operative levels:</strong> some agencies say they have several skill levels and charge a difference based on experience. Others pretend to have enormous \"herds\" of investigators by level.</li>\n<li>If an agency says so, the client should ask for:<ol>\n<li>Written descriptions and requirements for each level.</li>\n<li>How many investigators it currently has working undercover at each level.</li>\n<li>An investigator and a supervisor from each level to interview.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The best agencies do not have \"second-level or tenth-level\" employees. That system is too complicated and expensive to maintain.</li>\n<li><strong>Undercharging is much more common than overcharging</strong>, and it does not benefit the client:<ul>\n<li>Good agencies that are struggling cut corners where they can and take unnecessary risks.</li>\n<li>It unfairly reduces the profits of healthy agencies.</li>\n<li>It reduces the ability of all to pay their investigators well and give them benefits.</li>\n<li>It fills the market with inexperienced and unscrupulous competitors (inexperienced cutthroats), with dubious quality.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>No business keeps providing a service if it does not make money. Undercover is no different.</li>\n<li>Due diligence is essential when choosing an agency and operative. Police resources and contacts help identify agencies with a good ethical and success record.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e9\">2.10 The Future of Undercover Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>The future is solid: more and more organizations see that undercover solves what other security methods do not.</li>\n<li>To compete in the global economy, companies cannot assume losses that can be discovered and corrected discreetly. Nor can they risk their reputation because of what their employees do.</li>\n<li>Types that are growing: cybercrime and social media abuses, such as bullying and child predators. Workplace harassment may also require undercover.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-2-e10\">Key terms</h3><p>corporate undercover investigation · operative · surreptitious placement · surrogate vendor / customer · covert operation · sub-rosa · interactive investigation · Pinkerton · The Spy of the Rebellion · Molly Maguires · entrapment · conversion · conspiracy · reasonable expectation of privacy · protected union activities · overt vs. covert · cover up · return on investment (ROI) · attorney–client privilege · work product · discovery · chief security officer (CSO) · bar association rules · conflict of interest · need to know · exposure / compromise · pull-the-plug · investigative team · project management team · project manager · client representative · corporate attorney · law enforcement · self-reporting · remedial action · five phases (planning and preparation, information gathering, verification and analysis, disciplinary and corrective action, prevention and education) · unfair labor practice · embezzlement · additional insured · occurrence-based vs. claims-made · preemployment screening · cold hire · controlled hire · cover story · back story · fence · relationship building · daily report · chronological report · structured narrative report · third person · reauthentication · alphanumeric code · investigative summary · special report · custodian of record · case file · physical evidence · false imprisonment · malicious prosecution · statute of limitations · even the score · diminishing return · qualify the dealer · buy down, not up · buy-bust · sting operation · users vs. dealers · plea bargain · cognitive consistency · shrink · organized retail crime (ORC) · tender · personally identifiable information · public–private partnership · visible loss prevention agents · property markers · corroborating evidence · multiple buys · recoveries · civil recovery · drug diversion · fraud · cover pay · out-of-pocket expenses · nickeled and dimed · undercharging</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"2.1 What Is Undercover Investigation?"},{"id":"e1","t":"2.2 The History of Undercover Investigations"},{"id":"e2","t":"2.3 When Should Undercover Be Used?"},{"id":"e3","t":"2.4 When Undercover Operations Are Not Appropriate"},{"id":"e4","t":"2.5 The Need for Undercover Investigation"},{"id":"e5","t":"2.6 The Five Phases of the Investigation"},{"id":"e6","t":"2.7 Removing an Operative"},{"id":"e7","t":"2.8 Types of Undercover Investigations"},{"id":"e8","t":"2.9 The Cost of Undercover Operations"},{"id":"e9","t":"2.10 The Future of Undercover Investigations"},{"id":"e10","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"2.2 The History of Undercover Investigations","h":"the book writes \"Alan Pinkerton\" and \"McParlan\". Possible typo: the usual spellings are \"Allan\" and \"McParland\". It is also possibly a typo that Pinkerton founded the United States Secret Service. On the exam, answer according to the book."},{"s":"2.5 The Need for Undercover Investigation","h":"the text dates the proposal to 2011, but the cited source is from 2019 (a proposed amendment to rule 8.4)."},{"s":"2.5 The Need for Undercover Investigation","h":"the book describes four different compositions of the \"team\". In 2.5: company, attorney, and agency. In Figure 2.1: outside counsel, in-house attorneys, security executives, and agency executives. In 2.6.1 (investigative team, minimum): corporate decision maker, human resources, security, and labor attorney. In 2.6.2 (project management team): five roles. Do not confuse them."},{"s":"2.5 The Need for Undercover Investigation","h":"Figure 2.1 cites \"Oberheiden, P.C., 2020\". In the references that source has no year; it only says \"accessed Aug 4, 2020\"."},{"s":"2.6 The Five Phases of the Investigation","h":"that sentence in the book is confusing and does not explain the contrast with \"litigation\". It is reproduced as it stands."},{"s":"2.6 The Five Phases of the Investigation","h":"the book uses \"attorney–client product and privilege\" (in 2.5 and in case files) and here \"work product\". Possible typo: attorney–client privilege and work product are distinct concepts."},{"s":"2.6 The Five Phases of the Investigation","h":"it calls relationship building the \"most important phase\", although it is not one of the five phases."},{"s":"2.6 The Five Phases of the Investigation","h":"the text calls Chapter 5 \"Interview and Interrogation\", but in the table of contents it is titled \"Interviewing\"."},{"s":"2.6 The Five Phases of the Investigation","h":"2.6.3 places the interviews after the operative is withdrawn. But 2.7 says they can be left in their position during the interviews, and that withdrawing them earlier exposes them as an informant."},{"s":"2.8 Types of Undercover Investigations","h":"do not confuse. In drug cases, the operative never invites another to commit a crime. In theft, they can take part in a plan to steal, with an explicit purpose and prior approval."},{"s":"2.8 Types of Undercover Investigations","h":"possible typo. Shrink is usually measured as a percentage of sales, not of the bottom line. Memorize 1.62%, 2019, and $62 billion."},{"s":"2.8 Types of Undercover Investigations","h":"do not confuse total U.S. shrink ($62 billion, National Retail Federation) with worldwide ORC losses (hundreds of billions, Matas)."}]},"investigations-3":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-3-e0\">3.1 Definitions</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Definition (paraphrase)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International (2021, Security Glossary)</td>\n<td>The care expected of a prudent person in examining and evaluating risks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Black's Law Dictionary (Garner 2016)</td>\n<td>The diligence reasonably expected, and ordinarily exercised, by someone who seeks to satisfy a legal requirement or an obligation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Merriam-Webster (2021), sense 1</td>\n<td>The care of a reasonable person to avoid harming other people or their property.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Merriam-Webster (2021), sense 2</td>\n<td>The investigation and analysis of a company or organization before a business transaction, for example a merger or a purchase of securities.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Common business usage</td>\n<td>The investigative process to evaluate a target company or its assets before a buyer acquires it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broad sense</td>\n<td>\"Doing your homework\": investigating a matter, a person, or a deal to know whether it is legitimate.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Origin</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is born in the securities industry. Today it is a way of managing risk.</li>\n<li>The first common use of the term is in the Securities Act of 1933 (U.S. Securities Act of 1933).</li>\n<li>That law created the \"Due Diligence Defense\" for broker-dealers.</li>\n<li>It applied when investors accused them of not disclosing material information.</li>\n<li>If the broker-dealer had investigated and shared its findings, it was not liable for the negative that it had not discovered.</li>\n<li>That is why broker-dealers adopted it as standard practice. Today the term is used in almost any sector.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>When it applies and what features it has</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It applies when a party decides to do (or not do) something based on current conditions or on what another party states.</li>\n<li>There is no absolute standard. It depends on five things: the objective of the investigation, the situation, the jurisdictions or geographic areas, privacy laws, and the time frame.</li>\n<li>Each case is unique. Appendix 3A has a suggested checklist for acquiring a company.</li>\n<li>It is partly subjective: one reviewer may conclude that due diligence was done and another that it was not.</li>\n<li>It is judged after the fact: when the deal has already closed and the investigation is compared with what really happened.</li>\n<li>In situations that repeat (mergers, choosing someone for a fiduciary position) models can be created. Not everything fits in a checklist, but a lot does.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Due diligence, negligence, and due care</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Diligence</td>\n<td>The positive side of due care.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negligence</td>\n<td>Failing to exercise due care.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Due care</td>\n<td>Giving reasonable protection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Due diligence</td>\n<td>The effort and steps that show or prove that there was due care.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The idea of due diligence can be compared with the doctrine of negligence.</li>\n<li>Like negligence, failing to do due diligence can end in a judgment for damages if another party is harmed.</li>\n<li>Even if there is no harm to third parties, the one who fails also pays: they lose money, reputation, opportunities, and time through bad decisions.</li>\n<li>In short, it is a proactive investigative process that supports security risk management decisions.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-3-e1\">3.2 Relevance to Assets Protection and Security Risk Management</h3><ul>\n<li>The main reason to do it is to protect the organization's assets.</li>\n<li>Not knowing whether a person, company, organization, or situation is legitimate and reliable greatly raises the risk of any deal.</li>\n<li>Due diligence is the point where investigations and security risk management intersect.</li>\n<li>It can be voluntary (a prudent practice) or required by law, regulation, or internal policy.</li>\n<li>It can be very informal, such as vetting a vendor, or very formal and lengthy, such as preparing a large joint venture. Also everything in between.</li>\n<li>The objective is always the same: to be as well informed as possible before entering a situation.</li>\n<li>It is especially critical in dealings with third parties.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Example: a manufacturer seeks a distributor</h4>\n<p>The manufacturer must check that the candidate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>knows how to sell;</li>\n<li>has the financial and logistical capacity to maintain inventory;</li>\n<li>has a good reputation, so the association does not tarnish the manufacturer;</li>\n<li>has secure and adequate warehouses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without these guarantees, the manufacturer risks product, time, and reputation. Losses can be direct (the product), of opportunity, of market share, and of reputation. A warehouse without security is already a vulnerability that a threat can exploit.</p>\n<p>Before signing, the manufacturer would want to know the following (13 categories):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>What it wants to know</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Business history</td>\n<td>How many years it has been operating, how successful it is, and what products it distributes today.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distribution method</td>\n<td>How it distributes, whether it has its own trucks and how many.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Product tracking</td>\n<td>What system it will use to track the product and protect it from the warehouse to the points of sale. What methods it has to identify the product throughout the chain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security and insurance</td>\n<td>How secure the warehouse and the distribution network are. What insurance it has.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationships</td>\n<td>Whether it is affiliated with other companies or with competitors. Whether its relationship history is good or problematic. Whether it can ally with others to distribute, sell, or market.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Litigation</td>\n<td>Whether it has had lawsuits, with whom, and why. Whether they are the normal cost of business or reflect underlying problems. Whether it was plaintiff or defendant. Whether it tends to break contracts or sue its partners.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reputation</td>\n<td>The reputation of the company and of its managers, officers, and decision makers. Whether associating with it helps or harms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financial and credit issues</td>\n<td>Its financial and credit situation and history. Whether it has resources to maintain inventory. Where its funds come from and how much there is. How much debt it has. Whether it has ever declared bankruptcy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sales and marketing</td>\n<td>Whether it will help sell and promote. What channels it has, how large its sales team is, and in what media it advertises.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Officers and managers</td>\n<td>Who decides and whether they have \"skeletons in the closet\". Their history, reputation, and financial situation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total sales volume</td>\n<td>How much it sells today and how much more it could handle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facilities</td>\n<td>What facilities it has, owned or leased. Their size, condition, and location.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Profitability</td>\n<td>Its current and historical profitability, as a measure of operating efficiency.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Getting reliable answers to these questions is doing due diligence. The list is not exhaustive.</li>\n<li>A broader due diligence may also review nine areas: strategy, information technology (IT) infrastructure, data security, regulatory compliance, operations, outsourcing protocols, human capital, global reach, and culture.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Friendly and hostile deals</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of deal</th>\n<th>Access to information</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Friendly (acquisition or alliance)</td>\n<td>The parties exchange information easily so the deal goes through.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hostile</td>\n<td>It is harder to obtain data from the target.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>A common mistake is to rely only on the information the target provides.</li>\n<li>That information is usually biased (sometimes unintentionally), incomplete, or with things overlooked.</li>\n<li>If the target wants to close the deal, it will present the most favorable version.</li>\n<li>Verifying with primary sources is key to an impartial and complete due diligence.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-3-e2\">3.3 Applications of Due Diligence Investigations</h3><p>Transactions where it is used (7):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mergers and acquisitions.</li>\n<li>Divestiture actions.</li>\n<li>Joint ventures.</li>\n<li>Hiring and executive placement.</li>\n<li>Fiduciary level of trust determinations.</li>\n<li>Financial and real estate investments.</li>\n<li>Key outsourcing decisions, for example choosing a data center or cloud provider.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Internal use: to verify that policies, procedures, standards, governance processes, and documentation are aligned with the organization's goals and responsibilities. For example, that there are protocols throughout the company to protect information assets and privacy.</p>\n<h4>Market entry planning</h4>\n<p>It applies in six situations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Launching a new product or service line.</li>\n<li>Taking a product or service line to a new market, region, or country.</li>\n<li>Signing with a new partner or creating a joint venture.</li>\n<li>Opening an office or a research and development, manufacturing, or distribution center.</li>\n<li>Bringing in a new partner or a new strategy in the supply chain.</li>\n<li>Closing an office or facility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It focuses on two things: (1) protecting intellectual property (IP) and information assets, and (2) complying with local laws, regulations, and policies.</p>\n<p>The process can include seven steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identify and value IP and intangible assets.</li>\n<li>Thoroughly review the markets for infringements of intellectual property rights (IPR).</li>\n<li>Review what IPR registration each market, region, or country requires.</li>\n<li>Evaluate whether the IP laws of each jurisdiction allow continued use, ownership, and control of the IP, information, and intangibles during the deal and afterward.</li>\n<li>Inspect in person the new facilities or organizations involved.</li>\n<li>Review that contracts, agreements, security plans, licenses, registrations, insurance, and management practices are valid.</li>\n<li>Hire a trusted consultant or advisor who knows the culture, customs, language, laws, and regulation of each place.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>What makes a good due diligence</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Since there is no single list, the approach and results vary greatly.</li>\n<li>Done well, it is the basis of a good decision. Done badly, it can completely mislead the parties.</li>\n<li>It does not stop at verifying: it uncovers new information, raises questions, and points to new areas to investigate.</li>\n<li>Example: failing to detect an internal investigation of the chief financial officer (CFO) for fraudulent accounting can sink the deal.</li>\n<li>It must be done, impartially and thoroughly, even with parties one believes one knows.</li>\n<li>It also helps detect risks that could keep the organization from meeting its objectives.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>U.S. legal framework</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The USA Patriot Act of 2001 gave new obligations to financial institutions and multinationals.</li>\n<li>They must make the maximum effort to detect violations of the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act and the Patriot Act.</li>\n<li>The Department of Justice (DOJ) published the guidance Evaluation of Corporate Compliance Programs (ECCP) in three versions: 2017, 2019, and 2020.</li>\n<li>The ECCP requires due diligence in mergers, acquisitions, and third-party relationships. It looks for evidence of compliance failures, fraud, or other misconduct.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Missing information also counts</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Due diligence helps make good decisions even if it does not review every piece of data. It does not guarantee success, but it gives valuable insight.</li>\n<li>The gaps also inform:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Gap</th>\n<th>What it may indicate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>It is almost impossible to get the financial statements</td>\n<td>The company may have reasons to hide them, such as covering up a fraud.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gaps in a person's work history</td>\n<td>Technically it is a lack of data, but it says a lot about their history.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No reputation in their sector, no press, and no presence in trade magazines</td>\n<td>They may not have the track record they advertise.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>At a minimum, a good due diligence raises issues to investigate, confirms or contradicts what was received, and points to new sources.</li>\n<li>It does not guarantee success or prevent all errors of judgment. But with good practices, the report serves as a basis for further consultation and negotiation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Madoff case</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Bernard Madoff attracted investors to his hedge fund by promising higher returns than any other fund or securities investment.</li>\n<li>At the end of 2008 it was discovered that he was not making the investments he claimed. It was a $50 billion Ponzi scheme.</li>\n<li>Because of his good reputation, investors skipped due diligence in exchange for speed, high returns, and getting into the fund.</li>\n<li>According to the Wall Street Journal, some analysts repeated his supposed trades and did not achieve the same returns. They doubted that Madoff made them.</li>\n<li>Barron's suggested that his returns probably came from front running on the clients of his own brokerage.</li>\n<li>Madoff discouraged questions and hinted that asking could close the door to investing.</li>\n<li>Lesson: use outside investigation experts, attorneys, and accounting firms for a complete and impartial due diligence.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text attributes the analysts' point to the Wall Street Journal. The chapter's references only cite a New York Times article (Berenson &amp; Henriques 2008).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-3-e3\">3.4 Digital and Cyber Aspects of Due Diligence Investigations</h3><p>They are two related areas, but the book says they must be treated separately:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>What it consists of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Online and social media due diligence</td>\n<td>Investigating public open-source information about the person or entity: their footprint on the internet and on social media.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cyber and data security due diligence</td>\n<td>Asking the target about its internal policies, protocols, security measures, incident response plans, and known data breaches. It seeks to reduce the risk of hidden cyber vulnerabilities.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>3.4.1 Online and Social Media Due Diligence</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Almost every company is on social media. They use it to make the brand known, connect with customers, market to online audiences, and sell.</li>\n<li>Online sources help assess reputation, risks, and negative publicity.</li>\n<li>Example: an increase in complaints and negative comments around a date is seen.</li>\n<li>It is useful to confirm data from other sources, such as a new chief executive officer (CEO), a launch, or an alliance.</li>\n<li>Consumers demand social responsibility and hold companies and their executives to account on social media. This reveals the brand's reputation and its relationship with customers.</li>\n<li>It can bring to light key events, executives, and milestones that would not be published otherwise.</li>\n<li>It can include executives' public personal accounts. A personal comment by an executive can damage the company's reputation and profits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.4.2 Cyber and Data Security Due Diligence</h4>\n<p><strong>Marriott–Starwood case</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In 2015, Marriott announced the purchase of Starwood Hotels &amp; Resorts for more than $12 billion. It thus became the largest hotel chain in the world.</li>\n<li>A few years later it faced fines of more than $100 million from U.S. and other countries' regulators, as well as class actions.</li>\n<li>The cause was a breach of Starwood guest data that, according to what was published, began in 2014.</li>\n<li>It exposed data of hundreds of millions of people, including millions of passport numbers.</li>\n<li>Lesson: Marriott did not detect the breach in its due diligence before the purchase and paid dearly for it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What to do</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cybersecurity and data security must be part of every due diligence.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to work with cyber forensic auditors, IT and cybersecurity experts, and legal counsel.</li>\n<li>The aim is to build cyber risk profiles and evaluate how an incident is prepared for, responded to, and mitigated.</li>\n<li>A breach, even if no one has detected it, affects the company's resources, valuation, and public image.</li>\n<li>Role of the security professional: security experts, especially cybersecurity ones, must come in early and take part often.</li>\n<li>It is not a risk only for technology companies: it affects all industries.</li>\n<li>One must review what sensitive information, proprietary or trade secret, is vulnerable: IP, designs, formulas, and financial information.</li>\n<li>Other possible components: threat intelligence, Dark Web research, leaked customer data, penetration testing, and network vulnerability assessment.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-3-e4\">3.5 Techniques and Steps in Due Diligence Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>There is no single, objective criterion.</li>\n<li>First step: examine the matter (the deal, the person, the investment) and understand which factors to look at.</li>\n<li>The strategy must answer at least these nine questions:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>What is the nature of the situation? What is at risk (for example assets, reputation, market share)?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>What background does it have? How well do the parties know each other?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Who are the main players?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>What factors matter to those making the deal?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Which problems would be the most costly? Which minor problems could have an influence?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>What type of transaction do they expect? Of what size and how complex?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>How much time and money will investigating cost? How hard will it be to obtain the information?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>What risks do the players run? How much does it cost them if the deal fails?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Does the objective fit within the organization's risk appetite, risk tolerance, and risk capacity?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The key is to understand what the client needs and the specific transaction.</li>\n<li>If the investigator does not understand the object of the investigation, they will almost surely fail: they will not ask the right questions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.5.1 Information Sources</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Primary sources</th>\n<th>Secondary sources</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Interviews with people</td>\n<td>Government public records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct observation of people, places, or events</td>\n<td>Commercial sources of records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Annual reports and other company documents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>News and other media</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Federal criminal and civil records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>State criminal and civil records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Local and municipal records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>Social media intelligence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>General rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many secondary sources are on the internet, but the data must be confirmed with the original source.</li>\n<li>The book gives U.S. examples. Other countries have agencies with equivalent records.</li>\n<li>In the U.S., some investigations fall under the Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA, federal law on credit reports). This is because their result counts as a consumer report.</li>\n<li>Some sites require a current private investigator license. Others are open to the public.</li>\n<li>Access and records change daily, so sources must be checked regularly.</li>\n<li>Advantage of government records: they are usually impartial.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sources the investigator can consult (19):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>What it provides</th>\n<th>Limits and notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clerk records (county clerk records)</td>\n<td>Liens, judgments, litigation, marriages, divorces, properties, mortgages, and other vital data. They show relationships, addresses, and patterns of behavior.</td>\n<td>They usually give leads on where to keep looking.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SEC filings (filings with the Securities and Exchange Commission, the U.S. securities commission)</td>\n<td>Data on publicly traded companies, licensed broker-dealers, and investment funds. The 10-K (annual report) covers organization, activities, finances, legal matters, and officers. The 10-Q (quarterly) updates the 10-K with important changes.</td>\n<td>Private companies are not required to file them. They are consulted at www.sec.gov, in the SEC's public reading rooms, or by mail. Appendix 3B lists regulators of other countries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secretary of State or Division of Corporations (each state's corporate registry)</td>\n<td>Documents of companies incorporated in the state: annual reports, name changes, mergers and acquisitions, date of incorporation, status, and names and addresses of officers.</td>\n<td>Varies by state. Many give access online, by phone, by mail, or in person.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State regulatory agencies and licensing boards</td>\n<td>License status, complaints, judgments, specialties, training, and other data. They regulate doctors, lawyers, therapists, private investigators, security guards, and gaming workers.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) filings</td>\n<td>Sellers or creditors file them to record commercial debts. They show type and date of the transaction, amount, creditor and debtor (or seller and buyer), and how the debt is secured.</td>\n<td>They are on the internet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Freedom of Information Act (FOIA) documents</td>\n<td>It allows requesting from agencies documents that are normally not public. The FBI, local police, the CIA, and the State Department have FOIA officers to give access to formerly classified documents. Some state and county offices too.</td>\n<td>They take a long time to arrive. They often come with large redacted parts that cannot be published.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criminal records</td>\n<td>State and local ones, in most county clerk offices. Federal ones, in the federal court files at PACER.gov.</td>\n<td>There is no legal way for the public to search the whole country at once: each state and county has its own rules. It is advisable to use reputable screening or investigation firms. There are online international searches for more than 200 countries, but they are hard to verify, slow, and expensive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Property records</td>\n<td>The county assessor, recorder, or auditor's office gives owner, value, sales, deed transfers, and previous owners. Google Maps and other free satellite services give aerial and street-level views.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Patent records</td>\n<td>The U.S. Patent and Trademark Office gives free online access. Google Patents also allows searching for free.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Credit reports</td>\n<td>TransUnion, Equifax, and Experian give financial, organizational, and personnel data on companies and individuals.</td>\n<td>Signed authorization from the subject is required. By themselves they are not a good source of public information. What they say must be confirmed with primary sources.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corporate reports</td>\n<td>Dun &amp; Bradstreet produces reports, especially on publicly traded companies. Lexis-Nexis and Dialog gather news on public and private companies. The company's own annual reports are also useful.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watchdog data</td>\n<td>CorpWatch has a step-by-step guide with links for researching companies. Others: Corporate Watch (the British version of CorpWatch), CSRWire, OpenCorporates, and Violation Tracker.</td>\n<td>They can be biased by each group's point of view.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interviews</td>\n<td>They give information and context, and lead to other sources.</td>\n<td>The interviewee tells things their own way. A skilled investigator still obtains important data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct observations</td>\n<td>Visual inspection of facilities, materials, or activities.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>News media</td>\n<td>Labor problems, lawsuits, public criticism, investments, and details that do not appear in other public records. They also give leads.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General Internet searches</td>\n<td>Websites, blogs, and social media with a great deal of corporate and personal information.</td>\n<td>Everything must be verified separately: nothing guarantees it is accurate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Federal records</td>\n<td>Screening firms access PACER, the federal repository of criminal, civil, bankruptcy, and appellate cases.</td>\n<td>One must ensure the record is the subject's. Expertise is needed to obtain enough personal identifiers (the book abbreviates them \"PII\").</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State and local courts</td>\n<td>Screening firms usually have direct access to state criminal and civil records. If they do not, they send trained investigators or court runners (people who go to the courthouse).</td>\n<td>One must validate that the record corresponds to the subject, as with federal ones.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social media</td>\n<td>Of a company: reputation, relationship with customers, events, milestones, and news. Of a person: lifestyle and possible conflicts of interest. It can reveal critical details that are not in other sources.</td>\n<td>It is a difficult source: identifiers are often missing to ensure the right person. An attorney's guidance is advisable: some jurisdictions restrict these searches.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Prudent policy: limit the social media search to four categories:<ol>\n<li>Potentially violent behavior.</li>\n<li>Potentially illegal activity.</li>\n<li>Sexually explicit material.</li>\n<li>Ties to criminal, terrorist, or hostile groups.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.5.2 Outsourcing Due Diligence Investigations</h4>\n<p><strong>Who offers the service (5):</strong> private investigation firms, market research firms, preemployment screening companies, law firms, and accounting firms.</p>\n<p><strong>How to choose:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each firm understands due diligence in its own way and not all investigate with the same depth.</li>\n<li>Interview them to understand their sources and methods before hiring.</li>\n<li>It is essential to ask for references and a sample report.</li>\n<li>Some providers are excellent and specialize by sector, region, or type of transaction.</li>\n<li>It works if the provider is properly verified and the objectives and tactics are clearly agreed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why outsource only a part:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lack of time.</li>\n<li>Lack of in-house experience.</li>\n<li>Topics that the internal investigative unit does not master.</li>\n<li>Investigations so sensitive that an outside expert is advisable.</li>\n<li>The risk of a leak to staff exists with outside attorneys or experts as much as with internal resources.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Client–investigator relationship:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The client must give detailed background and work closely with the firm.</li>\n<li>Communication is critical to success.</li>\n<li>They must agree on the topics that matter most to the client, such as its goals and objectives.</li>\n<li>During the investigation, the investigator reports findings. The client decides whether a lead is followed or whether the information is already enough.</li>\n<li>Those decisions depend in part on the organization's risk appetite.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>International demand:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Big, high-profile frauds, such as Madoff's, make due diligence more critical and more routine in any project.</li>\n<li>International demand grows because of the global business environment, the economy, and threats such as terrorism and international organized crime.</li>\n<li>Examples: finding out whether a future joint venture partner had poor labor or environmental practices, or whether it was targeted by animal rights extremists.</li>\n<li>International free trade zones also increase the importance and demand for this due diligence.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.5.3 Reporting Due Diligence Findings</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>How it is done and reported depends on the purpose and the audience.</li>\n<li>There may be formats set by sector or region, legal limits, or the organization's own requirements.</li>\n<li>The report must be coherent, easy to read, and complete.</li>\n<li>The investigator delivers it in writing and must be ready to defend it if needed.</li>\n<li>Content of the report (7):<ol>\n<li>Identity of the client and the provider.</li>\n<li>Purpose and background.</li>\n<li>Scope of work.</li>\n<li>General methodology.</li>\n<li>Findings.</li>\n<li>Conclusion.</li>\n<li>Legal disclaimer, with any limits the investigation had.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Confidentiality and dissemination controls are critical to the integrity of the report. Client and provider must agree on them before starting.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-3-e5\">Key terms</h3><p>Due diligence · Due care · Negligence · Due Diligence Defense · Securities Act of 1933 · Broker-dealer · Target company · Vetting · Friendly deal · Hostile deal · Primary sources · Secondary sources · Mergers and acquisitions · Divestiture · Joint venture · Fiduciary level of trust · Market entry planning · Intellectual property rights (IPR) · Intangible assets · USA Patriot Act · Foreign Corrupt Practices Act · Evaluation of Corporate Compliance Programs (ECCP) · Ponzi scheme · Front running · Online and social media due diligence · Cyber and data security due diligence · Open-source information · Cyber risk profile · Threat intelligence · Dark Web · Penetration testing · Risk appetite · Risk tolerance · Risk capacity · Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA) · Consumer report · SEC filings · 10-K · 10-Q · Secretary of State / Division of Corporations · Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) filings · Freedom of Information Act (FOIA) · PACER · Court runners · Watchdog data · Social media intelligence · Legal disclaimer · Dissemination controls</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"3.1 Definitions"},{"id":"e1","t":"3.2 Relevance to Assets Protection and Security Risk Management"},{"id":"e2","t":"3.3 Applications of Due Diligence Investigations"},{"id":"e3","t":"3.4 Digital and Cyber Aspects of Due Diligence Investigations"},{"id":"e4","t":"3.5 Techniques and Steps in Due Diligence Investigations"},{"id":"e5","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"3.3 Applications of Due Diligence Investigations","h":"the text attributes the analysts' point to the Wall Street Journal. The chapter's references only cite a New York Times article (Berenson &amp; Henriques 2008)."}]},"investigations-4":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-4-e0\">4.1 The Need for Background Investigations</h3><ul>\n<li>A single bad hire (a thief, a drug user, or a violent criminal) can damage productivity, profits, reputation, and morale.</li>\n<li>The ideal program is comprehensive, lawful, fairly applied, and both confirmatory and investigative. It finds the right person and reduces losses.</li>\n<li>Losses are enormous on three fronts: employee theft and fraud, workplace violence, and use of illegal drugs and alcohol.</li>\n<li>The two main loss prevention strategies are candidate screening and employee socialization.</li>\n<li>The best way to reduce internal theft and violence is to hire people of integrity.</li>\n<li>Employees are often involved in workplace crimes, as perpetrators or as victims.</li>\n<li>In HR terms: hiring someone takes days or weeks, but getting rid of the wrong candidate often takes years.</li>\n<li>A good investigation reduces the risk of lawsuits for negligent hiring, for discrimination, and for costly mistakes.</li>\n<li>It is essential for the brand, staff morale, security, and asset protection.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Commonly confused terms</h4>\n<p>They are used as synonyms, but they are related and distinct disciplines.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Background check</td>\n<td>It is confirmatory: it verifies what the candidate provides, such as education and work history.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Background investigation or screening</td>\n<td>It actively searches: it obtains new references and looks for criminal records. Its goal is to discover what the candidate might hide, such as crimes or dismissals.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Background screening, according to the ASIS Glossary</td>\n<td>Inquiring into the history and conduct of a person being considered for employment, credit, access to sensitive assets (for example national defense information), or other purposes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preemployment screening</td>\n<td>Determines whether the candidate is reliable or able to do the job. It can include all of the above plus tests.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personal risk assessment (PRA)</td>\n<td>The book mentions it among the terms that get confused, but does not define it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference check</td>\n<td>Contacting former bosses, supervisors, coworkers, and teachers. It verifies employment, education, character, attendance, knowledge, skills, and abilities.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The investigation can range from just checking jobs and degrees to reviewing civil, criminal, and medical histories.</li>\n<li>Preemployment screening can also include six tests:<ol>\n<li>Drug or alcohol screening.</li>\n<li>Psychological tests (personality and integrity).</li>\n<li>General intelligence or aptitude tests.</li>\n<li>Medical exam and physical ability tests.</li>\n<li>Polygraph.</li>\n<li>Position-specific tests.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The legality of each test and the moment when it can be applied are heavily regulated in many places. An attorney must be consulted.</li>\n<li>The background investigation and references are the main ways to obtain information from someone other than the candidate.</li>\n<li>The investigation looks for whether the candidate fails to qualify for four reasons:<ol>\n<li>A relevant criminal conviction.</li>\n<li>Motor vehicle violations.</li>\n<li>Poor credit history, when it matters for the position.</li>\n<li>Lies about education or work history.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Legal liability</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the U.S. and other countries, screening reduces the risk of being held liable for an improper hire.</li>\n<li>It is well established that the employer is liable for the negligence of the employee on duty or work-related.</li>\n<li>There are many precedents that make it liable also for intentional conduct by the employee. This occurs if it knew, or should have known, of a background that anticipated them.</li>\n<li>Example 1: landlords found liable for hiring ex-convicts who attacked residents of the building.</li>\n<li>Example 2: employers found liable for hiring people with records who injured coworkers in ways similar to their previous crimes.</li>\n<li>Some jurisdictions give statutory immunity \"for obtaining references\". Giving false, misleading, or malicious information about the former employee is prohibited.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text says immunity for \"obtaining\" references, but the next sentence deals with \"giving\" information. Possible typo.</li>\n<li>When giving a reference, the employer must be truthful and first have the employee's signed authorization and consent.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Paul Calden case (Hull 2005)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Fireman's Fund asked Allstate Insurance for references on Calden. He brought a reference letter signed by an Allstate executive. Allstate only confirmed the dates of employment.</li>\n<li>Once hired, he said he came from Mars and needed daily transfusions to clean his \"Martian blood\". He threatened a coworker with death for parking in \"his\" spot.</li>\n<li>They fired him. He kept calling to meet with the executives, threatened them with death, and was a suspect in the burglary at the HR manager's home.</li>\n<li>Eight months after the dismissal he came back and shot the five executives who had fired him. He killed three and injured two for life. Then he killed himself.</li>\n<li>At Allstate the same thing had happened: the Mars story, the transfusions, and a meeting he arrived at armed. They paid him a settlement to leave on his own.</li>\n<li>Allstate did not tell Fireman's Fund. This gave rise to the first lawsuit for negligent referral.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Hull's reference is listed with the date 2005, but its link points to a 1994 file.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Context and evolution</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>For centuries it was easy to learn about someone: people lived in small communities, moved little, and knew each other at least by reputation.</li>\n<li>Today companies are global and the requirements of each position are more defined.</li>\n<li>The preemployment screening industry was born in the U.S. in the 1980s.</li>\n<li>Worldwide interest grew because of terrorism, insider threats, IP theft, and workplace violence.</li>\n<li>Today large companies implement and periodically review screening policies for candidates, employees, and trusted third parties.</li>\n<li>Many countries restrict preemployment evaluations by law. The main concerns are privacy and data protection.</li>\n<li>Many jurisdictions give exceptions for screening candidates.</li>\n<li>What information exists and how it can be used varies greatly from place to place. Many countries are still deciding how to regulate it. In others there is no data because it is poorly recorded.</li>\n<li>2019 report of the Professional Association for Background Screeners (formerly the National Association of Background Screeners, NABS): 96% of employers use one or more types of screening (HR.com 2019).</li>\n<li>Most do it at hiring. Others do it throughout the employment life cycle.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e1\">4.2 Issues That Require Investigation</h3><h4>4.2.1 Résumé Fraud</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is not easy to hire someone honest, nonviolent, and drug-free: the honest and the dishonest look alike.</li>\n<li>Many résumés are misleading.</li>\n<li>HireRight 2020 study: the most common lies are in previous employment, criminal records, and degrees. They appear at all levels, including senior management (C-suite).</li>\n<li>Example: in January 2020, a senior executive of the UK's National Health Service received a two-year suspended prison sentence. He had lied with a degree in classics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2.2 Employee Theft</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is one of the biggest problems for companies today.</li>\n<li>Global Fraud Survey 2018 (Ernst &amp; Young 2018): survey of senior executives of large companies in 55 countries. Four findings:<ol>\n<li>Although some countries improved, fraud and corruption did not decline worldwide since the 2012 survey.</li>\n<li>Younger respondents more readily justify fraud or corruption to meet financial goals or survive a crisis.</li>\n<li>Government fines reached levels never seen: more than $11 billion since 2012, imposed by U.S. and UK authorities.</li>\n<li>93% said their leaders show commitment to compliance and 95% that they set a good ethical example. However, unethical conduct and business failures did not decline.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2.3 Workplace Violence</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a serious concern for security professionals.</li>\n<li>In the U.S., homicide, the most extreme form, is the fourth leading cause of workplace death.</li>\n<li>Bureau of Labor Statistics, Census of Fatal Occupational Injuries: in 2018 there were 453 workplace homicides, out of a total of 5,250 workplace deaths.</li>\n<li>Society for Human Resource Management (SHRM 2019): almost half of HR professionals said their organization had a violence incident in 2019.</li>\n<li>Most said they have a process to detect employees with a violent history. Even so, 1 in 7 employees feels unsafe at work.</li>\n<li>Conclusion: a preemployment screening that detects candidates with problem behaviors is advisable.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text cites \"Bureau of Labor Statistics 2019\", but the reference is dated 2020. It also says \"outlined about\": a typo for \"above\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2.4 Drug and Alcohol Use Among Workers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Use at work harms everyone, owners and employees.</li>\n<li>Obvious effects: absenteeism, accidents, and errors. Less obvious but also costly effects: low morale and more illness.</li>\n<li>2019 National Survey on Drug Use and Health (U.S.):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Illegal drug use in the past year</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Adults over 26</td>\n<td>About 17%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employees over 26</td>\n<td>About 20%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employees aged 18 to 25</td>\n<td>About 42%, roughly double</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Source cited: Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration 2019. Exam note: the reference is dated 2020.</li>\n<li>Some states legalized marijuana for medical use, recreational use, or both.</li>\n<li>When the book was published, marijuana, and specifically its component THC (tetrahydrocannabinol), was still illegal at the federal level.</li>\n<li>Many companies have drug-free policies: a positive test for THC can lead to sanction.</li>\n<li>According to the text, there has to be an incident or a suspicion that justifies the test. Example: an accident with a company vehicle.</li>\n<li>For legal liability, and even if there are state laws, the company can create and enforce drug-free policies to protect its employees (HF&amp;C/Assurex Global 2014).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the incident point clashes with 4.7.1. There the drug test is a normal part of the final phase, and federal law requires it for some positions.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e2\">4.3 Benefits of Preemployment Screening</h3><ul>\n<li>The main purpose of investigating backgrounds is to prevent losses and reduce harm.</li>\n<li>Legally, it protects against lawsuits for negligent hiring and for discrimination.</li>\n<li>If a bad employee steals, the value of what was stolen is lost. The investigation and the recruiting, hiring, and training of the replacement are also paid for.</li>\n<li>That is why the asset protection program must gather and analyze all the relevant information the law permits.</li>\n<li>The policy must be cost-efficient: the most expensive components are applied last.</li>\n<li>Screening must be done and evaluated before the position is offered.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Deters anyone with something to hide and encourages honesty</td>\n<td>The candidate knows that what they say will be checked.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shows that the company did due diligence</td>\n<td>It can serve as a defense at trial. It attracts good candidates, who prefer a safe environment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Confirms that the candidate has the background, education, experience, and skills they claim</td>\n<td>The decision is based on facts, not on hunches or promises.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e3\">4.4 Developing a Screening Program</h3><p>Its function is to screen candidates in order to hire the best. It requires the participation of many people and areas.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Senior management</td>\n<td>First approves and backs the policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security and HR team</td>\n<td>Ideally designs and implements the policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Procurement (purchasing)</td>\n<td>Can analyze the bid packages if the investigation is outsourced.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal counsel</td>\n<td>At the end reviews and endorses the program, because of the many judicial and personal data compliance issues.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The program changes according to four factors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Access to information.</li>\n<li>Nature of the position.</li>\n<li>Legal liability if the work is done badly.</li>\n<li>Industry regulation.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>4.4.1 Access to Information</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In a global environment, obtaining and verifying candidate data is a challenge of its own.</li>\n<li>International companies find it hard to maintain uniform hiring policies because the available information varies.</li>\n<li>Candidates from several countries bring their own complications. Customs and rules vary enormously.</li>\n<li>The most important thing is to know the privacy laws of each jurisdiction:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Jurisdiction</th>\n<th>Rule or regulation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Canada</td>\n<td>Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act (PIPEDA).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>European Union</td>\n<td>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). Other countries have similar measures.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>U.S., Australia, and Canada</td>\n<td>They prohibit certain questions about gender, ethnicity, age, disability, and other personal data. Elsewhere they may be legal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>U.S.</td>\n<td>The FCRA regulates access to credit, insurance, and criminal records. Criminal convictions are usually public.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>U.S. (FOIA)</td>\n<td>\"Public interest\" records must be available. Credit history is not covered by this law and is not considered public.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Cultural differences (Infocubic.com 2020):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Country</th>\n<th>What is needed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>China</td>\n<td>The name in Chinese characters and the Chinese identification number. Many institutions do not have the name translated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mexico and other Latin American countries</td>\n<td>The mother's maiden name, to search records thoroughly.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>That is why a local provider or a company specialized in global investigations can be hired.</li>\n<li>Whether done in-house or outside, the criminal and privacy laws of the country where the data are located must be well understood.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.4.2 Nature of the Position</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Not all positions require the same level of screening.</li>\n<li>More scrutiny is required for positions that:<ul>\n<li>handle highly sensitive information or large sums of money or other financial instruments;</li>\n<li>have close contact with the public, work in customers' homes, or with children;</li>\n<li>handle hazardous materials or protect other people.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The organization must review its job descriptions and choose the appropriate level.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.4.3 Liability Issues</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Some positions hardly allow for error. It is taken for granted that ship captains, pilots, doctors, and child caregivers have the right training, skills, and education.</li>\n<li>It is up to the employer to verify it. Rule: the more responsibility, the deeper the screening.</li>\n<li>Questions the book raises: what if a pilot does not have the training or the alcohol and drug history they declared? What if a child caregiver has a violent background? What if the chief financial officer has a history of embezzlement?</li>\n<li>Accidents and injuries from the employee's error or incompetence usually end up being the employer's responsibility.</li>\n<li>Lawsuits and settlements can cost so much that they close the company.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.4.4 Industry Regulations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the U.S., federal and state laws set screening requirements in many sectors.</li>\n<li>They are usually sectors that require a professional license, that deal with vulnerable populations (children, the elderly), or that are high-risk.</li>\n<li>Examples: banking, transportation, child care, education, and security.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Although it varies according to those four factors, every program has the same basis (3):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Data collection: a standard application specific to the company, plus supplementary documents.</li>\n<li>Investigation and screening according to written and standardized policies and processes.</li>\n<li>Data analysis and hiring decision.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>4.4.5 A Comprehensive Preemployment Screening Policy</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n<th>Details</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Write clear job descriptions for all positions</td>\n<td>They describe the tasks and the personal qualities needed. Only clearly necessary qualities are requested. A requirement that cannot be justified can end in a complaint to a regulator or a civil lawsuit. In the U.S., employers with at least 15 employees must comply with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) when setting requirements. The description is the yardstick of the first filter, which is why it is crucial.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Assess the risk of each job classification</td>\n<td>The reasonable or foreseeable potential for problems is measured with seven questions (see below).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Define the scope of screening for each classification</td>\n<td>Standard and consistent measures for temporary, permanent, full-time, part-time, contractors, and volunteers. Apply them rigorously across the whole classification, to avoid accusations of unfair or illegal hiring. Different levels by position are valid if they are appropriate and applied equally to all. Example: checking credit for all accountant applicants and for no cafeteria worker. Set periods, for example checking felonies from seven years back for a whole classification. Sometimes the law imposes it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Assemble a hiring packet with HR and legal</td>\n<td>It includes a complete application and the authorization and release forms. Legal reviews that it complies with the law. If the investigation finds inconsistencies, HR is notified that more investigation may be needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Set criteria for evaluating the information</td>\n<td>Define what information disqualifies and who decides. For example, which prior crimes prevent holding certain positions. Many companies use a team of HR, security, and legal to review negative findings. The standard of proof for not hiring is \"good faith\".</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Communicate the policy and its purpose to staff</td>\n<td>On bulletin boards, intranet, employee handbook, or wherever it fits the culture. Highlight more security, less turnover, and greater operational effectiveness.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Periodically review compliance</td>\n<td>Ensures it is applied equally throughout the company.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Questions for step 2 (7):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Will the person have access to financial instruments or sensitive information?</li>\n<li>Will the person have regular or unsupervised contact with the public? Examples: meter reader, appliance technician, carpet installer.</li>\n<li>Will the person receive keys, alarm codes, or after-hours access?</li>\n<li>Does the position require credentials, licenses, or a specific professional standing?</li>\n<li>Will the person have to carry a firearm?</li>\n<li>Will the person have access to hazardous materials, such as explosives or certain chemicals?</li>\n<li>Will the person have to drive a vehicle?</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e4\">4.5 Data Collection: The Basic Information Gathering Tools</h3><ul>\n<li>The investigation begins with what the candidate provides: the job application, the personal history questionnaire, the résumé or CV, and other biographical documents.</li>\n<li>Even if the candidate brings a résumé, they must be required to fill out the standard application designed by the company.</li>\n<li>A single tool is never used to evaluate a candidate. That way all the relevant and legal information possible is obtained.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.5.1 Résumé</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the first sign of whether the candidate qualifies. It serves for the first filter.</li>\n<li>If it does not show the necessary experience or education, the candidate leaves the process.</li>\n<li>It is a promotional tool: it is hard to hold it strictly to account for what it says.</li>\n<li>That is why it is not accepted in place of the job application or a complete work history.</li>\n<li>It is always reviewed, because it provides additional information.</li>\n<li>The main source is the company's own application, which is reviewed before investigating.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.5.2 Application</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the starting point of screening.</li>\n<li>Its body is questions to know whether the candidate meets the minimums for the position.</li>\n<li>Role of the security manager: to help ensure the application allows tracing the candidate's history and has only lawful questions.</li>\n<li>In the U.S., the application counts as a \"test\", like any other selection tool.</li>\n<li>That is why it must be shown not to have adverse impact on candidates protected by federal anti-discrimination laws. An attorney helps verify it.</li>\n<li>Example application: Appendix 4A.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What it must include:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>What to ask or state</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Material omissions and misstatements</td>\n<td>State that omitting or falsifying something material (important or significant) is grounds for ending the process when discovered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correct and complete name</td>\n<td>Also any other name used in schools, addresses, or jobs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Current and previous address</td>\n<td>The current one, and the permanent or legal one if different. Also, all addresses during jobs, studies, or any period that will be verified.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>For each level: (1) name and address of the school; (2) course or program; (3) dates of attendance; (4) final status, for example graduated, degree earned, or withdrawn; (5) degree or certificate earned; (6) grade average or rank; (7) reason for withdrawal, if any. Many schools only confirm attendance or graduation; the candidate's authorization helps obtain more.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Licenses and certifications</td>\n<td>Verify them with the issuer: that they were granted and have not expired.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employment</td>\n<td>The whole history, including self-employment, for the period to be reviewed. If 5, 7, or 10 years are reviewed, the candidate presents them in chronological order, with employment, self-employment, and unemployment. A form with only two or three boxes suggests nothing more is expected. The instructions must ask for the complete history and an extra sheet if needed. There must be a space to indicate whether the current employer can be contacted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data for each previous job</td>\n<td>(1) Name and address of the employer; (2) starting and final position; (3) period; (4) reason for leaving; (5) name and phone number of the last direct boss. Appendix 4B has a reference form.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>References</td>\n<td>At least three people who have known the candidate for a year or more and are not relatives. Ask for names, work and home addresses, email, and phone. Also ask how long and in what capacity they know the candidate (neighbor, friend, coworker). After several interviews a trend should be visible about their performance and work style. The references are asked for another list of people who know the candidate; that second list can give a more accurate and complete picture. A former boss who still knows them is an excellent reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Military service (U.S.)</td>\n<td>(1) Period; (2) branch; (3) type of discharge or separation; (4) rank at discharge. Outside the U.S., consult an attorney first. The Privacy Act limits what the military can reveal without a subpoena about unfavorable service, but the candidate can reveal it. That is why they are asked whether they had an honorable or dishonorable discharge and may be asked for the discharge document.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criminal record</td>\n<td>It is the most common screening method. Laws vary, so it is advisable to consult an attorney before drafting the questions. Ideally ask broadly: convictions, guilty pleas, and nolo contendere (no contest) pleas over a period, for example 7 or 10 years. State that a conviction does not automatically disqualify.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Release or authorization</td>\n<td>The candidate agrees to the preemployment screening. The authorization also serves for future screenings for retention, promotion, or change of position. Many sources are regulated by law or have become reluctant to give data. In the U.S., if an outside service is used, the FCRA requires: (1) notifying the candidate that a consumer report may be requested, and (2) their written authorization. Both must be separate from the application. Consult an attorney about the laws of each state and municipality.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Ban-the-Box and the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the U.S. there is a strong trend to protect the rights of convicted persons, called \"Ban-the-Box\".</li>\n<li>Federal contractors, many states, and municipal governments prohibit asking about criminal records on the application until after a conditional offer.</li>\n<li>The U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) has prosecuted employers with a blanket ban on hiring people convicted of felonies.</li>\n<li>That blanket ban violates its requirement of an \"individualized assessment\" of each conviction.</li>\n<li>An attorney must take part in the policies on criminal records.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e5\">4.6 Early Application Review and Initial Interview</h3><ul>\n<li>The first review of the application is an important tool.</li>\n<li>The candidate's falsehoods are of two types:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Willful omissions of material facts</td>\n<td>Deliberately omitting important facts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Misrepresentations of material facts</td>\n<td>Falsifying important facts.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The candidate may omit what hurts them. They may have been falsifying for years and believe no one will check. Far too often they are right.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.6.1 Red Flags in Early Application Review</h4>\n<p>The most common omissions and falsehoods are eight:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Unsigned forms.</li>\n<li>Incomplete addresses or P.O. boxes. At this point the text adds that some give schools without dates or degrees, and that an unknown school is always verified.</li>\n<li>Dates that do not add up among employment, education, or military service.</li>\n<li>Employment gaps. Any gap of more than a month in employment or in life is investigated.</li>\n<li>Changes of occupation. Abnormal work patterns are reviewed in detail.</li>\n<li>Incomplete employment data or a former employer that has already closed.</li>\n<li>Social Security number that does not add up (U.S. only). The number may not match the area where they say it was issued. On its own it does not weigh much, but it is a clue if the investigation needs to be expanded or the candidate questioned further.</li>\n<li>Not giving the reasons for leaving. The candidate avoids them if they do not know what the former employer will say. If asked directly, they almost always answer something.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in point 2, the text about schools without dates does not fit its title (\"incomplete addresses\"). Possible editing typo.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.6.2 Initial Applicant Interview</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Best in person and after the candidate hands in the application.</li>\n<li>It serves to clarify omissions: missing signatures and dates, unanswered questions, or incomplete histories.</li>\n<li>No negative questions or questions that make the source judge are asked, because they give misleading answers.</li>\n<li>If it is clear that the candidate does not qualify, they are told on the spot so as not to give false hope.</li>\n<li>Videoconferencing has become more accepted, and the COVID-19 pandemic accelerated its use because of social distancing.</li>\n<li>According to recruiters, the key points of the in-person interview apply equally remotely: punctuality, attention, and preparation (Stuart 2020).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e6\">4.7 Final Selection Phase</h3><ul>\n<li>The best selection comes from a cooperative effort within the organization.</li>\n<li>If the candidate passes the first phases, they enter the final phase, where the deepest screening is.</li>\n<li>Most wait until the candidate is chosen to verify the résumé, references, criminal records, licenses, education, and other credentials.</li>\n<li>In this phase the candidate may take six tests:<ol>\n<li>Drug or alcohol screening.</li>\n<li>Psychological tests (personality and integrity).</li>\n<li>General intelligence or aptitude tests.</li>\n<li>Medical exam and physical ability tests.</li>\n<li>Polygraph.</li>\n<li>Job tests.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The legality and timing of each test are heavily regulated. An attorney must be consulted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>FCRA process for an adverse decision (U.S.)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What it requires</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Pre-adverse notice (letter)</td>\n<td>If the candidate is expected to be rejected based in part on a consumer report (for example a background report from a third party), they are notified before deciding. The letter goes with several documents, the FCRA Summary of Rights, and a copy of the report.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Reasonable period</td>\n<td>The candidate must have time to review the report, see whether it is accurate and complete, and dispute whatever is inaccurate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Adverse decision letter</td>\n<td>If they do not dispute and it is decided not to hire them in part because of the report, this second letter is sent.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>An attorney must always be consulted to set up this program: errors often end in class actions.</li>\n<li>iReviewNow is a proprietary system that reduces these risks. The candidate reviews their report, disputes errors, and comments at the same time the employer sees it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.7.1 Drug and Alcohol Screening</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>U.S. federal law requires mandatory testing in some positions.</li>\n<li>Where it is not mandatory, the risk assessment from step 2 helps set the level.</li>\n<li>Every organization that tests must have a written policy with three points: when tests are done, how they are done, and what consequences the result has.</li>\n<li>The policy is communicated to all candidates and employees.</li>\n<li>After the legalization of marijuana in some countries and U.S. states (first medical and then recreational), many employers stopped testing for it.</li>\n<li>In safety-sensitive positions, such as truck drivers or pilots, candidates and employees continue to be tested. They may be subject to U.S. or other countries' federal laws that require it (Gates 2019).</li>\n<li>Where it is legal, the employer must understand the legal, contractual, and cultural issues.</li>\n<li>In some jurisdictions one cannot discriminate against someone who tests positive for marijuana, unless not doing so would lose benefits under a higher law.</li>\n<li>One must be transparent with staff about what is expected, in preemployment and in later tests (Chapa 2015).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.7.2 Testing</h4>\n<p>The goal of the tools used before hiring is to decide with information.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>What it measures or what it is like</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Personality tests</td>\n<td>Basic traits: attitudes, emotional stability, motivations, interpersonal skills, and interests.</td>\n<td>They serve to see whether the person fits the position. Example: someone shy or introverted may not fit in sales.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrity or honesty tests</td>\n<td>The tendency toward undesirable behaviors: lying, stealing, and abusing drugs or alcohol.</td>\n<td>There are two types. Overt or direct: explicit questions about past dishonest acts. Personality-oriented or indirect: they measure traits such as reliability and respect for authority.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Biographical questionnaires</td>\n<td>Open-ended questions that lead the candidate to reveal data and allow follow-up questions to be prepared.</td>\n<td>Example from the book: \"How many times have you hit a coworker out of anger in the last 24 months?\". The book says they are objective.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intelligence tests</td>\n<td>Logic problems, verbal problems, pattern identification, and other questions.</td>\n<td>They are used because intelligence predicts performance well.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aptitude tests</td>\n<td>Cognitive ability in an area: memory, vocabulary, or numerical perception.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medical screening</td>\n<td>Only if it is directly related to a requirement of the position.</td>\n<td>If it reveals an illness or disability that does not affect the position, employment cannot be denied.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical ability tests</td>\n<td>Strength, endurance, or range of movement.</td>\n<td>They say whether the person will be able to perform the essential functions without risk to themselves or others.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polygraph</td>\n<td>Before the Employee Polygraph Protection Act of 1988 it was used to verify backgrounds in the U.S.</td>\n<td>That law prohibits the preemployment polygraph. Some government agencies and local, state, and federal police still use it. Its validity is questioned, especially as evidence at trial.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Job-specific tests</td>\n<td>The skills and knowledge they will use on the job.</td>\n<td>Examples: a writing test for someone who will write reports, or a knowledge assessment for a subject matter expert.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Rules for tests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Psychological ones are done in writing or with specialized software. It is essential to administer, score, and interpret them properly.</li>\n<li>Their results are just one more factor. Misused, they can harm the organization. It is advisable to consult an attorney before starting.</li>\n<li>Intelligence and aptitude ones are useful if properly administered and scored, but they are not infallible. They are one more factor and are discussed beforehand with an attorney.</li>\n<li>The medical exam is delicate because of how personal the tests are and the legal risk.</li>\n<li>Medical and physical ones, and the program that directs them, must be reviewed and endorsed by attorneys before being implemented. The reason is the risk of lawsuits for discrimination or disparate impact.</li>\n<li>That is why review of medical history is not part of a typical background investigation.</li>\n<li>In the U.S. these tests may count as a medical exam. In some states they are only applied after the job is offered.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The polygraph appears in the lists of tests (4.1 and 4.7), but the text itself says the 1988 law prohibits it in preemployment in the U.S.</li>\n<li>The biographical questionnaire is described with \"open-ended questions\", but the example is a closed counting question and the text calls it \"objective\".</li>\n<li>In 4.1 the investigation can include \"medical history\". In 4.7.2 it is said that medical review is not part of a typical investigation.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e7\">4.8 Investigation Procedures</h3><p>Once the policy is approved, it must be decided who investigates. The three most common resources are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The security or asset protection department.</li>\n<li>The HR department.</li>\n<li>External consumer reporting and investigative agencies.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>They are not mutually exclusive: many organizations split the parts of the investigation among them.</li>\n<li>What weighs most in choosing is the size and structure of the organization.</li>\n<li>Available staff, experience, efficiency, and cost also count, to decide what is done in-house and what outside.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.8.1 Using the Security or Assets Protection Department</h4>\n<p>It needs three resources:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Experienced and well-trained investigators.</li>\n<li>Sufficient reference material.</li>\n<li>Adequate communications.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Administrative staff can do database searches and gather employment, education, and reference data.</li>\n<li>But the investigation and the analysis of results must be done by an experienced investigator.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.8.2 Using the Human Resources Department</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>If it has enough staff, HR can have an important role.</li>\n<li>It knows labor law and interview techniques.</li>\n<li>It is ideal for the first filter, for example the first calls to references to see whether the candidate has the necessary experience.</li>\n<li>That first filter usually decides whether the candidate continues. If they continue, the rest is done by an experienced investigator.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.8.3 Using a Contract Consumer Reporting and Investigative Agency</h4>\n<p>The number of screening firms has grown enormously. To choose, the book proposes 16 questions:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Does it give information to the client and to those investigated in compliance with the law (for example the FCRA in the U.S. and PIPEDA in Canada)?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Can it guide the client on regulation?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Is the information the most current and accurate available?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Does it offer all the services the client needs, or will more than one provider be needed?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Does it have convenient ways to place orders?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>How long does it take to deliver the information?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>How much does it charge and how does that compare with the competition?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>What does it do to confirm the candidate's true identity?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>What quality controls does it use to ensure accuracy?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Does it have errors and omissions insurance or other applicable coverage?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Does it have adequate procedures to protect the security and confidentiality of the information?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Has it provided similar services to organizations in the client's sector?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Will it give references of clients who can be contacted?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>What satisfaction guarantees does it offer?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Does it provide material or updates on legal and practical screening issues?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Will it provide a sample report?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>4.8.4 Data Analysis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Analysis begins when all the results arrive.</li>\n<li>In the best case, everything about the candidate is confirmed and there is nothing negative. If something negative turns up, the decision gets complicated.</li>\n<li>The security manager consults the written policy on which prior crimes disqualify.</li>\n<li>The local privacy law can have an influence.</li>\n<li>In the U.S., the employer must communicate negative findings to the candidate and let them correct false or misattributed data.</li>\n<li>The application must say that material omissions and misstatements are grounds for ending the process when discovered.</li>\n<li>That way the process can be ended for the candidate's dishonesty and not only for the negative data.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e8\">4.9 Future of Background Investigations and Preemployment Screening</h3><ul>\n<li>Privacy laws will greatly shape the future of these investigations.</li>\n<li>Many countries debate how to collect, store, and transfer data securely, balancing privacy and human rights.</li>\n<li>Technology speeds up verification and makes investigating cheaper worldwide.</li>\n<li>Many online databases respond almost instantly. But they can have major limitations and unconfirmed data, so they are only used as a complement.</li>\n<li>Technology also improves how information is gathered and shared within the company. It simplifies a slow and tedious process, and makes it more efficient and secure.</li>\n<li>Preemployment screening will remain essential in every good security program.</li>\n<li>Role of the security professional: screening can prevent employee theft, workplace violence, and other incidents from the start.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-4-e9\">Key terms</h3><p>Background check · Background investigation · Background screening · Preemployment screening · Personal risk assessment (PRA) · Reference check · Negligent hiring · Negligent referral · Statutory immunity · Employee socialization · Résumé fraud · C-suite · Insider threats · Drug-free policy · Tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) · Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act (PIPEDA) · General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) · Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA) · Freedom of Information Act (FOIA) · Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) · Job description · Job classification · Hiring packet · Good faith · Adverse impact · Disparate impact · Material omissions and misstatements · Willful omissions · Misrepresentations · Nolo contendere · Ban-the-Box · Individualized assessment · Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) · Privacy Act · Subpoena · Release · Consumer report · Red flags · Pre-adverse notice · Adverse decision letter · FCRA Summary of Rights · iReviewNow · Safety-sensitive positions · Personality tests · Integrity/honesty tests · Overt (direct) tests · Personality-oriented (indirect) tests · Biographical questionnaires · Intelligence tests · Aptitude tests · Physical ability tests · Employee Polygraph Protection Act of 1988 · Job-specific tests · Consumer reporting and investigative agency · Errors and omissions insurance</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"4.1 The Need for Background Investigations"},{"id":"e1","t":"4.2 Issues That Require Investigation"},{"id":"e2","t":"4.3 Benefits of Preemployment Screening"},{"id":"e3","t":"4.4 Developing a Screening Program"},{"id":"e4","t":"4.5 Data Collection: The Basic Information Gathering Tools"},{"id":"e5","t":"4.6 Early Application Review and Initial Interview"},{"id":"e6","t":"4.7 Final Selection Phase"},{"id":"e7","t":"4.8 Investigation Procedures"},{"id":"e8","t":"4.9 Future of Background Investigations and Preemployment Screening"},{"id":"e9","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"4.1 The Need for Background Investigations","h":"the text says immunity for \"obtaining\" references, but the next sentence deals with \"giving\" information. Possible typo."},{"s":"4.1 The Need for Background Investigations","h":"Hull's reference is listed with the date 2005, but its link points to a 1994 file."},{"s":"4.2 Issues That Require Investigation","h":"the text cites \"Bureau of Labor Statistics 2019\", but the reference is dated 2020. It also says \"outlined about\": a typo for \"above\"."},{"s":"4.2 Issues That Require Investigation","h":"the incident point clashes with 4.7.1. There the drug test is a normal part of the final phase, and federal law requires it for some positions."},{"s":"4.6 Early Application Review and Initial Interview","h":"in point 2, the text about schools without dates does not fit its title (\"incomplete addresses\"). Possible editing typo."},{"s":"4.7 Final Selection Phase","h":""}]},"investigations-5":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-5-e0\">Introduction (unnumbered) — Context</h3><ul>\n<li>Purpose of an investigation: to reach, objectively, a reasonable conclusion based on the facts.</li>\n<li><strong>The \"three I's\"</strong> of the investigator: <strong>information, interrogation, instrumentation</strong>.</li>\n<li>The chapter deals with information obtained by interview and interrogation. It focuses on the interview, because interrogation is associated more with the police than with private investigation.</li>\n<li>The people involved are the most available and valuable sources of information.</li>\n<li><strong>Interview:</strong> a question-and-answer session designed to obtain information.</li>\n<li>Interviews of victims, witnesses, informants, and confidential informants are the most critical aspect, because they are a primary source.</li>\n<li>The value of each interview depends on three things:<ol>\n<li>The investigator's knowledge.</li>\n<li>Their skill in eliciting information.</li>\n<li>Their ability to evaluate the truthfulness of what is obtained.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The investigator's most valuable talent is obtaining information by asking. That way the case is closed successfully, at low cost, and on time.</li>\n<li>Interviews are always conducted in accordance with statutes, regulations, and standards of fairness.</li>\n<li>Fields:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>What it investigates</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Private investigator</td>\n<td>Domestic cases, parental abductions, traffic accidents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corporate</td>\n<td>Corporate fraud and similar misconduct. Limited to matters that can harm staff, financial stability, or the company's reputation. The information serves to improve loss prevention and gather intelligence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public sector</td>\n<td>Violations of the law</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Possible locations: a private residence, a street corner, or a formal interview room.</li>\n<li>Possible purposes:<ul>\n<li>To learn a candidate's background.</li>\n<li>To obtain information about someone indirectly involved.</li>\n<li>To obtain a witness's written statement.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The quality of the interview is crucial for closing the investigation and for any later litigation.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e1\">5.1 Definitions</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Nonaccusatory term</th>\n<th>Accusatory term</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS Investigations standard (ASIS International 2015)</td>\n<td><strong>Interview:</strong> conversational exchange to gather information that reveals the facts and the truth about what is being investigated</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Common usage among investigators</td>\n<td>Interview: process of gathering information</td>\n<td><strong>Interrogation:</strong> questioning an accused or suspected person</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deming 1997, <em>Interviewing Techniques</em></td>\n<td><strong>Informational interview:</strong> nonaccusatory</td>\n<td><strong>Confrontational interview:</strong> may be accusatory; in general, highly structured</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ferraro 2015, <em>Investigative Interviewing: Psychology, Method and Practice</em></td>\n<td><strong>Administrative interview:</strong> simple and normally unstructured. Applied to witnesses, friends, supervisors, and anyone who is not the subject or suspect</td>\n<td><strong>Investigative interview:</strong> structured. Used when there are reasons to believe the subject committed the offense, alone or with others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inbau 2013, <em>Essentials of the Reid Technique</em></td>\n<td><strong>Interview:</strong> free, nonaccusatory meeting or chat to gather information</td>\n<td><strong>Interrogation:</strong> accusatory interaction with a suspect, in a controlled environment, to persuade them to tell the truth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>This chapter</td>\n<td><strong>General interview</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Confrontational interview</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Deming's terms are recommended for the corporate and private setting. This distinguishes the corporate security professional's approach from the police approach.</li>\n<li>The key difference between interview and interrogation is <strong>the person interviewed</strong>.</li>\n<li>The three nonaccusatory variants (interview, informational, administrative) are equivalent: free conversation to gather information about what is being investigated.</li>\n<li>The three accusatory variants (interrogation, confrontational, investigative) are also equivalent: controlled environment and persuasion for the suspect to tell the truth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Definitions used in the chapter:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>General interview:</strong> nonaccusatory questions to gather information about what is being investigated. <strong>All</strong> people are interviewed: victims, witnesses, and subjects.</li>\n<li><strong>Confrontational interview:</strong> confrontational interrogation of someone suspected of committing a crime or a serious violation of corporate policy. It is reserved for people whose involvement is <strong>reasonably certain</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Subject:</strong> person believed to have committed the offense. Based on accumulated evidence, proximity to the incident, motive, access to restricted areas, and witness statements. The book leaves this list open. They are often called <em>suspect</em>, but investigators prefer <em>subject</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Witness:</strong> any person, other than the subject, with information about the incident.<ul>\n<li>May not have seen the incident directly, but know pertinent facts.</li>\n<li>May be a one-time informant who wants to help for moral reasons.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Caution:</strong> <strong>anonymous informants</strong> are the most likely to give false or biased information.</li>\n<li>The informant's safety is a priority.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Purpose and objectives:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>At its most basic, the interview helps discover the truth.</li>\n<li>Questions must be objective, factual, and focused on obtaining evidence.</li>\n<li>John E. Reid and Associates, Inc., a firm that teaches interviewing and interrogation, points out <strong>at least four objectives</strong>:<ol>\n<li>Obtain valuable facts.</li>\n<li>Eliminate the innocent.</li>\n<li>Identify the guilty.</li>\n<li>Obtain a confession.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.1 — Differences between general and confrontational interview</strong> (the differences change how the interviewer prepares):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>General interview</th>\n<th>Confrontational interview</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nonaccusatory</td>\n<td>Accusatory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dialogue</td>\n<td>Monologue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Variable setting</td>\n<td>Controlled setting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private or semiprivate</td>\n<td>Absolute privacy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guilt unknown</td>\n<td>Guilt indicated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No rights warning*</td>\n<td>Rights warning*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not long</td>\n<td>Long</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cooperative</td>\n<td>Hostile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unstructured</td>\n<td>Structured</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Some planning</td>\n<td>Extensive planning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Note taking is acceptable</td>\n<td>Note taking inhibits</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>*Varies by country.</p>\n<p>Note: in the extracted text \"Hostile\", \"Cooperative\", and the heading of the confrontational column came out with corrupted characters. They were reconstructed from the pattern of the source. Pending: confirm in the original.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Figure 5.1 says the general interview is \"not long\". But in 5.3.2 the book says informational interviews of possible perpetrators can last several hours.</p><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e2\">5.2 Psychosocial Aspects</h3><ul>\n<li>The interview is a human interaction. Each party brings emotions, experiences, and desires.</li>\n<li>The goal is to obtain truthful information. To do so, the interviewer must recognize the interviewee's emotions and handle them with dignity and sensitivity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.2.1 Behavioral Science</h4>\n<p><strong>Human needs according to Maslow (Maslow 1987):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Book's examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Physiological/survival</td>\n<td>Food, drink, health</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety: physical and emotional needs</td>\n<td>Clothing, shelter, protection from attack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Affection</td>\n<td>Belonging to a family or other small group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esteem</td>\n<td>Self-respect and achievements recognized and appreciated by others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-fulfillment</td>\n<td>Using one's potential to the fullest, working with and for others</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>When a primary need is satisfied, it stops driving behavior. The next higher-order one replaces it.</li>\n<li>These needs are also present in the interview.</li>\n<li>If the interviewer understands the interviewee's needs and connects with them, communication is better.</li>\n<li>A successful interviewer satisfies as many of the interviewee's needs as they can.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.2.2 Motivation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>All behavior is motivated and seeks a goal. The force that drives it is satisfying a need.</li>\n<li>Behavior always goes <strong>toward a perceived reward</strong> or <strong>away from a perceived punishment</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Theft triangle:</strong> historical explanation of why people steal.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Side</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Need or want (desire)</td>\n<td>The person needs or wants the item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rationalization (motive)</td>\n<td>They can justify to themselves taking the item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Opportunity</td>\n<td>They have access to the item</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> a bookkeeper who works excessive hours and almost never takes vacations. They may be doing something dishonest and not want others to see the records.</li>\n<li>Needing and wanting are not synonyms. Example: someone needs treatment for a serious illness, but wants a big television. They can justify that the pride of having the television outweighs the fear of dying.</li>\n<li>Other factors in rationalization: believing there is little chance of being discovered, or that the punishment will be minimal.</li>\n<li><strong>Key to confession:</strong> the interviewer helps the subject build a socially acceptable rationalization of what they did.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book labels rationalization as \"(motive)\". It also treats \"needing vs. wanting\" as part of the rationalization side. But in its own definition, need and desire form the \"desire\" side.</p>\n<h4>5.2.3 Interviewer</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They must look professional, show confidence, and relate well to others.</li>\n<li>If possible, the interview is held where the subject feels comfortable and safe.</li>\n<li>The interviewer's attitude sets the tone. A negative attitude gives negative results.</li>\n<li>Making positive comments about the investigation and the interview conveys professionalism and an expectation of success.</li>\n<li>They must know their own skills and emotions and recognize the interviewee's personal traits.</li>\n<li>They must evaluate the truthfulness of what they hear while formulating new lines of questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Floating-point strategy:</strong> continually reevaluate the hypothesis as information arrives, and adjust the questions.</li>\n<li>Inexperienced investigators tend to neglect listening when they interview suspects.</li>\n<li><strong>Active listening</strong> = attentiveness, concentration, acceptance, detachment, and patience.</li>\n<li>They must contrast the facts already established with what the interviewee says.</li>\n<li><strong>The key to success is gaining the interviewee's cooperation.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>The most important factor is establishing good rapport</strong> (a relationship of trust) with the interviewee.</li>\n<li>The good interviewer recognizes personality traits useful for gaining cooperation. They try to understand the interviewee's behavior and satisfy the needs behind it.</li>\n<li>Persevering and listening attentively is critical. They must resist the temptation to think of the next question while the other answers.</li>\n<li>The inexperienced interviewer is usually impatient. Active listening is acquired with patience and a lot of practice.</li>\n<li>They do not take on an adversary role. They are an objective and neutral fact-finder.</li>\n<li>Interviewees who lie usually suffer a lot of stress. They show it with verbal and nonverbal behaviors that the interviewer must know how to evaluate.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e3\">5.3 Preparation</h3><ul>\n<li>The <strong>most critical</strong> preliminary action is to know the case information thoroughly.</li>\n<li>Normally there is time to prepare. Sometimes urgency prevents completing all the steps.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.3.1 Gathering Information</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Before interviewing, all available historical information must be known. Sometimes there are only a few minutes to mentally review the facts. The available time must be used.</li>\n<li>Ideally the interviewer knows in detail:<ol>\n<li>Who is going to be interviewed?</li>\n<li>What is the interview about?</li>\n<li>Under what conditions will it be held?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Matter Under Investigation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the initial phase, all the basic information that can be lawfully obtained is gathered.</li>\n<li>Every investigation ends up developing a <strong>theory or theme</strong>. The theory ties together the scattered evidence and explains how the crime was committed.</li>\n<li>The interviewer must understand the current theory of the case and review all the known facts.</li>\n<li>Special attention to <strong>undisclosed facts</strong>, not revealed to employees or the public. Whoever knows them is a witness or a participant in the crime.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Background Information:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Knowing their personal traits makes rapport easier and helps evaluate their truthfulness.</li>\n<li>In a fraud, <strong>motive, ability, and opportunity</strong> are relevant.</li>\n<li>Useful data: age, address, criminal record, work history, education, and hobbies.</li>\n<li>Criminal investigators make a <strong>personality profile</strong>: mental, emotional, and psychological traits.</li>\n<li>Ethnic influence on nonverbal responses is covered later (5.6.4).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Records Review:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Review the records of the organization and of other entities.</li>\n<li>In large companies, investigation and personnel files are usually in databases.</li>\n<li>Example: a product shortage in a warehouse. The records must be searchable by product name, product number, warehouse, and people involved.</li>\n<li>Other investigations, open or closed, about that product, facility, or staff may suggest a line of investigation or an interview method.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Record</th>\n<th>What it provides</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Personnel files (high priority)</td>\n<td>Performance evaluations; reports of habitual tardiness, fights, or other unacceptable conduct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payroll files</td>\n<td>Financial stability. Garnishments or wage withholdings and child support orders may indicate a need for money</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criminal justice files</td>\n<td>Previous problems with the police. Access must be ethical and in accordance with laws and policies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Discussion with Legal Counsel:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The policy of the investigating company or the client usually dictates what is consulted with the attorney.</li>\n<li>Often the client does not know the legal nuances and depends on the investigator.</li>\n<li>Planning is done with the end in mind: possible civil or criminal actions must always be considered.</li>\n<li><strong>U.S.:</strong> searches must comply with the <strong>Fourth Amendment</strong> of the Constitution. It includes inspection of lockers if there is an expectation of privacy, for example if employees use their own locks.</li>\n<li>In some jurisdictions, investigation reports and statements are subject to <strong>discovery</strong> in civil or criminal proceedings.</li>\n<li>What is done on the attorney's advice may be protected as <strong>attorney work product</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>U.S.:</strong> an admission obtained by a private citizen who interrogates in violation of due process, but who otherwise follows constitutional conduct, <strong>may be admissible</strong>.</li>\n<li>When in doubt, consult the attorney.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.3.2 Planning the Interview</h4>\n<p>If there is time, these key factors are considered.</p>\n<p><strong>Location:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The setting must look fair to an objective outside observer.</li>\n<li>A judge, jury, or arbitrator may weigh the circumstances when deciding whether to admit a statement.</li>\n<li>The interviewee's privacy is a main consideration.<ul>\n<li>Acceptable: a quiet talk in an area away from the scene.</li>\n<li>Unacceptable: an intense talk with the interviewee in front of a door open to a busy hallway during a shift change.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A competent opposing attorney will point out any condition that seems unfair.</li>\n<li>The choice of location depends on four factors:<ol>\n<li>The nature and phase of the investigation.</li>\n<li>The interviewee's apparent involvement.</li>\n<li>The interviewee's confidentiality.</li>\n<li>The level of formality desired.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Recommended location</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Witness with no apparent involvement</td>\n<td>Office or home</td>\n<td>Less tension and more trust; but normal activity may interrupt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Some cases</td>\n<td>At or near the scene of the incident</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subject who is not expected to cooperate</td>\n<td>Security office or interview room</td>\n<td>Controlled setting, with suitable equipment and furniture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Need for confidentiality</td>\n<td>Discreet place: private residence, public restaurant, or other site unrelated to the incident or to security</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Age or illness of the interviewee</td>\n<td>Hospital or nursing home</td>\n<td>The physical environment may be outside the interviewer's control</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Flexibility is required. In every location privacy and confidentiality are maintained.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Room layout:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No unnecessary objects that distract. The subject's office is not ideal: it usually has photos and mementos.</li>\n<li>If there is no dedicated room, a small meeting room is enough.</li>\n<li>Straight chair, with padded seat and back, <strong>without wheels</strong>.</li>\n<li>Avoid plush or reclining armchairs: they invite leaning back. An overly relaxed suspect does not give full attention.</li>\n<li>The subject faces the interviewer. Any witness sits to one side.</li>\n<li>The subject must not see distractions in front, such as a window.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not interview across a desk or table:</strong> it makes rapport harder and prevents observing all the nonverbal behavior.</li>\n<li>The technique used dictates the space between chairs. In general, <strong>about 5 feet (≈1.5 m)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid continuous eye contact:</strong> it can be seen as an attempt to dominate.</li>\n<li>The subject must <strong>always</strong> have a clear path to the exit. Otherwise, accusations of <strong>duress or false imprisonment</strong> can be alleged and succeed.</li>\n<li>Interview <strong>one person at a time</strong>, with no one able to overhear. That way one witness does not influence another.</li>\n<li>It is useful to understand current slang, because the interviewee may use it. The interviewer must not use profanity or informal language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Time Factors:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Time elapsed since the incident.</li>\n<li>Time already scheduled for interviews of other investigations.</li>\n<li>Interviewee's availability.</li>\n<li>Interviewer's availability.</li>\n<li>Expected length of the interview.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Incident: interview <strong>as soon as possible</strong>, because details and nuances are forgotten quickly.</li>\n<li>Routine investigation: do not summon at an inconvenient time for the interviewee. In the company, interview during normal working hours, unless circumstances call for otherwise.</li>\n<li>The general interview is usually short: the information is gathered and the witness statement is quickly prepared.</li>\n<li>Informational interviews of possible perpetrators can last several hours. Even so, they must end in a reasonable time to avoid accusations of <strong>duress</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Questioning Order</strong>, from first to last:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Those who probably will not be available later. Example: an employee who will be transferred far away.</li>\n<li>Those believed to have the most pertinent information.</li>\n<li>Those who are likely to be hostile witnesses.</li>\n<li><strong>The perpetrator or perpetrator group goes last.</strong> The theory of the case helps identify them.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>What the nonsuspects say can confirm or refute the subject's guilt before questioning them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Questions to Ask:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The interviewer must be clear on the key points of the case and the people involved.</li>\n<li>They must also know the <strong>elements of proof</strong> of the alleged offense.</li>\n<li>With those two bases a general format of questions is assembled.</li>\n<li>Using prepared notes or memorizing the questions is a matter of technique.<ul>\n<li>Advantage of notes: they show careful preparation.</li>\n<li>Disadvantage: they distract if overused.</li>\n<li>They are acceptable if not consulted constantly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The essential thing is to prepare. Asking at random does not give results and shows a lack of professionalism, and the interviewee notices.</li>\n<li>Do not try to write everything down: <strong>only the pertinent facts</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Persons Present:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normally there are one or two interviewers and the interviewee. No one confesses before a crowd.</li>\n<li>A second person present (normally another investigator) has two functions:<ul>\n<li>Protect the interviewer from accusations by the interviewee.</li>\n<li>Corroborate statements against interest and confessions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>That witness sits to one side of both, at a reasonable distance. The arrangement keeps the interviewee's attention on the interviewer.</li>\n<li>The interviewee must understand everything. If the interviewer does not master their language, a <strong>qualified interpreter</strong> is needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Different sexes:</strong> a witness of the same gender as the interviewee is advisable. If there is none, the door is left <strong>open</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> the interviewee sits with their back to the door.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> here the book asks to write down only the pertinent facts, not everything. In 5.4.2 it asks to briefly note <strong>all</strong> the subject's answers.</p><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e4\">5.4 Documentation of the Interview</h3><h4>5.4.1 Audio and Video Recording</h4>\n<p><strong>Advantages:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Confirms what everyone present said.</li>\n<li>Confirms that the interviewee participated voluntarily and prevents accusations of duress.</li>\n<li>With modern cameras, the recording can go unnoticed.</li>\n<li>It is the <strong>most reliable</strong> means of documenting evidence verbatim.</li>\n<li>Video acts as a <strong>neutral third party</strong> in the interview.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Technical requirements:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Test the equipment before each interview.</li>\n<li>It may end up at trial. The audio must be reasonably clear and the video must distinguish the participants.</li>\n<li>In both cases, the voices must be understandable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Legal requirements:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Record in accordance with the law and in circumstances a court considers fair. Otherwise, the recording can harm the case.</li>\n<li>It is best that the interviewee consents and that it is recorded openly. It helps to explain to them that the recording protects everyone.</li>\n<li>To record without their knowledge, it is <strong>critical to consult the attorney first</strong>. Recording and assuming no one will find out is bad practice.</li>\n<li>If they do not consent and it is not possible to record discreetly, <strong>notes</strong> are taken.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What the recording must capture, at a minimum:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Interviewee's consent at the start.</li>\n<li>Date, time, and place.</li>\n<li>Full identification of the interviewee.</li>\n<li>Identification of everyone present.</li>\n<li>Possible uses of the information provided.</li>\n<li>Who leaves the room and at what time.</li>\n<li>Who enters the room and at what time.</li>\n<li>Start time, end time, and reason for each break.</li>\n<li>Reaffirmation of consent to the interview and to the recording.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>5.4.2 Notes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Notes document the subject's answers.</li>\n<li>To distract as little as possible, all answers are noted briefly, in a kind of shorthand.</li>\n<li>Example: one must stay calm and write down a phrase such as \"suppose I am guilty\".</li>\n<li>Look at the subject while they answer and then take brief notes.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid noting opinions or general impressions</strong> about the subject.</li>\n<li>A skilled interviewer waits a few minutes before writing something down. That way the subject does not notice what caught their attention.</li>\n<li><strong>The subject must never see what is written.</strong></li>\n<li>In the question-and-answer phase, verbal and nonverbal answers are noted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.4.3 Interview Log</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A log is kept with the <strong>same data</strong> required for the audio recording (the 9 points of 5.4.1).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e5\">5.5 Subject Factors</h3><h4>5.5.1 Intoxication and Impairment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Information from someone under the influence of alcohol or another substance is unreliable to some degree. Their statement can be thrown out at trial.</li>\n<li>They must be asked about alcohol consumed in the <strong>last 24 hours</strong> and about any medication.</li>\n<li>The amount of alcohol and the name and dose of the medication are noted.</li>\n<li>Each case is assessed separately.<ul>\n<li>It may be impossible to interview a homeless alcoholic sober, but they may be the best witness available.</li>\n<li>Someone very active, with dilated pupils, who has gone days without eating or sleeping may be on <strong>amphetamines</strong>. They will be, at most, unreliable.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The usefulness of the information is weighed according to the interviewee's state.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5.2 Physical and Psychological Condition</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It greatly affects the reliability and admissibility of the information.</li>\n<li><strong>Stockholm syndrome:</strong> hostages form an emotional bond with their captors. That makes their interview doubtful.</li>\n<li>After a physically exhausting situation: interview <strong>briefly on the crucial points</strong> and then let them rest.</li>\n<li>Psychological effects may not be evident, but they weaken just the same. Stress colors the answers, which may be inaccurate.</li>\n<li>They may persist a long time, even subliminally. Asking about something distant may provoke an unexpected emotional reaction.</li>\n<li>Do not subject someone who is weakened or stressed to long interviews. Their attorney can later use the duration and their state to throw out the statement.</li>\n<li>Ask the interviewee to confirm that no physical or mental condition prevents the interview.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5.3 Prejudice</h4>\n<p><strong>Of the interviewee:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Their prejudice toward a person or group contaminates the interview.</li>\n<li>They may answer with generalities about the traits they attribute to the group, for or against.</li>\n<li>Those answers serve to evaluate them, but rarely provide concrete facts of the case.</li>\n<li>The interviewer must make sure the answers refer to the case and the specific people.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Of the interviewer:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Their prejudice damages the whole process. It can leave the interview without results and harm future interviews and the whole investigation.</li>\n<li>They must suppress it and remain neutral.</li>\n<li>They commit the <strong>tort of slander</strong> if, knowingly, they say something false and harmful about the subject.</li>\n<li>If they especially dislike the person or group, it is better for another investigator to interview.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5.4 Perception</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>During the interview, the interviewee's physical and mental perception is established.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical:</strong> whether they could see the events or documents. Crucial factors:<ul>\n<li>Corrected visual acuity.</li>\n<li>Available light.</li>\n<li>Distance to the event or document.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Mental:</strong> equally important.<ul>\n<li>Example: someone in finance immediately recognizes a financial document, but struggles with a detailed map of the facility.</li>\n<li>A maintenance worker understands the map, but does not see the importance of the financial document.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5.5 Age</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Age can greatly affect the interview.</li>\n<li><strong>Children:</strong><ul>\n<li>In the corporate setting, children are rarely interviewed. In some areas of private investigation it is common.</li>\n<li>They tend to be shy and distrust strangers; they are taught to be cautious.</li>\n<li>Sitting them on a parent's lap gives them physical security and eases their cooperation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Older people:</strong><ul>\n<li>They must be interviewed in all areas of investigation.</li>\n<li>They may ramble or enjoy the interviewer's company.</li>\n<li>They may pay extraordinary attention to detail.</li>\n<li>A few extra minutes of friendly chat can yield unexpected cooperation and detailed information.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e6\">5.6 Conducting the Interview</h3><h4>5.6.1 Opening Procedures</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Identify the interviewee</td>\n<td>Name, address, date and place of birth and, if applicable, position and workplace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Identify the interviewer</td>\n<td>Name, position and workplace, and the name and role of anyone else present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Clarify the subject</td>\n<td>It is best to ask the interviewee what they understand to be the reason for the interview. Their words or nuances may reveal something about the case. Their misunderstandings are corrected. Details are not needed yet; they are explained later</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Establish rapport</td>\n<td>See below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Rapport:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Merriam-Webster (2021) defines it as a relationship of agreement, mutual understanding or empathy that makes communication possible or easy.</li>\n<li>The interviewer's role is to obtain information. For that, clear communication and good rapport are needed.</li>\n<li>What damages rapport and provokes a defensive response:<ul>\n<li>A haughty or superior attitude.</li>\n<li>Displaying authority.</li>\n<li>Standing in front of a seated interviewee.</li>\n<li>Pointing at them with an accusing finger.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If the interviewee attacks verbally, the interviewer stays controlled, understanding and nonjudgmental.</li>\n<li>Technique: when introducing the others present, invite them to spend a few minutes with the interviewee. This tends to provoke an affirmative response and makes cooperation easier.</li>\n<li>The interviewee should show <strong>signs of congruence</strong> that indicate a positive attitude.</li>\n<li><strong>Hidden persuaders:</strong> starting a general conversation before the direct questions.<ul>\n<li>The interviewee is usually tense; their image of an interview comes from detective shows.</li>\n<li>Talking about pets, gardening or sports predisposes them favorably.</li>\n<li>That conversation gives clues about their personality that are useful later.</li>\n<li>Objective: to get to know them and present oneself as someone helpful and understanding.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Maintaining eye contact favors rapport.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in 5.3.2 the book says to avoid continuous eye contact because it appears dominant. Here it says that maintaining it favors rapport.</p>\n<h4>5.6.2 Investigative Questions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Interviewing a witness, a record custodian or another person not directly involved is usually straightforward. Both are identified, the investigation is briefly explained and the information is gathered.</li>\n<li>Start with <strong>broad, general questions</strong> and then move on to more <strong>specific</strong> ones.</li>\n<li>Even with a case theory and a question format, a broad answer may suggest other questions. Any answer may correct the theory or the format.</li>\n<li><strong>First question:</strong> normally the interviewee is asked to relate the facts.<ul>\n<li>Do not interrupt or guide during this phase.</li>\n<li>If they stray too far from the subject, they are redirected.</li>\n<li>The interviewer takes mental notes of points to develop.</li>\n<li>When the account ends, the interviewer asks about the relevant points and moves on to narrower questions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Types of questions:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>When</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Closed</td>\n<td>Specific, with few possible answers: \"yes\", \"no\" or other short answers</td>\n<td>At the start, to put the interviewee at ease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open-ended</td>\n<td>Seek a narrative answer. Built with who, what, when, where, why and how. Example: asking them to explain how an account is verified at the end of the day</td>\n<td>As the interview progresses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leading — <strong>avoid</strong></td>\n<td>Suggest the answer or contain the information the examiner wants confirmed</td>\n<td>Never</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Questions as simple as possible, depending on the case and the interviewee's involvement.</li>\n<li>The interviewee must understand each question precisely. If in doubt, rephrase it.</li>\n<li>Each answer must be complete before the next question.</li>\n<li>Several open-ended questions at once produce mixed answers that then have to be clarified.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says to start with broad questions and ask for an account of the facts (open-ended). Then it says the start should have closed questions.</p>\n<h4>5.6.3 Verbal and Nonverbal Behavior</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Throughout the process, verbal and nonverbal responses are observed.</li>\n<li>Especially when the subject answers about the details of the case, their alibi and their relationship with the victim.</li>\n<li>The book says this matters especially \"in the primary phase\". Pending: the book does not clarify which phase that is.</li>\n<li>Two things are analyzed and compared: the evidence or factual information, and the behavior of the person giving it.</li>\n<li>Every investigator who asks about procedures, sequences or motives evaluates behavior and draws conclusions about truth or deception.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.6.4 Types of Responses</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Includes</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Verbal</strong></td>\n<td>Spoken words. Also gestures that replace words: nodding (yes) or shaking the head (no). Also vocal traits: tone, speed, pitch and clarity. The good listener also considers timing and emphasis</td>\n<td>A socialized person does not like to lie. Deception creates a conflict that generates anxiety and stress. An evasive response or objection to a direct question seeks to avoid the anxiety of openly denying</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nonverbal</strong></td>\n<td>Body movements, changes of position, gestures, facial expressions and eye contact</td>\n<td>It is internally motivated to reduce anxiety</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Nonverbal ways of relieving anxiety after a deceptive answer:<ul>\n<li><strong>Distraction:</strong> changes of posture, bringing a hand to the face, crossing the arms.</li>\n<li><strong>Displacement behavior:</strong> picking lint off clothing, pacing, rapid repetitive movements.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Preliminary Cautions:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>No isolated word or behavior automatically means that someone is lying or telling the truth. Each behavior is assessed in its context, according to the intensity of the environment and compared with the subject's normal behavior.</li>\n<li>The evaluation is based on the <strong>overall pattern</strong> of behavior, not on a single observation.</li>\n<li><strong>Timing</strong> and <strong>consistency</strong> are evaluated. To be reliable, the changes must occur right after the question or at the same time as the answer.</li>\n<li>The subject's intelligence, sense of social responsibility and maturity are considered.\n   - The most reliable behaviors are shown by socially responsible people, those with more at stake (family, employment, reputation), or more mature ones.\n   - Caution is needed with emotionally or psychologically unstable people.</li>\n<li>A truthful person who feels fear, anxiety, anger or distrust may show behaviors that suggest deception.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Verbal responses — truthful vs. deceptive:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Truthful person</th>\n<th>Deceptive person</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Form</td>\n<td>Direct, frank, spontaneous and sincere, especially in response to simple and clear questions</td>\n<td>Takes time to answer or repeats the question to gain time and make something up (\"Let me see now\"). Some answer too quickly, before the question is finished</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reflection</td>\n<td>Does not need to think about the answer. The good interviewer uses <strong>silence</strong></td>\n<td>Pretends to think seriously to remember what they said before or to camouflage their reactions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consistency</td>\n<td>A single answer, substantially the same even if the question is repeated</td>\n<td>Evasions. To \"did you steal the money from the safe?\" answers \"I was at home all day\" or \"I don't even know the combination\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structure</td>\n<td>Rational</td>\n<td>Irrational; fragmented or incomplete sentences (\"It's important that\", \"If you think\", \"I hope that you\")</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Memory</td>\n<td>Reasonable</td>\n<td>Fails under incisive questions, with half-lies (\"I don't remember\", \"as far as I know\"). Or else an excessively good or selective memory; the result is usually not very credible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reinforcers</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Phrases such as \"to be totally honest\" or \"to be frank\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sophisticated liar</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Plans a \"protective verbal layer\": \"at this moment\", \"if I remember correctly\", \"as I understand it\", \"if my memory serves me\", \"I may be wrong, but\". Looks for an escape hatch without lying openly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explanations</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Complicated, detailed, with data and broader than the question asks. The pathological liar invents stories even if the truth is simpler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vocabulary</td>\n<td>Hard, realistic words: steal, rape, kill, rob, stab</td>\n<td>Avoids them to ease their guilt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Religion and oaths</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Religious facade or oaths: \"I swear to God\", \"on a stack of Bibles\", \"with God as my witness\", \"on my mother's grave\". Using religion as a defense (\"I couldn't, I'm [religion]\") usually indicates a lie</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voice</td>\n<td>Speaks clearly (\"No, I didn't steal the money\")</td>\n<td>Mumbles or speaks so low it cannot be heard; or speaks fast; erratic changes of tone or pitch. Nervous laughter or a joke to camouflage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Details</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Argues over minimal details (\"they say it was at 1:30 and I got back at 1:45\"). Offers excuses or justifications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Denials</td>\n<td><strong>General</strong>: \"I didn't steal that $12,000 or whatever; not a penny\"</td>\n<td><strong>Very specific</strong>: \"I didn't take that $12,437.18\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Summary from the book:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The truthful person</th>\n<th>The deceptive person</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Denies in a general and broad way</td>\n<td>Denies in a very specific way</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gives unqualified, direct and spontaneous answers</td>\n<td>Avoids realistic words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shows a reasonable memory</td>\n<td>Makes seemingly irrelevant comments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Answers rationally and with a clear voice</td>\n<td>Gives delayed, evasive or vague answers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Shows an unusually poor, selective or remarkable memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Qualifies their answers or uses religion or oaths to support them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Speaks irrationally, in a fragmented, mumbled or muted way</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Nonverbal Responses:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Much of the meaning of a message is nonverbal.</li>\n<li>Words are thought out and consciously controlled. Movements and gestures almost never are.</li>\n<li>The true meaning is seen by combining verbal and nonverbal content.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> the subject is asked about embezzlement.<ul>\n<li>Sincere message: answers \"absolutely not, I had nothing to do with it\" in a firm voice, leaning forward, with an open posture and sustained eye contact.</li>\n<li>Insincere message, with the same words: tension-relieving gestures, weak voice, shifts position in the chair, looks at the floor, crosses arms and legs and leans back.</li>\n<li>Most people would notice the difference even if they could not explain it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Activities Suggesting Deception:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Significant change of posture</td>\n<td>Rapid, abrupt movements of the trunk or legs; even leaving the room during the questions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grooming gestures</td>\n<td>Rubbing or wringing the hands; stroking the back of the neck; touching nose, earlobes or lips; biting or tearing at the nails; dragging, tapping, swinging or arching the feet; readjusting clothing or jewelry; brushing, removing lint or pulling threads from clothing; adjusting or cleaning glasses; smoothing or stroking the hair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supportive gestures</td>\n<td>Covering the mouth or eyes while speaking; crossing arms or legs; hiding hands or feet; holding the forehead with the hand; placing the hands under or between the legs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>If the suspect <strong>repeatedly</strong> combines these reactions with their verbal responses, it is a strong indication that they are not telling the truth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Comparative Postures:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Truthful person</th>\n<th>Deceptive person</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sits upright, but not rigid</td>\n<td>Slouches or leans back in the chair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Faces the interrogator</td>\n<td>Sits stiffly and unnaturally</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leans toward the interrogator when emphasizing something</td>\n<td>Sits sideways, not facing the interrogator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Appears relaxed and natural</td>\n<td>Keeps elbows against the body, arms crossed and locked in front, legs crossed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Changes posture quickly and erratically</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Eye Contact:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deceptive:</strong> does not look directly; looks at the floor, to one side or at the ceiling. This relieves their anxiety and makes it easier to lie.<ul>\n<li>Avoids the gaze with compensatory movements, <strong>or</strong> overreacts by staring defiantly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Truthful:</strong> is not defensive and holds the gaze effortlessly.<ul>\n<li>Speaks fluently and gets to the point.</li>\n<li>Does not move excessively or fidget while answering.</li>\n<li>Is relaxed and confident even if apprehensive.</li>\n<li>Is not worried about whether they are believed. Is attentive and their naturalness is genuine.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence is the cornerstone of every investigative decision.</strong> Knowing how to obtain complete information and evaluate its truthfulness guides the investigation. Someone who knows and analyzes these behaviors is better able to tell fact from fiction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Influence of Culture:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The culture of the interviewer and that of the interviewee must be considered. What is normal in one culture may offend in another.</li>\n<li>Respecting the interviewee's culture facilitates rapport. Properly understood, these differences can be used to the interviewer's advantage.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Behavior</th>\n<th>Reading according to culture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Eye contact</td>\n<td>In the West it indicates sincerity. In Asian cultures, looking a superior in the eye is discourteous. Avoiding it may seem insincere but be courtesy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hand gestures</td>\n<td>In some cultures they are commonly used for emphasis. A demonstrative person uses them a lot; a less demonstrative one avoids them. This does <strong>not</strong> equal truth or lies (prevarication)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>5.6.5 Behavior Analysis Interview</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Behavior Analysis Interview (BAI)</strong> was developed by <strong>John E. Reid</strong>, founder of John E. Reid &amp; Associates, Inc.</li>\n<li>It is a systematic evaluation of the subject's verbal and nonverbal behavior. It is done in a <strong>structured</strong> interview with two types of questions:<ul>\n<li><strong>Investigative:</strong> who, what, when, where, why and how (already covered in 5.6.2).</li>\n<li><strong>Behavior-provoking:</strong> seek responses or behaviors that distinguish the truthful from the liar.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Theoretical models of the expected differences were developed, then tested and statistically validated.</li>\n<li>There are <strong>more than 30</strong> behavior-provoking questions (Horvath 2005). The book presents 5.</li>\n<li>Context of the example: theft of credit cards in a bank's mailroom.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Idea of the question</th>\n<th>Truthful response</th>\n<th>Deceptive response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Purpose</strong></td>\n<td>What do you understand to be the purpose of this interview?</td>\n<td>Describes the facts accurately. Uses direct words such as \"steal\". May mention how many cards or the victims' names</td>\n<td>Vague, imprecise description. Non-descriptive language (\"an incident\", \"something happened\") or qualifiers (\"apparently\", \"it seems\", \"maybe\"). Gives no number of cards or names</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>\"You\"</strong></td>\n<td>Cards have gone missing; if you had anything to do with it, tell me now. A novice does not dare to ask it, but it is usually the shortest path to the perpetrator</td>\n<td>Brief, direct and unequivocal denial (\"No, I didn't steal any cards\"). Broad and total language (\"Absolutely not! I haven't stolen anything from here\")</td>\n<td>Longer, unemotional denial. Evasive (\"I don't know anything about this\"), \"I didn't even know cards were missing\", or objection: \"why would I risk my job?\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Knowledge</strong></td>\n<td>Do you know for certain who stole any of the cards?</td>\n<td>Usually offers suspicions (\"not for certain, but I have ideas\"). May show concern or anger (\"I wish I knew\")</td>\n<td>Brief, unemotional denial (\"No, I don't know\"). Offers no ideas or spontaneous feelings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Suspicion</strong></td>\n<td>Whom do you suspect?</td>\n<td>Thinks about it carefully. If they suspect someone, they can justify why</td>\n<td>\"I have no idea\", without thinking. May name unlikely suspects, without opportunity or access</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vouch</strong>, also called <strong>\"Trust\"</strong></td>\n<td>Is there anyone you work with who is above suspicion?</td>\n<td>Thinks about it and usually rules out possible suspects</td>\n<td>Does not vouch for anyone, so as not to shrink the list of suspects and not to increase their own risk of being discovered</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Combining these questions with the investigative ones gives more clarity about the subject's truthfulness.</li>\n<li>It allows suspects to be ruled out with more confidence and the process to move on to the confrontational interview of the probable culprit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text cites \"Horvath 2005\", but the references list Horvath and Buckley, <strong>2004</strong>.</p>\n<h4>5.6.6 Confrontational Interview</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is conducted when all the general interviews have been done and the evidence points to a specific person as the probable perpetrator.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reid Nine Steps of Interrogation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Often considered the most effective process for persuading a deceptive subject to tell the truth.</li>\n<li>It is applied only when the investigator is <strong>reasonably certain</strong> of the subject's involvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>What the interrogator does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Positive Confrontation</strong></td>\n<td>Enters with a file summarizing the investigation. After greeting, accuses the suspect. The direct accusation is made only if guilt seems very clear; otherwise it is less direct. Pauses to observe the reaction and repeats the statement. Sets the file aside, sits directly in front of the suspect and moves to an understanding tone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>Theme Development</strong></td>\n<td>Presents a moral justification for the conduct. Blames another person or external circumstances: the perpetrator tends to minimize their responsibility. Examples: the employer paid them little; someone left the card as a temptation; unusual family expenses; desperate circumstances; the influence of a friend; revenge for an argument; drug or alcohol dependence. Minimizing makes it easier to admit. Understanding tone; showing interest in solving the problem breaks the ice. Speaks in a <strong>monologue</strong>: the suspect does not speak until ready to admit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Handling Denials</strong></td>\n<td>The more times they deny, the harder it is to admit later. Interrupts each \"I didn't do it\" with a blocking statement. Maintaining the theme weakens the guilty person's denials. Many guilty people go from defense to attack and raise objections. The innocent person does not ask permission to speak: they maintain their innocence immediately, without pleasantries. <strong>The innocent person never gets past this stage</strong> and stays firm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Overcoming Objections</strong></td>\n<td>Two groups: <strong>trait objections</strong>, such as \"I wasn't raised that way\" or \"you'd have to be stupid to do that\"; and <strong>factual objections</strong>, such as \"I don't even have the combination\", \"I didn't even know him\", \"I don't need the money\". They are weak explanations even if partly true. Do not argue or show surprise or irritation. A detached reaction discourages the subject, who feels that the objection failed. Once the objections are overcome, the subject usually withdraws and thinks about the punishment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>Keeping a Subject's Attention</strong></td>\n<td>The guilty person becomes pensive, apathetic and quiet. The most important thing is that they listen to the theme (the psychological justification) and do not think about the punishment, which would reinforce their refusal. The interrogator <strong>moves physically closer</strong>: this recovers their attention. Begins to steer the theme toward the likely alternatives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><strong>Handling a Subject's Passive Mood</strong></td>\n<td>Some cry, which usually expresses remorse. Others adopt a defeated posture: head and shoulders drooping, legs relaxed, vacant gaze. The interrogator intensifies the theme and focuses on the psychological justification. Gestures of sympathy, such as a hand on the shoulder, help them tell the truth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><strong>Presenting an Alternative Question</strong></td>\n<td>Two <strong>incriminating</strong> options about aspects of the act, derived from the theme. Example: \"did you plan it or did it happen suddenly?\". Either option is an admission. It is based on assuming guilt. Do <strong>not</strong> ask \"did you do it or not?\": it invites denial. Accepting one option is the <strong>first admission</strong>; it is then turned into an acceptable confession</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><strong>Having the Subject Relate Details</strong></td>\n<td>After the answer, a <strong>reinforcement statement</strong> that acknowledges the admission. Objective: a brief oral review of the sequence, with enough detail to corroborate guilt. Help them rationalize and not be embarrassed. No ambiguous questions. Short, clear questions, with short answers. It is premature to say \"tell me everything\". Open-ended questions without emotionally charged words. Then, detailed questions that the investigation can corroborate. Afterward one can return to the chosen alternative to establish the real purpose and intent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td><strong>Converting an Oral Confession</strong> to a written one</td>\n<td>After \"warning\" the subject (Pending: the book does not say about what), the interviewer leaves, supposedly to check something. Returns with a witness, introduced as someone who takes part in the investigation. Repeats the essential details of the confession; the witness asks a few confirming questions. The oral confession is turned into a written one</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in step 8 the book asks for short questions with limited answers. On the same page it asks for open-ended questions.</p>\n<p><strong>The four written statement formats (step 9):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Statement written by the subject personally.</li>\n<li>Statement drafted by the interviewer, read and signed by the subject.</li>\n<li>Statement taken by the witness and typed, for the subject to read and sign.</li>\n<li>Statement recorded (audio or video) by the subject.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Rules for the statement of guilt:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It must be legible and understandable to someone who does not know what the subject did. For that reason it includes an account of the facts.</li>\n<li>It uses the vocabulary, grammatical errors, profanity and idioms that the subject would use when speaking.</li>\n<li>Each error, change or crossing-out carries the subject's initials and an \"OK\" in the margin.</li>\n<li>It must state that they were treated well, that there were no threats or promises and that they made the statement freely.</li>\n<li>When they finish reading, they are asked to \"write your name here\" while pointing to the line. \"Sign here\" is avoided, because \"sign\" sounds like too much legal significance.</li>\n<li>The subject signs each page in front of the interviewer and the witness. Then both sign.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Requirements for a written statement or confession to be credible and admissible in a US court:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It must be given <strong>voluntarily</strong>, be <strong>trustworthy</strong> and include:<ol>\n<li>Date, time and place where it was prepared.</li>\n<li>Full identification of the declarant: full name, date and place of birth and address.</li>\n<li>All relevant points from the interview, with emphasis on the <strong>elements of proof</strong> of the crime.</li>\n<li>A statement that they are declaring freely, after fair treatment, without payment, threat, coercion or promises.</li>\n<li>Consecutive numbering of all pages, each signed by the declarant.</li>\n<li>The declarant's initials on each addition, deletion or change.</li>\n<li>Signature on the last page by the declarant, the interviewer and a witness.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>After the confession:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The written confession <strong>is not the finish line</strong>. It must be verified and supporting evidence obtained for trial.</li>\n<li>Before trial, either attorney may request a formal sworn statement (<strong>deposition</strong>). It seeks to learn how the investigation was conducted, what evidence was obtained and what witnesses and subjects said.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.6.7 Other Suspect Interviewing Techniques</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The Reid method is common in the US, but there are other methods for obtaining a confession (Stevenson-Cooper 2020).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Origin</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PEACE Model</strong></td>\n<td>Created for UK police; adopted by police forces elsewhere in the world</td>\n<td>Acronym for <strong>P</strong>reparation and Planning, <strong>E</strong>ngage and Explain, <strong>A</strong>ccount, <strong>C</strong>losure, <strong>E</strong>valuate. Seeks to <strong>reduce false confessions</strong>. Focuses on fairness, openness, accountability and fact-finding, not just on achieving a confession</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>WZ Non-Confrontational Method</strong></td>\n<td>Wicklander-Zulawski &amp; Associates</td>\n<td>Structured, conversational alternative. Produces fewer denials and objections and eliminates conflict. Begins by gaining credibility with an introductory statement; builds rapport by rationalizing the reasons for the act; and encourages telling the truth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Kinesic Interview</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Evaluates truthfulness by the subject's reaction to stress. Often combined with other techniques. Uses hypothetical questions, such as asking their opinion on the punishment the crime would deserve. Verbal and nonverbal responses are analyzed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>5.6.8 False Confessions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Some subjects confess to crimes they did not commit. These \"false confessors\" consume time and resources.</li>\n<li>They may have read or seen news reports and press releases. They may even believe they committed the crime.</li>\n<li><strong>Technique to detect them:</strong> present them with an alternative scenario with false details and misleading photos. Observe whether they corroborate the invented crime.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Defending against the later accusation of a \"false confession\":</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Let the suspect provide details without guiding them.</li>\n<li>The true perpetrator may give details that only they know.</li>\n<li>Do not suggest answers or give details at the start, which a gullible interviewee could repeat.</li>\n<li>After obtaining the confession, present an invented crime and see whether they confess to it as well.\n   - Key: add facts that make the fictitious crime <strong>a little more serious</strong> than the real one.</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e7\">5.7 Closing Procedures</h3><ul>\n<li>The closing may not be the last meeting. Another interview may be needed, or the interviewee may be called as a witness.</li>\n<li>Show appreciation for the cooperation.</li>\n<li>Even if they did not cooperate, <strong>do not end on a hostile note</strong>. There are cases solved because someone called later with new information.</li>\n<li>Best closing strategy, in addition to the general conversation:<ul>\n<li>Ask whether they want to add anything.</li>\n<li>If necessary, ask whether they can be contacted again.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e8\">5.8 General Legal Aspects</h3><ul>\n<li>The interviewer must be well versed in the legal requirements of their jurisdiction.</li>\n<li><strong>US — Weingarten case</strong> (<em>NLRB v. Weingarten, Inc.</em>, 420 U.S. 251, 1975). NLRB is the National Labor Relations Board. It recognizes the interviewee's right to have a <strong>union representative</strong> present at an investigative interview.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.8.1 Statements and Confessions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A confession or sworn statement must be <strong>competent, material, relevant and voluntarily obtained</strong>, under legally acceptable circumstances.</li>\n<li>Only then will it be admissible in a later proceeding or hearing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.8.2 Promises and Guarantees</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Sometimes something has to be promised. The interviewer must be sure that what is promised can be legally fulfilled.</li>\n<li>Avoid any promise or guarantee, explicit or implied, that:<ol>\n<li>Is illegal.</li>\n<li>Cannot be confirmed by the client or senior management.</li>\n<li>Cannot be fulfilled.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>If the interviewee asks for an opinion about the outcome, the investigator cannot and must not guarantee it. Their role is to gather facts.</li>\n<li>Saying that cooperating will lead to a more favorable outcome will <strong>probably invalidate</strong> any confession.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.8.3 Threats, Intimidation, and Duress</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Any coercion, duress or psychological restraint is unacceptable and can make the confession inadmissible.</li>\n<li>Verbal abuse, threats and intimidation are unprofessional, unethical and unacceptable.</li>\n<li>Either type of abuse invalidates confessions and statements. It may also give rise to civil or criminal action against the interviewer.</li>\n<li>The interviewee must understand that they <strong>can leave</strong> whenever they wish.</li>\n<li>Indicating, expressly or by implication, that they cannot leave opens the door to accusations of <strong>false imprisonment</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.8.4 Consent</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The interviewee must consent to the interview and to any statement.</li>\n<li>Consent must be entirely voluntary and free of coercion. Otherwise the statement will be inadmissible.</li>\n<li>They must be <strong>legally competent</strong> to consent. The following cannot consent:<ul>\n<li>A minor.</li>\n<li>A person under the influence of alcohol or other substances.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Consent <strong>can be withdrawn at any time</strong>, and the interviewee must know it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.8.5 Access to Restrooms and Meals</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Access to the restroom at reasonable intervals and, above all, whenever requested.</li>\n<li>In a long interview, meals are scheduled at appropriate times.</li>\n<li>Breaks for coffee or another drink serve to <strong>change the mood</strong> of the interview.</li>\n<li>In a public area, the environment is not conducive to discussing the subject. Even so, meals and breaks can improve communication.</li>\n<li>To avoid interruptions, before starting the interviewer asks:<ol>\n<li>Are you feeling unwell in any way?</li>\n<li>Are you thirsty?</li>\n<li>Do you need to use the restroom?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e9\">5.9 Conclusion</h3><ul>\n<li>One key to the process is knowing how to interview suspects.</li>\n<li>Television series suggest that a clue at the scene leads to the perpetrator. In reality, <strong>most cases are solved with information from interviews</strong> and the later work to corroborate or refute it.</li>\n<li>The good interviewer is <strong>patient, persistent and prepared</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each interview is conducted professionally, with respect for the subject and in compliance with all legal requirements.</li>\n<li>Every interview and interrogation must <strong>withstand the scrutiny of judicial review</strong>.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-5-e10\">Key Terms</h3><p>three I's (information, interrogation, instrumentation) · interview · interrogation · general interview · confrontational interview · informational interview (Deming) · administrative interview (Ferraro) · investigative interview (Ferraro) · subject · suspect · witness · anonymous informant · confidential informant · Maslow's hierarchy of needs · theft triangle (desire, rationalization, opportunity) · socially acceptable rationalization · floating-point strategy · active listening · rapport · hidden persuaders · congruence · theory/theme of the case · elements of proof · attorney work product · discovery · Fourth Amendment · false imprisonment · duress · statements against interest · interview log · Stockholm syndrome · tort of slander · corrected visual acuity · closed questions · open-ended questions · leading questions · verbal responses · nonverbal responses · displacement behavior · escape hatch · grooming gestures · supportive gestures · Behavior Analysis Interview (BAI) · behavior-provoking questions · purpose / \"you\" / knowledge / suspicion / vouch (\"trust\") questions · Reid Nine Steps of Interrogation · positive confrontation · theme development · blocking statement · trait objections · factual objections · alternative question · statement of reinforcement · deposition · PEACE model · WZ Non-Confrontational Method · kinesic interview · false confession · Weingarten rights · consent</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Introduction (unnumbered) — Context"},{"id":"e1","t":"5.1 Definitions"},{"id":"e2","t":"5.2 Psychosocial Aspects"},{"id":"e3","t":"5.3 Preparation"},{"id":"e4","t":"5.4 Documentation of the Interview"},{"id":"e5","t":"5.5 Subject Factors"},{"id":"e6","t":"5.6 Conducting the Interview"},{"id":"e7","t":"5.7 Closing Procedures"},{"id":"e8","t":"5.8 General Legal Aspects"},{"id":"e9","t":"5.9 Conclusion"},{"id":"e10","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"5.1 Definitions","h":"Figure 5.1 says the general interview is \"not long\". But in 5.3.2 the book says informational interviews of possible perpetrators can last several hours."},{"s":"5.2 Psychosocial Aspects","h":"the book labels rationalization as \"(motive)\". It also treats \"needing vs. wanting\" as part of the rationalization side. But in its own definition, need and desire form the \"desire\" side."},{"s":"5.3 Preparation","h":"here the book asks to write down only the pertinent facts, not everything. In 5.4.2 it asks to briefly note <strong>all</strong> the subject's answers."},{"s":"5.6 Conducting the Interview","h":"in 5.3.2 the book says to avoid continuous eye contact because it appears dominant. Here it says that maintaining it favors rapport."},{"s":"5.6 Conducting the Interview","h":"the book says to start with broad questions and ask for an account of the facts (open-ended). Then it says the start should have closed questions."},{"s":"5.6 Conducting the Interview","h":"the text cites \"Horvath 2005\", but the references list Horvath and Buckley, <strong>2004</strong>."},{"s":"5.6 Conducting the Interview","h":"in step 8 the book asks for short questions with limited answers. On the same page it asks for open-ended questions."}]},"investigations-6":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-6-e0\">Introduction — General Concepts of Evidence</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Evidence:</strong> that which provides proof or indication of an assertion.</li>\n<li>Courts constantly accept new types of evidence. They arise from advances in medicine, technology, surveillance techniques, information systems and diagnostic tools.</li>\n<li><strong>Why it matters in private security:</strong> its interests are broad (criminal, civil, contractual and corporate). In addition, it must find the root cause of vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li>For this reason the security professional needs advanced evidence-collection skills. It is a key capability of every corporate or organizational investigations unit.</li>\n<li>Many units outsource collection and analysis. They use specialized investigation firms, crime labs or consultants, especially for digital evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Golden rule:</strong> handle evidence to the criminal and civil standard even if it will not go to litigation. That keeps the option open and lends credibility to any administrative or disciplinary action.</li>\n<li><strong>There is no universal standard</strong> for evidence handling. Every nation, state, jurisdiction and agency has its own procedures and rules.</li>\n<li>All recognized standards follow a single basic model, which is the one the chapter presents.</li>\n<li>The investigator follows best practices. They also comply with the rules of the jurisdiction, agency or laboratory (public or private) in each case.</li>\n<li>A single mistake can be costly. The evidence may not reach trial if the chain of custody is not preserved or the standards of that jurisdiction are not followed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Basic Forms and Categories</h4>\n<p>Forms of evidence: oral, documentary and physical.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Includes</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Example from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct evidence</td>\n<td>Real and material</td>\n<td>First-hand knowledge</td>\n<td>A witness sees a driver run a red light and crash into another car</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indirect evidence</td>\n<td>Circumstantial and hearsay</td>\n<td>Well-founded inference. As circumstantial evidence, a fact is deduced from testimony about a series of events</td>\n<td>The witness was around the corner. Hears the screech of brakes and the crash, runs over and sees the light change from red to green. Deduces that the driver ran the red light</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Use of Evidence After an Incident</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The security professional collects the evidence, or supervises its collection, with care. They do so whether or not the police become involved.</li>\n<li>Evidence serves four purposes: establishing a timeline, resolving the matter, analyzing how the incident occurred and, if necessary, changing security measures to prevent others.</li>\n<li>At trial, the judge decides the competency and materiality of the evidence. Normally this relies on case law.</li>\n<li>In non-criminal cases, the presentation of evidence allows a more open interpretation and more concessions.</li>\n<li>In both criminal and non-criminal cases, the defense is entitled to that information during discovery.</li>\n<li>The investigator must be able to recognize a scene that has been staged to mislead the investigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Requirements for Presenting Physical Evidence at Trial</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A clearly demonstrated chain of custody. It is defined as the uninterrupted control of the evidence.</li>\n<li>Correctly identified evidence.</li>\n<li>Evidence relevant to the case.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Chain of custody best practices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mark the evidence with the investigator's initials at the time it is collected.</li>\n<li>Limit to a minimum the number of people who handle it.</li>\n<li>Document each transfer. That way the prosecution can show that it is the same evidence that was seized.</li>\n<li>Always apply these practices, even if the case will not go to a court or a hearing.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-6-e1\">6.1 Oral Evidence</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Oral evidence:</strong> spoken statement about what a person knows. It can be direct or indirect and come from any source.</li>\n<li><strong>Hearsay evidence:</strong> it is indirect evidence. It is normally not admitted at trial.</li>\n<li><strong>Materiality:</strong> the rule most used to measure how much a piece of evidence matters and whether it serves to prove something. It asks about its quality, its substance and its connection with the incident or the case.</li>\n<li><strong>Res gestae</strong> (Latin, \"things done\"): exception to the rule against hearsay. What is said naturally, spontaneously and without thinking during an event leaves little room for misunderstanding. For that reason courts give it great credibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Exceptions to the Hearsay Rule Mentioned in the Book</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Exception</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dying declaration</td>\n<td>Statement by a dying person to a credible witness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Statements against self-interest</td>\n<td>A person who repeats what another said makes statements that go against their own interest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Res gestae</td>\n<td>Spontaneous statements made during the event (three categories)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The three categories of res gestae:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Words or phrases that form part of a physical act or explain it.</li>\n<li>Exclamations so spontaneous that it is impossible for them to be made up (belie concoction).</li>\n<li>Statements that show someone's state of mind.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Source: the section adapts for security the manual of the National Institute of Justice (NIJ), <em>Eyewitness Evidence: Trainer's Manual for Law Enforcement</em> (2003). It follows the Principle / Policy / Procedure structure.</p>\n<h4>6.1.1 Initial Report and First Response</h4>\n<p><strong>Answering the Call</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Principle: the call taker obtains complete and accurate information without suggesting answers (non-suggestive).</li>\n<li>The information may include the name or description of the perpetrator.</li>\n<li>They must be alert to signs that cast doubt on the credibility of the caller or the plausibility of the accusation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Preliminary Scene Investigation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Principle: the scene must be preserved and documented, with the information from witnesses and the physical evidence. The preliminary investigator's methods influence the quantity and accuracy of the information in the entire investigation.</li>\n<li>Policy: obtain, preserve and use the maximum amount of accurate information from the scene.</li>\n<li>Procedure: first secure the scene, calm the victim and attend to the injured. Then, six steps:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Preliminary investigator's action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Identify the perpetrators and detain them if they are still at the scene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Determine what type of incident occurred</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Broadcast an updated description of the incident, the perpetrators and the vehicles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Verify the identity of the witnesses, because they will have to be contacted again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Separate the witnesses and ask them not to discuss details among themselves. They must not hear the account of others. It is advisable to find out whether they already spoke to each other before separating them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Canvass the area for more witnesses. Some are reluctant to talk or have already left. Nearby employees, contractors, guests or visitors may have seen or heard something</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Exception in a union environment:</strong> there the employer's investigator must not demand confidentiality from the employees being interviewed. There are four exceptions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>When witnesses need protection.</li>\n<li>When the evidence is in danger of being destroyed.</li>\n<li>When the testimony is in danger of being fabricated.</li>\n<li>When a cover up must be prevented.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Obtaining Information from Witnesses</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Principle: the way the information is obtained determines its quantity and accuracy.</li>\n<li>Policy: obtain the information from the witnesses, document it accurately and preserve it.</li>\n<li>Procedure (8 steps):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What it is for and detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Establish rapport</td>\n<td>The witness feels comfortable and gives more information. It allows learning their communication style: how they tell an everyday event versus how they tell the incident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Ask how they are doing (\"How are you doing?\")</td>\n<td>Reinforces rapport. Alerts to physical or mental states that affect their recall or their account: intoxication, medication, shock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Open-ended questions, supplemented with closed questions; never leading questions</td>\n<td>Open-ended: \"What can you tell me about the car?\". Closed: \"What color was the car?\". Leading, to be avoided: \"Was the car red?\". The leading question hints at the answer and can distort perception or memory. Only what the witness knows matters, without influence from what the investigator expects or knows from other sources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Clarify the information with the witness</td>\n<td>Ensures it was understood and recorded correctly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Document the information, including the witness's identity</td>\n<td>It will be needed for the follow-up interview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Encourage them to make contact again if they remember anything else</td>\n<td>Witnesses often remember useful details later. Nothing is trivial. Example: if they remember that the perpetrator drank from a soda can, there may be fingerprints or saliva on it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Ask them to avoid the media and its news about the incident</td>\n<td>The media can contaminate their memory. An interview request or an offer of payment may push them to make things up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Ask them not to discuss details with other potential witnesses</td>\n<td>Independence among witnesses makes it possible to check whether their account fits the other statements and evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Summary: the witness's information can corroborate other evidence. That is why it must be documented accurately.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.1.2 Interviews by the Follow-Up Investigator</h4>\n<p><strong>Pre-Interview Preparations and Decisions</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Principle: preparing the interview increases the witness's cooperation and the interviewer's efficiency.</li>\n<li>Policy: review all the information from the witness and the case, and organize an efficient and effective interview.</li>\n<li>Procedure (6 steps):<ol>\n<li>Review the available information, such as police reports and scene information. That way the interview is tailored to get the most out of it.</li>\n<li>Conduct the interview as soon as the witness is physically and emotionally fit.</li>\n<li>Choose a place that is comfortable for the witness and has minimal distractions.</li>\n<li>Have key materials ready (notebook, recorder, camera) so as not to interrupt.</li>\n<li>Separate the witnesses. Their independent statements serve to corroborate.</li>\n<li>Find out what prior contacts the witness has had with the police, with security or with the company. That context helps assess their credibility and reliability, and later to build rapport.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conducting the Interview</strong> — principles for cooperating witnesses:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Social dynamics: the social dynamics between interviewer and witness.</li>\n<li>Facilitation of the witness's memory and thinking: helping the witness's memory and reasoning.</li>\n<li>Communication between the interviewer and witness: the communication between the two.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book's list ends in \"and\" after the third point, as if a fourth principle were missing. The text does not include it and develops only three. Pending: confirm in the printed book whether a fourth exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Social Dynamics</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maintain or regain rapport.</li>\n<li>Encourage the witness to tell the story actively and voluntarily, not to answer passively.</li>\n<li>Rapport helps especially when personal or intimate information is requested.</li>\n<li>Show understanding and interest: ask about their health, put yourself in their place, do not judge and look for common ground.</li>\n<li>Personalize the interview and treat them as an individual:<ul>\n<li>Avoid questions that sound formulaic or artificial. Example to avoid: \"Is there anything you can tell me that would further assist this investigation?\".</li>\n<li>Call them by their name.</li>\n<li>Ask questions that follow from their previous answers.</li>\n<li>Repeat the concerns they express.</li>\n<li>Lean forward and look them in the eye.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Five techniques to get them to provide information without being asked:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>State expectations</td>\n<td>The witness may not know what is expected of them, or may expect something else. Tell them clearly that they must provide information on their own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ask open-ended questions</td>\n<td>The witness does most of the talking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoid interruptions</td>\n<td>Interrupting takes away their active role and cuts off their memory. Note down the questions and ask them afterward</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Allow pauses</td>\n<td>When they fall silent, the witness organizes their ideas and continues the account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In-person interviews</td>\n<td>Whenever possible. If not, by teleconference with video and not only audio, to see their reactions in real time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Facilitation of the Witness's Memory and Thinking</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduce physical distractions, such as noise or the presence of other people. They can be invited to close their eyes and concentrate on the memory.</li>\n<li>Ask them to mentally reconstruct the incident, for example what they were thinking or feeling at that moment.</li>\n<li>Adjust the questions to what they are describing. If they are thinking about the perpetrator's face, ask about the face and not the license plate. First, an open-ended question about that subject; then, non-suggestive closed questions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Communication Between the Interviewer and Witness</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The interviewer pursues several aims: resolving the incident, enforcing company rules and preparing for possible civil litigation.</li>\n<li>The witness knows the details. For the interview to be productive, both must communicate what they need and what they know.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Weight and Risks of Oral Evidence</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jurors and judges give great weight to oral evidence. The security employee who investigates must master how to obtain it for later proceedings.</li>\n<li>Do not promise anything or offer rewards in exchange for a statement. If discovered, the statement is voided. In addition, the investigator may be accused of witness tampering.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-6-e2\">6.2 Documentary Evidence</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Documentary evidence:</strong> information, normally letters, figures or other marks, that is on or in an object. Examples: paper products, rubber stamps, plastic bags and seals.</li>\n<li>If police labs do not examine it, the security professional can turn to private laboratories and experts (see Appendix 6A).</li>\n<li>The questioned document examination is adapted from the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) <em>Handbook of Forensic Services</em>, 2019 edition.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2.1 Handwriting, Hand Printing, and Signatures</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Analyzing the characteristics of writing sometimes makes it possible to determine the origin or authenticity of a questioned writing.</li>\n<li>Sometimes altered or crossed-out writing can be deciphered, and forged signatures detected.</li>\n<li><strong>Application to security:</strong> keep images of the anonymous letters and threatening documents that have caused concern. That way the letter in one case can be linked to those in others.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exemplars of persons of interest</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reproduce the original conditions as closely as possible: text, paper size, writing space, writing instrument and style (cursive or print).</li>\n<li>Give instructions, oral or written, about the text to be written.</li>\n<li>Do not give instructions about spelling, punctuation or layout of the writing.</li>\n<li>In addition, eight rules:<ol>\n<li>Each sample goes on a separate sheet.</li>\n<li>The writer and a witness put their initials and the date on each page.</li>\n<li>The writer does not see their earlier samples or the questioned writing.</li>\n<li>Ask for many repetitions, until their normal writing emerges.</li>\n<li>Ask for samples with the right hand and with the left.</li>\n<li>Ask for hand printing in upper and lower case.</li>\n<li>Gather enough samples to cover the natural variation in their writing.</li>\n<li>Obtain undictated writing: business records, personal letters and canceled checks.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Types of false signatures</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>How it is done</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Traced</td>\n<td>An authentic signature is used as a template or pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simulated</td>\n<td>An authentic signature is copied or drawn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Freehand</td>\n<td>The forger writes in their normal handwriting, without trying to imitate the other person's style</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>6.2.2 Typewriting</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Today it is uncommon. It may matter if a typewriter or an impact printer was used.</li>\n<li>Also in old or \"cold\" cases, dormant for years, that are reopened because of new information.</li>\n<li>Sometimes a questioned typewritten text can be linked to the machine that typed it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2.3 Photocopies</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Sometimes they are linked to the machine that made them. Condition: that the samples and the questioned copies date from close to one another.</li>\n<li>Make 10 copies with each of these four methods:<ol>\n<li>With no document on the glass and the lid down.</li>\n<li>With no document on the glass and the lid up.</li>\n<li>With the document on the glass and the lid down.</li>\n<li>With the document run through the automatic document feeder.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Note on each page the date, the name of the person collecting it and the conditions.</li>\n<li>Note about the machine: make, model and serial number; toner and components; whether the paper is in sheets or on a roll; and options for color, reduction, enlargement, zoom, mask and trim.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2.4 Other Materials and Images</h4>\n<p><strong>Graphic Arts and Photographs</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A printed item can sometimes be linked to a common source or to known printing material: originals, negatives or plates.</li>\n<li>The examination of films, negatives, digital images, paper copies and video recordings makes it possible to:<ul>\n<li>Compare the people in questioned images with those in known images (photos, videos).</li>\n<li>Compare firearms, vehicles, clothing and other objects that appear.</li>\n<li>Determine the place, time and date of capture.</li>\n<li>Determine whether the image is a composite, an alteration or a copy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Measurements can be calculated with mathematical formulas or by comparison on site, through photogrammetry. Examples: a person's height and the length of a weapon or tool.</li>\n<li>When collecting or using photos as evidence, it is crucial to check whether the image was altered and to document it. Common programs such as Adobe Photoshop make it possible to falsify them without it being noticeable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Paper and Documents</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Torn edges can sometimes be matched beyond any doubt.</li>\n<li>If there is a watermark, the manufacturer can sometimes be determined.</li>\n<li>The impressions left by writing pressure (indentations) can be examined. Do not rub them with a pencil or write over the evidence.</li>\n<li>Soaked documents can be dried and preserved. Frozen ones can be thawed and preserved.</li>\n<li>The information in burned or charred documents can sometimes be deciphered. Touch them as little as possible and transport them in one of three ways:<ol>\n<li>In the container in which they were burned.</li>\n<li>In a polyester film sleeve.</li>\n<li>Between layers of cotton inside a rigid container.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The earliest date on which a document could have been made can sometimes be derived from watermarks, embossed writing, printing or typewriting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ink</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The analysis is usually limited to comparing the components of the organic dye.</li>\n<li>Even if two inks have the same formula, it cannot be asserted that they come from the same source and not from another.</li>\n<li>It is not possible to know how long the ink has been on the document.</li>\n<li>Package ink evidence separately from any document or surface with ink marks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other Media</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Medium</th>\n<th>What it may reveal</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Used carbon paper or carbon film ribbon</td>\n<td>Sometimes the content of the text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impression from a checkwriter</td>\n<td>Sometimes the machine that made it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Embossed or stamp impression</td>\n<td>Sometimes the instrument that made it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rubber stamp impression</td>\n<td>Sometimes the stamp that made it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Plastic bags (sandwich or trash bags)</td>\n<td>Sometimes the roll or box they came from</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>6.2.5 Shipping Documentary Evidence</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep it as it was found.</li>\n<li>Do not fold, tear, mark, soil or seal it, or write on it. Do not touch it unnecessarily.</li>\n<li>Prevent it from accidentally receiving writing impressions or other marks.</li>\n<li>It can be discreetly marked in pencil with initials, date and other data.</li>\n<li>Whenever possible, send the original to the laboratory. Photocopies lose detail and complicate the examination.</li>\n<li>Copies are enough for searching reference files. Do not store photocopies in plastic sleeves.</li>\n<li>To send it to the laboratory, package it well and send it by registered mail or by a carrier that tracks the shipment. That preserves the chain of custody.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-6-e3\">6.3 Physical Evidence</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Physical evidence:</strong> tangible evidence. Examples: airborne particles, a puddle of liquid, soil or dust, clothing, tools, electronic devices, weapons, vehicles and body fluids. It includes everything that can be touched or detected physically or technologically.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Corpus delicti</td>\n<td>Proof that a crime was committed</td>\n<td>The corpse in a homicide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Associative evidence (also called circumstantial evidence)</td>\n<td>Links a suspect to the scene</td>\n<td>Fingerprints, DNA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identifying evidence</td>\n<td>Associative evidence that establishes who the suspect is</td>\n<td>Fingerprints, bite marks, blood at the scene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tracing evidence (not to be confused with trace evidence)</td>\n<td>Helps identify and locate the suspect</td>\n<td>Credit card receipt, clothing label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trace evidence</td>\n<td>Examined by the forensic section of the laboratory</td>\n<td>Fibers, paints, dyes, glass and similar materials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impression evidence</td>\n<td>Impressions and marks</td>\n<td>Firearms, tool marks, bites, footprints</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book calls indirect evidence \"circumstantial\" (in the introduction) and also associative evidence (in 6.3). Fingerprints and bites appear in more than one category. Distinguish tracing (used to locate the suspect) from trace (microscopic remnants).</p>\n<h4>6.3.1 Evidence Searches</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Physical evidence is more productive if the search is planned, coordinated and carried out by trained security employees.</li>\n<li>Four ideas they must keep in mind:<ol>\n<li>The best search options are usually the most difficult and slow.</li>\n<li>Physical evidence can never be overdocumented.</li>\n<li>The search can be cautious in visible areas, to avoid losing or contaminating evidence, or intensive in hidden areas.</li>\n<li>It must be done in time, while the evidence still exists. Example: a bank forgery from 6 years ago may leave no physical evidence, because the law only requires the bank to keep records for 5 years.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Team Members</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If possible, the team discusses the search with the personnel involved before arriving.</li>\n<li>It checks that everyone knows the common types of evidence and how to handle them.</li>\n<li>If possible, it assigns roles before arriving. One person may have two or more responsibilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Functions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Person in charge</td>\n<td>Secures the scene; keeps the administrative log; does the initial walkthrough; writes the narrative description; solves problems; makes the final decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Photographer</td>\n<td>Photographs and records the evidence and the scene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sketch preparer</td>\n<td>Draws and records the scene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence recorder</td>\n<td>Acts as custodian of the evidence and records it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scene supervisor</td>\n<td>The nine functions below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Functions of the scene supervisor:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Communicates with the appropriate company managers, including legal counsel if needed, to resolve problems that arise in the search.</li>\n<li>Coordinates agreements between departments when several take part in the search.</li>\n<li>Obtains the materials and equipment to collect and package evidence.</li>\n<li>Prepares the paperwork that documents the search.</li>\n<li>Provides personnel with protective clothing, communications, lighting, shelter, transportation, equipment, food, water, medical assistance and security.</li>\n<li>In long searches, organizes shifts of two or more teams. The paperwork and responsibility pass from one team to another according to a prior plan.</li>\n<li>Stays alert to the evidence.</li>\n<li>Takes extensive notes.</li>\n<li>Looks after the safety of all personnel.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Procedures: team tasks at the site, in order</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Task</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Secure and Protect the Scene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Conduct a Preliminary Survey</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Evaluate Evidence Possibilities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Produce a Narrative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Photography</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Sketches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Physical Evidence Collection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Final Survey</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Release</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>1. Secure and Protect the Scene</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Take control of the scene immediately and assess how well it was protected.</li>\n<li>Consult those who know how it was originally.</li>\n<li>Do not let anyone in without authorization and record who enters and who leaves.</li>\n<li>Appoint one person for the final decisions and problem resolution.</li>\n<li>One or more people take extensive notes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Conduct a Preliminary Survey</strong> — serves to plan the search:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Walk through the scene carefully and take preliminary photos.</li>\n<li>Delimit the search area and be ready to expand the initial perimeter.</li>\n<li>Detect the especially problematic areas.</li>\n<li>Organize methods and procedures.</li>\n<li>Choose the narrative technique: written, audio or video.</li>\n<li>Formulate and develop a general theory of the incident.</li>\n<li>Take extensive notes on the scene, the physical and environmental conditions and the movements of personnel.</li>\n<li>Identify and protect transient evidence, that which may soon be lost.</li>\n<li>Estimate what personnel and equipment are needed and assign specific tasks.</li>\n<li>Maintain administrative and emotional control at all times.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Evaluate Evidence Possibilities</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Attend first to evidence that may be lost. Leave the least transient for last.</li>\n<li>Search first in accessible areas and then in those not in view. Look for hidden objects.</li>\n<li>Check that there are enough collection and packaging materials and equipment.</li>\n<li>Everyone must think about all types of evidence, not only that of their specialty.</li>\n<li>Assess whether the evidence appears to have been moved and whether the scene appears contrived.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4. Produce a Narrative</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a continuous description of the scene, made methodically.</li>\n<li>Everything that merits attention merits being recorded.</li>\n<li>Photos and sketches complement the narrative; they do not replace it.</li>\n<li>In general, no evidence is collected during the narrative.</li>\n<li>Minimum content: (1) case identifier; (2) date, time and place; (3) weather and lighting; (4) identity and tasks of personnel; (5) condition and position of the evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>5. Photography</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Photograph the scene as soon as possible.</li>\n<li>Before entering, obtain if possible prior photos, blueprints or maps of the site.</li>\n<li>With a digital camera, take some test photos first to rule out distortion. Distortion comes from an incorrect point of view, inadequate perspective or misleading tones such as shadows.</li>\n<li>Shoot at eye level to show the normal view.</li>\n<li>Follow a progression: overall, medium and close-up views.</li>\n<li>Start with the most fragile areas. Photograph all phases of the investigation, including the findings.</li>\n<li>Photograph the evidence before collecting it.</li>\n<li>Every photo for examination includes a scale. First it is taken without a scale and then with a scale.</li>\n<li>Important evidence is photographed twice:<ol>\n<li>At medium distance, showing its position relative to other evidence.</li>\n<li>Up close, with a scale and filling the frame.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Photos carry the photographer's name and the date.</li>\n<li>Interior: series of overlapping photos, with a normal lens if possible. Overall views can be made with a wide angle.</li>\n<li>Exterior: locate the scene with a series of overall views that include a landmark.</li>\n<li>Photograph entrances and exits from inside and outside.</li>\n<li>Cover the full 360 degrees. Consider aerial photography.</li>\n<li>Keep a photographic log with all the photos and the description and location of the evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>6. Sketches</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They serve to record the location of objects, conditions and relative distances and sizes.</li>\n<li>Number them so that they match the numbers in the evidence log.</li>\n<li>Each sketch includes:<ol>\n<li>Case identifier.</li>\n<li>Date, time and place.</li>\n<li>Weather and lighting.</li>\n<li>Identity and tasks of personnel.</li>\n<li>Measurements of rooms, furniture, doors and windows.</li>\n<li>Distances between objects, people, bodies, entrances and exits.</li>\n<li>Measurements that locate the evidence relative to two fixed elements, such as doors or walls.</li>\n<li>Key, legend, compass orientation, scale or scale disclaimer, or a combination of them.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Comparison</th>\n<th>Narrative</th>\n<th>Sketch</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Common elements</td>\n<td>Case; date, time and place; weather and lighting; personnel and tasks</td>\n<td>The same four</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Own elements</td>\n<td>Condition and position of the evidence</td>\n<td>Room and furniture measurements; distances; location relative to two fixed points; key, legend, compass and scale</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>7. Physical Evidence Collection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the most critical moment and the one with the most mistakes. Extreme care must be taken when searching, photographing, recovering, marking, identifying and packaging.</li>\n<li>It is done by an evidence technician or the investigator.</li>\n<li>It is essential to isolate and protect the scene to preserve the evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Application to security:</strong> that is why every basic police or security officer course must teach how to secure a scene.</li>\n<li>Choose a search pattern: grid, strip or lane, or spiral.</li>\n<li>Check entrances and exits. Go from the general to the particular. Be alert to any evidence.</li>\n<li>Fingerprints are the most common physical evidence. Collect and preserve them first.</li>\n<li>Use latex or cotton gloves so as not to leave new prints.</li>\n<li>Once recovered, do not touch the evidence more than necessary.</li>\n<li>Seal all packages at the same scene.</li>\n<li>Time and temperature deteriorate organic materials. Measures must be taken to prevent it.</li>\n<li>Obtain known standards, such as fibers from a known carpet.</li>\n<li>Two people must see the evidence in place, during recovery and while it is being marked.</li>\n<li>Properly marking the evidence when seizing and transferring it is what establishes the chain of custody.</li>\n<li>Mark directly on the evidence only when necessary. Before that, try to mark the container.</li>\n<li>The minimum to certify a piece of evidence is the investigator's initials.</li>\n<li>Photograph everything before collecting it and note it in the photographic log.</li>\n<li>Evaluate the scene completely.</li>\n<li>If possible, a single person acts as the custodian of the evidence.</li>\n<li>Indicate on the sketch where each piece of evidence was.</li>\n<li>Constantly review the paperwork, packaging and data for errors.</li>\n<li>Document the chain of custody throughout the process, with all the people who touched the evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>8. Final Survey</strong> — review all aspects of the search:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discuss the search with all personnel.</li>\n<li>Check that the documentation is correct and complete.</li>\n<li>Photograph the final condition of the scene.</li>\n<li>Confirm that all the evidence is secured and that all equipment was collected.</li>\n<li>Confirm that no hiding place or hard-to-reach area was overlooked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>9. Release</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>After the final survey, assess whether specialists such as human resources, labor relations or safety should see the scene.</li>\n<li>Confirm that the evidence was collected according to legal requirements, documented and marked.</li>\n<li>The release document states the time and date, to whom the scene was released and who released it.</li>\n<li>It is released only when all personnel are sure that the search was correct and complete.</li>\n<li>Only the person in charge can release the scene.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.3.2 Requesting Evidence Examinations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every request is in writing, on organization letterhead and addressed to the appropriate laboratory.</li>\n<li>Requirements vary by laboratory. It is usual to include:<ol>\n<li>Name, organization, address, email and phone number of the sender.</li>\n<li>Previous case numbers, previous evidence submissions and communications about the case.</li>\n<li>Nature and basic facts of the case related to the examinations.</li>\n<li>Names and description of those involved (subject, person of interest or victim).</li>\n<li>Case number of the submitting organization.</li>\n<li>List of the evidence enclosed or sent separately, with the means of shipment.</li>\n<li>Types of examination requested.</li>\n<li>Address to which the evidence is to be returned.</li>\n<li>Address to which the laboratory report is to be sent.</li>\n<li>Notice of whether another expert has already examined the evidence, whether there is local controversy or whether other organizations are interested in the case.</li>\n<li>Reasons for requesting an urgent examination, if any.</li>\n<li>Separate communications for different cases.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.3.3 Shipping Evidence</h4>\n<p>Steps for sending evidence to a laboratory:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Before packing and shipping, call the laboratory to ask for specific instructions.</li>\n<li>Wrap and seal each piece of evidence separately so they are not contaminated.</li>\n<li>Place it in a clean, dry, unused inner container.</li>\n<li>Seal the inner container with tamper-evident tape or filament tape.</li>\n<li>Put the \"BIOHAZARD\" label on the inner container if applicable. If latent prints are to be searched for, also put the \"LATENT\" label.</li>\n<li>Place the sealed inner container in a clean, dry, unused outer container, with clean padding material. Do not use loose foam.</li>\n<li>Include in each package a copy of the examination request, in an envelope separate from all the evidence.</li>\n<li>Completely seal the outer container so that any tampering is noticeable.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>In the US, every shipment of hazardous materials complies with the standards of the U.S. Department of Transportation and the International Air Transport Association.</li>\n<li>A trained and qualified evidence technician must help label, package and ship any hazardous material.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Shipping live ammunition</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Package and ship the ammunition separately from the weapons.</li>\n<li>Label the outside of the container: \"ORM-D, Cartridges, Small Arms\". ORM-D is a shipping category for regulated material; the book does not explain the acronym.</li>\n<li>Record on the Declaration of Dangerous Goods the number of packages and the gross weight in grams.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>It is advisable to use courier services with detailed tracking.</li>\n<li>Security personnel note the shipping method and tracking numbers on the chain of custody form.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Disposal of hazardous materials</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Resource Conservation and Recovery Act (RCRA) is administered by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency.</li>\n<li>It is known as the \"cradle-to-grave\" rule. It follows chemicals from the time they are generated (cradle) until they are disposed of (grave).</li>\n<li>It imposes obligations on generators and transporters, and on transportation, storage and disposal facilities.</li>\n<li>When a material is declared hazardous, full responsibility lies with the generator.</li>\n<li>Deciding whether a material is hazardous waste falls to qualified personnel.</li>\n<li>Even a new material in its original container can be waste if it no longer has a use.</li>\n<li>Hazardous waste contractors and transporters can help remove materials from the scene.</li>\n<li>Hazardous materials removed from a scene are evidence. The RCRA waste rules do not apply to them.</li>\n<li>But when the case is adjudicated or the material is no longer needed, a qualified contractor who knows the local rules must be sought promptly.</li>\n<li>Examples of scenes where waste may need to be removed: clandestine drug labs and environmental incidents.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.3.4 Handling Physical Evidence</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In the private sector, almost all investigators call the police to take charge of the evidence in serious cases, such as homicides and terrorist attacks.</li>\n<li>The guidelines below serve for other cases or until the police arrive.</li>\n<li>They are adapted mainly from the FBI <em>Handbook of Forensic Services</em> (2019), which covers more types of evidence.</li>\n<li>The book lists 26 types, in this order: weapons and ammunition; audio and video recordings; biological samples and DNA; construction materials; unknown chemicals; computers; controlled substances and drugs; electronic devices; glass; latent prints; lubricants; metals; paint; pepper spray; polymers; product tampering; rope; safe insulation; serial numbers; footwear and tire impressions; soil; tape; tool marks; toxicology; video; and wood.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Ammunition and Firearms</h4>\n<p><strong>Guns and Gun Parts</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Examining parts makes it possible to determine the caliber and model of the weapon they came from.</li>\n<li>Before the examination, make sure all weapons are unloaded.</li>\n<li>Touch weapons as little as possible so as not to lose or destroy evidence.</li>\n<li>No object should enter the barrel, chamber or other operating surfaces, or touch them. Never put anything down the barrel.</li>\n<li>When handling a recovered fired bullet, protect the rifling marks.</li>\n<li>Pick up cartridges by the open end with tweezers or another tool. That way partial prints and other evidence on the outside are not destroyed.</li>\n<li>Do not mark the item.</li>\n<li>Identify the parts with a tag indicating caliber, make, model and serial number.</li>\n<li>On the container: date, time, owners' names, place, name of the person collecting it, case number and evidence number.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says \"spare cartridges\" and asks that they be picked up by the \"open end\". Possible typo for \"spent\" (already fired casings). Exam note: here it says \"do not mark the item\". By contrast, the general rule asks to mark with initials and Appendix 6B asks to mark each item on the item itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Bullets, Jackets, and Casings</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fired bullets reveal general rifling characteristics (caliber and shape of the marks) and the bullet manufacturer.</li>\n<li>Cartridge casings, shotshell casings and wadding reveal caliber or gauge, manufacturer and whether there are marks useful for comparison.</li>\n<li>Images of questioned casings can be scanned into the National Integrated Ballistics Information Network. That way they are compared with evidence from other shootings.</li>\n<li>The microscopic characteristics of bullets and casings indicate whether they were fired from a specific weapon.</li>\n<li>Unfired cartridges reveal caliber or gauge and whether there are useful marks. Also whether they were loaded into a specific weapon and extracted from it.</li>\n<li>Fired and unfired cartridges can be linked by manufacturing marks.</li>\n<li>Package each component (bullets, casings, shotshell casings) separately. On the container: date, time, place, who collects it, case number and evidence number.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Gunshot Residue on Clothing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The residue left on clothing changes according to the distance between the muzzle and the target.</li>\n<li>The pattern is reproduced by firing the same weapon and ammunition combination at test materials at known distances. That way the distance between the muzzle and the garment is estimated.</li>\n<li>Handle the clothing with care, air-dry it and wrap each garment separately in paper.</li>\n<li>If the clothing has blood: air-dry and put \"BIOHAZARD\" on the inner and outer containers.</li>\n<li>On the container: date, time, place, who collects it, case number and evidence number.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Audio and Video Recordings</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Write-protect the original recordings.</li>\n<li>Never use pause when viewing an original video recording.</li>\n<li>Send the original (film or video) whenever possible, because it has the maximum detail. If it does not exist, send first-generation audio, video and photos.</li>\n<li>When requesting forensic examinations of video images:<ul>\n<li>Leave the original cued near the moment of interest.</li>\n<li>State in writing the date and time of that moment, according to the date and time stamp or the counter.</li>\n<li>Send as a reference the prints of the relevant frames, if there are any.</li>\n<li>Develop all the film, including surveillance film, before sending it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Labels on the outer container for shipping: \"FRAGILE, SENSITIVE ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT\" or \"FRAGILE, SENSITIVE AUDIO/VIDEO MEDIA\", and also \"KEEP AWAY FROM MAGNETS OR MAGNETIC FIELDS\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Biological Samples and DNA</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is analyzed in body fluids, stains and other biological samples.</li>\n<li>The DNA of questioned samples is compared with that of known samples. That way victims or persons of interest can be linked to each other or to the scene.</li>\n<li>If it is not properly documented, collected, packaged and preserved, the DNA does not meet the legal and scientific requirements for a court to admit it.</li>\n<li>Risks: that its origin is challenged, that it loses biological activity, that it is contaminated, or that it decomposes and deteriorates until it is useless.</li>\n<li>The method of collection, packaging and preservation depends on whether the evidence is liquid or solid and on its condition.</li>\n<li>The more intact it reaches the laboratory, the more useful examinations can be performed.</li>\n<li>In general, these samples should be collected by the police.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Blood Samples (blood samples from a person)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only qualified medical personnel draw blood from a person.</li>\n<li>For DNA: at least two 5-milliliter purple-top tubes, with EDTA as the anticoagulant. The book does not explain the acronym EDTA.</li>\n<li>For drug or alcohol analysis: gray-top tubes with sodium fluoride.</li>\n<li>Each tube carries the date, time, name of the person, place, who drew it, case number and evidence number.</li>\n<li>Refrigerate without freezing. During shipping, use cold packs, not dry ice.</li>\n<li>Package each tube separately, in foam or in cylindrical tubes, surrounded by absorbent material. Send to the laboratory immediately.</li>\n<li>Outer container labels: \"KEEP IN A COOL DRY PLACE\", \"REFRIGERATE ON ARRIVAL\" and \"BIOHAZARD\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Blood on a Person or Surface</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liquid blood or clots: absorb them with a clean cotton cloth or a clean swab.</li>\n<li>Dried blood: absorb it with a cotton cloth or swab moistened with distilled water.</li>\n<li>Leave a part of the cloth or swab unstained as a control.</li>\n<li>Air-dry and package in clean paper or in an envelope with the corners sealed. Do not use plastic.</li>\n<li>Blood in snow or water: collect it right away so it does not dilute further. Put it in a clean, airtight container and freeze it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Blood Stains</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Air-dry stained clothing, taking care to preserve the stain pattern.</li>\n<li>Wrap the dry clothing in clean paper.</li>\n<li>Put any debris or residue that falls from the clothing in clean paper or in an envelope with the corners sealed.</li>\n<li>Objects that cannot be moved: cut a large sample with a clean, sharp instrument. Also collect an unstained sample as a control.</li>\n<li>Package in such a way that rubbing during shipping does not erase the stain. Use clean paper. Do not use plastic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Saliva, Urine, and Semen</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are collected the same way as blood and blood stains.</li>\n<li>Cigarette butts: pick them up with gloves or clean tweezers. Do not send the ash. Air-dry them.</li>\n<li>Gather the butts from the same place (for example, an ashtray) in clean paper or in an envelope with the corners sealed.</li>\n<li>Do not send the ashtray, unless a search for latent prints is requested. In that case, package it separately.</li>\n<li>Chewing gum, envelopes and postage stamps: pick them up with gloves or clean tweezers and put them in a clean envelope. Do not use plastic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Oral (Buccal) Swabs</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rub the inside of the cheeks with clean cotton swabs.</li>\n<li>Air-dry them and place them in clean paper or in an envelope with the corners sealed.</li>\n<li>They do not need refrigeration. Do not use plastic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hair</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pick it up carefully with clean tweezers so as not to damage the root tissue.</li>\n<li>Air-dry it if it is mixed with body fluids.</li>\n<li>Package each group of hairs separately in clean paper or in an envelope with the corners sealed. Do not use plastic.</li>\n<li>Refrigerate and send as soon as possible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tissues, Bones, and Teeth</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pick them up with gloves or clean tweezers.</li>\n<li>Tissues: in a clean, airtight plastic container, without formalin or formaldehyde.</li>\n<li>Teeth and bones: in clean paper or in an envelope with the corners sealed.</li>\n<li>Freeze and package with dry ice for overnight shipping.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> tissues are the only biological sample that goes in plastic. Blood tubes are refrigerated but not frozen, and are sent without dry ice. By contrast, blood in snow or water is frozen, and tissues, bones and teeth are frozen and go with dry ice.</p>\n<h4>Building Materials</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>When a building material is drilled or damaged, its debris sticks to people, clothing, tools, bags and vehicles.</li>\n<li>If possible, send the entire piece of evidence so that the laboratory examiners remove the debris.</li>\n<li>Package each piece of evidence in its own paper bag.</li>\n<li>Do not process the tools for latent prints.</li>\n<li>Collect known samples from the drilled or damaged areas for comparison.</li>\n<li>Send the known and the questioned debris separately so they are not contaminated.</li>\n<li>Send the debris in airtight containers, such as film canisters or plastic pill bottles. Do not use paper or glass.</li>\n<li>Package so that the chunks stay whole.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> objects go in paper bags, but loose debris never goes in paper, only in airtight containers.</p>\n<h4>Chemicals (General Unknowns)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>These are powders, liquids and stains of unknown origin or difficult to classify.</li>\n<li>It may not be possible to identify them fully, but they can almost always be classified in general terms.</li>\n<li>If there is a known sample, an opinion can be given as to whether the unknown substance is consistent with it.</li>\n<li>Send powders and liquids in airtight containers.</li>\n<li>Do not send large stained objects. If possible, send a small sample in a heat-sealed or zip-closure plastic bag.</li>\n<li>Collect an unstained sample as a control, package it separately and send it along with the stained evidence.</li>\n<li>If the sample cannot be cut, rub the stain with a clean cotton swab, dry or dampened with alcohol.</li>\n<li>Air-dry the swab and package it in a heat-sealed or zip-closure plastic bag. Send an unused swab as a control.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Computers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Today the examination of hardware, software, data media and peripherals is key in almost every investigation.</li>\n<li>What examiners can do:<ol>\n<li>Determine what types of files are on the computer.</li>\n<li>Compare files with known documents and files.</li>\n<li>Determine when and in what order the files were created.</li>\n<li>Recover deleted files by means of a bit stream backup.</li>\n<li>Convert files to other formats.</li>\n<li>Search for words or phrases in the files.</li>\n<li>Recover passwords.</li>\n<li>Decrypt encrypted files.</li>\n<li>Analyze the source code.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The Computer Analysis Response Team (the FBI's computer analysis team) combines investigation and laboratory support in its forensic examinations, including those involving malware.</li>\n<li>Investigators seize printouts and other copies of data, and mark and control them. That way the forensic team performs a clean examination that does not alter the original media.</li>\n<li>Critical points:<ol>\n<li>Know the type of computers and operating systems.</li>\n<li>If there is a network, know the network software, where the servers are and how many computers there are.</li>\n<li>Know whether there is encryption or passwords.</li>\n<li>Decide whether the computers and media are seized or examined on site.</li>\n<li>In general, send only the central processing units and the internal and external storage media.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Packaging for shipping:<ul>\n<li>Use a sturdy cardboard box. If possible, the original box with its custom-fitted padding.</li>\n<li>Protect with large bubble wrap or foam rubber pads.</li>\n<li>Do not use loose foam pieces: they get into the equipment and create static electricity. That can erase data or damage the boards.</li>\n<li>Close the box with strong packing tape.</li>\n<li>Pack and ship the central processing units upright. Label the outside: \"THIS END UP\".</li>\n<li>Pack disks, cartridges, tapes and hard drives so they do not move.</li>\n<li>Label the outside: \"FRAGILE, SENSITIVE ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT\" and \"KEEP AWAY FROM MAGNETS OR MAGNETIC FIELDS\".</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Appendix 6B contains a model policy for seizing computer equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> here it asks that only the central processing unit and the media be sent to the laboratory. Appendix 6B, by contrast, asks that monitors, keyboards, mice, printers and other peripherals also be seized. What is seized is one thing and what is sent is another.</p>\n<h4>Controlled Substances and Pharmaceuticals</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The examination of controlled substances can detect traces of a drug, identify it and quantify it.</li>\n<li>Send each piece of evidence in its own heat-sealed or zip-closure plastic bag.</li>\n<li>Fold clothing to preserve traces.</li>\n<li>Do not send used field test kits with the evidence.</li>\n<li>The examination of pharmaceuticals can identify their components, active ingredients, quantity and weight.</li>\n<li>When sending them to the laboratory, state the name of the drugs and the information on their use.</li>\n<li>If possible, send them in their original container.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Electronic Devices</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Data can be extracted from mobile phones and from \"interception of communication\" devices (transmitters and receivers for eavesdropping on conversations).</li>\n<li>Their operating characteristics and modifications can also be identified.</li>\n<li>Sometimes they have to be disassembled during the examination.</li>\n<li>Label the outside: \"FRAGILE, SENSITIVE ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT\" and \"KEEP AWAY FROM MAGNETS OR MAGNETIC FIELDS\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Glass</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The comparative examination tells whether glass particles came from a specific broken piece.</li>\n<li>The fracture examination tells the direction and type of the force that broke it, and the order of the shots.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Comparison</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Send samples from each window or broken piece in airtight containers, such as film canisters or plastic pill bottles. Do not use paper or glass.</li>\n<li>Laminated glass (for example, a windshield): samples from each face, labeled \"INSIDE\" and \"OUTSIDE\". Package them separately in airtight containers. Do not use paper or glass.</li>\n<li>Send the relevant clothing air-dried, each garment in its own paper bag.</li>\n<li>Look for particles in hair, skin and wounds. Send them in airtight containers. Do not use paper or glass.</li>\n<li>Look for particles in vehicles by vacuuming each area separately. Do not use adhesive tape to collect glass. Send what was vacuumed in airtight containers. Do not use paper or glass.</li>\n<li>Send the known and the questioned debris separately so they are not contaminated.</li>\n<li>Do not process the evidence for latent prints.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fracture</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Label the faces of the glass in the frame (\"INSIDE\" and \"OUTSIDE\"). Label which part of the frame it was taken from: \"TOP\", \"BOTTOM\", \"LEFT\" and \"RIGHT\".</li>\n<li>Send all the pieces. That way they can be fitted together to see the radial cracks next to and at the impact points, and it is easier to match the edges. Package each piece separately and well secured so that it does not move or break.</li>\n<li>Laminated glass: send the entire piece if possible, secured between sheets of plywood or sturdy cardboard. Do not place anything over the impact area.</li>\n<li>Do not process the evidence for latent prints.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Latent Fingerprints</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The best place to develop latent prints is the laboratory. Sometimes they must be developed at the scene.</li>\n<li>Care must be taken not to destroy them. Measures to protect them at the scene:<ol>\n<li>Photograph the prints before any treatment.</li>\n<li>Examine all the evidence with the naked eye and with a laser or an alternate light source before using another method.</li>\n<li>When using a development method, follow the manufacturer's instructions and safety warnings.</li>\n<li>Use personal protective equipment: safety glasses, mask, gloves and lab coat.</li>\n<li>Consult the FBI's <em>Processing Guide for Developing Latent Prints</em> (2001). It is a complete list of methods and protocols for developing prints.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Following that guide gives more options for developing prints and preserves the evidence in case other forensic examinations are needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Lubricants</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They include petroleum derivatives, natural fatty ester oils and polyalkylene glycol oils.</li>\n<li>Automobile fluids (engine oil, brake fluid), some cosmetics (bath oils, lotions) and some polishing products contain lubricants.</li>\n<li>They are examined in cases of sexual assault, vehicular homicide or sabotage of heavy machinery.</li>\n<li>Guidelines:<ol>\n<li>Send the entire item (for example, the clothing) if possible. Air-dry it and package each in its own paper bag.</li>\n<li>Absorb the lubricant with a clean cotton cloth or swab, and leave a part unstained as a control. Air-dry and package in a heat-sealed or zip-closure plastic bag.</li>\n<li>Package the lubricants separately in airtight containers.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Metals</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Subtype</th>\n<th>What the examination can determine</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparison</td>\n<td>Whether two metals or metal objects come from the same source or one from the other. Analyzes surface characteristics and microstructure: fractured areas, accidental damage and manufacturing marks. Reveals whether they share an origin, the manufacturing method, the mechanical properties (how the metal responds to a force or load) and the chemical composition (alloy and trace elements)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Broken or Mechanically Damaged Metal</td>\n<td>The cause of the breakage or damage. The magnitude of the force or load, how it reached the metal and in what direction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Specification Frauds and Noncompliant Materials</td>\n<td>Whether in a procurement fraud parts were replaced with lower-quality ones. Whether composition and properties meet the contract or the standards. Sometimes, the country of origin. Also the precious metal content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Burned, Heated, or Melted Metal</td>\n<td>The approximate temperature it reached, the type of heat source and whether the metal was in a short circuit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cut or Severed Metal</td>\n<td>The cutting method: sawing, shearing, milling, turning or thermal cutting. Sometimes, the heat source, such as a burner bar or an electric arc welder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metal Fragments</td>\n<td>How they were formed. Whether they were formed by an abrupt load (very brief and with very rapid deformation), whether an explosive was detonated and its detonation velocity. Often, also what object produced them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watches, Clocks, and Timers</td>\n<td>What caused them to stop or fail. Whether the timer of a device damaged by fire or explosion was on or off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lamp Bulbs</td>\n<td>Whether the bulb was lit when its glass broke or when it received a blow, as in a vehicle collision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal Components</td>\n<td>X-ray radiography shows the internal construction without destroying it, and whether there are defects, voids or foreign materials. Also the position of switches and other mechanical parts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Pending: the book gives no packaging guidelines for metals.</p>\n<h4>Paint</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The layer structure of a questioned paint can be compared with known sources.</li>\n<li>For each layer, the order, relative thickness, color, texture, number of layers and chemical composition are compared.</li>\n<li>From an original factory paint chip, the color, manufacturer, model and year of the automobile can be determined.</li>\n<li>Paint from safes, vaults, window sills and door frames transfers to tools, and vice versa. The paint on an object can be compared with that on a tool.</li>\n<li>In an accident or incident, paint transfers from one car to another, from a car to an object or from an object to a car.</li>\n<li>Collect control chips:<ul>\n<li>Take them near the damaged area, but not from it.</li>\n<li>If there is no visible damage, take them from several areas of the surface.</li>\n<li>Each layer is a point of comparison. The control must have all the layers, down to the base surface.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Three ways to obtain the control:<ol>\n<li>Cut the paint with a clean, sharp instrument.</li>\n<li>Lift or pry up loose chips.</li>\n<li>Dislodge the paint by gently tapping the opposite side of the painted surface.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Package in airtight containers such as vials or pillboxes.</li>\n<li>Do not stick the particles to adhesive tape.</li>\n<li>Do not use plastic bags, cotton or envelopes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Pepper Spray or Foam</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Oleoresin capsicum is a resin from several peppers. It is used in self-defense sprays and foams.</li>\n<li>They may also contain ultraviolet dye or tear gas. They can be analyzed for oleoresin, dye or tear gas.</li>\n<li>Guidelines:<ol>\n<li>Send the entire item (for example, the clothing) if possible. Air-dry it and package each in its own paper bag.</li>\n<li>Moisten a clean cotton cloth or swab with isopropanol (rubbing alcohol) and wipe it over the spray or foam.</li>\n<li>Prepare a second moistened cloth or swab as a control.</li>\n<li>Air-dry and package each in its own heat-sealed or zip-closure plastic bag.</li>\n<li>Send the spray cans when possible.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Polymers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are usually pieces of plastic or other artificial materials.</li>\n<li>In general, composition analysis does not reveal their origin, use or manufacturer.</li>\n<li>In hit-and-run cases, the vehicle's moldings can be compared with the plastic left on what was struck.</li>\n<li>If the molding has the manufacturer's part number, the manufacturer, make, model and year of the vehicle are known.</li>\n<li>The plastic of cable insulation and other plastics, such as buttons, can be compared with known sources.</li>\n<li>After an accident, pieces may be left at the scene, such as those from a headlight. If their edges are intact, they can be fitted together with the pieces remaining on the car.</li>\n<li>Packaging:<ul>\n<li>Send the fragments in airtight containers, such as film canisters or plastic pill bottles. Do not use cotton or paper.</li>\n<li>Package each damaged part of the vehicle separately, in zip-closure plastic bags or in boxes.</li>\n<li>Package objects (for example, clothing) that may have polymer debris or stains separately, in paper bags and not plastic.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Product Tampering</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is deliberately altering a commercial product to harm someone or to extort.</li>\n<li>Examples: tampering with medications in a medical setting or adulterating food in a supermarket.</li>\n<li>Guidelines:<ol>\n<li>Send control samples of the unadulterated product.</li>\n<li>Package and send each sample separately so they are not contaminated.</li>\n<li>Use airtight containers, such as film canisters or plastic pill bottles. Do not use paper or glass.</li>\n<li>Take care not to destroy latent prints.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Ropes and Cords</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A piece of rope can be compared with a questioned rope.</li>\n<li>Its composition, construction, color and diameter can be determined. If it has a tracer thread, the manufacturer is known.</li>\n<li>Guidelines:<ol>\n<li>Send the entire rope.</li>\n<li>If it has to be cut, indicate which end was cut when collecting it.</li>\n<li>Label the known and the questioned samples.</li>\n<li>Handle the pieces carefully so as not to contaminate them or lose traces.</li>\n<li>Send in plastic or paper bags, heat-sealed or zip-closure.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Safe Insulation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The insulation of a safe can be compared with a known source. Sometimes the manufacturer is determined.</li>\n<li>Collect samples from the damaged areas.</li>\n<li>Insulation sticks to people, clothing, tools, bags and loot, and transfers to vehicles. That is why the evidence must be sent to the laboratory and the examiners remove the debris.</li>\n<li>Package each piece of evidence in its own paper bag.</li>\n<li>Do not process the tools for latent prints.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Serial Numbers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Obliterated serial or identification numbers on metal, wood, plastic or fiberglass can often be recovered.</li>\n<li>If possible, remove the part bearing the number and note where it came from.</li>\n<li>If it cannot be removed, make a cast and send it to the laboratory. Call the laboratory to find out which casting kit to use and how.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Shoeprints and Tire Treads</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They often appear at scenes, in two- or three-dimensional form.</li>\n<li>Almost all of them, even partial ones, are useful for a forensic comparison.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Photographing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>General photos of the scene, close up and from afar, to place the impressions in it.</li>\n<li>Examination-quality photos, for maximum detail. They are taken directly above the impression, with a tripod and lighting.</li>\n<li>The goal is a detailed negative that can be enlarged to actual size.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Setting</th>\n<th>Value the book asks for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Camera</td>\n<td>Digital single-lens reflex (SLR) camera of 12 megapixels or more, or a 35 mm film body. With tripod and cable or electronic release</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>File format</td>\n<td>Uncompressed RAW, RAW with lossless compression or TIFF with lossless compression. JPEG is not accepted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensitivity</td>\n<td>ISO 400, with the right white balance to capture the true color</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mode and aperture</td>\n<td>Aperture priority or manual mode, to control depth of field. Aperture f/16 or f/22</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lens</td>\n<td>Fixed focal length, twice the camera's normal focal length</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scale</td>\n<td>A linear ruler next to the impression and in the same plane. Plus a label that ties it to the scene notes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tires</td>\n<td>A long tape measure as scale. Photos in overlapping sections until a full revolution is covered: 7 to 9 feet (2 to 3 meters)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Focus</td>\n<td>On the bottom of the impression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flash</td>\n<td>Electronic flash at a very low angle, 10 to 15 degrees above the ground, so that raking light enhances the detail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of photos</td>\n<td>Shoeprints: at least 3, moving the light around the entire impression. Tires: 4, lighting from the four sides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Files</td>\n<td>Copy them to a hard drive, CD or DVD. At least 2 copies marked as original or master, plus copies for viewing, processing and printing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Impressions in Snow</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are hard to photograph because they have little contrast. It is best to photograph them as if they were in soil.</li>\n<li>To provide contrast, spray them lightly with snow print wax or with colored spray paint.</li>\n<li>Hold the spray at least 2 or 3 feet from the impression, so that the force of the spray does not damage it.</li>\n<li>Apply little paint, at an angle of about 30 to 45 degrees, so that it touches only the high points.</li>\n<li>Painted impressions absorb heat from the sun. They must be shaded until they are photographed and cast, so they do not melt.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Casting</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the evidence itself cannot be sent, it is cast with dental stone and the cast is sent.</li>\n<li>Plaster of Paris, modeling plasters and ordinary dental plasters are not hard enough.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Guideline</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Keep the pre-measured plaster in zip-closure plastic bags. Mix it with just enough water inside the bag, until it is like pancake batter or thick cream</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Do not pour it directly onto the impression, because its weight and volume destroy the detail. Pour it beside it and let it flow in on its own. Fill the impression until it overflows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>If it is too thick, vibrate a finger or a stick on the surface so that it flows. Do not sink them more than 1/4 inch, because they damage the impression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Before it fully hardens, write on it the date, the initials of the person collecting it and other data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Leave it undisturbed for at least 20 to 30 minutes in warm weather, and longer in cold. In sand or loose soil it comes out easily; in mud or clay it may require careful digging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Let it air-dry for at least 48 hours. Package it in paper, not in plastic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Only a laboratory examiner cleans the cast</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Lifting Two-Dimensional Impressions</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lifting an impression transfers the residue or dust to a lifting film. That way it can be sent to the laboratory to be photographed and examined.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Surfaces</th>\n<th>When it works and guidelines</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrostatic lifts</td>\n<td>Porous and nonporous, without damaging the impression</td>\n<td>Dry dust impressions or residue on clean surfaces. It does not work if the impression is wet or gets wet. Follow the device's instructions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Black gelatin lifters</td>\n<td>Porous and nonporous</td>\n<td>Light-colored impressions, dry or damp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>White gelatin lifters</td>\n<td>Porous and nonporous</td>\n<td>Impressions developed with fingerprint powder, or dark enough to contrast with the white background</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>White adhesive lifters</td>\n<td>Only smooth and nonporous</td>\n<td>Impressions developed with fingerprint powders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transparent adhesive lifters</td>\n<td>Only smooth and nonporous</td>\n<td>Impressions developed with black powders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transparent tape (for example, 2-inch fingerprint tape)</td>\n<td>Only smooth and nonporous</td>\n<td>Impressions with powder, if they are then transferred to a white card</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>How to store the electrostatic film:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is easily damaged if stored improperly. Its residual charge attracts dust and dirt, and makes it stick to other surfaces.</li>\n<li>Tape one edge inside a clean, smooth paper folder. Or tape the edges inside a flat photographic paper box.</li>\n<li>Do not use low-quality cardboard boxes, such as pizza boxes. The charge attracts their dust and contaminates the impression.</li>\n<li>Objects with a dry residue impression are not wrapped or stored in plastic, because part of the impression would transfer to the plastic.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Soil</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The examination compares color, texture and composition to determine whether two soils have the same origin.</li>\n<li>Collect the samples as soon as possible, because the soil at the scene can change a great deal.</li>\n<li>Where to collect:<ul>\n<li>In the immediate area of the scene and along the logical routes of entry or escape.</li>\n<li>Where the color, texture or composition changes to the naked eye.</li>\n<li>At the same depth from which the questioned soil could have come.</li>\n<li>If possible, at the alibi locations, such as a garden or a workplace.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Mark on a map where each sample was taken.</li>\n<li>Do not remove the soil stuck to shoes, clothing and tools.</li>\n<li>Do not process the tools for latent prints.</li>\n<li>Air-dry the soil and the clothing, and package them separately in paper bags.</li>\n<li>Carefully remove the soil stuck to vehicles. Air-dry it and package it separately in paper bags.</li>\n<li>Send the known and the questioned soil separately so they are not contaminated.</li>\n<li>Send them in airtight containers, such as film canisters or plastic pill bottles. Do not use paper envelopes or glass.</li>\n<li>Package so that the chunks stay whole.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book asks that dry soil be packaged in paper bags and, at the same time, sent in airtight containers and \"not in paper envelopes\". It is inconsistent. Pending: clarify which packaging applies to each case.</p>\n<h4>Tape</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Its composition, construction and color can be compared with known sources.</li>\n<li>A torn piece can be compared with a roll of tape.</li>\n<li>Laboratories examine duct tape, vinyl electrical tape and packing tape. They may not examine cellulose acetate tape (for example, Scotch tape).</li>\n<li>Guidelines:<ol>\n<li>Whenever possible, send the tape stuck to its surface. That way fewer traces, latent prints and contact impressions are lost.</li>\n<li>If that is not possible, peel it off by hand and place it adhesive side down on a clean, colorless plastic sheet (for example, transparency film or tubular plastic on a roll).</li>\n<li>Do not place it on cardboard, paper or vinyl document sleeves.</li>\n<li>Do not deform or tear the tape when peeling it off.</li>\n<li>If it has to be cut, document each cut and put your initials. Cut in a way that the pattern is unique, for example with pinking shears.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Toolmarks</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Tools have unique microscopic characteristics, due to their manufacture and use. Those characteristics transfer to the surfaces they touch.</li>\n<li>The marks on the evidence can be compared with the recovered tools.</li>\n<li>With no questioned tool, the examination says what type of tool made the mark and whether the mark is suitable for comparison.</li>\n<li>It also includes the examination of locks and keys.</li>\n<li>Guidelines:<ol>\n<li>Take samples of the material left on the tools. Send them in airtight containers, such as film canisters or plastic pill bottles.</li>\n<li>To avoid contamination, do not bring the tool near the marked evidence.</li>\n<li>Send the tool, not test cuts or marks.</li>\n<li>Mark the ends of the evidence and indicate which end was cut when collecting it.</li>\n<li>Photos help locate the marks, but they are not useful for identifying the tool.</li>\n<li>If the marked evidence cannot be sent, make a cast and send it. Call the laboratory to find out which kit to use and how.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Toxicology</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The examination detects alcohol, drugs and poisons in biological samples and in food.</li>\n<li>It helps understand what happened in incidents and accidents involving drugs or poisons.</li>\n<li>Because there are a great many toxic substances, they are sometimes sought by families of poisons:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Volatile compounds</td>\n<td>Ethanol, methanol, isopropanol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy metals</td>\n<td>Arsenic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nonvolatile organic compounds</td>\n<td>Drugs of abuse, pharmaceuticals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Miscellaneous substances</td>\n<td>Strychnine, cyanide</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In drug-facilitated assaults, the biological evidence must include a urine sample. It is collected as soon as possible, and never more than 96 hours after the alleged drugging.</li>\n<li>The amount of sample depends on four factors: whether the toxic substance is known, the route of administration, the time between exposure and collection, and whether the people are alive or dead.</li>\n<li>Call the laboratory before sending the samples, to send the right amount.</li>\n<li>Shipping guidelines:<ol>\n<li>Each sample goes separately, labeled and sealed, in a glass tube, a plastic cup or a heat-sealed or zip-closure plastic bag.</li>\n<li>Put \"BIOHAZARD\" on the inner and outer containers.</li>\n<li>Refrigerate or freeze the samples during storage and shipping, so they do not deteriorate.</li>\n<li>Package without breakage, leaks or contamination.</li>\n<li>Send a copy of the incident report.</li>\n<li>Describe the person's symptoms at the time of the incident.</li>\n<li>State the drugs, known or suspected, that the person took or had been prescribed.</li>\n<li>Describe any environmental exposure to toxic substances, known or suspected.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Video</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authenticity examinations:</strong> tell whether a recording is original, continuous, unaltered and consistent with the device that recorded it.</li>\n<li><strong>Enhancement examinations:</strong> seek the maximum clarity of the video signal, with video processors and time-base correctors.</li>\n<li>Enhanced prints can be made from the video images.</li>\n<li>Audio and video signals can be combined into a single recording.</li>\n<li>Special effects, such as a mosaic, can be added to hide a person's identity.</li>\n<li>Write-protect the original recording and never use pause when viewing it.</li>\n<li>When sending originals to a laboratory, indicate where the images are on the recording and describe them.</li>\n<li>Label the outside: \"FRAGILE, SENSITIVE ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT\" or \"FRAGILE, SENSITIVE AUDIO/VIDEO MEDIA\", and also \"KEEP AWAY FROM MAGNETS OR MAGNETIC FIELDS\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> this section repeats almost all the guidelines in \"Audio and Video Recordings\".</p>\n<h4>Wood</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The examination can match faces, ends and fractures, and determine the species of the wood.</li>\n<li>It also compares wood particles found on clothing, vehicles or other objects with the wood at the scene.</li>\n<li>Send the wood in plastic or paper bags.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Review Table: Packaging According to the Book</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Packaging</th>\n<th>Types of evidence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clean paper or envelope with sealed corners; never plastic</td>\n<td>Cloths or swabs with blood; bloodstained clothing and its debris; saliva, urine and semen; cigarette butts; chewing gum, envelopes and postage stamps (clean envelope); buccal swabs; hair; teeth and bones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrapped in paper or in a paper bag</td>\n<td>Clothing with gunshot residue (each garment separately); objects with construction debris; clothing with glass; whole objects with lubricant or pepper spray; clothing with polymer debris; objects with safe insulation; soil and clothing with soil; shoeprint casts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airtight container (film canister, plastic pill bottle); never paper or glass</td>\n<td>Construction debris; glass samples, particles and vacuumings; soil (nor paper envelopes); polymer fragments (nor cotton); samples of tampered products; material left on tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airtight container (no further detail)</td>\n<td>Chemical powders and liquids; lubricants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vials or pillboxes; never plastic bags, cotton, envelopes or adhesive tape</td>\n<td>Paint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heat-sealed or zip-closure plastic bag</td>\n<td>Small samples and swabs of chemicals; lubricant swabs; pepper spray swabs; controlled substances; toxicology samples (also glass tube or plastic cup)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Airtight plastic container without formalin</td>\n<td>Tissues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zip-closure plastic bags or boxes</td>\n<td>Damaged vehicle parts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Plastic or paper (either)</td>\n<td>Ropes and cords; wood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clean, airtight container, frozen</td>\n<td>Blood in snow or water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Never plastic, for other reasons</td>\n<td>Objects with a dry residue impression (the impression transfers to the plastic); storage media (static, according to Appendix 6B)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Review Table: Temperature</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n<th>Guideline</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Blood tubes from a person</td>\n<td>Refrigerate, do not freeze. Cold packs, not dry ice. Send immediately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blood in snow or water</td>\n<td>Freeze in an airtight container</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buccal swabs</td>\n<td>Do not need refrigeration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hair</td>\n<td>Refrigerate and send as soon as possible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tissues, bones and teeth</td>\n<td>Freeze and send with dry ice, overnight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Toxicology samples</td>\n<td>Refrigerate or freeze during storage and shipping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organic materials in general</td>\n<td>Time and temperature deteriorate them: take measures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computer equipment in transit (Appendix 6B)</td>\n<td>Avoid extreme heat and cold</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Review Table: Shipping Labels</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Label</th>\n<th>When it is used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"BIOHAZARD\"</td>\n<td>Inner container with biological hazard; bloody clothing and toxicology samples (inner and outer); blood tubes (outer); containers for contaminated sharps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"LATENT\"</td>\n<td>Inner container when latent prints are to be searched for</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"KEEP IN A COOL DRY PLACE\" and \"REFRIGERATE ON ARRIVAL\"</td>\n<td>Outside of blood tubes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"FRAGILE, SENSITIVE ELECTRONIC EQUIPMENT\"</td>\n<td>Computers, electronic devices, audio and video recordings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"FRAGILE, SENSITIVE AUDIO/VIDEO MEDIA\"</td>\n<td>Audio and video recordings (in place of the previous one)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"KEEP AWAY FROM MAGNETS OR MAGNETIC FIELDS\"</td>\n<td>Computers, electronic devices, audio and video recordings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"THIS END UP\"</td>\n<td>Computer central processing units, which are shipped upright</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"ORM-D, Cartridges, Small Arms\"</td>\n<td>Outside of the live ammunition shipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"INSIDE\" and \"OUTSIDE\"</td>\n<td>Faces of laminated glass and faces of the glass in the frame</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"TOP\", \"BOTTOM\", \"LEFT\" and \"RIGHT\"</td>\n<td>Part of the frame the glass was taken from</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"investigations-6-e4\">6.4 Incident Scene Safety</h3><ul>\n<li>It covers the hazards, precautions and personal protective equipment (PPE) for working common scenes.</li>\n<li>Security personnel must consult environmental and occupational health and safety laws, and waste disposal rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Application to security:</strong> security managers and heads of investigation units must include personal safety measures in contracts for outsourced investigation services that involve collecting or handling evidence.</li>\n<li>Neglecting the safety of contracted personnel exposes the organization to liability and reputational damage if a contractor is injured.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.4.1 Routes of Exposure</h4>\n<p>Anyone who works in or near a contaminated environment must know the routes by which a hazard enters the body and harms it.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Route</th>\n<th>How it occurs</th>\n<th>Prevention according to the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Inhalation</td>\n<td>The substance enters the respiratory system. It can come as dust, aerosol, smoke, vapor, gas or fume. Solids and liquids are also dangerous if they give off vapors, mists or fumes</td>\n<td>Good work practices and adequate ventilation. Respiratory protection is mandatory where there are airborne contaminants. Only certified personnel may use it and work in those areas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skin contact</td>\n<td>Direct contact or absorption. Severity depends on the concentration and the exposure time. What is absorbed circulates through the body and causes systemic effects: dizziness, tremors, nausea, blurred vision, liver and kidney damage, shock or collapse</td>\n<td>Personal protective equipment: gloves, safety glasses, goggles, face shields and protective clothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ingestion</td>\n<td>The substance enters through the mouth. It can seriously damage the mouth, throat and digestive system</td>\n<td>Always wash hands before eating, smoking or applying makeup. Do not bring food, drink or cigarettes into areas that may be contaminated, even if protection is worn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Injection</td>\n<td>Needle stick or wound from glass, metal or another contaminated sharp object. The contaminant passes directly into the blood and spreads quickly</td>\n<td>Take extreme care with sharp objects. Always wear work gloves</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>6.4.2 Safety</h4>\n<p>The book covers five areas: bloodborne pathogens, chemicals, light sources, confined spaces and X-rays.</p>\n<p><strong>Bloodborne-Pathogen Safety</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the US, the standards of the Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA, the federal occupational safety and health agency) are in Title 29, section 1910.1030, of the Code of Federal Regulations (CFR): 29 CFR 1910.1030.</li>\n<li>The basis of the standard is the concept of universal precautions.</li>\n<li>It requires treating all blood, body fluid or other potentially infectious material as if it were infected.</li>\n<li>Diseases it cites: hepatitis B virus (HBV), hepatitis C virus (HCV) and human immunodeficiency virus (HIV).</li>\n<li>Measures to avoid direct contact:<ol>\n<li>Use barriers, such as disposable gloves, coveralls and shoe covers, if contact is possible.</li>\n<li>Change gloves if they tear, are punctured or cease to work as a barrier.</li>\n<li>Use adequate eye and face protection.</li>\n<li>Remove gloves and the rest of the equipment without contaminating the skin or clothing. Wash hands afterward.</li>\n<li>Do not eat, drink, smoke or apply makeup where there is blood, fluids or other potentially infectious materials, even if protective equipment is worn.</li>\n<li>Do not bend, recap, remove or handle needles or other contaminated sharp objects.</li>\n<li>Place contaminated sharp objects in closable, airtight, puncture-resistant containers for transport or disposal. Label them \"BIOHAZARD\".</li>\n<li>Decontaminate equipment after use. Use household bleach diluted 1:10 and prepared each day, 70% isopropyl alcohol or another suitable disinfectant.</li>\n<li>Noncorrosive disinfectants are available for sale. Allow enough contact time to fully disinfect.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>In addition, engineering controls and prudent practices reduce or eliminate exposure.</li>\n<li>Examples of engineering controls: puncture-resistant containers for storing and disposing of sharp objects; paint stirrers and long-handled mirrors to find and retrieve evidence from narrow or hidden spaces.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chemical Safety</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Hazard</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Flammable or combustible</td>\n<td>Gasoline, acetone, ether</td>\n<td>Ignite easily with air and an ignition source, such as a spark or a flame</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explosives</td>\n<td>Nitroglycerin, nitroglycerin-based dynamite</td>\n<td>Over time they deteriorate and become chemically unstable. All are sensitive to heat, shock and friction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pyrophoric</td>\n<td>Phosphorus, sodium, barium</td>\n<td>Liquids or solids. Ignite on their own in air at below 130 °F (54 °C), without an ignition source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oxidizers</td>\n<td>Nitrates, hydrogen peroxide, concentrated sulfuric acid</td>\n<td>Release oxygen and fuel combustion. Do not store them with flammables or combustibles, or with substances that accelerate their decomposition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corrosives</td>\n<td>(the book gives no examples of substances)</td>\n<td>Destroy living tissue and objects such as wood and steel. The damage depends on the concentration and the duration of contact</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the explosives row, the book gives as an example that ether forms peroxides around the mouth of the container. But ether is listed among the flammables, not the explosives.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the US, manufacturers, distributors and importers must provide a Safety Data Sheet for each hazardous chemical.</li>\n<li>The sheet gives information on the hazards, how to dispose of it, how to package and ship it, and how to act in an emergency. Other countries also require it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Light-Source Safety</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>With ultraviolet light, lasers and other light sources, protect the eyes from direct and indirect exposure.</li>\n<li>Not all laser beams are visible. Direct or reflected light can cause irreversible eye damage.</li>\n<li>Also avoid prolonged skin exposure.</li>\n<li>All personnel nearby must wear glasses suited to that light source.</li>\n<li>Goggles must have enough protective material and fit well, so that no light enters from any angle.</li>\n<li>In the US, they must bear the mark of the American National Standards Institute (ANSI) certifying eye protection.</li>\n<li>Laser glasses must have the optical density suited to the laser's maximum wavelength.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Confined Space Safety</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Confined space:</strong> an enclosed area large enough for a person to enter and work in, but with limited or difficult entry and exit.</li>\n<li>Examples: sewers, open pits, tank cars and vats. They are not designed for continuous occupancy.</li>\n<li>Hazards: toxic gases, explosive or oxygen-deficient atmospheres, electrical hazards and materials that can engulf the person entering.</li>\n<li>They must always be considered dangerous.</li>\n<li>Do not enter until a trained person evaluates the space and, if necessary, an entry permit is issued.</li>\n<li>Monitor the atmosphere continuously with a calibrated direct-reading instrument. It measures oxygen, carbon monoxide, flammable gases and vapors, and toxic contaminants.</li>\n<li>Record the periodic monitor readings.</li>\n<li>Only personnel certified for confined spaces may work inside. Rescue services must be available immediately.</li>\n<li>Mandatory practices:<ol>\n<li>Never enter before identifying and documenting the atmospheric, engulfment, mechanical and electrical hazards.</li>\n<li>Ventilate, without the ventilation equipment interfering with entry, exit or rescue.</li>\n<li>Set up barriers that warn unauthorized persons and protect those entering from external hazards.</li>\n<li>Maintain constant communication between the entrant and the attendants watching from outside.</li>\n<li>Have backup communication ready before entering.</li>\n<li>Use the right equipment: self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA), full-body harness and head protection.</li>\n<li>Never attempt a rescue unless you are part of the designated rescue team.</li>\n<li>Have personnel certified in first aid and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) on site. Rescue services must also be available immediately.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Reference standard: OSHA, Permit-Required Confined Spaces, 29 CFR 1910.146.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>X-Ray Safety</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Portable handheld X-ray machines are used to see what is inside suspicious packages. At the scene they pose a risk of radiation exposure.</li>\n<li>To work safely:<ol>\n<li>Shield the device, the suspicious object and the operator.</li>\n<li>Remove all nonessential personnel from the X-ray field.</li>\n<li>Limit the time personnel spend in the area.</li>\n<li>Wear the dosimeters assigned for X-ray radiation.</li>\n<li>Have and follow standard operating procedures, with adequate training according to federal and state standards.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.4.3 Personal Protective Equipment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>At every scene, equipment is chosen based on a hazard-risk assessment done by trained and qualified personnel.</li>\n<li>That assessment identifies the possible contaminants and the hazards of each product.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hand Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The glove is chosen according to the material handled and its hazards. The manufacturer provides the detailed information.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Glove material</th>\n<th>Protects against</th>\n<th>Limitations or notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nitrile</td>\n<td>Acids, alkaline solutions, hydraulic fluid, photographic solutions, fuels, lubricants, aromatics, petroleum and chlorinated solvents</td>\n<td>Some resistance to cuts and snags</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neoprene</td>\n<td>Oil, grease, acids, solvents, alkalis, bases and most refrigerants</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)</td>\n<td>Alkalis, oils and limited concentrations of nitric and chromic acid</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latex (natural rubber)</td>\n<td>Mild acids, caustics, detergents, germicides and ketone solutions</td>\n<td>Swells and degrades with gasoline or kerosene. Degrades with prolonged excessive heat or direct sun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Powder-free, reduced protein</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Reduce the risk of developing latex allergy. A person who is allergic to latex can usually use nitrile or neoprene</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Glove use guidelines:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Check that they have no holes, punctures or tears before putting them on. Remove rings and other objects that could puncture them.</li>\n<li>With heavily contaminated materials, double glove.</li>\n<li>Change them if they tear, are punctured or cease to work as a barrier.</li>\n<li>Remove disposable ones by pulling on the cuffs to turn them inside out. That way the skin and clothing are not contaminated. Throw them in the designated containers and do not reuse them.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Eye Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use safety glasses and goggles when handling biological, chemical and radioactive materials.</li>\n<li>Face shields protect better if there is a risk of splashes or flying debris. They are always used together with safety glasses or goggles.</li>\n<li>Anyone who wears contact lenses must wear safety glasses or goggles. If a chemical splashes the eye, it is hard to remove the lens to flush it, and the contaminant can become trapped behind it.</li>\n<li>Protective glasses are worn over prescription glasses. Prescription safety glasses can also be made.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Foot Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Footwear that covers and protects the entire foot is essential.</li>\n<li>Use protective footwear if there is a risk of falling or rolling objects, objects piercing the sole or electrical hazards.</li>\n<li>Standards recognized by OSHA: ASTM International F2412 and F2413 (ASTM is a technical standards body; the book does not explain the acronym).</li>\n<li>In some cases, waterproof shoe covers protect the footwear and prevent carrying contaminants out of the scene.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Respiratory Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some scenes, such as bombings and clandestine labs, produce harmful fumes and other airborne contaminants. There, respiratory protection must be used.</li>\n<li>In the US, whenever respirators are used, compliance with the standard 29 CFR 1910.134, Respiratory Protection, is mandatory.</li>\n<li>Five critical elements for using respirators safely:<ol>\n<li>A written program.</li>\n<li>Training.</li>\n<li>Medical evaluation.</li>\n<li>Fit testing.</li>\n<li>A respirator maintenance program.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Head Protection</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wear a protective helmet at scenes with structural damage, or where there may be.</li>\n<li>Standard recognized by OSHA: ANSI Z89.1-2014, Requirements for Industrial Head Protection.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Review Table: Standards Cited in the Chapter</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Standard or body</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bloodborne pathogens</td>\n<td>OSHA, 29 CFR 1910.1030</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Permit-required confined spaces</td>\n<td>OSHA, 29 CFR 1910.146</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Respiratory protection</td>\n<td>29 CFR 1910.134</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protective footwear</td>\n<td>ASTM F2412 and F2413</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protective helmet</td>\n<td>ANSI Z89.1-2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protective glasses</td>\n<td>ANSI compliance mark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shipping of hazardous materials</td>\n<td>US Department of Transportation and International Air Transport Association</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hazardous waste</td>\n<td>Environmental Protection Agency, RCRA (\"cradle-to-grave\")</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chemical sheets</td>\n<td>Safety Data Sheets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Exam note (text versus references):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The text cites \"ANSI Z89.1-2014, Requirements for Industrial Head Protection\". The references cite \"ANSI/ISEA Z89.1-2014 R2019, American National Standard for Industrial Head Protection\".</li>\n<li>The references cite the footwear standard as \"ASTM F2413-1a\". Possible typo for \"F2413-18a\", like the \"F2412-18a\" that appears next to it.</li>\n<li>The references include 29 CFR 1910.147 (lockout/tagout) and 49 CFR 172.101 (hazardous materials table). The chapter text does not mention them.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-6-e5\">Key Terms</h3><p>Evidence · direct evidence · indirect evidence · real and material evidence · circumstantial evidence · hearsay · dying declaration · statements against self-interest · res gestae · materiality · competency · case law · discovery · staged scene · chain of custody · oral evidence · call taker · non-suggestive · preliminary investigator · canvass · union environment · cover up · rapport · open-ended questions · closed-ended questions · leading questions · follow-up investigator · cooperative witness · social dynamics · witness tampering · documentary evidence · questioned document examination · exemplars · undictated writing · traced signature · simulated signature · freehand signature · typewriting · impact printer · cold case · photocopies · automatic document feeder · photogrammetry · watermark · indented writing · polyester film encapsulation · ink analysis · carbon film ribbon · checkwriter · embossed impression · rubber stamp · physical evidence · corpus delicti · associative evidence · identifying evidence · tracing evidence · trace evidence · impression evidence · person in charge · photographer · sketch preparer · evidence recorder · scene supervisor · administrative log · preliminary survey · initial walkthrough · transient evidence · contrived scene · narrative · photographic log · overall, medium, close-up · scale · sketch · scale disclaimer · search pattern · grid · strip or lane · spiral · known standards · evidence custodian · final survey · release · evidence examination request · tamper-evident tape · filament tape · BIOHAZARD · LATENT · ORM-D · Declaration of Dangerous Goods · Resource Conservation and Recovery Act (RCRA) · cradle-to-grave · rifling marks · gauge · shotshell · wadding · National Integrated Ballistics Information Network · gunshot residue · muzzle-to-garment distance · write-protect · first-generation copy · DNA · EDTA · purple-top tube · gray-top tube · sodium fluoride · buccal swab · formalin · dry ice · cold packs · general unknowns · bit stream backup · Computer Analysis Response Team · malware · sterile examination · interception of communication devices · radial cracks · laminated glass · alternate light source · Processing Guide for Developing Latent Prints · polyalkylene glycol · specification fraud · impulsive loading · detonation velocity · X-ray radiography · substrate · control sample · oleoresin capsicum · polymers · hit-and-run · product tampering · tracer · safe insulation · serial number restoration · cast · examination-quality photographs · oblique lighting · snow print wax · dental stone · plaster of Paris · electrostatic lift · gelatin lifter · adhesive lifter · cellulose acetate tape · pinking shears · toolmarks · toxicology · drug-facilitated assault · authenticity examination · enhancement examination · time-base corrector · incident scene safety · personal protective equipment (PPE) · routes of exposure · inhalation · skin contact · ingestion · injection · bloodborne pathogens · universal precautions · HBV · HCV · HIV · engineering controls · flammable · explosive · pyrophoric · oxidizer · corrosive · Safety Data Sheet · optical density · confined space · confined space permit · attendant · SCBA · CPR · x-ray safety · hazard-risk assessment · nitrile · neoprene · PVC · latex · powder-free gloves · face shield · fit testing · OSHA · 29 CFR 1910.1030 · 29 CFR 1910.146 · 29 CFR 1910.134 · ASTM F2412/F2413 · ANSI Z89.1-2014</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Introduction — General Concepts of Evidence"},{"id":"e1","t":"6.1 Oral Evidence"},{"id":"e2","t":"6.2 Documentary Evidence"},{"id":"e3","t":"6.3 Physical Evidence"},{"id":"e4","t":"6.4 Incident Scene Safety"},{"id":"e5","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"6.1 Oral Evidence","h":"the book's list ends in \"and\" after the third point, as if a fourth principle were missing. The text does not include it and develops only three. Pending: confirm in the printed book whether a fourth exists."},{"s":"6.3 Physical Evidence","h":"the book calls indirect evidence \"circumstantial\" (in the introduction) and also associative evidence (in 6.3). Fingerprints and bites appear in more than one category. Distinguish tracing (used to locate the suspect) from trace (microscopic remnants)."},{"s":"6.3 Physical Evidence","h":"the book says \"spare cartridges\" and asks that they be picked up by the \"open end\". Possible typo for \"spent\" (already fired casings). Exam note: here it says \"do not mark the item\". By contrast, the general rule asks to mark with initials and Appendix 6B asks to mark each item on the item itself."},{"s":"6.3 Physical Evidence","h":"tissues are the only biological sample that goes in plastic. Blood tubes are refrigerated but not frozen, and are sent without dry ice. By contrast, blood in snow or water is frozen, and tissues, bones and teeth are frozen and go with dry ice."},{"s":"6.3 Physical Evidence","h":"objects go in paper bags, but loose debris never goes in paper, only in airtight containers."},{"s":"6.3 Physical Evidence","h":"here it asks that only the central processing unit and the media be sent to the laboratory. Appendix 6B, by contrast, asks that monitors, keyboards, mice, printers and other peripherals also be seized. What is seized is one thing and what is sent is another."},{"s":"6.3 Physical Evidence","h":"the book asks that dry soil be packaged in paper bags and, at the same time, sent in airtight containers and \"not in paper envelopes\". It is inconsistent. Pending: clarify which packaging applies to each case."},{"s":"6.3 Physical Evidence","h":"this section repeats almost all the guidelines in \"Audio and Video Recordings\"."},{"s":"6.4 Incident Scene Safety","h":"in the explosives row, the book gives as an example that ether forms peroxides around the mouth of the container. But ether is listed among the flammables, not the explosives."}]},"investigations-7":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-7-e0\">Chapter Introduction — Basic Concepts (pp. 257–258)</h3><ul>\n<li>Testimonial evidence is usually a large part of the evidence presented at trial.</li>\n<li>Because of the incidents in their work, security personnel are frequently called to testify.</li>\n<li>Where they may testify (complete list):<ul>\n<li>Regulatory hearings, formal or informal.</li>\n<li>Interviews with government authorities.</li>\n<li>Formal sworn statements (depositions).</li>\n<li>Before a grand jury.</li>\n<li>Civil or criminal courts of any level.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>They are usually accompanied by their counsel. During the testimony, the attorney may not be able to help them or intervene.</li>\n<li>Testifying in court is intimidating. That is why it is advisable to know the rules and the processes.</li>\n<li>Laws vary between countries, and even within the same country. As an example, the book follows two US federal codes: the Federal Code of Civil Procedures and the Federal Code of Criminal Procedures.<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> possible typo in the names. The official names in the US are usually \"Federal Rules of Civil/Criminal Procedure\".</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Testimony must always be truthful. It must also follow the court's guidelines and the attorney's strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Fact witness</th>\n<th>Opinion witness</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Other names</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Expert witness, forensic witness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What they testify to</td>\n<td>Only what they perceived: what they saw, heard, smelled, touched or tasted</td>\n<td>An opinion on the matter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Requirement</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Demonstrate training, education and experience in a specific subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basis</td>\n<td>Their senses</td>\n<td>Analysis of the evidence with accepted scientific standards and academic research methods</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Security personnel can be either.</li>\n<li><strong>Hearsay:</strong> telling what another person said. It may or may not be admissible.</li>\n<li>Typical exception to hearsay: the <strong>dying declaration</strong>. For example, the last words of a victim who identifies the attacker.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-7-e1\">7.1 The Journey</h3><ul>\n<li>Testimony is the end of a process that began with an earlier incident.</li>\n<li>They may be asked to testify from the very moment up to years later, even if they have already left the job.</li>\n<li>From their first day in the position they must know that the verdict may depend on four things: their observations, reports or findings, plus their demeanor and their ability to explain the facts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.1.1 Procedure</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every security incident must be treated as if it were going to end up in court.</li>\n<li>That requires following established procedures or, at a minimum, consistent personal procedures.</li>\n<li><strong>Marking evidence:</strong> initials, date and tracking or report number. If possible, always in the same place on the item.</li>\n<li>If the procedure was not followed, they must know why and be ready to explain it.</li>\n<li>Causes of deviation the book gives: the weather, the urgency of the situation and the type of evidence being marked (the book leaves the list open).</li>\n<li>The witness must know well the evidence they say they marked.</li>\n<li>They must examine it before taking the stand for two reasons: its appearance may have changed and it may be presented in a different order than expected.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.1.2 Report</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Give the attorney all relevant reports and notes to review.</li>\n<li>It is also advisable to explain to the attorney what they will testify to.</li>\n<li>The witness must know the facts well, but without carrying a script of the testimony.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.1.3 Testimony Preparation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Preparation begins long before litigation is even considered.</li>\n<li>It begins when recording the facts and impressions of the incident in a report or notes, accurately and on time.</li>\n<li>Written documentation is much more credible than memory, because time and subsequent events weaken and alter memory.</li>\n<li>Shortly before testifying, preparation may include:<ul>\n<li>Visiting the site.</li>\n<li>Reviewing notes, reports, photos and diagrams.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The case is reviewed thoroughly with the prosecuting attorney to see its strengths and weaknesses.</li>\n<li>The attorney wants to make sure the testimony supports the case.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Parol / \"spoken word\"</td>\n<td>Another name for oral testimonial evidence presented at trial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-trial meeting</td>\n<td>The opposing parties meet and exchange the information each believes necessary for its position. It is the norm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duces tecum</td>\n<td>Request between the parties for all tangible evidence to be produced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interrogatories</td>\n<td>Written questions that the parties exchange</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prior interviews or interrogations</td>\n<td>May be conducted with witnesses, the complainant and the defendant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deposition</td>\n<td>Formal sworn statement. It is admissible at trial</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Remote deposition:</strong> it used to be done in person. The COVID-19 pandemic made remote ones more common.</li>\n<li>For a remote deposition, one must prepare just as for an in-person one:<ul>\n<li>Dress professionally.</li>\n<li>Sit in front of a neutral background.</li>\n<li>Answer as well as possible.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-7-e2\">7.2 Courtroom Participants</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Participant</th>\n<th>Role according to the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Judges</td>\n<td>Public officials who decide questions of law. They are the final authority for regulating the litigation. In every trial they enforce procedural rules. In a <strong>bench trial</strong> (trial without a jury) they also decide the outcome: guilty or innocent, liable or not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Juries</td>\n<td>Selected citizens (jurors) who decide based on the facts presented. They are not professionals at judging facts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Counsel (attorneys, lawyers)</td>\n<td>Advocates hired to represent their client as well as possible, within the procedural rules and with integrity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Witnesses</td>\n<td>Of fact or of opinion (expert or forensic). Their testimony can support or contradict other evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>7.2.1 Judges</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>See the table. They decide questions of law, enforce procedure and, in a bench trial, decide the merits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.2.2 Juries</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Their names usually come from the voter rolls of the jurisdiction.</li>\n<li>After being sworn, the candidates go through <strong>voir dire</strong>. In it, the attorneys, the judge or both question them to see whether they are fit for that trial.</li>\n<li>Once chosen, the judge instructs them on:<ul>\n<li>What they may consider evidence: relevancy, materiality and competency.</li>\n<li>Giving different weight to each piece of evidence.</li>\n<li>Delivering the verdict within the applicable standard of proof.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.2.3 Counsel</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It may suit the attorney to point out contradictions between what was stated before trial and what was stated at trial.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.2.4 Witnesses</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Example: someone with a lot of experience in automobile painting may be recognized as an expert in the investigation of an accident.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-7-e3\">7.3 Testifying</h3><ul>\n<li>The employee can improve their effectiveness as a witness by learning about the points in 7.3.1 through 7.3.6.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3.1 Pre-Testimony Review</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In any forum, before testifying:<ul>\n<li>Review their notes.</li>\n<li>Talk with the attorney about their testimony, including their doubts about the weak points of the case.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Each attorney has their own ideas about:<ul>\n<li>Strategy.</li>\n<li>How to present witnesses.</li>\n<li>The form of the answers.</li>\n<li>What to say on direct examination and what to leave for cross-examination (the one conducted by the opposing attorney).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The security employee is usually just one piece of the attorney's strategy.</li>\n<li>Some attorneys want short answers without superlatives. Others prefer long, highly descriptive answers.</li>\n<li>In all cases: answer only what is asked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A good pre-testimony review examines three things:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>What to review</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Incident notes and reports</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Evidence that is available, accessible and recoverable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Documentary proof of subsequent actions or hearings</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>If physical evidence will be used, the employee should examine it in person at the pre-trial conference.</li>\n<li>It is the only way to verify that the evidence exists in full, as described by the documents and the evidence tags.</li>\n<li>They must be ready to testify about how the evidence was collected and about the chain of custody.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3.2 Incident Site and Courtroom</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Incident site:</strong> visit it, ideally under conditions similar to those of the incident. The book cites: time of day, time of year, lighting and weather.</li>\n<li>Benefits of the visit:<ul>\n<li>Clarifies points in the report.</li>\n<li>Puts them in perspective.</li>\n<li>Greatly improves recall.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Courtroom:</strong> visit it before testifying. Especially if it is their first time or their first in that country.</li>\n<li>What to observe on the visit:<ul>\n<li>The testimony of other witnesses.</li>\n<li>The style of the different attorneys.</li>\n<li>What the judge does.</li>\n<li>How the jury behaves.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>That way the \"mystery\" surrounding trials disappears.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3.3 Public Place Discussions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not discuss the case in public, or with anyone who is not the attorney on your side.</li>\n<li>If a witness gives opinions in front of other witnesses, it creates two risks:<ul>\n<li>Problems of <strong>impeachment</strong> (discrediting the witness), for example if their words are misinterpreted.</li>\n<li>Revealing their testimony prematurely to the opposing party.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Also do not comment or post about the case on social media or news websites.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3.4 Demeanor and Attire</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Avoid</th>\n<th>Seek</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stiffness</td>\n<td>Being personable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arguing (argumentative)</td>\n<td>Being charming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arrogance</td>\n<td>Professionalism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evasiveness</td>\n<td>Courtesy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indifference (uncaring)</td>\n<td>Seriousness and sincerity (earnest)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Taking questions personally</td>\n<td>Showing knowledge</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Security personnel must also convey self-confidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Attire:</strong> suited to the jury and the jurisdiction.<ul>\n<li>The uniform may appeal to the jury or not.</li>\n<li>A business suit may convey arrogance.</li>\n<li>Whatever the style: clean, tidy, respectable and without ostentation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3.5 Questioning and Response Techniques</h4>\n<p><strong>Opposing counsel's traps, in the deposition and at trial (source: McElheny 2006):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Trap</th>\n<th>How to respond</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gauging whether the witness will withstand tough cross-examination</td>\n<td>Go in prepared. Remember that they probably know more than the attorney about security response and reporting procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Summarizing the witness's answer in the attorney's words</td>\n<td>Do not answer with a simple \"yes\". Clarify that the summary is accurate only in very general terms and does not reflect what was said. The attorney's words can change the meaning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skipping the rules of evidence by seeking hearsay, conversations, rumors and opinions</td>\n<td>Be aware that it is a trap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Asking for the witness's notes</td>\n<td>See rules on notes, below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trick questions</td>\n<td>Correct the false premises before answering</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rules on notes:</strong><ul>\n<li>Before testifying, ask the attorney whether to use notes and what to do if opposing counsel asks for them.</li>\n<li>If notes are used, opposing counsel may be entitled to receive a copy beforehand.</li>\n<li>Notes taken to the stand must be well organized.</li>\n<li>If opposing counsel takes them for a moment, do not continue testifying until they are returned.</li>\n<li>If they put them out of order, politely ask the judge for time to put them in order.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Trick question case:</strong> in a case over inadequate security, the attorney asks what the guard did first \"when he was working alone that night and Mary signaled to him in the parking lot to report an assault\".<ul>\n<li>Perhaps it was daytime, there were several guards or he intervened on his own initiative.</li>\n<li>If he only answers \"I checked whether she was hurt and called an ambulance\", he lets the falsehoods pass without clarifying them.</li>\n<li>Lesson: those falsehoods can influence the jury. They must be corrected.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>If they do not understand the question:</strong> ask the attorney to speak louder or to clarify it.</li>\n<li><strong>If they still do not understand it</strong>, they have three options:<ul>\n<li>Say that they are confused.</li>\n<li>Ask whether they can give a qualified answer (limited and explained).</li>\n<li>Say that they cannot answer with a yes or a no.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Never answer the question you think they are asking you.</li>\n<li>The attorneys and the judge may comment. The witness does what the judge says.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other rules for answering:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Never guess or offer information that has not been requested.</li>\n<li>If they do not know or do not remember, say so or ask whether they may consult their notes.</li>\n<li>Repeating \"I don't remember\" over and over damages credibility.</li>\n<li>If they are asked things they have no reason to know, their attorney will usually object.</li>\n<li>Do not nod or shake the head until the opposing attorney's question is complete.</li>\n<li>Keep up to date in the field of security. Ignoring a common and relevant practice cited by opposing counsel would make them look uninformed, unprofessional and unconvincing.</li>\n<li>Show confidence, speak clearly and give positive, direct and definitive answers whenever possible.</li>\n<li>They may take their time, but without obviously delaying or denying the obvious.</li>\n<li>If they use technical terms the jury may not know, explain them right away in simple words.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Jury biases:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Juries decide on facts, but they are influenced by their personality, culture and life experience.</li>\n<li>Fact (Anwar 2012): in criminal cases, many studies find that Black jurors convict less than white jurors, especially when the defendant is Black.</li>\n<li>Because of this \"cross-race effect\", it matters to build good rapport with the jury and show competence.</li>\n<li>Other characteristics that can bias the jury: gender, age, religion, ethnicity and sexual orientation.</li>\n<li>Observe the jury's reactions. If a negative reaction is noticed, adjust the way of speaking.</li>\n<li>Possible causes of a negative reaction:<ul>\n<li>Body language conveys laziness, lack of professionalism or coldness.</li>\n<li>The testimony is too long or has too much jargon.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Eye contact (Broda-Bahm 2015):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Before</th>\n<th>Today</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sometimes it was advised not to look at the jury</td>\n<td>Most experts believe that eye contact keeps the jury attentive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>While being questioned, look at the attorney.</li>\n<li>While answering, divide your gaze between the jury and the attorney.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Body language and response strategy (complete list, 9 points):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Look comfortable. Do not slouch, rest the head on the hand, chew gum, touch earrings or mustache, or fidget in the chair.</li>\n<li>Ask to go to the restroom or for a glass of water if needed.</li>\n<li>Speak with authority, without arrogance.</li>\n<li>Answer directly and do not offer anything that is not asked.</li>\n<li>Admit having met with the attorney.</li>\n<li>Admit having discussed the case with others, if true and those conversations are not privileged.</li>\n<li>Stay calm even if opposing counsel seems to lose their composure.</li>\n<li>Stick to the answer even if the attorney repeats the question many times, each time with more incredulity.</li>\n<li>Force opposing counsel to ask simple questions, not several questions in one.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the text the source is spelled \"McElheny 2006\". In the reference list it appears as \"McElhaney, J. W. 2006\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3.6 Inconsistencies</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Attorneys are trained to look for inconsistencies and exploit them.</li>\n<li>Four types that can make the jury believe the witness is lying:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Inconsistency between…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Parts of the same statement by the witness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Different statements by the same witness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The witness's statement and what others said</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The witness's statement and other evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"investigations-7-e4\">7.4 Expert Witnesses</h3><ul>\n<li>Unlike the fact witness, the expert may give an opinion on what caused the incident or what would have prevented it.</li>\n<li>To be accepted as an expert, they usually need: experience, education, teaching, research and published articles in their field.</li>\n<li>Other countries may define the expert differently.</li>\n<li>In the US, the legal requirements come from three Supreme Court cases:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>What it establishes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Daubert v. Merrell Dow Pharmaceuticals, Inc.</td>\n<td>1993</td>\n<td>The judge must verify that scientific testimony is based on sound science before admitting it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General Electric Co. v. Joiner</td>\n<td>1997 (the year appears only in the references)</td>\n<td>The trial judge may decide what type of scientific testimony the jury hears</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kumho Tire v. Carmichael Co.</td>\n<td>1999 (the year appears only in the references)</td>\n<td>Extends Daubert: any expert, scientific or not, must be examined before testifying</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text writes \"Kumho Tire v. Carmichael Co.\". The references say \"Kumho Tire Co., Ltd. v. Carmichael\". The text does not give the years of Joiner and Kumho.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Fact witness</th>\n<th>Expert</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How they come</td>\n<td>Asked or compelled to appear</td>\n<td>Hired to give their opinion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypothetical questions</td>\n<td>Not obliged to answer them</td>\n<td>Must answer them</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Who decides whether someone is an expert:</strong> the judge in the case. The judge decides on the person the attorney proposes and hires.</li>\n<li>Expert = someone with knowledge that a layperson or someone outside a defined field does not usually have.</li>\n<li>In some jurisdictions the judge appoints experts to assist the judge and the jury. In most, each party hires its own.</li>\n<li><strong>Example, lawsuit for inadequate security with injury from a violent act.</strong> Experts may be hired to:<ul>\n<li>Give an opinion on the who, what, when, where, how and perhaps the why, after reviewing the facts and analyzing relevant data.</li>\n<li>Purely security topics: premises liability, policies and procedures, personnel, technology, hardware and design, and their relation to the incident.</li>\n<li>Criminology topics.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Advice for the expert:</strong><ul>\n<li>Accept a case on its merits and because it fits their specialty.</li>\n<li>Be ready to document their experience and training at trial.</li>\n<li>Have no obvious conflicts of interest.</li>\n<li>Gather the relevant information and prepare to have their memory and opinions challenged.</li>\n<li>This may include a site inspection and a crime analysis (Meyer 2020).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Formula for a hypothetical:</strong> say that they understand the scenario, but that those are not the facts of the case, and that if they were they would answer in such a way.<ul>\n<li>That way they answer without applying the hypothetical to the real case.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Answer directly and briefly. Explaining too much gives opposing counsel more information and opens lines of questioning for which they may not be prepared.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chapter close, the complete journey:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>It begins with an incident.</li>\n<li>It depends on procedures and reports.</li>\n<li>Judges, juries, attorneys and witnesses take part.</li>\n<li>It requires a prior review.</li>\n<li>It ends with testimony given knowing the techniques for testifying and the opposing attorney's traps.</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"investigations-7-e5\">Key Terms</h3><p>testimonial evidence · fact witness · opinion witness · expert witness · forensic witness · hearsay · dying declaration · deposition · remote deposition · grand jury · regulatory hearing · parol (\"spoken word\") · pre-trial meeting · duces tecum · interrogatories · complainant · defendant · bench trial · jurors · voter rolls · voir dire · relevancy · materiality · competency · standard of proof · direct examination · cross-examination · pre-testimony review · pre-trial conference · evidence tags · chain of custody · impeachment · demeanor · trick questions · qualified answer · cross-race effect · rapport · privileged discussions · inconsistencies · Daubert v. Merrell Dow Pharmaceuticals (1993) · General Electric Co. v. Joiner (1997) · Kumho Tire v. Carmichael (1999) · premises liability · crime analysis · hypothetical questions</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Chapter Introduction — Basic Concepts"},{"id":"e1","t":"7.1 The Journey"},{"id":"e2","t":"7.2 Courtroom Participants"},{"id":"e3","t":"7.3 Testifying"},{"id":"e4","t":"7.4 Expert Witnesses"},{"id":"e5","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"Chapter Introduction — Basic Concepts","h":"possible typo in the names. The official names in the US are usually \"Federal Rules of Civil/Criminal Procedure\"."},{"s":"7.3 Testifying","h":"in the text the source is spelled \"McElheny 2006\". In the reference list it appears as \"McElhaney, J. W. 2006\"."},{"s":"7.4 Expert Witnesses","h":"the text writes \"Kumho Tire v. Carmichael Co.\". The references say \"Kumho Tire Co., Ltd. v. Carmichael\". The text does not give the years of Joiner and Kumho."}]},"investigations-8":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-8-e0\">Chapter Introduction (p. 267)</h3><ul>\n<li>The investigative unit (IU) must use all the resources it can gather.</li>\n<li>Force multipliers help the IU find and evaluate information faster.</li>\n<li>During the investigation and afterward, clear and complete reports are needed to communicate activities and findings.</li>\n<li>At the end, managers must do two things:<ul>\n<li>Eliminate the factors that allowed the criminal activity, dangerous conditions or other negative events.</li>\n<li>Evaluate the investigation process itself in order to improve it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-8-e1\">8.1 Investigative Force Multipliers</h3><ul>\n<li>The term originates in the military sphere. It is applied to electronic warfare, intelligence operations and perception management.</li>\n<li><strong>Original US Department of Defense (DoD) definition:</strong> a capability that, when added to and employed by a combat force, significantly increases its combat potential. It thereby improves the probability of accomplishing the mission.</li>\n<li><strong>Corporate adaptation:</strong> any activity, capability or resource that improves the effectiveness or efficiency of the investigative function.</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> the \"If You See Something, Say Something\" campaign.<ul>\n<li>It is a joint initiative of the US Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and local police.</li>\n<li>It is a national awareness program that asks the public to report suspicious or illegal activity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The four main force multipliers:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Multiplier</th>\n<th>Section</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Online information sources</td>\n<td>8.1.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Liaison (collaborative relationships)</td>\n<td>8.1.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Intelligence information</td>\n<td>8.1.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Technology</td>\n<td>8.1.4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>All four have costs. They are an investment in the investigative function.</li>\n<li>Both investigation managers and the executives who make decisions must understand this clearly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.1.1 Online Information Sources</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The core of every investigation is information.</li>\n<li>Anything that speeds up and makes information more efficient in four tasks is an important multiplier: gathering it, cross-checking it, analyzing it and organizing it.</li>\n<li>To use the Internet well, three things must be understood:<ul>\n<li>Its structure.</li>\n<li>What can be consulted and what cannot.</li>\n<li>How to evaluate the reliability of what is found.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>There is a lot of information and also a lot of disinformation. Extreme caution is advisable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Internet Structure (iceberg model)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>What it contains</th>\n<th>Access</th>\n<th>% of the total</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Surface web or clear web (tip of the iceberg)</td>\n<td>The public content found with basic searches: news, shopping, business and government websites, part of social media. These are billions of sites</td>\n<td>The only layer indexed by search engines such as Google</td>\n<td>About 4% (Ajaeb 2019)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deep web (below the water)</td>\n<td>What is behind a login or in internal databases: online databases, social media exchanges, internal company websites, financial records, research catalogs, file storage</td>\n<td>Not indexed by search engines because it is not public. That does not make it dangerous</td>\n<td>More than 93%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dark web</td>\n<td>Network of sites deliberately hidden, for anonymous activities, often dangerous and illegal. Most of it is drug trafficking, arms sales and other illicit exchanges. It is also used to evade censorship in some countries</td>\n<td>Only with special browsers, such as Tor</td>\n<td>About 3%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the figures add up to more than 100%. They are 4%, more than 93% and 3%.</li>\n<li>Search engines are useful for starting out, but the real value is in what lies beneath the surface.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sources for Online Information and Research</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>What used to take days or weeks is now obtained in minutes. For example, public records, court files or business registries.</li>\n<li>Social media gives a closer and more personal view of the subject's life.</li>\n<li>The book clarifies that its list is not exhaustive. It is a summary of what is most often sought.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Public Records Databases</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are among the most used sources.</li>\n<li>They may include:<ul>\n<li>Criminal and civil court files.</li>\n<li>Property and land records.</li>\n<li>Tax liens.</li>\n<li>Judgments.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A key step for many investigators: finding out the subject's criminal history.</li>\n<li>State, county, city and municipal courts can be consulted. Results vary widely and not all publish the details online.</li>\n<li>US federal cases: <strong>PACER (Public Access to Court Electronic Records)</strong>, at PACER.gov. It allows searching millions of federal criminal, civil and bankruptcy cases.</li>\n<li>Property, tax liens and civil judgments: at the county assessors' and recorders' offices. Many have online search.</li>\n<li>The key: know which agency holds each record and how much online access it gives.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Regulatory Databases (US; other countries have equivalents)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Searching for regulators' actions against a person or company shows their personal and professional conduct.</li>\n<li>It is an essential step of due diligence.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Agency</th>\n<th>What is sought</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SEC (Securities and Exchange Commission, securities regulator)</td>\n<td>Securities violations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OFAC (Office of Foreign Assets Control)</td>\n<td>Economic sanctions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FTC (Federal Trade Commission, commerce)</td>\n<td>Legal and enforcement actions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EPA (Environmental Protection Agency)</td>\n<td>Environmental violations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OSHA (Occupational Safety and Health Administration)</td>\n<td>Workplace violations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FEC (Federal Election Commission, elections)</td>\n<td>Political campaign contributions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secretary of State or State Corporations Division</td>\n<td>Business and company records in each state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State Licensing Boards</td>\n<td>Professional licenses and disciplinary sanctions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Online News and Media</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Court files do not always tell the whole story.</li>\n<li>The news, especially local news, covers relevant stories from the area. It provides witness statements and details of the incident.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to know the local and regional media of the area under investigation.</li>\n<li>They are useful for crimes, workplace incidents or a company's reputation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Library Resources and Online Archival Research</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A library card is one of the most versatile tools of the investigator.</li>\n<li>Many public libraries give access to digital resources.</li>\n<li>Their great value is searching news archives. Normal searches usually show only recent news.</li>\n<li>They are useful for earlier periods and for sources that exist only on paper. Many offer digitized press with keyword search for free.</li>\n<li>There are specialists in historical archives. Example: the AIIP (Association of Independent Information Professionals, AIIP.org), an association of experts in information research and retrieval.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Social Media</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Almost every investigation can make use of some part of social media.</li>\n<li>It provides information that is nowhere else.</li>\n<li>When records, databases and news fall short, the subject's digital footprint adds another dimension.</li>\n<li>Four out of five Americans use at least one social network.</li>\n<li>The average American has more than eight accounts.</li>\n<li>Settling for a quick search on Facebook can mean losing a lot of information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Usage data (Pew Research 2019; Statista.com 2020):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Network</th>\n<th>Users worldwide</th>\n<th>% of US adults who use it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Facebook</td>\n<td>2,700 million monthly active</td>\n<td>Almost 70%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Twitter</td>\n<td>353 million monthly active</td>\n<td>About 22%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Instagram</td>\n<td>1,200 million monthly active</td>\n<td>About 37%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LinkedIn</td>\n<td>700 million users</td>\n<td>About 27%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>YouTube</td>\n<td>2,000 million monthly active</td>\n<td>About 73%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Many companies now specialize in online and social media investigations.</li>\n<li>The investigator must understand on each platform: audiences, functions, privacy settings, configurations, advantages and drawbacks.</li>\n<li>It is a skill that changes constantly, as do the techniques for obtaining intelligence from social networks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Search Engines</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Examples from the book: Google, Bing and Yahoo.</li>\n<li>They allow a broad keyword search of the subject.</li>\n<li>They are not authoritative sources. But they find things that do not appear in public records or news, such as company websites, newsletters or event announcements.</li>\n<li>They make it possible to quickly gauge how much information there is about the subject at the outset.</li>\n<li>Caution, for two reasons:<ul>\n<li>Results depend on a complex mix of algorithms, settings, advertising and optimizations, which makes some pages appear higher (Ajaeb 2019).</li>\n<li>They also depend on the physical location of the person searching.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Commercial and Subscription-Based Databases</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only licensed professional investigators can use them.</li>\n<li>They are not public for two reasons: the sensitive information they contain and the laws that regulate their use.</li>\n<li>They are useful for obtaining background information and summary data on the subject.</li>\n<li>They are not exhaustive, but they give information that does not appear in search engines.</li>\n<li>Examples from the book: TLOxp, idiCORE, Tracers, IRB, Delvepoint and LexisNexis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cautions and Considerations</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Caution</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Not everything is online. In many jurisdictions records can only be obtained at the courthouse or the clerk's office</td>\n<td>Verify everything found in online databases against other records and documents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Information overload. It takes a lot of time and work, and demands an attentive eye and technical skill</td>\n<td>Make a plan, a checklist or an outline of what is relevant and of the sources to review</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Reliability and accuracy. There are errors even in official databases and court files</td>\n<td>Corroborate sources, look for more documents and think about what is missing from the record</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Biases</td>\n<td>Watch for one's own bias and that of the sources, especially on social media</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Special skills are needed, and tools and tactics change with technology, new platforms and privacy</td>\n<td>Dedicate budget to continuing training, or partner with specialized firms when it is cheaper</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>IU managers must evaluate their search needs and choose the most effective option for the mission and in cost.</li>\n<li>More information, easier access and a limited budget will lead to greater use of online sources.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.1.2 Liaison in Investigations</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is one of the most important multipliers.</li>\n<li>Forms: internal or external, formal or informal, individual or organizational.</li>\n<li>What it allows (Webster University 2020):<ul>\n<li>Leveraging the resources of others.</li>\n<li>Sharing best practices and lessons learned.</li>\n<li>Collaborating on specific cases.</li>\n<li>Better addressing common problems.</li>\n<li>Sharing information, equipment and facilities. In short, doing more with less.</li>\n<li>Being more proactive and adding more value to the organization.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Investigators must be allowed and encouraged to create and maintain their own formal and informal contacts.</li>\n<li>Large police forces have agents all over the world with access to senior officials. Corporate investigators usually do not.</li>\n<li>That is why they must build professional relationships in the community, with:<ul>\n<li>Law enforcement.</li>\n<li>Government services.</li>\n<li>Security colleagues.</li>\n<li>Associations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Those relationships yield direct results, or provide facts, evidence and witnesses that lead to them.</li>\n<li>Even if a large network already exists, liaison expands it and makes it more efficient.</li>\n<li><strong>Infrequent problems:</strong> large-scale fraud, cyberattacks, workplace violence or executive misconduct.<ul>\n<li>Key: coordinate early and closely with internal contacts, such as the general counsel and the head of human resources.</li>\n<li>External contacts, such as functional experts or consultants, are also useful to use adequate and current investigative protocols.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Proactive vs. reactive:</strong> it can occur in a reactive environment (crisis or emergency), but it is above all proactive.</li>\n<li>Being proactive improves the IU's effectiveness and credibility and demonstrates its value.</li>\n<li>Many see it as essential against social problems: illegal drugs, street violence, workplace violence and fraud.</li>\n<li>Police, corporate personnel, security professionals and the legal community, together, better protect assets, solve crimes and prevent losses (Webster University 2020).</li>\n<li><strong>Philosophical reason:</strong> professionalism. Professionals want to share what they know and learn from others. That strengthens the profession and one's own knowledge.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to Establish Liaison</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Relationships arise from introductions, referrals, contacts (formal and social) and mutual help.</li>\n<li>Professional and civic associations: a great opportunity.</li>\n<li>Encourage security directors, IU managers and investigators to join service, community, social, sports and professional groups.</li>\n<li><strong>Emergency plan exercises:</strong> a good place to meet those who will provide support in an emergency. Besides the crisis itself, they help to:<ul>\n<li>Review plans.</li>\n<li>Provide awareness training.</li>\n<li>Get to know the facilities.</li>\n<li>Exchange general information.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Multilateral forums</strong> worth fostering:<ul>\n<li>Local business and community security associations.</li>\n<li>Industry security associations (examples from the book: health care, utilities, retail).</li>\n<li>Local chapters of Infragard or similar organizations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If participation has to be justified to senior management: invite a representative of the forum to meet with the executives or train them.</li>\n<li>Professional associations are part of the public-private partnerships between police and corporate security.</li>\n<li>Training courses and professional development also serve to expand the network.</li>\n<li><strong>Advice from a veteran (Hudak 2005):</strong><ul>\n<li>Build relationships at different levels of the organizations.</li>\n<li>On arriving in a community, pay a courtesy visit to the highest-ranking official you can at the agency. Then use that meeting as a reference with others at that agency.</li>\n<li>Do not limit yourself to the police. Also visit: fire department, emergency services, utility companies, the mayor's office, the postal inspector's office, hospital security directors and any organization useful to the company.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Internal contacts:</strong> introductory visit, periodic follow-up visits and exchange of information and favors.</li>\n<li>A crisis or a major investigation is not the time to meet colleagues for the first time.</li>\n<li>Suggested internal contacts (complete list, 12):<ol>\n<li>Executive assistant and front office staff.</li>\n<li>General counsel.</li>\n<li>Head of procurement.</li>\n<li>Director of human resources.</li>\n<li>Facilities manager.</li>\n<li>Head of public relations or information, or equivalent.</li>\n<li>Director of accounting and finance or chief financial officer (CFO).</li>\n<li>Operations manager.</li>\n<li>Information technology (IT) manager or chief information officer (CIO).</li>\n<li>Director of security or of safety.</li>\n<li>Occupational health staff.</li>\n<li>Union or collective bargaining unit leaders.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Depending on the size of the company, it is advisable to have contacts at several levels in each of those functions.</li>\n<li><strong>International liaison:</strong> it is critical. Abroad, the following weigh heavily:<ul>\n<li>Culture.</li>\n<li>The laws and rules of the host country.</li>\n<li>The policies and procedures of the local police.</li>\n<li>Language.</li>\n<li>Knowledge of the terrain.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Local contacts help achieve results and help the team avoid problems and keep a low profile.</li>\n<li>Build international relationships long before a crisis or investigation.</li>\n<li>Many foreign agencies have special units for: counterterrorism, cybercrime, financial crimes, import and export, and organized crime.</li>\n<li>Contact direct counterparts and correspond with them periodically.</li>\n<li>When visiting facilities in other countries, meet with counterpart investigators and learn their policies, procedures and concerns.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to Maintain Liaison</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is based on a relationship of professional trust. It requires periodic exchange and mutual benefit.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Suggestion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Each party should obtain some benefit without abusing the other. It must be mutual, though not necessarily a quid pro quo (something in exchange for something)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Respect the legal limits and each agency's rules on the maximum value of liaison gifts. That avoids sanctions and damage to the relationship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Never betray a contact's trust. It usually ends the relationship and marks the investigator and their agency for a long time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Maintain periodic contact even if there is nothing specific to exchange. It shows that the relationship matters and keeps familiarity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Joint Task Forces (JTF)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are a highly structured form of liaison.</li>\n<li>They are used for complex and often multi-jurisdictional problems: retail theft, terrorism, cybercrime, human trafficking and serious procurement fraud.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>JTF type</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Case-specific</td>\n<td>National Capital Area Sniper Case (2002); Unabomber Task Force</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Longstanding</td>\n<td>The various Joint Terrorism Task Forces; Crimes Against Children Task Force</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure 8.1 — Joint Task Force Resources:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Resource</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Staffing resources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Intelligence from local law enforcement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Law enforcement databases and public resources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Task force member resources</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Note: the figure text came out with a broken font. It was reconstructed from the glyph pattern. It should be checked against the book.</li>\n<li>Possible members of a JTF: international, federal and local agencies, industry resources, auditors, analysts, consultants and prosecutors (the book leaves the list open).</li>\n<li>They bring special expertise and cross jurisdictional boundaries.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Diversity of members: advantages</th>\n<th>Diversity of members: drawbacks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Many benefits and leveraging the strengths of diversity</td>\n<td>Misunderstandings, conflicts and inefficiencies. They must be managed to offset them</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Four problems to watch for in a JTF:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Solution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Parochialism (localism, self-interest)</td>\n<td>Members arrive with their own agendas, personal or organizational. It causes conflict, open or subtle. It reduces effectiveness, limits results and erodes the trust of the public or of executives</td>\n<td>JTF management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protection of sensitive information</td>\n<td>Each member sees differently what is sensitive and for how long. There are several chains of command, different policies, pressure to report to different audiences and different secure communications</td>\n<td>Tailored OPSEC (operations security) plan, for physical and electronic information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data management</td>\n<td>The book only mentions it</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reporting chains</td>\n<td>Their confusion spreads fragmented and apparently contradictory information about progress, successes, needs and relationships</td>\n<td>Well-defined reporting chains, in the JTF and in each member agency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book lists four problems, but develops only three. \"Data management\" has no explanation.</li>\n<li><strong>Corporate application:</strong> this advice is useful for:<ul>\n<li>Multidisciplinary teams or integrated product teams.</li>\n<li>Collaborations among several organizations.</li>\n<li>Companies and NGOs that work with police or intelligence JTFs, or that support them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Summary:</strong> liaison ranges from informal face-to-face conversations to detailed international agreements. It is always a great multiplier.</li>\n<li>Paraphrased quote from a former Special Agent in Charge of the largest US Air Force mainframe systems fraud detachment (Smith 1997): good liaison is like a good informant program. Without it you cannot be proactive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.1.3 Intelligence Information</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a key multiplier, but in companies an organized approach to managing it is usually lacking.</li>\n<li>The IU manager must create a <strong>formal intelligence management program</strong>. It must have policies, procedures and systems to (complete list, 9):<ol>\n<li>Define general and specific objectives.</li>\n<li>Gather intelligence information.</li>\n<li>Integrate and analyze the information.</li>\n<li>Organize and store the information.</li>\n<li>Share it, internally and externally, when appropriate.</li>\n<li>Retrieve the information.</li>\n<li>Control access to the data.</li>\n<li>Control access to information about methods and sources.</li>\n<li>Evaluate and improve the program periodically.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Within the program:<ul>\n<li>Set general and specific objectives, with the priority topics.</li>\n<li>Define strategies for obtaining and using the information.</li>\n<li>Sometimes, assign intelligence requirements to specific investigators or locations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The possible sources depend on the type of investigation and the IU's functional focus.</li>\n<li>Experienced investigators detect and make better use of sources. These skills are taught in almost all basic and advanced courses.</li>\n<li><strong>Basic maxim:</strong> \"check your own files first\".<ul>\n<li>It is common to overlook valuable internal sources, especially in large, complex or multi-site organizations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Case management software:</strong> very useful if it meets three conditions:<ul>\n<li>Supports exhibits and photos.</li>\n<li>Allows link analysis.</li>\n<li>Is on the organization's network or in the cloud, accessible to the whole IU.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Using off-the-shelf commercial programs, many customizable, is cheaper than building them from scratch.</li>\n<li><strong>Types of sources by duration:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>One-time source of unique relevance</td>\n<td>It may be the most important. It may be used only once, but it provides critical data to solve the case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuing long-term sources</td>\n<td>Liaison contacts, controlled informants with more potential and confidential sources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Investigator's personal sources</td>\n<td>They provide certain information regularly. They must be shared with the IU, but contact is always coordinated through the investigator who developed them</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Protection of methods and sources:</strong> it is key in every intelligence program. Some are very sensitive.</li>\n<li>Only legal methods and sources should be used.</li>\n<li>Create a system of code names or numbers to hide the identity of persons, organizations or technical sources.</li>\n<li>That system must reduce the risk of exposure in two ways:<ul>\n<li>Accidental disclosure.</li>\n<li>Legal processes, such as discovery.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It is even more important if exposing the source would cause great embarrassment or a physical risk to persons or organizations.</li>\n<li>Sources vary according to the IU's functional focus, geographic location and industry.</li>\n<li><strong>Common intelligence sources (complete list, 21):</strong><ol>\n<li>News media.</li>\n<li>Online sources.</li>\n<li>Internal files and historical records.</li>\n<li>Local court files and records (province, state, county, city).</li>\n<li>Criminal justice agency files.</li>\n<li>IT department records and audit trails.</li>\n<li>Hiring and procurement records.</li>\n<li>Accounts payable and receivable records.</li>\n<li>Operations and production records.</li>\n<li>Filings with regulators and regulatory bodies.</li>\n<li>Tax returns.</li>\n<li>Vehicle records.</li>\n<li>Insurers and claims history.</li>\n<li>Workplace safety and incident records.</li>\n<li>Inventory management systems.</li>\n<li>Human resources data.</li>\n<li>Personnel evaluations, awards and disciplinary files.</li>\n<li>Payroll and travel records.</li>\n<li>Medical and financial records.</li>\n<li>Security files.</li>\n<li>Licenses, permits and other local government records.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Legal warning:</strong> medical, financial and other personal data records are protected by local, national and international rules.</li>\n<li>If obtained illegally, the information is useless. In addition, the source, the investigator and the company can be criminally prosecuted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.1.4 Technology</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a multiplier because it provides four improvements:<ul>\n<li>Faster data analysis and fusion.</li>\n<li>Better knowledge management.</li>\n<li>A more predictive approach.</li>\n<li>More efficient dissemination of information.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The biggest technology multiplier: data mining and data analytics tools. They make it possible to locate, gather, organize and analyze large volumes of information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Three ways to take advantage of them (they can be combined according to the IU's needs):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Approach</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Use the functions that standard programs already include, such as basic productivity suites</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Buy and install specific mining and analytics suites, some adapted to investigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Outsource to a company specialized in data mining and analytics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>These tools are also useful for open-source intelligence (OSINT) projects.</li>\n<li>OSINT is not used only to investigate. It also serves brand and reputation management and competitive intelligence.</li>\n<li><strong>Training:</strong> virtual reality (VR) environments and collaborative or interactive simulated cases, even with participants from around the world.</li>\n<li>Properly organized, these trainings do not reduce human contact. They facilitate liaison among organizations, specialties and regions.</li>\n<li><strong>Relationship with IT:</strong> the technological environment demands a close relationship among security, investigators and IT staff.<ul>\n<li>It must start at the top: the top security or investigations executive works with the CIO or equivalent.</li>\n<li>Then it goes down through the whole organization in both functions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Leveraging IT staff to investigate, always within the law, benefits both and generates creative solutions.</li>\n<li>Example: many IT departments do pattern analysis or keystroke monitoring. That can detect insider threats or system abuses.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-8-e2\">8.2 Investigative Documentation</h3><h4>8.2.1 Report Writing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Investigative report:</strong> written document in which the investigator records what was done and the evidence gathered.</li>\n<li>Even if it is not the final report, it can provide leads to other investigators.</li>\n<li>It serves as the basis for later administrative actions, such as a court proceeding or a disciplinary sanction.</li>\n<li>The reader must quickly understand what the investigator did, why, and with what result.</li>\n<li>Even the most careful investigation loses value if it is not reported well.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The five basic steps:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Step</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Gather the facts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Record the facts immediately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Organize the facts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Write the report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Evaluate or correct the report, if needed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Types of Reports</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>When and for what purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Initial report</td>\n<td>Submitted a few days after the investigation is opened. It describes progress and any pending leads. In a simple case it can also be the final or closing report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Progress report (or interim)</td>\n<td>A private investigation can last months; a public one, years. It is submitted at fixed intervals with progress and pending leads. In the private sector the normal interval is <strong>30 days</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special report</td>\n<td>Documents an action outside the normal course of the investigation. It can be a supplement to the progress report, but it must be readable on its own. Example: a report on a surveillance in an undercover investigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final report</td>\n<td>It must be logical. It is submitted in three cases: (1) the investigation is closed successfully; (2) all leads were followed without success and continuing would not be productive; (3) the person or office that authorized opening the case orders it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Elements of the Report</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The format varies widely between organizations.</li>\n<li>One method is to divide it by topic, such as statements or surveillance logs.</li>\n<li>It may be required on paper or in electronic format. Sometimes a \"certified\" copy, signed by hand, is requested.</li>\n<li>Whatever the format, it must contain the relevant information obtained and five parts:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Content and rules</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Administrative information</td>\n<td>The report must be understandable without consulting others. It includes: (1) name of the subject of the investigation; (2) case or file number; (3) type of case, if the number does not indicate it; (4) case status, open or closed; (5) reporting investigator; (6) date of the report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Summary or synopsis</td>\n<td>It goes at the beginning for a quick read. Rule: each important point in the report gets at least one sentence in the summary. Supporting facts are not cited. Conclusions are allowed if they fairly reflect the detail in the body</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Narrative</td>\n<td>It details the work in chronological order, with the facts of the case. Each step goes in a separate paragraph. It must be clear, simple, and brief, with easy vocabulary. Avoid technical terms or, at least, define them. The narrative of the undercover operative is in the <strong>first person</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conclusions</td>\n<td>Some organizations ask for conclusions and recommendations. Example: after a fraud, improve controls; after a burglary, more physical security (surveys, alarms, guards, and video surveillance). But the investigator is above all a collector of evidence. The appropriate authority decides on judicial or administrative actions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enclosures</td>\n<td>At a minimum: (1) the initial complaint; (2) statements from the complainant and the witnesses; (3) written statements from others interviewed; (4) documentation of the evidence and results of its analysis (physical, documentary, electronic, and technical); (5) surveillance logs, if applicable; (6) photos, audio, or video</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Distribution and Protection of Investigative Reports</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The report may have to be disclosed in the court process of discovery.</li>\n<li>In discovery, the unit can be forced to hand over the report and its files to the opposing party.</li>\n<li>For that reason it must be kept free of extraneous and irrelevant material, especially names of people and places unrelated to the case.</li>\n<li><strong>Protection technique:</strong> give it only to the company's attorney of record, who keeps it.<ul>\n<li>That way it will probably be considered attorney work product and be protected from a subpoena.</li>\n<li>It is not foolproof. But obtaining it usually requires a court order after a hearing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Qualified privilege:</strong> a well-controlled report does not usually give rise to a defamation lawsuit, even if it contains false data.<ul>\n<li>The exception: proof that the false data was included with malice.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Even so, avoid opinions and conclusions of the investigator.</li>\n<li>The content should be statements by others or personal observations of the investigator, identified as such.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says that some organizations ask for conclusions and recommendations. It then advises not including opinions or conclusions of the investigator.</li>\n<li><strong>Distribution, the final step of the report:</strong><ul>\n<li>Only to those with a real need to know.</li>\n<li>It may contain damaging information about employees or other people, or statements by them.</li>\n<li>Control copies closely.</li>\n<li>Whoever receives a copy or keeps a file must have adequate security protection. They must be told that it is sensitive and given handling instructions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If the report turns out to contain inaccurate and damaging material, the qualified privilege may be lost for having distributed it more than necessary.</li>\n<li>Many organizations distribute reports only through their in-house or outside attorney, so that they are protected by attorney-client privilege.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Protection</th>\n<th>How it is achieved</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Attorney work product</td>\n<td>Give the report only to the company's attorney of record, who keeps it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Qualified privilege</td>\n<td>Control the report and distribute it only on a need-to-know basis. It is lost with excessive distribution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attorney-client privilege</td>\n<td>Distribute only through the in-house or outside attorney</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>8.2.2 Other Reporting Formats</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Some purposes or types of investigation use other formats.</li>\n<li>A simple investigation of an incident, done by a security supervisor, may be documented in the incident report itself.</li>\n<li>Preemployment and background investigations may use somewhat different formats, as set by the organization or the authority that requests them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.2.3 Testifying and Depositions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In almost all fields, investigators are called to testify in judicial or administrative proceedings.</li>\n<li>The book refers to Chapter 7.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-8-e3\">8.3 Leveraging Investigative Results</h3><ul>\n<li>The natural end of an investigation is usually:<ul>\n<li>Gathering all the relevant facts.</li>\n<li>Documenting the findings.</li>\n<li>Deciding on the disciplinary sanction or restitution.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>But that should not be the end.</li>\n<li>Investigations almost always reveal something valuable. For example, a condition or weakness that:<ul>\n<li>Made it possible to commit a crime.</li>\n<li>Gave unauthorized access.</li>\n<li>Created an opportunity for malicious activity.</li>\n<li>Produced unintended hazards.</li>\n<li>Allowed another anomaly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Even background investigations allow trend analysis:<ul>\n<li>To detect changes in typical \"red flags\" or in indications of unsuitability.</li>\n<li>To see how they relate to adjudication standards and whether they need to be updated.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The organization needs mechanisms to leverage all findings and improve its processes. They should not be wasted.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two complementary objectives:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Objective</th>\n<th>Section</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Improve the organization's investigative processes or capabilities</td>\n<td>8.3.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Improve the security posture (security and safety) and the ability to manage current and future risks</td>\n<td>8.3.2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>8.3.1 Improving the Investigative Process</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>According to the software company Forensic Notes (2020), when a workplace investigation ends, human resources and the organization should analyze it. That way they see what to change for the future and what could have been done better.</li>\n<li>Two questions it proposes: do policies need to be updated? Is more training needed?</li>\n<li>Additional questions from the book (complete list, 7):<ol>\n<li>Are we using our investigative staff and resources efficiently? How can we improve?</li>\n<li>Are we using external or subcontracted resources efficiently and effectively? How can we improve?</li>\n<li>Was any tool or capability missing? Do we need to buy or budget for new equipment, services, or capabilities?</li>\n<li>Did we coordinate well with other departments? The book cites: legal, human resources, CIO, finance, and facilities.</li>\n<li>Was information shared with the right areas at the right time?</li>\n<li>Did new liaison opportunities, internal or external, or new contacts arise?</li>\n<li>Were there leaks, actual or potential, of sensitive information during the investigation? If so, how can they be prevented in the future?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.3.2 Improving the Organization's Security and Safety Posture</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The aim is to improve the processes by which the organization manages security and safety risk together.</li>\n<li>The strategy is summarized in the ASIS Information Asset Protection guideline.<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book cites it as \"forthcoming,\" that is, not yet published.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Every finding that shows a condition, policy, practice, or vulnerability that puts assets at risk must be documented and corrected.<ul>\n<li>This includes anything that could cause or worsen a reputation or brand risk.</li>\n<li>This achieves continuous improvement in protection and risk management, and mitigates known threats and vulnerabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Periodic review of reports</strong>, internal or from outside agencies, to extract useful information on:<ul>\n<li>Security vulnerabilities.</li>\n<li>Safety hazards.</li>\n<li>Attack methods, and the adversary's capabilities, intent, and objectives.</li>\n<li>Insider threats.</li>\n<li>Criminal intelligence.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>That information serves three purposes:<ul>\n<li>Updating business policies and practices.</li>\n<li>Improving security procedures.</li>\n<li>Opening new investigations into matters that had not come to light.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Post-investigation review:</strong> in many cases it is advisable. In practice it becomes a root cause analysis, if the report does not already include one.</li>\n<li><strong>Root cause, according to the ASQ (American Society for Quality):</strong> the factor responsible for a nonconformance. It is the underlying problem that sets in motion the whole chain of cause and effect that ends in the problem or problems.</li>\n<li>The root cause is often what made the investigation necessary.</li>\n<li><strong>Root cause analysis:</strong> uses approaches, tools, and techniques, from informal to sophisticated, to find the true cause.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Root cause tool</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Change analysis</td>\n<td>Investigates what changed and when, and how it led to the action: a crime, a security violation, a safety incident, or another loss. The change can be in people, equipment, a vendor, a procedure, a facility, or any other variable (the book leaves it that open)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timelines</td>\n<td>A useful tool for change analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barrier analysis</td>\n<td>Reviews which controls exist, and may have failed, to prevent or detect the problem (ASQ 2020)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Investigation</th>\n<th>Root cause analysis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Determines what happened and how</td>\n<td>Determines exactly why it happened and how to prevent it from happening again</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Root cause analysis can lead to (complete list, 4):<ol>\n<li>Improvements in physical, personnel, information, and cyber security programs.</li>\n<li>Better application of enterprise security risk management (ESRM).</li>\n<li>Better-informed business decisions.</li>\n<li>Improvements in the business and operating practices themselves.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The results also help to:<ul>\n<li>Restore employee morale after a high-profile incident.</li>\n<li>Answer questions from employees, the public, and other stakeholders.</li>\n<li>Explain what corrective measures will be taken and how they will affect operations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The essential point:</strong> correct every failure discovered. What is discovered in a workplace investigation is on record and must be addressed.</li>\n<li>Failing to correct it can lead to future legal action if the same mistakes are repeated (Forensic Notes 2020).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-8-e4\">8.4 Projections</h3><p>Four themes that emerge from the history of investigations (seen at the start of the book):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Theme</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>The line between public and private investigation is blurring</td>\n<td>Seen in Pinkerton and Burns and throughout history. Today, in the early 21st century, the private sector and the police depend more and more on each other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Strong and constant relationship between security and investigation (Webster University 2020)</td>\n<td>Examples: the deterrent (security) value of investigating; the investigation function assigned to the security department in many companies; companies that offer both services: Pinkerton, Burns, Wackenhut, Wells Fargo, G4S, and Securitas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Accusations of corruption, frequent in private agencies and in police forces</td>\n<td>Ethics must be kept high in reality and in image. Ethics gives credibility, and credibility gives effectiveness. The investigator follows a code of professional conduct. His or her credibility on the witness stand depends on not taking shortcuts against ethical safeguards. An investigator is worth only as much as the case he or she is working now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The \"current\" problems were already a concern in the past</td>\n<td>See the list below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> possible typo. The text says \"shortcuts that comprise ethical safeguards.\" The logical reading would be \"compromise\" (shortcuts that compromise the safeguards).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text cites \"Webster University 2020\" several times (also in 8.1.2), but it does not appear in the chapter's references. The references include \"Peterson 2020, The Liaison Advantage,\" which the text does not cite.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Historical problems that guide the investigation function (complete list, 12):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Terrorism (example: England in the 19th century).</li>\n<li>Burglaries and street crime.</li>\n<li>Violent crimes.</li>\n<li>Executive protection needs.</li>\n<li>Economic and white-collar crime.</li>\n<li>Corporate asset protection needs.</li>\n<li>International and cross-border crimes.</li>\n<li>Major thefts and crimes against cargo or goods in transit.</li>\n<li>Organized crime networks.</li>\n<li>Theft of information and business intelligence.</li>\n<li>Corporate compliance.</li>\n<li>Competitive intelligence and espionage.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>The tools and techniques of these crimes change, especially with digitalization. But the crimes remain relevant from a risk management standpoint.</li>\n<li>Advances in science, technology, legal theory, and international affairs will continue to have influence.</li>\n<li>Scientific advances will improve:<ul>\n<li>Investigative tools.</li>\n<li>Evidence collection and analysis techniques.</li>\n<li>Surveillance capabilities.</li>\n<li>Database and knowledge management.</li>\n<li>Information-sharing mechanisms.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The growing dependence on IT will require IUs to have more IT and computer forensics knowledge, in-house or subcontracted.</li>\n<li><strong>More self-reliance:</strong> companies, organizations, and communities will increasingly cover their own security and investigation needs.<ul>\n<li>According to the International Foundation of Protection Officers (Davies 2015), because of budget and other limitations, the police and society will expect companies to investigate their own white-collar crimes themselves.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Homeland security:</strong> it weighs heavily in public and corporate investigation.<ul>\n<li>Cooperation and information sharing among federal, state, local, and international agencies, and between government and the private sector, is key.</li>\n<li>The critical infrastructure protection efforts of the late 1990s brought federal agencies and companies closer together. They continue today, because threats are growing.</li>\n<li>From them came the ISACs (Information Sharing and Analysis Centers), semiformal and organized by key sector.</li>\n<li>Today these relationships are more productive under the umbrella of homeland security.</li>\n<li>Examples of public-private partnerships: the FBI's (Federal Bureau of Investigation) Infragard program and the U.S. State Department's OSAC (Overseas Security Advisory Council).</li>\n<li>These relationships, and the trust they are based on, must continue to grow.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trends that will continue in the 21st century (summary from the book):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>More investigative responsibilities and capabilities in the private sector.</li>\n<li>Close relationship between investigation and security risk management or asset protection.</li>\n<li>Emphasis on ethics and professionalism, also in the professional investigative services industry.</li>\n<li>Rapid incorporation of new scientific advances.</li>\n<li>Growing weight of the information sciences: database and knowledge management, and technological mechanisms for sharing information.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>This will affect the prior education, training, requirements, and use of investigative personnel, and the use of outside experts.</li>\n<li>Even so, there will continue to be heavy reliance on traditional techniques:<ul>\n<li>Interviews.</li>\n<li>Human sources.</li>\n<li>Physical surveillance.</li>\n<li>Report writing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-8-e5\">Key Terms</h3><p>force multiplier · investigative unit (IU) · \"If You See Something, Say Something\" · online information sources · surface web / clear web · deep web · dark web · Tor · public records databases · PACER · tax liens · judgments · regulatory databases · due diligence · SEC · OFAC · FTC · EPA · OSHA · FEC · State Licensing Boards · archival research · AIIP · social media footprint · search engines · commercial and subscription-based databases · information overload · liaison · Infragard · multilateral forums · courtesy visit · quid pro quo · liaison gifts · joint task force (JTF) · case-specific vs. longstanding JTF · parochialism · OPSEC · reporting chains · intelligence management program · \"check your own files first\" · case management software · link analysis · controlled informants · confidential sources · code names · discovery · data mining · data analytics · data fusion · OSINT · keystroke monitoring · insider threats · investigative report · initial / progress / special / final report · administrative information · synopsis · narrative · conclusions · enclosures · attorney of record · attorney work product · subpoena · qualified privilege · defamation · malice · need to know · attorney-client privilege · trend analysis · red flags · adjudication standards · post-investigation review · root cause · root cause analysis · barrier analysis · timelines · ESRM · computer forensics · homeland security · ISAC · OSAC</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Chapter Introduction"},{"id":"e1","t":"8.1 Investigative Force Multipliers"},{"id":"e2","t":"8.2 Investigative Documentation"},{"id":"e3","t":"8.3 Leveraging Investigative Results"},{"id":"e4","t":"8.4 Projections"},{"id":"e5","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"8.1 Investigative Force Multipliers","h":"the figures add up to more than 100%. They are 4%, more than 93% and 3%."},{"s":"8.1 Investigative Force Multipliers","h":"the book lists four problems, but develops only three. \"Data management\" has no explanation."},{"s":"8.2 Investigative Documentation","h":"the book says that some organizations ask for conclusions and recommendations. It then advises not including opinions or conclusions of the investigator."},{"s":"8.3 Leveraging Investigative Results","h":"the book cites it as \"forthcoming,\" that is, not yet published."},{"s":"8.4 Projections","h":"possible typo. The text says \"shortcuts that comprise ethical safeguards.\" The logical reading would be \"compromise\" (shortcuts that compromise the safeguards)."},{"s":"8.4 Projections","h":"the text cites \"Webster University 2020\" several times (also in 8.1.2), but it does not appear in the chapter's references. The references include \"Peterson 2020, The Liaison Advantage,\" which the text does not cite."}]},"investigations-A":{"html":"<h3 id=\"investigations-A-e0\">Appendix 1A — Security Incident Reports</h3><ul>\n<li>These are two sample formats, representative of government and commercial environments.</li>\n<li>Incident reports are often the initial complaint that opens an investigation.</li>\n<li>Formats courtesy of MVM Security, Incorporated, of Vienna, Virginia.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Security Incident Report — Sample 1 (2 pages)</h4>\n<p>Footer of each page: \"Rev. (date)\", \"Page 1 of 2\" or \"Page 2 of 2\", and \"Form No.\" Page 2 repeats \"Report No.\" in the header.</p>\n<p><strong>Header (fields 1–8)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Field</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Date of Report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Date and Time of Incident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Report No.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Type of Incident, see #43 (refers to the list in field 43)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Person Reporting Incident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Time Reported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Location Description/Building Address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Tenant</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Complaint/Incident Information — data on the complainant or affected person (fields 9–22)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Options</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Name</td>\n<td>Ms. / Mr.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Emergency Contact Person</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Home Address</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Home Phone</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Work Address</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Work Phone</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Injury Sustained</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Cause of Injury</td>\n<td>Falling Object, Tripped, Slipped, Fell, Other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16a</td>\n<td>Type of Shoe</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Condition of Area</td>\n<td>Liquid Spill, Food Spill, Dark Area, Uneven Surface, No Adverse Conditions, Other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Medical Services Contacted?</td>\n<td>Yes / No; if no, why?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>Responding Unit</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Transported to</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>Property Description</td>\n<td>Additional property goes in the narrative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>22</td>\n<td>Property</td>\n<td>Stolen, Missing, Damaged, Recovered, Other; also Serial # and Value $</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Suspect Information — data on the suspect (fields 23–31)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Options</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>23</td>\n<td>Name</td>\n<td>Ms. / Mr.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>Barring Notice</td>\n<td>Yes / No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>Sex</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>26</td>\n<td>Home Address</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>27</td>\n<td>Home Phone</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>28</td>\n<td>Work Address</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29</td>\n<td>Work Phone</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>Physical Description</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>31</td>\n<td>Additional Information</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Witness Information — data on the witness (fields 32–37)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Options</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>32</td>\n<td>Name</td>\n<td>Ms. / Mr.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>33</td>\n<td>Written Statement Requested?</td>\n<td>Yes / No. Pending: the extracted text ends at \"If no,\" and the continuation cannot be read.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>34</td>\n<td>Home Address</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>35</td>\n<td>Home Phone</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>36</td>\n<td>Work Address</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>37</td>\n<td>Work Phone</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Responsible parties and narrative (fields 38–42)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Field</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>38</td>\n<td>Print Name of Reporting Security Officer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>38a</td>\n<td>Signature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>39</td>\n<td>Supervisor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>Police Officer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>41</td>\n<td>Badge No.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>42</td>\n<td>Narrative: a) Incident Description; b) Action Taken; c) Notifications Made</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Field 43. Examples of Incidents (21)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Letter</th>\n<th>Incident</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a</td>\n<td>access denial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>b</td>\n<td>accidents/damage auto</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>c</td>\n<td>assaults, physical/verbal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>d</td>\n<td>bomb threats</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e</td>\n<td>disorderly conduct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>f</td>\n<td>alarm perimeter (door)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>g</td>\n<td>illegal weapon (concealed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>h</td>\n<td>facility/structural damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>i</td>\n<td>fire alarm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>j</td>\n<td>medical emergencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>k</td>\n<td>personal injuries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>l</td>\n<td>sexual assaults (exposure)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>m</td>\n<td>suspicious activities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>n</td>\n<td>stuck elevator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>o</td>\n<td>thefts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>p</td>\n<td>trespassing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>q</td>\n<td>unauthorized entry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>r</td>\n<td>unleashed animals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>s</td>\n<td>vandalism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>t</td>\n<td>domestic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u</td>\n<td>other</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Security Incident Report — Sample 2</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Pending: sample 2 (p. 81) is an image. Only scattered fragments are extracted (\"ATI\", \"ANCH\", \"Uniformed\", \"otec\", \"Typ\", \"de\", \"W\"). Its fields cannot be listed without seeing the page.</li>\n<li>Page 82 is blank.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Relationship to the Chapter Text</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Consistent:</strong> section 1.13.1 says that the security force's incident report usually starts the incident investigation. The appendix says that these reports are often the initial complaint.</li>\n<li><strong>Consistent:</strong> both discuss formats for commercial and government environments.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the catalog in field 43 does not match the sample guideline in 1.13.1.<ul>\n<li>The form does not capture unknown loss, safety hazard, unsecured area, employee or executive misconduct, fraud, loss of proprietary information, or contract irregularities.</li>\n<li>It includes types that the guideline does not mention: bomb threats, stuck elevator, unleashed animals, fire alarm, and access denial.</li>\n<li>It is not a direct contradiction, because both lists are examples.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e1\">Appendix 2A — Chronological Report</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> the operative's basic daily report in chronological or timeline format. The operative writes it at the end of the shift.</p>\n<p><strong>Header and fields:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Example value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Issuer</td>\n<td>The XYZ Investigation Company / Daily Report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marking</td>\n<td>*** CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION ***</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agent</td>\n<td>A1234B (a code, not a name)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assignment</td>\n<td>000-000-000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day / Date</td>\n<td>Thursday, 08/20/20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing</td>\n<td><strong><em>END OF REPORT</em></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Entries</strong> (24-hour time, in capital letters, in the third person \"OPERATIVE\"):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0600</td>\n<td>Arrives and clocks in.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0745</td>\n<td>On a break, sees Ben Richards (employee, suspect #1) and Mike Miller (employee, suspect #2) meet suspiciously.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1000</td>\n<td>Unscheduled break. It is said that slacking is no longer possible because supervisor Chris Watts (employee, suspect #3) is back. Watts returned from vacation on Wednesday 08/19/20. Watts said there would be a 12-hour shift on Friday.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1200</td>\n<td>Clocks out and goes to lunch with Mike Kilner (employee).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1310</td>\n<td>Leaves his area to talk with John (LNU, last name unknown; janitor).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1315</td>\n<td>Returns to his area. It was not break time; the normal break is from 1345 to 1355.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1315</td>\n<td>Buys marijuana from Ronny Sanders (employee, suspect #7). Refers to \"Incident Report #7\".</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1450</td>\n<td>Cleans his area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1500</td>\n<td>Clocks out and leaves.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1530</td>\n<td>Goes to J.J.'s Bar &amp; Grill and drinks beer with several employees.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1600</td>\n<td>Leaves J.J.'s.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>What it teaches as a model:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The facts go in logical sequence and each incident is found quickly by the time.</li>\n<li>It labels each person (employee, suspect #n) and refers to separate incident reports.</li>\n<li>It uses the third person (\"operative\"), but in telegraphic style. That is why the book says that going from 2A to 2C would require a lot of \"red ink.\"</li>\n<li>Compare with the rule of avoiding vague terms: \"suspiciously\" does not describe what was seen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figures:</strong> shift from 0600 to 1500; normal break from 1345 to 1355; 12-hour shift announced for Friday; suspects #1, #2, #3, and #7; Incident Report #7.</p>\n<p><strong>Contradictions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 2A says the operative bought marijuana from Ronny Sanders at 13:15. 2B, at that time, says Sanders sold to Mike Lance and the operative only watched.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 2A puts lunch at 12:00; 2B records off the clock from 10:00 to 11:00.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 2A presents the 10:00 event as a talk during an unscheduled break. 2B presents it as an announcement by Watts to the entire production department.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 2A calls Mike Miller \"employee, suspect #2.\" 2B identifies him as a production supervisor.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> it says the normal break is from 13:45 to 13:55, but it records a break at 07:45 without marking it as unscheduled.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> it refers to an \"Incident Report #7,\" a type of report that the chapter does not describe.</li>\n<li>2A does not include the absenteeism conversation at 14:10 that is in 2B.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e2\">Appendix 2B — Narrative Report</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> the operative's daily report in structured narrative format. It is one of the most popular formats. It is chronological, with more detail, a conversational tone, and in the third person.</p>\n<p><strong>Header and fields:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Case Name</td>\n<td>Diamond Back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Case Number</td>\n<td>000-000-000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date</td>\n<td>Thursday, August 20, 2020</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On Clock</td>\n<td>6:00 a.m.–3:00 p.m. (8.0)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Off Clock</td>\n<td>10:00 a.m.–11:00 a.m. (1.0)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Off Clock</td>\n<td>3:00 p.m.–4:00 p.m. (1.0)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Off Clock</td>\n<td>0.5 (report)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expenses</td>\n<td>Social Activities: $20 for beer and tip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Title</td>\n<td>OPERATIVE DAILY REPORT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing</td>\n<td>Submitted by: Operative code name / <strong><em>End of Report</em></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Entries</strong> (each is an independent paragraph with a thematic heading):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Heading</th>\n<th>Time and place</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Suspected Drug Activity</td>\n<td>Around 7:45 a.m., production area</td>\n<td>Ben Richards (insertion operator, temporary) leaves his machine accompanied by Mike Miller (production supervisor). They enter the supply room and close the door for about 3 minutes. Richards returns to his machine and Miller leaves the department. The operative plans to talk with Richards the next day.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Message from Management</td>\n<td>Around 10:00 a.m., production area</td>\n<td>Chris Watts (floor supervisor) announces a mandatory 12-hour shift on Friday, 21-Aug-2020, due to excess orders. He warns that no one should be absent or late. About 15 employees complain. Watts says it is normal to work long hours in the first quarter of the year.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>False Time Records</td>\n<td>Around 1:10 p.m., production area</td>\n<td>John (last name unknown, janitor, temporary) is seen punching three time cards. He says they belong to other janitors who were arriving late. No further questions are asked about his attitude. Description: white male, about 22 years old, 6'1\", 200 pounds, short brown hair, brown eyes, blue janitor coveralls and white sneakers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marijuana Sale</td>\n<td>Around 1:15 p.m., receiving department</td>\n<td>Ronny Sanders (receiving employee) hands 1/16 ounce of \"bud\" (marijuana) to Mike Lance (forklift operator) for $20, in two $10 bills. Lance puts it in his right pocket and leaves through dock door 11. Sanders says Lance buys from him at least once a week at work. Tony Lucas (receiving manager) calls Sanders to dock door 14 and the conversation is cut off.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Absenteeism</td>\n<td>Around 2:10 p.m., production area</td>\n<td>John says he will call in sick on Saturday, 22-Aug-2020, to go elk hunting with friends who are not employees. He says he is often absent. His physical description is repeated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sabotage</td>\n<td>Around 3:30 p.m., J.J.'s Bar &amp; Grill (Cedar Springs, Colorado); dated \"Thursday, 26-Aug-2005\"</td>\n<td>Kenny Sanchez (truck driver) says that he and two other drivers often puncture tires so they do not have to go to certain destinations. He has done it 3 times in 12 months. He changes the subject when Randy Clark (shipping manager) sits down.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>What it teaches as a model:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Third person (\"the operative\") or passive voice (\"was observed\"): it can be edited without having to be authenticated again.</li>\n<li>Each paragraph is independent and carries a thematic heading: it can be grouped and ordered by topic in the summary.</li>\n<li>Each paragraph opens with the date, approximate time, and place. It answers who, what, when, where, how, and why.</li>\n<li>Complete physical descriptions and concrete data (quantity, price, bills, doors): it avoids vague terms.</li>\n<li>It records hours on and off the clock, report-writing time, and expenses. That is useful for billing.</li>\n<li>It captures facts unrelated to the objective (false time punches, absenteeism, sabotage): this is the unexpected information that planning must anticipate.</li>\n<li>Each entry ends by saying that no further information was obtained.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figures:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Schedule: 8.0 hours on the clock (6:00 to 15:00 is 9 hours, minus 1.0 off the clock). Two off-clock blocks of 1.0 hour. 0.5 hours for the report. Expense of $20.</li>\n<li>Events: 12-hour shift announced; about 15 employees complaining; about 3 minutes in the room; three cards; John about 22 years old, 6'1\", and 200 pounds.</li>\n<li>Marijuana sale: 1/16 ounce for $20 in two $10 bills; dock doors 11 and 14; purchases \"at least once a week.\"</li>\n<li>Sabotage: 3 punctures in 12 months; two other drivers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contradictions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the \"Sabotage\" entry is dated \"Thursday, August 26, 2005\"; the report is from Thursday, 20-Aug-2020. It is a typo in the example.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Watts says it is normal to work long hours \"in the first quarter of the year,\" but the report is from August.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> according to 2B, the operative saw a sale from Sanders to Lance. According to 2A, the operative bought from Sanders.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> it marks off the clock from 10:00 to 11:00 a.m. But \"Message from Management\" places the operative in the production area at 10:00. And 2A puts lunch at 12:00.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text says that some investigators begin each paragraph with an alphanumeric code. 2B uses thematic headings, not codes.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the case is called \"Diamond Back\" with number 000-000-000. In 2C and 2D it is \"Diamond-Back\" with number 000-000-0000.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e3\">Appendix 2C — Investigative Summary Report</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> the summary that the project manager prepares for the client. It replaces the daily reports. It gathers the information from the dailies and arranges it by topic, in the third person.</p>\n<p><strong>Structure and fields:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Addressee</td>\n<td>The Very Big XYZ Company, Any Street, Any Town, USA 55555</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference</td>\n<td>Re: Diamond-Back, 000-000-0000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Title</td>\n<td>INVESTIGATIVE SUMMARY REPORT / *** CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION ***</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facility</td>\n<td>Cedar Springs facility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Period</td>\n<td>From Monday 17-Aug-2020 to Friday 21-Aug-2020</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Sale of cocaine and \"talk\" about marijuana use and sale. Also physical and workplace security issues (security/safety), employee misconduct, and company-employee relations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Results block</td>\n<td>\"To date our efforts have yielded\" and \"Additionally, we have identified\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thematic sections</td>\n<td>COCAINE SALE; SUSPECTED DRUG ACTIVITY</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing</td>\n<td><strong><em>End of Report</em></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Cumulative results:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cocaine purchases during working hours and on company premises</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cocaine purchases during working hours but off company premises</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marijuana purchases during working hours and on company premises</td>\n<td>7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employees who use cocaine during working hours and on company premises</td>\n<td>16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employees who use marijuana during working hours and on company premises</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Cocaine Sale (Tuesday 18-Aug-2020): sequence and chain of custody</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Around 11:35</td>\n<td>In the rail assembly area, Ben Richards (insertion operator, temporary) signals to the operative to go out through the door behind the \"rail blaster.\" He takes a cigar wrapper out of his left shirt pocket and empties out a large white rock wrapped in cellophane. He says it is so pure it can be smoked in a pipe as is. He says it is better than the \"stuff\" (cocaine) he sold him on Friday 7-Aug-2020.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Around 11:40</td>\n<td>The operative takes possession of the cocaine.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Around 11:45</td>\n<td>He puts it in an evidence bag left to him by Special Agent Kenny Berger. He locks it in the evidence box in the trunk of his car. He goes to First State Bank to meet Berger.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Around 12:00</td>\n<td>He hands over the locked box. Berger opens it, takes out the bag, inspects it, and gives him a receipt (Exhibit A). He returns the box to him open. He lets him photograph the package after examining it (Exhibit C). Berger keeps the only key to the box.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Around 12:10</td>\n<td>The operative leaves the bank parking lot and returns to the plant.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Around 12:35</td>\n<td>In the rail assembly area he pays Richards $300 and closes the purchase. He uses three $100 bills from the Colorado State Police Narcotics Task Force. Serial numbers: AB67547008I, AL04508146D, and AJ35735194A (Exhibit B).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Suspected Drug Activity:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thursday 13-Aug-2020, around 11:00 a.m., rail assembly. Richards says that Chris Watts (floor supervisor) uses crystal methamphetamine because it leaves the body in two or three days.</li>\n<li>He adds that when Watts cut his hand at work and was given stitches, he was not given a drug test like other injured workers.</li>\n<li>Thursday 20-Aug-2020, around 7:45 a.m. It repeats almost word for word the \"Suspected Drug Activity\" entry from 2B: Richards and Miller about 3 minutes in the supply room. It removes the sentence about the operative's follow-up.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What it teaches as a model:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It reorders the information by topic, not by day. That makes it more readable for the team.</li>\n<li>The third-person text of the dailies carries over into the summary without being rewritten (compare with 2B).</li>\n<li>It begins with totals in figures that are useful for decision-making.</li>\n<li>It shows how to coordinate a drug purchase with the police: evidence bag, locked box, handover to the agent, receipt, photo, sole key, and bills with recorded serial numbers.</li>\n<li>It applies the guidelines \"coordinate with law enforcement\" and \"avoid actual drug use\": the operative does not smoke it.</li>\n<li>It uses Exhibits (A, B, and C) as evidentiary enclosures.</li>\n<li>Compare with the guideline \"buy down, not up\": the example does not give the weight, only speaks of a \"large rock\" for $300.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contradictions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the stated period is from 17 to 21 August, but it includes an event from 13 August.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> it covers five working days, but the text says the typical summary is made after 10 consecutive days of work.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the introduction speaks only of \"talk\" about marijuana, but the totals include 7 marijuana purchases.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> 2D describes the same cocaine purchase dated Tuesday 11-Aug-2020; 2C says Tuesday 18-Aug-2020.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the case number has 10 digits (000-000-0000), versus 9 in 2A and 2B.</li>\n<li>Nuance: the text (2.6.2) says the file name must not carry a code name or case number. The sample reports do show them in the header; the rule is about the file name.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e4\">Appendix 2D — Special Report</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a report on a special or out-of-the-ordinary event. It has no fixed format. According to the text, it was made easily from 2C. If it may serve as criminal evidence, the operative reviews and signs it.</p>\n<p><strong>Structure and fields:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Addressee</td>\n<td>The Very Big XYZ Company, Any Street, Any Town, USA 55555</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference</td>\n<td>Re: Diamond-Back, 000-000-0000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Title</td>\n<td>SPECIAL REPORT #16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Body</td>\n<td>Chronological account of the cocaine purchase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing</td>\n<td><strong><em>End of Report</em></strong> + signature line \"Confidential Informant #1\" and \"Date\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Content:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the same event, with the same sequence of times, as the \"Cocaine Sale\" section of 2C: 11:35, 11:40, 11:45, 12:00, 12:10, and 12:35.</li>\n<li>Same $300 in three $100 bills, same serial numbers, and same Exhibits A, B, and C.</li>\n<li>Changes compared with 2C:<ul>\n<li>Date: Tuesday 11-Aug-2020 (in 2C, 18-Aug-2020).</li>\n<li>The operative is called \"Confidential Informant #1\" instead of \"the operative.\"</li>\n<li>It adds the description of Richards: Caucasian male, 5'11\", 200 pounds, shoulder-length brown hair, blue eyes, and a large blue dragon tattoo on the left forearm.</li>\n<li>It includes a line for the informant's signature and date.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What it teaches as a model:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A special event is documented separately, ready to be used as evidence.</li>\n<li>It is put together by reusing the third-person text of the summary.</li>\n<li>The operative signs it, here as a confidential informant, when it may be criminal evidence.</li>\n<li>It includes a complete physical description of the suspect, useful for identifying him.</li>\n<li>Special reports are numbered in sequence (#16).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contradictions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the same event has a different date than in 2C (11 versus 18 August 2020).</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text says 2D comes \"easily\" from 2C, but it changes the date, changes the operative's name, and adds the description.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the chapter does not explain why the operative comes to be called \"Confidential Informant #1.\"</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e5\">Appendix 3A — Sample Due Diligence Investigation Checklist</h3><p>Suggested checklist for acquiring a company (22 categories).</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Items</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Corporate Organization</td>\n<td>Articles of incorporation · Bylaws · Recent changes in corporate structure · Shareholder list (shares outstanding and percentage of each, plus stock option or stock appreciation rights plans) · Parent, subsidiaries, and affiliates · Shareholder agreements · Minutes of board meetings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business Information</td>\n<td>Product offering · Depreciation method · Patents · Management information system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business Plan</td>\n<td>Most recent five-year business plan · Previous business plan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capital Expenditures</td>\n<td>Last five years · Gross five-year projection · Detailed list of priorities, with all possible analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Competition</td>\n<td>By product line: name, address, phone, company size, size of the competing unit, market share, competitive advantages and disadvantages · Industry publications: name, address, and phone numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contracts</td>\n<td>Main contracts by product line, with their terms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Debt and Leases</td>\n<td>Lender · Terms · Interest rate · Payment schedule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employee Relations</td>\n<td>Unions: name of the union, name of the local president, address, phone, and copy of the contract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Environmental Liabilities</td>\n<td>Description · Environmental studies · Projected five-year remediation cost · Projected five-year compliance cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Equipment</td>\n<td>List · Age · Appraisal (orderly liquidation value and replacement value)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financial Statements</td>\n<td>Five years of audited statements: income statement, balance sheet, cash flow, and changes in equity · Internal monthly statements for the current year · Most recent five-year projections · Monthly sales projections with seasonality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Information Technology &amp; Security</td>\n<td>IT policies and procedures · Electronic records retention policies · Cybersecurity infrastructure · Other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insurance</td>\n<td>Property · Liability · Workers' compensation · Other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Last Corporate Transactions</td>\n<td>Description of each · Purchase and sale agreement for each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Litigation</td>\n<td>Current: description and possible damages · Potential: description and possible damages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management</td>\n<td>Organization chart · Résumés · Ownership interest · Compensation and contracts · Performance evaluation criteria · Profit-sharing policies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marketing</td>\n<td>Pricing strategy · Patents · Distribution channels · Promotion tactics · Customer base (top 10 by product line, with their volume) · Market share by product line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operating Data</td>\n<td>Accounts receivable: turnover or days, aging, credit control and policy, seasonality · Inventory: turnover or days, obsolescence policy, sources of supply, valuation method · Backlog: by product line, five-year history, current, seasonal issues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pension Plans</td>\n<td>Funding status · Balance sheet treatment · Projected ten-year cash expense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Property</td>\n<td>Description · Recent acquisitions or spin-offs · Expansion plans</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retiree Medical Benefits Liabilities</td>\n<td>Funding status · Balance sheet treatment · Projected ten-year cash expense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tax Status</td>\n<td>Historical tax rate · Net operating losses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> \"Patents\" appears twice, in Business Information and in Marketing.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e6\">Appendix 3B — Securities Regulatory Bodies</h3><p>Selected National Bodies. URLs are omitted.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Country or territory</th>\n<th>Body</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Australia</td>\n<td>Australian Securities and Investments Commission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brazil</td>\n<td>Comissão de Valores Mobiliários</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canada</td>\n<td>Canadian Securities Administrators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>France</td>\n<td>Autorité des marchés financiers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Germany</td>\n<td>Bundesbank and German Financial Supervisory Authority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Greece</td>\n<td>Hellenic Capital Market Commission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hong Kong</td>\n<td>Securities and Futures Commission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hungary</td>\n<td>Hungarian Financial Supervisory Authority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>India</td>\n<td>Securities and Exchange Board of India</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Italy</td>\n<td>Commissione Nazionale per le Società e la Borsa</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Japan</td>\n<td>Financial Services Agency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jersey</td>\n<td>Jersey Financial Services Commission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lithuania</td>\n<td>Securities Commission of the Republic of Lithuania</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malta</td>\n<td>Malta Financial Services Authority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mexico</td>\n<td>Comisión Nacional Bancaria y de Valores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Netherlands</td>\n<td>Authority for the Financial Markets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New Zealand</td>\n<td>Financial Markets Authority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poland</td>\n<td>Polish Financial Supervision Authority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portugal</td>\n<td>Comissão do Mercado de Valores Mobiliários</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>South Africa</td>\n<td>Financial Sector Conduct Authority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spain</td>\n<td>Comisión Nacional del Mercado de Valores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Switzerland</td>\n<td>Swiss Financial Market Supervisory Authority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Turkey</td>\n<td>Capital Markets Board of Turkey</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>United Kingdom</td>\n<td>Financial Services Authority</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>United States</td>\n<td>U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>United States</td>\n<td>U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>There are 26 bodies in 25 countries or territories. The U.S. has two.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in 3.5.1 the text calls this appendix a list of \"security regulatory bodies.\" The title says \"Securities.\"</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the United Kingdom body (Financial Services Authority) may be out of date. Possible typo.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e7\">Appendix 3C — Selected International Bodies</h3><p>URLs are omitted.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Bank for International Settlements</li>\n<li>European Securities and Markets Authority</li>\n<li>Financial Stability Board</li>\n<li>International Accounting Standards Board</li>\n<li>International Organization of Securities Commissions</li>\n<li>International Federation of Accountants</li>\n<li>International Finance Corporation</li>\n<li>International Monetary Fund</li>\n<li>North American Securities Administrators Association</li>\n<li>Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development</li>\n<li>The World Bank</li>\n<li>World Federation of Exchanges</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e8\">Appendix 4A — Sample Application Form</h3><p>This is a sample form from a security guard company in the U.S. Header: \"Please Print Clearly.\"</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Section</th>\n<th>Fields</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>General information</td>\n<td>Name (last, first, middle) · Date · Name changes? · Date you can start · Position applied for · Current address (street, city, state, ZIP code) · Time at that address · Phone · Can you legally work in the U.S.? (Yes/No) · Are you 18 or older? Over 21? · Have you worked for us before? (Yes/No) · How did you hear about us? · Experience, skills, or qualities of special value for the position · Pay you need at the start ($ per hour)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How to contact you?</td>\n<td>Email · Home phone · Cell phone · Message phone no. 1 (check if it belongs to a friend, neighbor, or relative)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>Three \"High School\" lines, each with city and state, years attended, and \"Did you graduate?\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Military service</td>\n<td>Were you in the U.S. Armed Forces? (Yes/No) · Branch · Dates · Do you have the DD-214? (Yes/No; the book does not explain what it is) · Did you receive military training useful for the position?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical requirements of the security job</td>\n<td>The job involves walking, standing, lifting, climbing ladders and stairs, and prolonged moderate physical activity. Also night and weekend work in bad weather, close attention to detail, and quick and effective response to emergencies. The applicant is asked to indicate any reason why he or she cannot meet them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Previous employment</td>\n<td>All positions in the last 10 years, from the most recent to the oldest. Includes temporary jobs, contract work, and unpaid volunteering. Use another sheet if needed. Explain all gaps. Columns: employer · phone/address · from (mm/dd/yy) · to (mm/dd/yy) · position · reason for leaving · May we contact this employer? (Yes/No). Six lines.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Personal references</td>\n<td>Three references who are NOT former employers or relatives: name and occupation · email · years known · phone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consent for background investigation</td>\n<td>The company does a rigorous check of all candidates. It may include tests of all the requirements for the guard license and for the job: criminal history, credit, drugs, driving records (DMV), Social Security number verification, references, and physical condition. All in accordance with the law and contracts with clients. The candidate gives consent: name, signature, and date.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criminal history</td>\n<td>Note: they are requested only because the licensing department requires it for the guard license. They are not used against the applicant unless they leave him or her without a license. Question: do you have open criminal cases or were you ever convicted? Includes pleading guilty or being found guilty, even with a deferred or suspended sentence already completed (Yes/No). If the answer is yes: details and official court documents (court, offense code, classification, plea or verdict, sentence, and final disposition). Explanation, name, signature, and date.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Driver's license and identification</td>\n<td>Name as it appears on the license (first, middle, last) · License number · State · Expiration date · Address on the license · Social Security number · Email · Date of birth · Name and current address</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application Agreement</td>\n<td>Certifies that the data is correct to the best of his or her knowledge · Falsifying it is grounds for dismissal · \"At will\" employment: the company or the employee can end it at any time, with or without cause or notice, paying only what has already been earned · Only the chairman of the board or the CEO can agree otherwise, in writing and signed · Authorizes contacting references about work habits, skills, and professional conduct, unless otherwise indicated · Authorizes the background investigation by the company and its agents: employment, education, driving history, criminal history (as required by the license), and public records · Signature and date · Application reviewer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Contradictions with the chapter text:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>What the chapter says</th>\n<th>What the form does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>FCRA authorization</td>\n<td>The notice and written authorization must be separate from the application.</td>\n<td>It puts the consent and authorization inside the application.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ban-the-Box</td>\n<td>Many places prohibit asking about criminal history on the application until a conditional offer.</td>\n<td>It asks about convictions on the application itself, justifying it by the guard license.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope of the criminal question</td>\n<td>Convictions, guilty pleas, and nolo contendere, over a period (for example 7 or 10 years).</td>\n<td>It asks about any conviction \"ever,\" with no time limit or mention of nolo contendere. It does include deferred or suspended sentences.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A conviction alone does not disqualify</td>\n<td>The application must say that a conviction is not an automatic rejection.</td>\n<td>It says it will not be used against the applicant unless it prevents the license. This matches only in part.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Omissions and falsehoods</td>\n<td>The application must say that omitting or falsifying something material ends the process when it is discovered.</td>\n<td>It only says that falsifying is grounds for dismissal. It does not mention omissions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope of the authorization</td>\n<td>It must also be valid for future screenings for retention, promotion, or change of position.</td>\n<td>It does not mention this.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Addresses</td>\n<td>It asks for all previous addresses in the period under review.</td>\n<td>It only asks for the current address, how long the applicant has lived there, and the one on the license.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>For each level: school and address, program, dates, final status, degree, grade average, and reason for withdrawal.</td>\n<td>Only three high school lines, with years attended and whether the applicant graduated. There is no higher education.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Licenses and certifications</td>\n<td>They must be requested and verified with the issuer.</td>\n<td>There is no section, except for the driver's license.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data on each job</td>\n<td>Starting and final position, and name and phone of the last direct supervisor.</td>\n<td>A single position field and no supervisor. The 10-year period does fit the text (5, 7, or 10).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>References</td>\n<td>At least three, known for a year or more, not relatives. Work and home addresses, and how they know the applicant. A former boss is an excellent reference.</td>\n<td>It excludes former employers. It does not ask for addresses, or how they know the applicant, or a minimum of one year. It adds the occupation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Military service</td>\n<td>Period, branch, type of discharge, and rank at discharge. Ask whether the discharge was honorable or dishonorable.</td>\n<td>It asks for branch, dates, whether the applicant has the DD-214, and training. It does not ask for type of discharge or rank.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Restricted questions</td>\n<td>In the U.S., Australia, and Canada, certain questions about age, disability, and other data are prohibited.</td>\n<td>It asks whether the applicant is 18 or 21, the date of birth, and whether he or she can meet physical requirements.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e9\">Appendix 4B — Sample Preemployment Reference Check</h3><p>Form addressed to a former employer of the candidate.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Options or scale</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Header</td>\n<td>The person named you as a former employer and authorized \"our security company\" to request his or her history. Please return it by fax, mail, or email as soon as possible. The information is strictly confidential.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Name of respondent</td>\n<td>Optional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Title of respondent</td>\n<td>Optional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date</td>\n<td>Open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did [Employee] work for [former employer]?</td>\n<td>Yes / No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dates of employment (if the answer was yes)</td>\n<td>From <strong>/</strong>/__ to <strong>/</strong>/__</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reason for separation</td>\n<td>Open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Would he or she be rehired?</td>\n<td>Yes / No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Job performance</td>\n<td>Good / Fair / Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attendance</td>\n<td>Good / Fair / Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Punctuality</td>\n<td>Good / Fair / Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Professionalism</td>\n<td>Good / Fair / Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cooperation with others</td>\n<td>Good / Fair / Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Honesty</td>\n<td>Good / Fair / Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attitude</td>\n<td>Good / Fair / Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comments</td>\n<td>Open</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>There are seven items, all with the same three-level scale.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in 4.6.2 the text asks not to ask questions that make the source pass judgment. This form asks the source to rate honesty and attitude.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> according to 4.1, reference checking covers character, attendance, knowledge, skills, and abilities. The form does not ask about knowledge, skills, or abilities.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e10\">Appendix 6A — Crime Lab Organizations</h3><p><strong>Criteria for choosing a laboratory or a provider</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assess the credibility, reputation, and reliability of the laboratories and of the personnel or companies that process and examine the evidence.</li>\n<li>Also assess the provider's specialty, capabilities, or area of expertise.</li>\n<li>Vet potential providers well before they are needed.</li>\n<li>In the middle of an investigation there is usually no time to investigate, do due diligence, and choose a provider.</li>\n<li>One way to find a professional laboratory is through an accreditation or professional organization.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Organizations cited in the book (9)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Organization</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>American Academy of Forensic Sciences</td>\n<td>Apply science to law</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>American Board of Forensic Entomology</td>\n<td>Promote the use of insect evidence to clarify matters of legal interest, often a crime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>American Society of Crime Laboratory Directors</td>\n<td>Pursue excellence in forensic science through leadership and innovation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Association for Crime Scene Reconstruction</td>\n<td>Encourage the exchange of information and procedures useful for reconstructing crime scenes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Canadian Society of Forensic Science</td>\n<td>Maintain professional standards and promote the study of forensic science</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The Chartered Society of Forensic Sciences</td>\n<td>United Kingdom organization that applies science to the cause of justice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>International Association for Identification</td>\n<td>Support those who work in forensic identification, investigation, and scientific examination of physical evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>International Association of Arson Investigators</td>\n<td>Promote fire investigation standards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>International Crime Scene Investigators Association</td>\n<td>Assist police who process crime scenes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e11\">Appendix 6B — Sample Policy on Computer Evidence</h3><p><strong>Part 1. Seizure and Disconnection of Computer Equipment</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Isolate the system, photograph it, and document how its computer, telephone, and power connections were found. Confirm that it is not deleting data and look for physical traps.</li>\n<li>Always wear latex gloves when handling computers and related equipment in a criminal investigation.</li>\n<li>Do not use fingerprint powder on the equipment.</li>\n<li>If the computer is off, leave it off.</li>\n<li>If it is on, do not touch the keyboard or let anyone touch it. Note the applications open on the screen. If necessary, the mouse can be moved to wake the screen, but do NOT open files or applications.</li>\n<li>On standalone equipment, do not shut down the system the normal way. Unplug first from the back of the machine and then from the power outlet.\n   - a. If it is a laptop that is on, remove the battery first and then disconnect it from power.\n   - b. On networked systems, a computer crimes investigator from the department will come to help.</li>\n<li>Photograph the back of the computer to record the cable connections.</li>\n<li>Label all cables, indicating which cable goes to which port on each device.</li>\n<li>Photograph and document all connected devices, such as printers.</li>\n<li>Note anything unusual on the computer or on nearby objects, such as loose screws.</li>\n<li>Disconnect all cables and connected devices.</li>\n<li>Remove the storage media and treat them as evidence.</li>\n<li>Put evidence tape over the disk drive openings and over the case. If someone uses a drive or opens the case, the tape will reveal it. Put initials and date on the edge of the tape. That way the drives are preserved until an exact copy is made for analysis, and the original disk is secured as evidence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Part 2. Seizure, Packaging, and Transport of Computer Items or Devices</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Seize other objects that store information: DVDs, CDs, flash memory (drives or cards), disks, tapes, or cartridges.</li>\n<li>Take into account mobile phones, data bank watches, personal digital assistants, pagers, and caller ID devices.</li>\n<li>Search the area for written-down passwords.</li>\n<li>Seize the devices connected to the computer: monitors, keyboards, mice, printers, external drives, cameras, modems, dongles, and power cables.</li>\n<li>Seize the software and its manuals.</li>\n<li>Pack the computers in their original boxes with their foam pieces, if any. If not, use other suitable boxes with bubble wrap or solid foam. Do not use packing peanuts or shredded paper, which generate static electricity and dust.</li>\n<li>Put loose storage media in paper boxes or bags. Do not use plastic bags, because they can generate static electricity.</li>\n<li>Mark each item on the item itself to identify it.</li>\n<li>Seal each package and put initials and date.</li>\n<li>Transport the evidence carefully and with these precautions:<ul>\n<li>a. Carry it inside the passenger compartment of the vehicle. Do not put it in the trunk next to a radio transmitter, which emits radio frequency energy and can damage the media.</li>\n<li>b. Consider turning off the vehicle's radio during transport.</li>\n<li>c. Do not expose the evidence to extreme heat or cold.</li>\n<li>d. Photograph the scene after removing the evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Note the equipment that was not seized.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Step 8 of Part 2 asks to mark each item on the item itself. The chapter asks to try marking the container first, and for firearms it says \"do not mark the item.\"</li>\n<li>Step 2 of Part 1 requires latex gloves. The chapter allows latex or cotton gloves.</li>\n<li>Point 6.b (networked systems) appears within the standalone equipment step, although it deals with a different case.</li>\n<li>Point 10.d (photograph the scene after removing the evidence) appears among the transport precautions, although it is not one of them.</li>\n<li>This policy seizes all peripherals, whereas the chapter asks to send to the laboratory only the central units and the media. Seizing is one thing and sending is another.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"investigations-A-e12\">Appendix — Standards in Security (pp. 294–298)</h3><ul>\n<li>Pending: p. 294 has no extractable text. It is probably the appendix cover page. It should be checked in the book to make sure it contains nothing else.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Definition and Principles</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Standard:</strong> a set of criteria, guidelines, and best practices that improves the quality and reliability of products, services, or processes.</li>\n<li>Thanks to national and international standards, many parts, processes, and systems work regardless of who creates or runs them, who uses them, or where.</li>\n<li>They are created to meet a market need or fill a gap in industry standards.</li>\n<li>They are coordinated through accredited standards developing organizations (SDOs). SDOs bring together stakeholders from the private and public sectors to identify best practices.</li>\n<li>They are the basis of innovation and competitiveness. They provide a common language that drives national and international trade.</li>\n<li><strong>Four development principles:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Principle</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Consensus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Openness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Due process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Transparency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In security, standards help nations, communities, societies, organizations, and individuals protect their assets.</li>\n<li>They also improve their resilience to natural and human-caused threats.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Types of Standards</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Features</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Voluntary standards</td>\n<td>Voluntary but widely adopted. Some are integrated into the regulatory framework of several countries</td>\n<td>ISO (International Organization for Standardization) and national standards institutes. In the U.S., trade or professional associations such as ASIS International (security management standards)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Statutory or regulatory standards</td>\n<td>Mandatory by law. Formal authorities can enforce them</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mixed standards</td>\n<td>The line blurs when a voluntary standard is incorporated into laws or regulations. This happens in fire, construction, and life safety. At other times the standard remains voluntary in theory, but mandatory in practice</td>\n<td>Insurers set the availability and cost of casualty insurance according to the use of approved materials or standardized practices. Contracts include standards as a requirement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Contracts show that the adoption of standards increasingly depends on what the client asks for (client driven).</li>\n<li><strong>ISO areas relevant to asset protection:</strong><ul>\n<li>Facility management.</li>\n<li>Health, environment, and occupational safety.</li>\n<li>Risk management.</li>\n<li>Security and resilience.</li>\n<li>IT and information security.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Other bodies that set standards:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body</th>\n<th>Scope</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>NFPA (National Fire Protection Association, fire protection)</td>\n<td>Not all of its standards are voluntary. Some are incorporated into regulations, such as building codes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UL (Underwriters Laboratories)</td>\n<td>Several standards on security equipment: locks, alarms, and access control systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Illuminating Engineering Society</td>\n<td>Lighting standards and practices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic Industries Association</td>\n<td>Electronic components and products</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>Broad library of security management standards and guidelines (asisonline.org, standards-guidelines section)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Benefits of Standards</h4>\n<p>According to the book, standards make the security professional's work easier. They can fulfill one or all of these functions (complete list, 9):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Put best practices and processes in writing (codify them), and share lessons learned.</li>\n<li>Provide tools to assess threats, risks, vulnerabilities, criticalities, and impacts.</li>\n<li>Define measurement methods.</li>\n<li>Document equipment performance requirements, to ensure its effectiveness and safety.</li>\n<li>Set design requirements so that devices, systems, and infrastructure withstand threats.</li>\n<li>Define effective methods to identify people.</li>\n<li>Increase knowledge and competence.</li>\n<li>Improve information exchange between jurisdictions and interoperability.</li>\n<li>Provide consistency in services.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Management System Standards</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They help organizations improve how they deliver services and run processes.</li>\n<li>They are widely accepted and used in many fields and disciplines.</li>\n<li>The best known:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Standard</th>\n<th>Topic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO 9001</td>\n<td>Quality management systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO 14001</td>\n<td>Environmental management systems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The management systems approach calls for analyzing the requirements of the organization and its stakeholders. Then, defining the processes that lead to success.</li>\n<li>A management system provides the framework for continual improvement. This raises the probability of achieving strategic, operational, tactical, and reputational objectives.</li>\n<li>At the same time it improves the organization's resilience.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The PDCA Cycle (Plan-Do-Check-Act)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the operating principle of management system standards.</li>\n<li>It is a structured method for solving problems, centered on continual improvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>According to the text</th>\n<th>According to Figure A.1</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Plan</td>\n<td>Identify and analyze the organization's problems and assets, and rank them by importance</td>\n<td>Define and analyze an issue and its context</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Do</td>\n<td>Prioritize the next steps, make a detailed action plan, and execute it</td>\n<td>Devise a solution, make a detailed action plan, and execute it systematically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Check</td>\n<td>Verify whether the solutions give results consistent with the plan</td>\n<td>Confirm the results against the plan and identify deviations and problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Act</td>\n<td>If the solutions solve the problems, standardize them across the organization, review the list of problems, and define the new ones. Here the cycle starts again</td>\n<td>Standardize the solution, review and define the next issues</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Figure A.1 and Its Source</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Title and source:</strong> \"Security and Resilience in Organizations and their Supply Chains – Requirements with Guidance,\" ASIS ORM.1-2017.</li>\n<li>Elements of the figure:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Block</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Input</td>\n<td>Stakeholders and Interested Parties → Requirements and Expectations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Center</td>\n<td>Organizational Resilience Management Systems, with the PLAN-DO-CHECK-ACT MODEL cycle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output</td>\n<td>Stakeholders and Interested Parties → Managed Risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phases within the cycle</td>\n<td>The four in the \"Figure A.1\" column of the table above</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Note: the text of the figure came out with a broken font. It was reconstructed from the glyph pattern. It should be checked against the book.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Contradictions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the <strong>Plan</strong> phase, the text speaks of identifying problems and assets and ranking them by importance. The figure speaks of defining and analyzing an issue and its context.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the <strong>Do</strong> phase, the text begins with \"prioritize the next steps.\" The figure begins with \"devise a solution\" and adds executing it \"systematically.\"</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in the <strong>Check</strong> phase, the figure adds \"identify deviations and problems,\" which the text does not mention.</li>\n<li class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text presents PDCA in general, for any management system standard. The figure is the version applied to organizational resilience from the ASIS ORM.1-2017 standard, which the text does not name.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Key Terms</h4>\n<p>standard · standards developing organizations (SDOs) · consensus · openness · due process · transparency · resilience · voluntary standards · statutory/regulatory standards · mixed standards · ISO · NFPA · UL · Illuminating Engineering Society · Electronic Industries Association · casualty insurance · client driven · management system standards · ISO 9001 · ISO 14001 · continual improvement · Plan-Do-Check-Act (PDCA) · ASIS ORM.1-2017 · Organizational Resilience Management Systems · stakeholders and interested parties · managed risk</p>\n<h4>Review Questions</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>What are the four principles with which standards are developed? → Consensus, openness, due process, and transparency.</li>\n<li>What distinguishes a voluntary standard from a statutory or regulatory one? → The statutory one is mandatory by law and is enforced by formal authorities. The voluntary one is not.</li>\n<li>Give an example of a standard that is voluntary in theory but mandatory in practice. → Insurers make casualty insurance conditional on the use of approved materials, or contracts require the standard.</li>\n<li>What are the two best-known management system standards? → ISO 9001 (quality) and ISO 14001 (environmental).</li>\n<li>What is PDCA? → The operating principle of management system standards: solving problems in a structured way with continual improvement.</li>\n<li>What standard is Figure A.1 from? → ASIS ORM.1-2017, Security and Resilience in Organizations and their Supply Chains – Requirements with Guidance.</li>\n</ol>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Appendix 1A — Security Incident Reports"},{"id":"e1","t":"Appendix 2A — Chronological Report"},{"id":"e2","t":"Appendix 2B — Narrative Report"},{"id":"e3","t":"Appendix 2C — Investigative Summary Report"},{"id":"e4","t":"Appendix 2D — Special Report"},{"id":"e5","t":"Appendix 3A — Sample Due Diligence Investigation Checklist"},{"id":"e6","t":"Appendix 3B — Securities Regulatory Bodies"},{"id":"e7","t":"Appendix 3C — Selected International Bodies"},{"id":"e8","t":"Appendix 4A — Sample Application Form"},{"id":"e9","t":"Appendix 4B — Sample Preemployment Reference Check"},{"id":"e10","t":"Appendix 6A — Crime Lab Organizations"},{"id":"e11","t":"Appendix 6B — Sample Policy on Computer Evidence"},{"id":"e12","t":"Appendix — Standards in Security"}],"ojo":[{"s":"Appendix 1A — Security Incident Reports","h":"the catalog in field 43 does not match the sample guideline in 1.13.1."},{"s":"Appendix 2A — Chronological Report","h":"2A says the operative bought marijuana from Ronny Sanders at 13:15. 2B, at that time, says Sanders sold to Mike Lance and the operative only watched."},{"s":"Appendix 2A — Chronological Report","h":"2A puts lunch at 12:00; 2B records off the clock from 10:00 to 11:00."},{"s":"Appendix 2A — Chronological Report","h":"2A presents the 10:00 event as a talk during an unscheduled break. 2B presents it as an announcement by Watts to the entire production department."},{"s":"Appendix 2A — Chronological Report","h":"2A calls Mike Miller \"employee, suspect #2.\" 2B identifies him as a production supervisor."},{"s":"Appendix 2A — Chronological Report","h":"it says the normal break is from 13:45 to 13:55, but it records a break at 07:45 without marking it as unscheduled."},{"s":"Appendix 2A — Chronological Report","h":"it refers to an \"Incident Report #7,\" a type of report that the chapter does not describe."},{"s":"Appendix 2B — Narrative Report","h":"the \"Sabotage\" entry is dated \"Thursday, August 26, 2005\"; the report is from Thursday, 20-Aug-2020. It is a typo in the example."},{"s":"Appendix 2B — Narrative Report","h":"Watts says it is normal to work long hours \"in the first quarter of the year,\" but the report is from August."},{"s":"Appendix 2B — Narrative Report","h":"according to 2B, the operative saw a sale from Sanders to Lance. According to 2A, the operative bought from Sanders."},{"s":"Appendix 2B — Narrative Report","h":"it marks off the clock from 10:00 to 11:00 a.m. But \"Message from Management\" places the operative in the production area at 10:00. And 2A puts lunch at 12:00."},{"s":"Appendix 2B — Narrative Report","h":"the text says that some investigators begin each paragraph with an alphanumeric code. 2B uses thematic headings, not codes."},{"s":"Appendix 2B — Narrative Report","h":"the case is called \"Diamond Back\" with number 000-000-000. In 2C and 2D it is \"Diamond-Back\" with number 000-000-0000."},{"s":"Appendix 2C — Investigative Summary Report","h":"the stated period is from 17 to 21 August, but it includes an event from 13 August."},{"s":"Appendix 2C — Investigative Summary Report","h":"it covers five working days, but the text says the typical summary is made after 10 consecutive days of work."},{"s":"Appendix 2C — Investigative Summary Report","h":"the introduction speaks only of \"talk\" about marijuana, but the totals include 7 marijuana purchases."},{"s":"Appendix 2C — Investigative Summary Report","h":"2D describes the same cocaine purchase dated Tuesday 11-Aug-2020; 2C says Tuesday 18-Aug-2020."},{"s":"Appendix 2C — Investigative Summary Report","h":"the case number has 10 digits (000-000-0000), versus 9 in 2A and 2B."},{"s":"Appendix 2D — Special Report","h":"the same event has a different date than in 2C (11 versus 18 August 2020)."},{"s":"Appendix 2D — Special Report","h":"the text says 2D comes \"easily\" from 2C, but it changes the date, changes the operative's name, and adds the description."},{"s":"Appendix 2D — Special Report","h":"the chapter does not explain why the operative comes to be called \"Confidential Informant #1.\""},{"s":"Appendix 3A — Sample Due Diligence Investigation Checklist","h":"\"Patents\" appears twice, in Business Information and in Marketing."},{"s":"Appendix 3B — Securities Regulatory Bodies","h":"in 3.5.1 the text calls this appendix a list of \"security regulatory bodies.\" The title says \"Securities.\""},{"s":"Appendix 3B — Securities Regulatory Bodies","h":"the United Kingdom body (Financial Services Authority) may be out of date. Possible typo."},{"s":"Appendix 4B — Sample Preemployment Reference Check","h":"in 4.6.2 the text asks not to ask questions that make the source pass judgment. This form asks the source to rate honesty and attitude."},{"s":"Appendix 4B — Sample Preemployment Reference Check","h":"according to 4.1, reference checking covers character, attendance, knowledge, skills, and abilities. The form does not ask about knowledge, skills, or abilities."},{"s":"Appendix 6B — Sample Policy on Computer Evidence","h":""},{"s":"Appendix — Standards in Security","h":"in the <strong>Plan</strong> phase, the text speaks of identifying problems and assets and ranking them by importance. The figure speaks of defining and analyzing an issue and its context."},{"s":"Appendix — Standards in Security","h":"in the <strong>Do</strong> phase, the text begins with \"prioritize the next steps.\" The figure begins with \"devise a solution\" and adds executing it \"systematically.\""},{"s":"Appendix — Standards in Security","h":"in the <strong>Check</strong> phase, the figure adds \"identify deviations and problems,\" which the text does not mention."},{"s":"Appendix — Standards in Security","h":"the text presents PDCA in general, for any management system standard. The figure is the version applied to organizational resilience from the ASIS ORM.1-2017 standard, which the text does not name."}]}};
